  'text',
  $rbq5491${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq5491$::jsonb,
  $rbq63210${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"Opening a Savings Jar","story":"Marta had been dropping coins and the odd note into a large jar since January, aiming at a second-hand camera she had seen for four hundred and forty pounds. In November she lifted the jar down, spread the money on her bed, and counted it twice. She had expected something close to four hundred. The balance was two hundred and sixty. The gap was not a mystery of arithmetic. It was a record of small withdrawals she had promised to replace and then forgotten.\n\nShe could remember a concert ticket, three lunches in a wet week, and a birthday card for her father. Each time she had told herself the jar was only lending the money to her future self. Without a note, the loan had no date and no plan to repay. She took a card, wrote every amount she could honestly recall, and taped it under the lid. New rules went on the same card. Anything taken out would be written on the day, and it would be repaid within seven days from her Saturday wages. Her Saturday job at the florist paid her in cash, which made those repayments easy to keep apart from her bus money. The jar itself moved to a high cupboard, so spending it would require a chair and a second thought.\n\nThen she worked forward instead of backward. If she saved twelve pounds a week and took nothing back, she would reach the camera in fifteen more weeks. She circled that date on a calendar above the desk. While she was stacking the coins again she found a folded note from her younger brother, Leo, with twenty pounds inside. He had written that it was an early birthday gift and that she was not to spend it on lunch. Marta laughed, then added his gift as a separate line under that day's date. She wanted her own target to stay visible beside his kindness, not buried under it. The jar closed on a plan as well as on the cash.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"balance","definition":"The amount of money left when it is counted."},{"word":"withdrawals","definition":"Sums taken out of the money that was saved."},{"word":"repay","definition":"To put back money that was taken."},{"word":"wages","definition":"Money paid to Marta for her Saturday work."},{"word":"calendar","definition":"A chart of dates used to mark a future plan."},{"word":"target","definition":"The amount or aim a person is trying to reach."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How much was in the jar when Marta counted it?","options":["Four hundred and forty pounds","Twenty pounds from her brother","Something close to four hundred pounds","Two hundred and sixty pounds"],"correctAnswer":"Two hundred and sixty pounds","explanation":"After counting twice, Marta found that the balance was two hundred and sixty pounds.","supportingSentence":"The balance was two hundred and sixty.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How soon did the new rule say taken money must be put back?","options":["Within seven days","On the same day it was taken","Within fifteen weeks","Before the end of January"],"correctAnswer":"Within seven days","explanation":"Money taken from the jar had to be written down and repaid within seven days.","supportingSentence":"Anything taken out would be written on the day, and it would be repaid within seven days from her Saturday wages.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What would most likely happen if Marta stopped writing down what she took?","options":["Leo would add twenty pounds every week","Small sums would leave the jar again with nothing to remind her","The florist would stop paying her in cash","The camera would drop in price to the sum in the jar"],"correctAnswer":"Small sums would leave the jar again with nothing to remind her","explanation":"Without a note, her earlier withdrawals were forgotten, so the same gap would be likely to return.","supportingSentence":"Without a note, the loan had no date and no plan to repay.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is true about the fifteen-week plan and Leo's gift?","options":["Marta had already made the plan before she found his money","His gift meant she no longer had to save each week","His gift was one Saturday's pay from the florist","His gift was the sum she would still need after fifteen weeks"],"correctAnswer":"Marta had already made the plan before she found his money","explanation":"She worked out the fifteen weeks and circled the date, and only then found Leo's twenty pounds.","supportingSentence":"If she saved twelve pounds a week and took nothing back, she would reach the camera in fifteen more weeks.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the word balance mean in this story?","options":["The amount of money in the jar when she counted it","The date she circled above the desk","The price she had hoped to see in the jar","A steady way of standing on a chair"],"correctAnswer":"The amount of money in the jar when she counted it","explanation":"The balance is the counted sum left in the jar, which was two hundred and sixty pounds.","supportingSentence":"The balance was two hundred and sixty.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq63210$::jsonb,
  'a290e7eb439684816be208b29f0ba9f34d49568baad72e3e59a1e3d715563286',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '7f949343-3fdf-42c8-ae02-b9906fecc65a'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4637${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":333,"questionCount":5}$rbq4637$::jsonb,
  $rbq66481${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"Printing Photographs at Home","story":"On quiet Sunday afternoons Elena Marquez turned the spare bedroom into a printing room and shut the door on the rest of the day. She had learned photography on her phone, although she preferred the slower pleasure of turning a chosen picture into an object she could hold. A second-hand printer, bought from a neighbour who was leaving the city, stood beside paper of different weights. However, the results depended less on the machine than on her patience, because a hurried sheet always showed the wrong colour or a crooked border.\n\nShe began with three photographs from a coastal trip: a boat at low tide, her brother laughing under a hat, and a long pier in fog. On the screen they looked brighter than they had seemed at the time, so she lowered the contrast and checked the skin tones before printing anything. Although the software offered many corrections, Elena changed only what she could justify. She did not want the pictures to become tidier than the weekend itself. The first sheet came out too dark. She dried it on a rack, adjusted the setting, and tried again.\n\nHer brother had asked for the laughing portrait, and a friend who ran a small cafe had offered wall space if Elena ever showed a series. The offer was flattering, although she was not ready to call a handful of pictures an exhibition. However, printing them at home made the decision feel practical rather than grand.\n\nBy late afternoon three prints lay on the bed, weighted at the corners with clean spoons so the paper would not curl. The boat was the most successful, because the wet sand held detail that the screen had flattened. Elena signed the back lightly in pencil, wrote the date, and slipped the portrait into an envelope for her brother. The pier could wait. She opened the door, let the smell of warm ink escape, and felt the satisfaction of a hobby that ended with something she could give away.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"contrast","definition":"The difference between the light and dark parts of a picture."},{"word":"corrections","definition":"Changes made to improve something."},{"word":"justify","definition":"To show that there is a good reason for a choice."},{"word":"flattering","definition":"Pleasing, because it feels like a compliment."},{"word":"weighted","definition":"Held down by something with weight."},{"word":"satisfaction","definition":"A feeling of being pleased with a result."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What was wrong with the first sheet Elena printed?","options":["It came out completely blank","It was too dark","The boat was missing from it","The paper tore inside the machine"],"correctAnswer":"It was too dark","explanation":"The first sheet came out too dark.","supportingSentence":"The first sheet came out too dark.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Which photograph was the most successful?","options":["The portrait of her brother","The boat at low tide","The view from the spare bedroom","The long pier in the fog"],"correctAnswer":"The boat at low tide","explanation":"The boat was the most successful because the wet sand kept its detail.","supportingSentence":"The boat was the most successful, because the wet sand held detail that the screen had flattened.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Has Elena agreed to hang the pictures in her friend's cafe?","options":["No, because the friend withdrew the offer","Not yet, because a few prints do not feel like a show","Yes, and they are already on the wall","Yes, but only the picture of the pier"],"correctAnswer":"Not yet, because a few prints do not feel like a show","explanation":"The offer pleased her, but she was not ready to call a handful of pictures an exhibition.","supportingSentence":"The offer was flattering, although she was not ready to call a handful of pictures an exhibition.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Elena bother to check the skin tones?","options":["The spoons had left marks on the paper","One of the chosen pictures showed a person","The printer could reproduce nothing except faces","The pier in the fog looked like a face"],"correctAnswer":"One of the chosen pictures showed a person","explanation":"She checked skin tones because the set included her brother laughing under a hat.","supportingSentence":"On the screen they looked brighter than they had seemed at the time, so she lowered the contrast and checked the skin tones before printing anything.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does weighted mean when the prints are lying on the bed?","options":["Judged against other people's pictures","Held down so the paper would stay flat","Signed by pressing very hard with a pencil","Sorted from the lightest sheet to the darkest"],"correctAnswer":"Held down so the paper would stay flat","explanation":"The prints were weighted with spoons so the paper would not curl.","supportingSentence":"By late afternoon three prints lay on the bed, weighted at the corners with clean spoons so the paper would not curl.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":333,"questionCount":5}$rbq66481$::jsonb,
  '7f9493433fdf02c87e02b9906fecc65aed09cf3877fdd311c4507f5917b342ff',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ad8c5fef-77d3-4607-a644-1f48396d0221'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4638${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":325,"questionCount":5}$rbq4638$::jsonb,
  $rbq63012${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"Restoring an Old Radio","story":"The radio had belonged to Marco Benedetti's grandfather, and for years it had sat silent beneath a layer of dust. Marco had always intended to restore it, although he knew little about valves, capacitors, or the patient work of soldering. One wet Saturday he carried the wooden case into the kitchen, spread a cloth over the table, and removed the back panel with a borrowed screwdriver. The components were smaller and more delicate than he had expected. However, a diagram glued inside the lid still showed where each wire belonged, and that diagram gave him more confidence than any instructions he had watched.\n\nHe cleaned the dial first, because the numbers were cloudy and he wanted one visible sign of progress. Although several screws were rusted, they turned eventually, and the speaker cloth, which had sagged, could be tightened with a needle and strong thread. His sister Giulia looked in twice to ask whether the kitchen would smell of hot plastic, and he promised to stop if anything grew warm.\n\nBy evening the case was empty and the table was covered with neat rows. Marco felt proud and slightly alarmed, since taking an object apart is easier than persuading it to work again. He reheated the dull joints, replaced one cracked capacitor from a specialist shop, and checked every connection against the diagram. However, when he switched the set on, there was only a faint hum and a smell of warm dust.\n\nThe next morning he noticed a loose wire behind the tuning knob. He soldered it again, more carefully, and switched the radio on with the volume low. A voice came through the static, thin but unmistakable, reading a weather bulletin as if the set had never been silent. Marco laughed, then lowered the volume so that the voice would not wake Giulia. The repair was far from perfect, although the old radio had found its way back into the life of the house.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"restore","definition":"To bring something back to a working condition."},{"word":"valves","definition":"Parts inside some older radios that help control the signal."},{"word":"capacitors","definition":"Small electrical parts that store a charge."},{"word":"soldering","definition":"Joining metal parts by melting a soft metal onto them."},{"word":"diagram","definition":"A drawing that shows how the parts fit together."},{"word":"static","definition":"A crackling noise that gets in the way of a radio sound."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Who had owned the radio before Marco started work on it?","options":["His sister Giulia","His grandfather","A specialist shop in town","The neighbour who lent a screwdriver"],"correctAnswer":"His grandfather","explanation":"The radio had belonged to Marco's grandfather.","supportingSentence":"The radio had belonged to Marco Benedetti's grandfather, and for years it had sat silent beneath a layer of dust.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did Marco find behind the tuning knob the next morning?","options":["A cracked wooden case","A pool of water","A missing speaker cloth","A loose wire"],"correctAnswer":"A loose wire","explanation":"He noticed a loose wire behind the tuning knob.","supportingSentence":"The next morning he noticed a loose wire behind the tuning knob.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the following morning show about Marco?","options":["He decided the diagram was useless","He took the radio back to the shop","He asked Giulia to finish the job","He was willing to continue after the first failure"],"correctAnswer":"He was willing to continue after the first failure","explanation":"The set had only hummed, but the next morning he soldered the loose wire and tried again.","supportingSentence":"He soldered it again, more carefully, and switched the radio on with the volume low.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How did Giulia seem to feel about the repair?","options":["She was worried the work might smell or get too hot","She told him to move the set to a shop","She wanted to sell the radio immediately","She already knew how to do the repair"],"correctAnswer":"She was worried the work might smell or get too hot","explanation":"Giulia asked about the smell of hot plastic, and Marco promised to stop if anything grew warm, so she was uneasy.","supportingSentence":"His sister Giulia looked in twice to ask whether the kitchen would smell of hot plastic, and he promised to stop if anything grew warm.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does restore mean in Marco's plan for the radio?","options":["Sell it while it is still dusty","Bring it back into working condition","Paint the case a new colour","Take it apart and throw the parts away"],"correctAnswer":"Bring it back into working condition","explanation":"To restore the radio means to make the old set work again.","supportingSentence":"Marco had always intended to restore it, although he knew little about valves, capacitors, or the patient work of soldering.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":325,"questionCount":5}$rbq63012$::jsonb,
  'ad8c5fef77d3960746441f48396d02218f672d2db64efcb50458b4cf03e29f4a',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '1335df83-a6f8-40e9-a9f5-80cb1c8997ed'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5384${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq5384$::jsonb,
  $rbq96926${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"Rewriting the Essay","story":"Marco received his history essay back on Monday with a grade that disappointed him. He had spent a whole weekend on it, although the comments in the margin suggested that effort alone had not been enough. His tutor, Adeyemi, had written that the introduction was promising but the argument drifted. Several paragraphs described events without explaining why they mattered, and there were long quotations where a short one would have been clearer. However, the final note was not unkind, and it said the ideas were worth revising.\n\nAt first Marco wanted to defend every sentence. He told a classmate that the question had been too broad and that the word limit was unfair. By Tuesday his irritation had cooled, so he read the feedback again with a pen and underlined the comments he could act on. Adeyemi had offered a short meeting, which Marco booked. In the tutor's office he was asked to say, in one sentence, what his essay was trying to prove, yet he could not do it. That silence was more useful than another hour of writing.\n\nTogether they built a simple plan. The essay needed a claim, two or three reasons, and evidence that actually supported those reasons, while background information had to be reduced. Words such as many people had to be replaced with precise references. Adeyemi also showed him a paragraph that was coherent and one that was only a list of facts. The difference was the linking sentence at the end, which told the reader how the evidence answered the question. Marco left with a checklist and a deadline of Friday.\n\nHe rewrote the essay in two evenings, cutting almost two hundred words before adding better ones. It was slower than he expected, because deleting his own sentences felt personal. On Friday he submitted the new version and kept the old file so he could see the change. The improved grade mattered, but the clearer method mattered more. Next time, he decided, he would plan the argument before he started typing.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"disappointed","definition":"Unhappy because something was worse than you had hoped."},{"word":"drifted","definition":"Moved gradually away from the main point."},{"word":"revising","definition":"Changing a piece of writing in order to improve it."},{"word":"coherent","definition":"Clear, connected, and easy to follow."},{"word":"precise","definition":"Exact and specific rather than vague."},{"word":"checklist","definition":"A list of points to remember or complete."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What criticism did Adeyemi make of the essay's shape?","options":["The essay was too short to mark","There was no introduction at all","The opening worked, but the argument lost its direction","The grade had been entered by mistake"],"correctAnswer":"The opening worked, but the argument lost its direction","explanation":"Adeyemi wrote that the introduction was promising, but the argument drifted.","supportingSentence":"His tutor, Adeyemi, had written that the introduction was promising but the argument drifted.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do Marco's comments to his classmate reveal?","options":["He was already satisfied with every paragraph","He thought Adeyemi had lost the only copy","He wanted to blame the task before examining his own writing","He had agreed a new question that afternoon"],"correctAnswer":"He wanted to blame the task before examining his own writing","explanation":"He complains that the question was too broad and the word limit unfair, instead of starting with the weaknesses in the essay.","supportingSentence":"He told a classmate that the question had been too broad and that the word limit was unfair.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does coherent mean in this story?","options":["Finished after the deadline","Clear and joined together, so the point is easy to follow","Filled with long quotations","Copied from another student's work"],"correctAnswer":"Clear and joined together, so the point is easy to follow","explanation":"Adeyemi contrasts a coherent paragraph with one that is only a list of facts.","supportingSentence":"Adeyemi also showed him a paragraph that was coherent and one that was only a list of facts.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What did Marco do with the two versions on Friday?","options":["He deleted both files after the meeting","He handed in the new one and kept the old one","He printed only the first draft","He asked a classmate to hand them in"],"correctAnswer":"He handed in the new one and kept the old one","explanation":"He submitted the new version and kept the old file so that he could see what had changed.","supportingSentence":"On Friday he submitted the new version and kept the old file so he could see the change.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Marco value most once the essay has been rewritten?","options":["Keeping the new grade secret from his tutor","Finishing in less time than the first attempt","A clearer way of working, ahead of the higher mark","Avoiding history essays from then on"],"correctAnswer":"A clearer way of working, ahead of the higher mark","explanation":"The better grade matters to him, but he decides that the clearer method matters more.","supportingSentence":"The improved grade mattered, but the clearer method mattered more.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq96926$::jsonb,
  '1335df83a6f8f0e949f580cb1c8997ed7f68c0e6e278e66ce0cc3d282c51c831',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '26650533-4608-4e11-aece-06653ea35197'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4660${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":330,"questionCount":5}$rbq4660$::jsonb,
  $rbq52332${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"Seeds from the Moon Market","story":"The moon market appeared in the lower field only when the moon was full and the grass was dry enough to hold a stall. Harun heard pans ticking as they cooled, and a low argument about prices that were never written down. He was the town cook, and ordinary seeds had given him ordinary soup. A feast for the harbor workers was two days away, and he wanted a flavor that would make tired people lift their heads.\n\nEach packet carried a label that had to be spoken honestly before the seed would grow. Harun was tempted by a gold packet marked with the taste of home. Vesa, the seller, told him to think carefully. Such a seed grew a memory so strong that later meals tasted thin beside it. A feast should comfort people and then let them return to their own kitchens.\n\nHe chose three smaller packets: patience, salt wind, and yellow heat. Vesa repeated the market's other rule. Plant only what you can tend. A neglected moon seed did not die quietly. It spoiled the soil around it and remembered the neglect.\n\nHarun planted salt wind and yellow heat in pots on his steps, where he would see them every morning. He almost left the patience seed in a drawer. Then he thought of the warning and set it in a box by the window, though he doubted that a moon seed could be slow. He was wrong in a useful way. The first two herbs greened by the next night and gave the feast a bright, sea-sharp taste that the workers praised without quite naming. The patience seed did nothing for a week.\n\nOn the eighth morning it put out a single curled leaf. Harun laughed, then stopped and watered it. The label had been literal, which was the market's habit. He had cooked the feast without that leaf, and he kept the plant. Some flavors were not late. They were keeping a promise about time.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"tempted","definition":"Strongly attracted to something that may be unwise."},{"word":"neglected","definition":"Not given the care that is needed."},{"word":"literal","definition":"Matching the exact meaning of the words."},{"word":"praised","definition":"Spoken of with approval."},{"word":"tend","definition":"To look after something so that it can grow."},{"word":"curled","definition":"Bent into a curved shape."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Who was the feast meant to feed?","options":["The sellers in the moon market","Travelers who had missed the full moon","The workers at the harbor","Vesa's neighbors in the lower field"],"correctAnswer":"The workers at the harbor","explanation":"The feast for the harbor workers was two days away.","supportingSentence":"A feast for the harbor workers was two days away, and he wanted a flavor that would make tired people lift their heads.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What risk did Vesa see in the gold packet?","options":["It would grow only after the feast was finished","Ordinary meals afterward would taste poor beside that memory","The seed would spoil every pot on the steps","The label could be spoken only in another town"],"correctAnswer":"Ordinary meals afterward would taste poor beside that memory","explanation":"A memory so strong that later meals tasted thin would make everyday food disappointing.","supportingSentence":"Such a seed grew a memory so strong that later meals tasted thin beside it.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did the patience seed do during its first week?","options":["It did nothing","It spoiled the soil in the window box","It supplied the bright taste for the feast","It became green on the first night"],"correctAnswer":"It did nothing","explanation":"The patience seed did nothing for a week.","supportingSentence":"The patience seed did nothing for a week.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does literal mean about the word on that label?","options":["Secret, so only the seller knows it","Decorative, like the color of a gold packet","Exact, with the word meaning just what it says","False, and used as a joke about the price"],"correctAnswer":"Exact, with the word meaning just what it says","explanation":"The seed named patience waited a full week, so the label meant exactly what it said.","supportingSentence":"The label had been literal, which was the market's habit.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why did Harun plant the slow seed instead of leaving it in the drawer?","options":["He needed its leaf for the feast that same night","The first two packets had already failed","He remembered the warning about failing to care for it","Vesa said seeds kept in drawers grew faster"],"correctAnswer":"He remembered the warning about failing to care for it","explanation":"He thought of the warning and planted it, even though he doubted a moon seed could be slow.","supportingSentence":"Then he thought of the warning and set it in a box by the window, though he doubted that a moon seed could be slow.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":330,"questionCount":5}$rbq52332$::jsonb,
  '266505334608ee111ece06653ea35197c5faa6187fbcbee442658fbc7a380775',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '3f5d8fe4-6a80-494c-a6fa-7bc51044777f'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4994${"topic":"technology","wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq4994$::jsonb,
  $rbq93902${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"Sharing a Family Tablet","story":"The Khan family had one tablet and four people who wanted it after school. Amir needed it for a language app, his sister Leila needed it for a history project, and their parents used it for video calls with grandparents. Arguments started when the battery died before Leila had finished, or when a game filled the storage and the project would not save. Although everyone agreed that the tablet was useful, nobody agreed whose turn it was.\n\nTheir mother, Farah, called a short family meeting on Sunday. She asked each person to name the tasks that truly had to be done on the tablet, in order to separate urgent work from entertainment. Homework and the weekly call had priority. Games were allowed only after those tasks, and only if the battery was above a set level. In addition, each person received a profile with a simple password, so downloads were no longer a surprise. Farah did not want to police every minute. She wanted a rule that the children could apply without a fresh debate.\n\nThe first evenings were clumsy. Amir forgot to sign out, and Leila's project opened under his profile. Even so, the source of the problem was now visible. They could see which app had used the storage, and they removed two large games that nobody had opened for a month. Meanwhile, they started charging the tablet in the kitchen at nine, rather than leaving it in a bedroom. That single change stopped the morning discovery of a dead battery.\n\nA week later, the grandparents' call began on time, and Leila exported her project before dinner. Amir still wanted longer game sessions. However, he admitted that the old habit had caused the worst arguments. The family reviewed the rules on the following Sunday and adjusted only one point. A finished project could borrow fifteen minutes from the next day. It was a compromise, yet it was clear. Sharing a device is easier when time, storage, and charging are planned instead of negotiated in a rush.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"priority","definition":"Something that should be done before other things."},{"word":"profile","definition":"A separate user space on a shared device."},{"word":"storage","definition":"The space a device has for saving files and apps."},{"word":"exported","definition":"Saved or sent a finished file out of an app."},{"word":"compromise","definition":"An agreement in which each side gives up a little."},{"word":"negotiated","definition":"Discussed in the moment until people reach a deal."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When could the children play games?","options":["Only while the grandparents were calling","Whenever the storage space was full","After the important tasks, with enough battery","Before school, with no time limit"],"correctAnswer":"After the important tasks, with enough battery","explanation":"Games came only after the set tasks, and only if the battery was above the agreed level.","supportingSentence":"Games were allowed only after those tasks, and only if the battery was above a set level.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What new evening habit prevented a flat battery in the morning?","options":["Charging the tablet in the kitchen from nine","Ending the Sunday meeting early","Leaving the device in a bedroom all night","Deleting the language app before bed"],"correctAnswer":"Charging the tablet in the kitchen from nine","explanation":"They started charging the tablet in the kitchen at nine, and that stopped the morning discovery of a dead battery.","supportingSentence":"Meanwhile, they started charging the tablet in the kitchen at nine, rather than leaving it in a bedroom.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did removing the unused games make possible?","options":["A longer video call during breakfast","An end to every password on the device","A separate tablet for each person","Space for work that had failed to save"],"correctAnswer":"Space for work that had failed to save","explanation":"A game had filled the storage so a project would not save, and the family then removed two large unused games.","supportingSentence":"They could see which app had used the storage, and they removed two large games that nobody had opened for a month.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the fifteen-minute change show about the family agreement?","options":["Most of it stayed, with one practical adjustment","The whole agreement was cancelled after a week","Only the grandparents had to follow it","Games replaced homework as the first task"],"correctAnswer":"Most of it stayed, with one practical adjustment","explanation":"The family adjusted only one point, which shows the rules mostly remained and could still be tuned.","supportingSentence":"The family reviewed the rules on the following Sunday and adjusted only one point.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"compromise\" mean here?","options":["A game that fills the storage space","A call that begins after dinner","A deal where each side gives something up","A password shared by the whole family"],"correctAnswer":"A deal where each side gives something up","explanation":"The extra fifteen minutes was a compromise: the rules stayed, but a finished project could borrow a little time.","supportingSentence":"It was a compromise, yet it was clear.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq93902$::jsonb,
  '3f5d8fe46a80c94c86fa7bc51044777ffb0bf02e04d93f64f031eac862035875',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

