  'text',
  $rbq4635${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":331,"questionCount":5}$rbq4635$::jsonb,
  $rbq76806${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"The Allotment in August","story":"By August the allotment at the end of Willow Lane had become a full occupation rather than a weekend hobby. Priya Raman arrived before the heat, unlocked the gate, and stood among tomatoes that had outgrown their canes. She had taken the plot in spring, although she had underestimated how quickly weeds, watering, and other people's advice would fill her evenings. Her neighbour Henrik was already picking beans into a crate. However, he did not linger, because a thunderstorm was forecast after lunch and everyone wanted the ripe fruit under cover before then.\n\nPriya began with the courgettes, which had hidden under wide leaves and grown larger than she liked. She cut six, left the smaller ones, and carried them to the shed where her trug and gloves lived. Although the shed was cramped, it held a watering can, a ball of twine, and a notebook recording what she had planted and what had failed. The beans sown too late were thin, yet the potatoes, almost forgotten under their mounds, sounded solid when she pushed in a fork.\n\nAt midmorning Henrik returned with basil and a warning about the communal compost. Someone had added bread, and the heap was attracting wasps. Priya was not the culprit, although she admitted that the rules about scraps still confused her. They agreed to put a clearer sign on the bin, and Henrik offered the basil in exchange for two courgettes. She accepted, partly because her own herbs had dried up during the previous hot week.\n\nWhen distant thunder sounded, Priya picked the reddest tomatoes, watered the pots that would suffer most, and locked the shed. She walked home with soil on her wrists and a bag pulling at her shoulder. The allotment would need her again the next day, and the weeds would not pause for the weather. However, the morning had given her more food than she could eat alone, and she was already deciding which neighbour would welcome a bowl of tomatoes.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"allotment","definition":"A small rented plot used for growing food."},{"word":"canes","definition":"Sticks that support tall plants."},{"word":"underestimated","definition":"Thought something would be easier or smaller than it was."},{"word":"trug","definition":"A shallow garden basket for carrying what you pick."},{"word":"communal","definition":"Shared by a group of people."},{"word":"compost","definition":"Rotted plant material used to improve soil."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How many courgettes did Priya cut at the start of the morning?","options":["Six","None, because they were still tiny","Twelve","Two"],"correctAnswer":"Six","explanation":"She cut six courgettes and left the smaller ones.","supportingSentence":"She cut six, left the smaller ones, and carried them to the shed where her trug and gloves lived.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why were wasps gathering around the compost?","options":["Someone had added bread to the heap","Henrik had covered it with fresh basil","The tomatoes had fallen into the bin","Priya had poured sugar over it"],"correctAnswer":"Someone had added bread to the heap","explanation":"Someone had added bread, and the heap was attracting wasps.","supportingSentence":"Someone had added bread, and the heap was attracting wasps.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did the solid sound under the soil suggest?","options":["Henrik had already taken the crop","The potatoes had developed well","The fork had struck a buried pipe","The beans were hidden in the same place"],"correctAnswer":"The potatoes had developed well","explanation":"The potatoes sounded solid when she pushed the fork in, which suggests they had grown properly.","supportingSentence":"The beans sown too late were thin, yet the potatoes, almost forgotten under their mounds, sounded solid when she pushed in a fork.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Priya pick and water as soon as she heard thunder?","options":["The storm they had expected was arriving","She had to return a tool before noon","Henrik had locked the gate early","The wasps had moved onto the tomatoes"],"correctAnswer":"The storm they had expected was arriving","explanation":"Thunder meant the forecast storm was starting, so she picked the ripest tomatoes and watered the pots that would suffer.","supportingSentence":"When distant thunder sounded, Priya picked the reddest tomatoes, watered the pots that would suffer most, and locked the shed.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does underestimated say about Priya's expectations in spring?","options":["She thought the plot would demand less of her time","She arrived after Henrik every morning","She planted the tomatoes much too late","She refused every offer of advice"],"correctAnswer":"She thought the plot would demand less of her time","explanation":"She underestimated the work, so the weeds, watering, and advice took more of her evenings than she had expected.","supportingSentence":"She had taken the plot in spring, although she had underestimated how quickly weeds, watering, and other people's advice would fill her evenings.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":331,"questionCount":5}$rbq76806$::jsonb,
  'b7a3acd47adfe5e14ff4c47320c46bdcc1e16ea4af6032c7c1ba5a00dc98ef5e',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'eaf08182-a738-41ed-ae54-f203d604ac8b'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4888${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq4888$::jsonb,
  $rbq30322${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"The Block Meeting","story":"The residents of Cedar Court gathered in the ground-floor room on a wet Thursday, where Amina, who had volunteered to chair the meeting, set out chairs and printed a short agenda. The main item was the broken entrance gate, which had been sticking for months. Although everyone wanted it repaired, they disagreed about the cost, because some people thought the management company should pay, while others accepted that the residents' fund would have to cover at least part of the bill.\n\nThe caretaker, Denis, reported that a contractor had quoted a considerable sum. However, the discussion soon became heated. One resident said the gate was only a nuisance for drivers, while parents with pushchairs argued that it was a safety issue because it no longer closed properly at night. Another person blamed the neighbours who held it open with a brick. Amina asked everyone to speak in turn, and she wrote the main points on a board so that complaints would not be repeated endlessly. When voices rose, she reminded them that a proposal had to be specific before anyone could vote on it.\n\nThe second topic was the shared garden, where weeds had taken over the path and the bench was rotten. A few residents offered to work on a Saturday if the block bought gloves, bags, and a few plants, although not everyone could help physically. An older resident named Edith said she would contribute money but could not kneel. They reached a compromise shortly before nine. The gate repair would be approved up to a stated limit, and the management company would be asked in writing to pay the rest. The garden morning would go ahead, with jobs shared according to what people could actually do.\n\nThe vote was not unanimous, yet the majority agreed, and Amina recorded the decision carefully. As people left, the mood was calmer than it had been at the start. A meeting could not fix every frustration in the block, but it had turned vague annoyance into a plan.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"agenda","definition":"A list of subjects to be discussed at a meeting."},{"word":"nuisance","definition":"Something that causes annoyance."},{"word":"proposal","definition":"A clear suggestion put forward for a decision."},{"word":"compromise","definition":"An agreement in which each side gives up part of what it wanted."},{"word":"unanimous","definition":"Agreed by everybody, with nobody against it."},{"word":"frustration","definition":"The feeling of being upset because a problem continues."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Which problem was placed first on the agenda?","options":["Weeds covering the garden path","Noise from the top-floor flat","The entrance gate that had been sticking for months","A broken light in the meeting room"],"correctAnswer":"The entrance gate that had been sticking for months","explanation":"Amina's agenda put the broken entrance gate first, and it had been sticking for months.","supportingSentence":"The main item was the broken entrance gate, which had been sticking for months.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why are the parents more alarmed than the resident who called the gate a nuisance?","options":["A loose brick has torn their pushchairs","They only use the entrance during the daytime","An open gate at night could put families in danger","They want drivers to pay the whole bill alone"],"correctAnswer":"An open gate at night could put families in danger","explanation":"They treat it as a safety problem because it no longer closes properly at night, not merely as an annoyance for drivers.","supportingSentence":"One resident said the gate was only a nuisance for drivers, while parents with pushchairs argued that it was a safety issue because it no longer closed properly at night.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How did the meeting deal with the cost of the gate repair?","options":["The repair would wait until the following year","Denis would mend the gate without charging anyone","Every home would hand over the same fee that night","Residents would pay up to a limit and ask the company to cover the rest"],"correctAnswer":"Residents would pay up to a limit and ask the company to cover the rest","explanation":"The repair was approved only up to a stated limit, and the company would be asked in writing to pay whatever remained.","supportingSentence":"The gate repair would be approved up to a stated limit, and the management company would be asked in writing to pay the rest.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the result of the vote show?","options":["The meeting ended before a vote was possible","Every person in the room wanted exactly the same outcome","Most residents could accept a decision that not everyone shared","Amina refused to write the decision down"],"correctAnswer":"Most residents could accept a decision that not everyone shared","explanation":"The vote was not unanimous, but the majority agreed, so agreement did not require every resident to think the same.","supportingSentence":"The vote was not unanimous, yet the majority agreed, and Amina recorded the decision carefully.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does nuisance mean in this story?","options":["Something that causes annoyance","The person who chairs a meeting","Money kept in a residents' fund","A formal limit on spending"],"correctAnswer":"Something that causes annoyance","explanation":"One resident uses it to say the gate is merely annoying for drivers, not a danger.","supportingSentence":"One resident said the gate was only a nuisance for drivers, while parents with pushchairs argued that it was a safety issue because it no longer closed properly at night.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq30322$::jsonb,
  'eaf08182a73891ed9e54f203d604ac8b2fa36b3c78e0a1501a273166d1bfeb8d',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ea200929-6547-40ab-a5ac-5a9b5998111c'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4562${"topic":"travel","wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq4562$::jsonb,
  $rbq70585${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"The Border Queue","story":"The queue at the mountain border began well before the booths, where coaches, vans, and private cars crept forward in the cold. Adrian Petrov had expected a brief pause to show his passport, although the officer at the information cabin said that inspections were taking much longer than usual. A new rule required drivers to open their luggage, and several vehicles near the front had already stood with their doors wide for half an hour. However, passengers on foot were being processed at a separate window, and Adrian decided that walking the last stretch might save him the worst of the wait.\n\nHe lifted his rucksack and joined a shorter line beside a fence of faded notices. A student named Leila said she had crossed here many times and had never seen the booths so heavily staffed. Although the officers were polite, they asked about cash, gifts, and the purpose of each visit, and anyone with an unclear answer was sent to a bench inside. Adrian checked the envelope in his jacket, where he kept the address of the family he planned to visit and a letter confirming his stay.\n\nWhen his turn came, the officer compared his photograph with his face and asked why he was travelling alone. Adrian said he was spending a week with old friends and that his outward ticket was already booked. The officer nodded, glanced at the letter, and waved him through without opening the rucksack. However, on the far side of the barrier Adrian saw that his bus had not been so fortunate. It remained in the vehicle lane, and the driver was lifting bags onto a table while passengers stood in the cold.\n\nHe watched the bus inch forward and decided that walking had been the better choice, although he could not tell how long the driver would be delayed. Adrian messaged his hosts, warning them not to trust the original arrival time, and he kept his passport in his hand until the bus finally cleared the booth.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"inspections","definition":"Careful official checks."},{"word":"rucksack","definition":"A bag worn on the back."},{"word":"booths","definition":"Small offices where officials examine travellers."},{"word":"staffed","definition":"Supplied with people who are working there."},{"word":"barrier","definition":"A gate or obstacle that people must pass."},{"word":"fortunate","definition":"Lucky, or in a better situation."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the new rule require drivers to do?","options":["Turn around and use another road","Open their luggage","Pay a fee at the information cabin","Leave their vehicles and cross on foot"],"correctAnswer":"Open their luggage","explanation":"A new rule required drivers to open their luggage.","supportingSentence":"A new rule required drivers to open their luggage, and several vehicles near the front had already stood with their doors wide for half an hour.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How long did Adrian say his visit would last?","options":["Two months","The whole winter","A single night","A week"],"correctAnswer":"A week","explanation":"Adrian said he was spending a week with old friends.","supportingSentence":"Adrian said he was spending a week with old friends and that his outward ticket was already booked.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did Adrian's crossing compare with the wait on his bus?","options":["The bus reached the far side before he did","He was already through while their bags were still being checked","The passengers were allowed to stay in their seats","Leila had to go back and wait with the driver"],"correctAnswer":"He was already through while their bags were still being checked","explanation":"Adrian was beyond the barrier, but the bus was still in the vehicle lane with bags being lifted out.","supportingSentence":"It remained in the vehicle lane, and the driver was lifting bags onto a table while passengers stood in the cold.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Adrian message his hosts about the arrival time?","options":["The bus was still delayed after he had crossed","He had lost the address of the house","Leila had asked him to stay at the border","The officer had kept his papers"],"correctAnswer":"The bus was still delayed after he had crossed","explanation":"He warned them not to trust the original time because the bus had not yet cleared the booth.","supportingSentence":"Adrian messaged his hosts, warning them not to trust the original arrival time, and he kept his passport in his hand until the bus finally cleared the booth.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does heavily staffed say about the booths?","options":["Many officers were working there","The windows were closed for repairs","The buildings had just been painted","Travellers were checking their own papers"],"correctAnswer":"Many officers were working there","explanation":"Heavily staffed means a large number of officers were on duty.","supportingSentence":"A student named Leila said she had crossed here many times and had never seen the booths so heavily staffed.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq70585$::jsonb,
  'ea2009296547f0abf5ac5a9b5998111c74528f129519ad12202e074890118964',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '56569b0e-2264-42e3-a2e3-d8af5f495edc'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4876${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":330,"questionCount":5}$rbq4876$::jsonb,
  $rbq88454${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"The Broken Compass","story":"The compass failed on the second morning, not with a crack but with a lazy swing that never settled. Ruth noticed when she checked their bearing at the edge of a pine wood. The needle drifted toward the metal clip on her pack, then toward nothing she could name. A fall the day before had left a pale mark on the case, and she understood that the mark was more than a scratch.\n\nEd, who had trusted the instrument without reading the map, wanted to keep the heading they had used since breakfast. Ruth spread the map on a flat stone and made him look at the land itself. The contours showed a long slope falling toward a river, and that river would lead south to a footbridge marked in red. If they chased a broken needle, they might circle until the light went.\n\nThe old tricks were weaker than the stories. Moss did not grow on only one side of the trunks, because the wood was damp all round. The sun was a pale disc, enough for a rough sense of east, but clouds kept taking it away. What stayed reliable was the slope under their boots. Water, Ruth said, could be believed when a magnet could not.\n\nThey walked downhill and marked each pause with a small pile of cones, so they would know if they crossed their own route. After an hour the ground grew softer, and the air smelled of wet leaves. Then a thin sound became a stream and, soon after, the river on the map. The current moved with a purpose their compass no longer had.\n\nBy midafternoon they reached the footbridge and a path with fresh boot prints. Ed wrapped the compass in a cloth and put it at the bottom of his pack, where it could not pretend to help. They still had a long walk to the road, but they were no longer asking a damaged tool to invent the way.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"bearing","definition":"The direction in which someone is traveling."},{"word":"drifted","definition":"Moved slowly without holding a firm direction."},{"word":"contours","definition":"Lines on a map that show the shape of the land."},{"word":"reliable","definition":"Able to be trusted."},{"word":"current","definition":"The flow of water in a river or stream."},{"word":"invent","definition":"To make something up rather than find the real thing."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where did the map say the downward slope would lead them?","options":["Back to the pine wood they had just left","In a circle until the daylight was gone","South, by a river, to a footbridge marked in red","East, toward the metal clip on the pack"],"correctAnswer":"South, by a river, to a footbridge marked in red","explanation":"The contours showed a slope falling toward a river, and that river led south to a footbridge marked in red.","supportingSentence":"The contours showed a long slope falling toward a river, and that river would lead south to a footbridge marked in red.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does reliable mean in this passage?","options":["Hidden by cloud for most of the day","Damaged in a fall the day before","Able to be trusted","Interesting but impossible to prove"],"correctAnswer":"Able to be trusted","explanation":"The slope under their boots stays reliable after the compass, the moss, and the sun have all failed, so it is something they can trust.","supportingSentence":"What stayed reliable was the slope under their boots.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why did they leave a pile of cones each time they paused?","options":["To build a new instrument that could settle","To notice if they crossed their own route and began to circle","To give Ed a heading he could follow without a map","To keep the pale sun visible through the trees"],"correctAnswer":"To notice if they crossed their own route and began to circle","explanation":"They marked each pause so they would know if they crossed their own route, which would mean they were going in a circle.","supportingSentence":"They walked downhill and marked each pause with a small pile of cones, so they would know if they crossed their own route.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did Ed do with the compass when they reached the footbridge?","options":["He traded it for a better map","He wrapped it and put it at the bottom of his pack","He hung it from the rail for other walkers","He left it beside the last pile of cones"],"correctAnswer":"He wrapped it and put it at the bottom of his pack","explanation":"Ed wrapped the compass in a cloth and put it at the bottom of his pack.","supportingSentence":"Ed wrapped the compass in a cloth and put it at the bottom of his pack, where it could not pretend to help.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What was true after they found the footbridge?","options":["They needed the compass again before they could move","The river on the map had been flowing the wrong way","A long walk remained, but they were no longer inventing the route","The boot prints showed that they had walked in a circle"],"correctAnswer":"A long walk remained, but they were no longer inventing the route","explanation":"They still had a long walk, but they were no longer asking a damaged tool to invent the way, so the route itself was real.","supportingSentence":"They still had a long walk to the road, but they were no longer asking a damaged tool to invent the way.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":330,"questionCount":5}$rbq88454$::jsonb,
  '56569b0e2264a2e3a2e3d8af5f495edc21b8dc64efac76932812263e4da2a990',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'e4ee3f61-8f05-467d-ae93-2ad40873ee06'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4641${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq4641$::jsonb,
  $rbq45921${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"The Choir Rehearsal","story":"Nadia Kravets, who had joined after moving to the town, took her place among the altos and rubbed her hands while the others unwound scarves. Their conductor, a retired teacher named Calder, was late, although nobody treated the delay as an excuse to gossip. They had a concert in ten days, and the new piece, a setting of a winter poem, still fell apart where the rhythm changed without warning. However, most of the singers could now find their notes without staring at a neighbour, which had not been true a month earlier.\n\nWhen Calder arrived, he apologised, set his bag on the piano, and asked them to sing the difficult passage standing rather than sitting. Although several voices still rushed the entrance, the sound was fuller, and the basses held their line long enough for the sopranos to finish a phrase. Nadia watched his left hand, which shaped the quiet ending, and she tried to match the breath of the woman beside her. A pencil mark in her score reminded her to sing more softly on the word snow, where she had a habit of pushing too much air.\n\nDuring the break, the secretary, June Pell, passed around ticket numbers and a reminder about black clothing. Someone had brought biscuits, and the tenors argued over whether the final chord should be sustained for three beats or four. Nadia stayed out of that debate, because she was still unsure of the bar before it. However, she asked June whether another rehearsal was planned, and she was relieved to hear that Sunday afternoon was reserved for the new piece alone. The rest of the programme, June said, was already secure.\n\nThey spent the last half hour on the closing pages, repeating them until the cut-off was clean. Calder did not praise them excessively, although he admitted that the middle section no longer frightened him. Nadia would help with the tickets, not because she felt confident, but because the concert had begun to feel like something she belonged to.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"altos","definition":"The lower female voices in a choir."},{"word":"conductor","definition":"The person who directs the singers."},{"word":"rhythm","definition":"The pattern of beats in a piece of music."},{"word":"sustained","definition":"Held on for a length of time."},{"word":"rehearsal","definition":"A practice before a performance."},{"word":"cut-off","definition":"The moment when all the singers stop together."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When is the choir's concert?","options":["In ten days","On Thursday morning","In two months","Tomorrow evening"],"correctAnswer":"In ten days","explanation":"The singers have a concert in ten days.","supportingSentence":"They had a concert in ten days, and the new piece, a setting of a winter poem, still fell apart where the rhythm changed without warning.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What were the singers reminded to wear?","options":["Bright winter coats","Black clothing","Whatever they had worn that evening","Matching red scarves"],"correctAnswer":"Black clothing","explanation":"June passed around a reminder about black clothing.","supportingSentence":"During the break, the secretary, June Pell, passed around ticket numbers and a reminder about black clothing.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What can the reader conclude about the new piece?","options":["It is easier than every other song","It is not yet as secure as the other music","It has been removed from the concert","June wrote it herself"],"correctAnswer":"It is not yet as secure as the other music","explanation":"June said the rest of the programme was already secure, which means the new piece is the part that still needs work.","supportingSentence":"The rest of the programme, June said, was already secure.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How had Calder felt about the middle section before this rehearsal?","options":["It had worried him","He wanted it sung much louder","He had never heard it","He had already cut it from the score"],"correctAnswer":"It had worried him","explanation":"He said the middle section no longer frightened him, so it had worried him before.","supportingSentence":"Calder did not praise them excessively, although he admitted that the middle section no longer frightened him.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In the tenors' argument, what does sustained mean?","options":["Held on without stopping","Played on the piano instead","Removed from the final chord","Sung by one person only"],"correctAnswer":"Held on without stopping","explanation":"They argued about holding the final chord for three beats or four, so sustained means kept sounding.","supportingSentence":"Someone had brought biscuits, and the tenors argued over whether the final chord should be sustained for three beats or four.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq45921$::jsonb,
  'e4ee3f618f05967dae932ad40873ee06f878b6cab82589ed35d9929116dce220',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '21f02627-50d9-4a96-adab-2c141cc6f57b'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4665${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":335,"questionCount":5}$rbq4665$::jsonb,
  $rbq74753${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The Clockmaker of Lantern Lane","story":"Lantern Lane kept its own evening. The lamps were lit by hand, and the clocks in the windows did not quite agree with the clocks anywhere else. At the end of the lane, Edra mended watches for people who had lost more than minutes. A paper sign said that she repaired timepieces but did not sell hours that belonged to somebody else.\n\nSami brought a silver watch that stopped every day at the same moment. The hands reached sixteen minutes past four and then refused to move. His father used to leave for the harbor at that time, and the watch had been still since their last morning at the gate. Sami wanted it to run like an ordinary watch, because a stopped gift felt like a locked door.\n\nOpening the case, Edra heard a tick that was not the tick of the present. She explained that some mechanisms held a wound memory. The watch was not broken so much as loyal. If she freed the spring, the preserved minute would leave, and no tool on the bench could put that exact minute back. He could keep a watch that remembered, or a watch that told him when to catch a bus. He could not honestly keep both.\n\nSami sat with the choice until the lane lamps were lit. He thought of school mornings when he turned his wrist and felt punished by the same four numbers. Memory already lived in him, and it did not need a machine to trap it. He asked Edra to let the watch run. While she worked, he wrote the old minute on the first page of his notebook, large enough that he would not pretend to have forgotten it.\n\nEdra warned him that the watch would now be late or early like any other, and that this was a kind of health. Outside, Lantern Lane was glowing. For the first time in months, the weight on his wrist matched the hour the rest of the city was using.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"timepieces","definition":"Clocks or watches."},{"word":"loyal","definition":"Faithful to a person, a promise, or a remembered moment."},{"word":"preserved","definition":"Kept as it was, without being lost or changed."},{"word":"mechanisms","definition":"The working parts inside a machine."},{"word":"punished","definition":"Made to suffer, as if for doing something wrong."},{"word":"ordinary","definition":"Normal, and not special or unusual."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does loyal say about the stopped watch?","options":["It always agrees with the clocks in the city","It has no glass and can never be wound","It is staying faithful to a minute it will not leave","It can be sold to any customer in the lane"],"correctAnswer":"It is staying faithful to a minute it will not leave","explanation":"Edra says the watch is not broken so much as loyal, after she hears a tick that is not the tick of the present.","supportingSentence":"The watch was not broken so much as loyal.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"At what moment did the hands of Sami's watch stop?","options":["After every lamp in the lane was lit","At sunrise, when the harbor gate opened","At sixteen minutes past four","When he began writing in his notebook"],"correctAnswer":"At sixteen minutes past four","explanation":"The hands reached sixteen minutes past four and then refused to move.","supportingSentence":"The hands reached sixteen minutes past four and then refused to move.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Sami agree to let the kept minute go?","options":["He wanted Edra to store that minute in the shop","He needed the watch to stay stopped so he would miss the bus","He understood that the memory already lived in him","He found the same minute written on the door sign"],"correctAnswer":"He understood that the memory already lived in him","explanation":"Because the memory already lived in him, it did not need a machine to trap it, so the watch could be allowed to run.","supportingSentence":"Memory already lived in him, and it did not need a machine to trap it.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How did Edra describe a watch that might run early or late?","options":["As a minute that nobody could put back","As proof that the repair had failed","As a kind of health","As a locked door"],"correctAnswer":"As a kind of health","explanation":"She warned him that the watch might be early or late like any other, and that this was a kind of health.","supportingSentence":"Edra warned him that the watch would now be late or early like any other, and that this was a kind of health.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What limit does the paper sign put on Edra's work?","options":["She will repair timepieces, but she will not sell hours that belong to someone else","She refuses any customer who arrives after the lamps are lit","She sells only watches that stop at the same moment each day","She keeps every notebook a customer brings into the shop"],"correctAnswer":"She will repair timepieces, but she will not sell hours that belong to someone else","explanation":"The sign says she repairs timepieces but does not sell hours that belonged to somebody else.","supportingSentence":"A paper sign said that she repaired timepieces but did not sell hours that belonged to somebody else.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":335,"questionCount":5}$rbq74753$::jsonb,
  '21f0262750d9ba964dab2c141cc6f57b6896f8ac07942f54550f4ede4bdd6a8b',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '5952af06-80c1-45d1-ab81-33821fbcc743'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4993${"topic":"technology","wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq4993$::jsonb,
  $rbq86458${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"The Community Wifi","story":"The community hall on Plover Street had never had a reliable internet connection. Tutors printed worksheets at home, and job-club visitors had to leave if a form needed a link. When a local grant paid for a router and a stronger line, the committee put the wifi password on the noticeboard so that anyone in the building could connect. For two days the idea worked. On the third afternoon the connection crawled. A software update on several phones, plus three video calls, had filled the available bandwidth.\n\nRavi, who volunteers with the hall's equipment, checked the router log. Twenty-eight devices were connected, although only eleven people were in the room. Some phones belonged to neighbours in the flats upstairs, who could see the network name and had copied the password from the board. The hall was paying for the line. However, it could not carry the whole street. Ravi explained the difference between a guest network and a private one, and the committee agreed to change the setup before the evening class.\n\nThey created a guest network for visitors and a separate one for the office computer and the tutor laptop. The guest password changed every Monday, and it was written on a small card at the reception desk rather than on the public board. In addition, video calls were asked to use the meeting room, where the signal was reserved during classes. Large downloads were left until after nine, when the hall was empty. These limits were posted in plain language.\n\nThe following week, the job club completed six online forms without a single timeout. The evening language class streamed a short listening clip, and it played smoothly. One neighbour came to the desk and asked for the old password. Ravi refused politely and offered the daytime guest hours instead. Access had to be shared fairly, or it would fail for the people the grant was meant to help. The wifi was still public in spirit. It was no longer open to every device within reach.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"reliable","definition":"Able to be trusted to work when people need it."},{"word":"bandwidth","definition":"How much data a connection can carry at one time."},{"word":"grant","definition":"Money given to pay for a particular purpose."},{"word":"reserved","definition":"Kept for a particular use and not given to everyone."},{"word":"timeout","definition":"A failure that happens when a connection takes too long."},{"word":"access","definition":"The ability to connect and use something."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does \"bandwidth\" mean in this story?","options":["A paper card kept at the reception desk","A password written on the noticeboard","A form the job club could not open","How much data the connection can carry at once"],"correctAnswer":"How much data the connection can carry at once","explanation":"The updates and video calls filled the bandwidth, meaning they used up the line's carrying capacity.","supportingSentence":"A software update on several phones, plus three video calls, had filled the available bandwidth.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"How many devices did the router log show?","options":["Twenty-eight","Three","Eleven","Six"],"correctAnswer":"Twenty-eight","explanation":"Twenty-eight devices were connected, although only eleven people were in the room.","supportingSentence":"Twenty-eight devices were connected, although only eleven people were in the room.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was a password on the public board a poor way to share the line?","options":["People outside the hall could join and fill it","The grant could be spent only on paper forms","The office computer had no separate network","Tutors refused to print any more worksheets"],"correctAnswer":"People outside the hall could join and fill it","explanation":"Neighbours upstairs copied the password from the board, so the hall line was carrying more than the people in the room.","supportingSentence":"Some phones belonged to neighbours in the flats upstairs, who could see the network name and had copied the password from the board.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How often was the visitor password changed?","options":["Only when the line failed","Once, on the day of the grant","Every Monday","Every evening after nine"],"correctAnswer":"Every Monday","explanation":"The guest password changed every Monday and was kept on a card at the desk.","supportingSentence":"The guest password changed every Monday, and it was written on a small card at the reception desk rather than on the public board.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do the six completed forms suggest about the new limits?","options":["The noticeboard password had been restored","Video calls were banned in every room","The neighbours were paying for the line","Ordinary hall tasks could finish without the line stalling"],"correctAnswer":"Ordinary hall tasks could finish without the line stalling","explanation":"The job club finished six forms with no timeout, which suggests the limits left enough capacity for hall work.","supportingSentence":"The following week, the job club completed six online forms without a single timeout.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq86458$::jsonb,
  '5952af0680c1e5d17b8133821fbcc743a03dcbfcd4f4c837170247ff3b6574d0',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'c1648fe4-cff0-46ae-a40a-ffce428008e3'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4563${"topic":"travel","wordCount":328,"questionCount":5}$rbq4563$::jsonb,
  $rbq4956${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"The Delayed Connection","story":"Mara Ellison had left a generous interval between her morning flight and the afternoon train, although the winter timetable offered little room for mistakes. When the aircraft finally reached the coastal city, almost two hours late, she hurried through the arrivals hall with a suitcase and a printed ticket that was already losing its value. Above the station entrance, the departure board listed her connection in amber letters. However, the crowd gathering there suggested that confirmation would come too late for anyone who still needed to cross the city.\n\nA clerk named Tomas explained that the aircraft was not the only disruption, because signalling work on the inland route had reduced the number of carriages. Although he could not promise a seat, he offered to stamp her ticket so that a conductor further along the line would treat the delay as the railway's responsibility rather than her own. Mara accepted the stamp with relief, yet she remained uneasy, since the next reliable service would not leave until evening and she had promised to meet a colleague before dark.\n\nMara stood apart from the crowd, calculating whether a taxi to the following station might recover the lost time. The idea was tempting, although the fare would be considerable and the roads were already crowded. When the train finally appeared, it was shorter than anyone had expected, and the doors opened with a reluctant sigh.\n\nShe found a narrow space beside a luggage rack and sent a brief message admitting that she would arrive after the meeting had begun. The carriage grew warm as more travellers pressed in, and the conductor checked the stamped ticket without comment. However, once the train had cleared the suburbs, Mara noticed an unexpected benefit. The low winter sun was still above the hills, and the route she had nearly abandoned looked remarkably beautiful. She slid the ticket into her notebook and decided that the connection, late as it was, had not entirely failed her.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"interval","definition":"A stretch of time between two events."},{"word":"disruption","definition":"Something that stops a plan from running as expected."},{"word":"amber","definition":"A deep yellow-orange colour."},{"word":"considerable","definition":"Large enough to be important."},{"word":"reluctant","definition":"Slow or unwilling."},{"word":"suburbs","definition":"The districts on the edge of a city."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How late was Mara's aircraft when it reached the coastal city?","options":["Less than thirty minutes","Almost two hours","A little over one day","Nearly four hours"],"correctAnswer":"Almost two hours","explanation":"The aircraft reached the city almost two hours late.","supportingSentence":"When the aircraft finally reached the coastal city, almost two hours late, she hurried through the arrivals hall with a suitcase and a printed ticket that was already losing its value.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Tomas stamp Mara's ticket?","options":["So a later conductor would see the delay as the railway's responsibility","So the airline would refund her fare","So the last seat would be kept for her","So she could board without any ticket at all"],"correctAnswer":"So a later conductor would see the delay as the railway's responsibility","explanation":"Tomas stamped the ticket so a conductor would treat the delay as the railway's responsibility.","supportingSentence":"Although he could not promise a seat, he offered to stamp her ticket so that a conductor further along the line would treat the delay as the railway's responsibility rather than her own.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What happened to Mara's idea of taking a taxi?","options":["She walked to the next station instead","She asked Tomas to pay the driver","She gave it up and travelled by train","She waited until evening and then called a car"],"correctAnswer":"She gave it up and travelled by train","explanation":"The taxi stayed only an idea. Mara was soon on the train, sending a message from the carriage.","supportingSentence":"She found a narrow space beside a luggage rack and sent a brief message admitting that she would arrive after the meeting had begun.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How did the conductor treat Mara's stamped ticket?","options":["The conductor ordered her off at the next stop","The conductor sold her a second ticket","The conductor checked it and raised no objection","The conductor said the stamp was unofficial"],"correctAnswer":"The conductor checked it and raised no objection","explanation":"The conductor checked the stamped ticket and said nothing, so the stamp was accepted.","supportingSentence":"The carriage grew warm as more travellers pressed in, and the conductor checked the stamped ticket without comment.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does reluctant suggest about the train doors?","options":["They belonged to a much longer train","They opened slowly, as if unwilling","They slammed shut on the luggage","They were painted a bright colour"],"correctAnswer":"They opened slowly, as if unwilling","explanation":"A reluctant sigh means the doors seemed slow and unwilling as they opened.","supportingSentence":"When the train finally appeared, it was shorter than anyone had expected, and the doors opened with a reluctant sigh.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":328,"questionCount":5}$rbq4956$::jsonb,
  'c1648fe4cff0f6ae940affce428008e361a704e390e41bc7eaef9bf52b5f7061',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'b0f25d5f-563e-4e79-a8cf-af9833b47657'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5380${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":334,"questionCount":5}$rbq5380$::jsonb,
  $rbq17580${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Evening Class","story":"Rosa enrolled on the Tuesday evening class because her manager had said that stronger writing would help her move beyond the reception desk. She arrived straight from work, still wearing her name badge, and chose a seat near the door. Although she had lived in the city for years, classrooms made her feel younger than she was and somehow less prepared. The tutor, Martin, asked the group to introduce themselves in two sentences, but Rosa managed only one and then stopped.\n\nThere were twelve students, and their reasons varied. A delivery driver wanted to write clearer messages to customers, while a grandfather hoped to help his grandson with homework. Rosa said she froze whenever a formal email had to leave her desk. Martin nodded as if the problem were ordinary, which helped, and he did not ask anyone to read aloud in the first hour. Instead, they rewrote a confusing notice so that a visitor could follow it. Rosa spotted an instruction that contradicted the sentence before it, and when she said so, nobody laughed.\n\nHowever, the weekly task was harder. They had to describe a process from their jobs in plain language, in under two hundred words. Rosa started three times and disliked every opening, so she almost skipped the second class. What changed her mind was a short comment Martin had written on her notice: precise, and worth developing. At the next session he showed the group how to remove empty phrases and how to put steps in order, and he set a rule that feedback had to be specific and kind.\n\nBy the fifth week Rosa could draft a short email without deleting the whole thing. She still hesitated over tone, and she still checked with a colleague before writing to senior staff. Nevertheless, she participated more, and she no longer sat by the door. The course would not transform her job overnight, yet it was already changing the moment when a blank message used to make her close the screen.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"enrolled","definition":"Officially joined a course."},{"word":"contradicted","definition":"Said the opposite of something else."},{"word":"precise","definition":"Exact, clear, and carefully worded."},{"word":"hesitated","definition":"Paused because of uncertainty."},{"word":"participated","definition":"Took an active part."},{"word":"transform","definition":"Change something in a complete way."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What led Rosa to join the Tuesday class?","options":["The lessons were held during her lunch break","Her manager said stronger writing could help her move past reception work","Her grandson needed help with homework","Martin visited reception and signed her up"],"correctAnswer":"Her manager said stronger writing could help her move past reception work","explanation":"She enrolled because her manager had connected better writing with moving beyond the reception desk.","supportingSentence":"Rosa enrolled on the Tuesday evening class because her manager had said that stronger writing would help her move beyond the reception desk.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Martin's behaviour in the first hour suggest about his teaching?","options":["He wanted the problems to feel ordinary before anyone had to perform","He expected a long reading from every student at once","He thought emails were not worth mentioning","He preferred to cancel writing for the whole course"],"correctAnswer":"He wanted the problems to feel ordinary before anyone had to perform","explanation":"He treats Rosa's difficulty as ordinary, which helps, and he does not ask anyone to read aloud in that first hour.","supportingSentence":"Martin nodded as if the problem were ordinary, which helped, and he did not ask anyone to read aloud in the first hour.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What was the regular writing task?","options":["A letter addressed to the head of the college","A plain description of a work process in under two hundred words","Twelve grammar rules learned by heart","A novel read aloud for a full hour"],"correctAnswer":"A plain description of a work process in under two hundred words","explanation":"Each week they had to describe a process from their jobs in plain language, in under two hundred words.","supportingSentence":"They had to describe a process from their jobs in plain language, in under two hundred words.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Rosa's move away from the door suggest?","options":["She feels more at ease in the class than she did at the start","She now plans to leave before the lesson begins","Martin has told her not to take part","The seats beside the door have been removed"],"correctAnswer":"She feels more at ease in the class than she did at the start","explanation":"She chose the door at first, but later she participates more and no longer sits there, which suggests she is more comfortable.","supportingSentence":"Nevertheless, she participated more, and she no longer sat by the door.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does contradicted mean in this story?","options":["Repeated the same instruction more clearly","Told the visitor to leave the building","Said the opposite of the sentence before it","Was written in very large letters"],"correctAnswer":"Said the opposite of the sentence before it","explanation":"Rosa notices an instruction that goes against the sentence just before it.","supportingSentence":"Rosa spotted an instruction that contradicted the sentence before it, and when she said so, nobody laughed.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":334,"questionCount":5}$rbq17580$::jsonb,
  'b0f25d5f563e3e7958cfaf9833b476574582e5eaa8dee27f65496ae7cb33d77e',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'c7b30c2b-c298-4c72-ace0-ab2d292b2b78'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5313${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":333,"questionCount":5}$rbq5313$::jsonb,
  $rbq7472${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"The First Aid Course","story":"The community hall smelled of floor polish and instant coffee when Helen arrived for the evening first-aid course. Owen, the instructor, said that confidence without a method could be as risky as panic. He asked the class to picture a casualty on a park path and to resist acting before they had looked. The first job was to assess the scene, because a helper who became a second patient helped nobody. Helen wrote the order in large letters, starting with danger and ending with breathing.\n\nThey practised on a plastic model whose chest clicked when the head was tilted. Owen showed how a hand under the chin could lift the tongue clear of the airway, and he corrected Helen when she pressed on the soft throat. The movement felt awkward until she stopped rushing it. In the next exercise a classmate pretended to have a deep cut on the forearm. Helen had to sit him down and press a clean compress over the wound while wearing gloves. She forgot the gloves, and Owen made the pair begin again.\n\nDuring the break Helen admitted that she had joined because her father lived alone and she feared freezing if he fell. Owen did not answer with a guarantee. He said a calm voice could reassure a frightened person even before an ambulance was arranged, and that this was a skill rather than a gift. Helen practised a few plain sentences: she would give her name, say what she was doing, and say that help was on the way.\n\nThe final scenario placed three problems in the room, and the class had to choose an order. Helen checked for danger, spoke first to the quiet figure, and sent another learner to the bleeding wrist. Owen watched without interrupting. Afterwards he said the sequence had been sound, although her compress had been too gentle to control the pretend bleeding. Helen packed the gloves in her bag and felt that fear and preparation could share the same evening.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"casualty","definition":"A person who has been injured."},{"word":"assess","definition":"To look carefully at a situation before acting."},{"word":"airway","definition":"The passage that carries air to the lungs."},{"word":"compress","definition":"A clean pad pressed onto a wound."},{"word":"reassure","definition":"To help a frightened person feel calmer."},{"word":"scenario","definition":"A practice situation that imitates a real emergency."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did Owen tell the class to do before helping?","options":["Take the plastic model out of the hall","Check the scene so the helper did not become a second patient","Phone a relative and wait away from the path","Run straight to the injured person and move them"],"correctAnswer":"Check the scene so the helper did not become a second patient","explanation":"He said the first job was to assess the scene, because a helper who got hurt would help nobody.","supportingSentence":"The first job was to assess the scene, because a helper who became a second patient helped nobody.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What weakness did Owen still find in Helen's final practice?","options":["She had refused to give her name","She had sent the other learners out of the room","She had failed to notice the quiet person","Her pressure on the pretend wound was too light"],"correctAnswer":"Her pressure on the pretend wound was too light","explanation":"He said the order of her actions was sound, but the compress was too gentle to control the bleeding.","supportingSentence":"Afterwards he said the sequence had been sound, although her compress had been too gentle to control the pretend bleeding.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the repeated glove exercise show about Owen?","options":["He thinks gloves matter only for hospital staff","He enjoys making learners feel foolish for no reason","He has lost track of the order of the lesson","He treats a missed safety step as something that must be done again"],"correctAnswer":"He treats a missed safety step as something that must be done again","explanation":"Forgetting the gloves meant starting the exercise again, so that step was not optional.","supportingSentence":"She forgot the gloves, and Owen made the pair begin again.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Owen keep quiet during the final practice?","options":["He wanted to see whether the class could order the problems without him","He could not tell which injury was supposed to matter","He had gone outside to wait for a real ambulance","He was refusing to watch because of the earlier glove mistake"],"correctAnswer":"He wanted to see whether the class could order the problems without him","explanation":"He watched without stepping in and judged the sequence afterwards, so the silence was part of the test.","supportingSentence":"Owen watched without interrupting.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does reassure mean in Owen's comment about a calm voice?","options":["Check whether the person is breathing","Win a debate about the right treatment","Help a frightened person feel steadier","Prove that no ambulance will be required"],"correctAnswer":"Help a frightened person feel steadier","explanation":"He says a calm voice can reassure a frightened person, which means it helps them feel less afraid.","supportingSentence":"He said a calm voice could reassure a frightened person even before an ambulance was arranged, and that this was a skill rather than a gift.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":333,"questionCount":5}$rbq7472$::jsonb,
  'c7b30c2bc2989c724ce0ab2d292b2b788722df118d4f991914b31a1fce1b9b1c',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'a4d72a89-7a5f-44c2-a9f0-721b3ddc6cb9'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4633${"topic":"science","wordCount":331,"questionCount":5}$rbq4633$::jsonb,
  $rbq71826${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"The Greenhouse Trial","story":"The college greenhouse was warm even before the first lesson. Tutor Leah had set up a small trial to test whether tomato seedlings grew taller under a white cloth shade than on the open bench. The question sounded simple. The design had to be careful, or the answer would be useless. She divided thirty similar seedlings into two groups of fifteen. One group stayed in full light. The other sat under the cloth, which reduced the brightness but did not block the air.\n\nStudents measured each plant on Monday and again ten days later. They recorded height in millimetres, counted leaves, and wrote down any plant that looked wilted. Watering was the same for both groups, and the pots were the same size. In addition, the trays were swapped in position halfway through the trial, so that one side of the greenhouse would not favour a group. Leah called these the controlled conditions. If too many things changed at once, they could not tell which change had mattered.\n\nThe shaded plants were, on average, a little taller, whereas the open-bench plants had thicker stems and darker leaves. Two shaded seedlings had bent toward the cloth edge, where the light was stronger. Consequently, the class did not announce that shade was better. Height alone was a poor way to choose a winner. A taller plant might be stretching for light rather than growing in a healthy way. They therefore reported both results and described the limit of the trial. Ten days is a short period, and the tomatoes still had weeks of growth ahead.\n\nLeah asked what they would alter next time. The students suggested a third group with only afternoon shade, plus a note of soil temperature. They also wanted more plants, so that one weak seedling could not shift the average. The trial had not produced a rule for every greenhouse. It had produced a clearer question, and a habit of comparing like with like before anyone trusted a result.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"seedlings","definition":"Very young plants grown from seed."},{"word":"trial","definition":"A test set up to compare what happens."},{"word":"wilted","definition":"Soft and drooping instead of firm."},{"word":"controlled","definition":"Kept the same so they do not confuse the result."},{"word":"average","definition":"A typical figure found from a whole group."},{"word":"brightness","definition":"The strength of the light in a place."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why was extra height a weak reason to prefer the shade?","options":["The unshaded plants were sturdier in other ways","The shaded plants received no water at all","The cloth blocked every bit of air","The open bench had no tomato plants"],"correctAnswer":"The unshaded plants were sturdier in other ways","explanation":"The shaded plants were a little taller, but the open-bench plants had thicker stems and darker leaves, so height alone was a poor winner.","supportingSentence":"The shaded plants were, on average, a little taller, whereas the open-bench plants had thicker stems and darker leaves.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What could a reader not tell if the trays had never been moved?","options":["Whether the cloth or the greenhouse side caused the change","Whether Monday was a measuring day","Whether the plants were tomatoes or herbs","Whether any pot had soil in it"],"correctAnswer":"Whether the cloth or the greenhouse side caused the change","explanation":"The trays were swapped so one side of the greenhouse would not favour a group, otherwise the side and the cloth would be mixed together.","supportingSentence":"In addition, the trays were swapped in position halfway through the trial, so that one side of the greenhouse would not favour a group.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How many seedlings were placed in each group?","options":["Ten","Fifteen","Two","Thirty"],"correctAnswer":"Fifteen","explanation":"Leah divided thirty similar seedlings into two groups of fifteen.","supportingSentence":"She divided thirty similar seedlings into two groups of fifteen.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does \"wilted\" mean in this story?","options":["Taller because of a white cloth","Moved to the other side of the bench","Measured again after ten days","Soft and drooping instead of firm"],"correctAnswer":"Soft and drooping instead of firm","explanation":"They wrote down any plant that looked wilted, meaning soft and drooping.","supportingSentence":"They recorded height in millimetres, counted leaves, and wrote down any plant that looked wilted.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Which things did both groups receive in equal measure?","options":["The same height and the same leaf colour","The same brightness and the same shelf","The same water and the same size of pot","The same cloth and the same bending"],"correctAnswer":"The same water and the same size of pot","explanation":"Watering was the same for both groups, and the pots were the same size.","supportingSentence":"Watering was the same for both groups, and the pots were the same size.","skill":"detail"}],"wordCount":331,"questionCount":5}$rbq71826$::jsonb,
  'a4d72a897a5fc4c249f0721b3ddc6cb98f473e4421fe9a3227e9ba23f146ae4f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ad093cd3-1db6-4c77-a147-04c27b61f8f7'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5200${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq5200$::jsonb,
  $rbq99197${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"The Harvest Supper","story":"The harvest supper at Low Whin village hall had never pretended to be a banquet, and the organisers wanted to keep it that way. Elsie, who had chaired the committee for years, pinned a handwritten menu by the door: soup, a pot of beans, baked apples, and whatever bread the mill could spare. Guests could bring a surplus from their gardens if it was labelled and easy to share. The aim was a communal table, not a contest over which dish looked finest.\n\nOn Saturday the hall filled with crates of late tomatoes, a sack of potatoes, and jars that had been saved for winter. Elsie sorted them with firm kindness, setting bruised fruit aside and thanking the giver anyway. She asked two teenagers to wash the produce. When a newcomer offered a tray of shop pastries still in plastic, Elsie refused it politely and explained that the supper was about what the valley had grown, even if the result was plain. The newcomer stayed to peel apples, which Elsie quietly counted as the better gift.\n\nBy evening the long tables wore mismatched cloths, and the seating was loose so neighbours would not sit in the same old clusters. A pot almost too wide for the stove simmered at the back, watched by Elsie's nephew with a wooden spoon. The queue moved slowly because people stopped to read the labels. Elsie thanked the growers, said the leftovers would go to the Monday lunch club, and sat down before the clapping could turn into a speech.\n\nThe room grew noisy in a comfortable way. A farmer who rarely attended meetings explained how he stored carrots in dry sand, and a child listened as if the method were a secret worth keeping. Elsie watched the jars empty and felt a plain gratitude for a meal that nobody had needed to perform. There would be washing-up until late, and a surplus of apples to turn into preserve on Sunday, but the hall had done the job it was built for.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"harvest","definition":"The season when ripe crops are gathered."},{"word":"surplus","definition":"An amount that is left over beyond what is needed."},{"word":"communal","definition":"Shared by a group rather than kept private."},{"word":"seating","definition":"The way places to sit are arranged."},{"word":"gratitude","definition":"A feeling of being thankful."},{"word":"preserve","definition":"Fruit or vegetables kept in jars so they can be eaten later."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the organisers not want the supper to turn into?","options":["An evening when neighbours sat at long tables","A contest over which dish looked the finest","A chance for the mill to supply bread","A meal that used vegetables from local gardens"],"correctAnswer":"A contest over which dish looked the finest","explanation":"The aim was a shared table, not a competition about appearance.","supportingSentence":"The aim was a communal table, not a contest over which dish looked finest.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Where did Elsie say the leftover food would go?","options":["Back to each person who had brought a dish","On sale at the hall door that night","Into the garden behind the hall","To the lunch club meeting on Monday"],"correctAnswer":"To the lunch club meeting on Monday","explanation":"She told the room that the leftovers would go to the Monday lunch club.","supportingSentence":"Elsie thanked the growers, said the leftovers would go to the Monday lunch club, and sat down before the clapping could turn into a speech.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Elsie appear to value more than an unsuitable donation?","options":["A long speech in praise of the committee","A prize for the neighbour with the most jars","Food that is still sealed in shop packaging","A person who stays and helps with the work"],"correctAnswer":"A person who stays and helps with the work","explanation":"After refusing pastries that did not fit the supper, she treated apple peeling as the better gift.","supportingSentence":"The newcomer stayed to peel apples, which Elsie quietly counted as the better gift.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the farmer's conversation with the child suggest about the evening?","options":["Quiet neighbours had a natural way to pass on everyday knowledge","The child was scoring dishes for an official contest","The farmer was complaining that the queue was too slow","Elsie had asked him to advertise a carrot business"],"correctAnswer":"Quiet neighbours had a natural way to pass on everyday knowledge","explanation":"A man who rarely came to meetings explained how he stored carrots, and the child listened closely.","supportingSentence":"A farmer who rarely attended meetings explained how he stored carrots in dry sand, and a child listened as if the method were a secret worth keeping.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does surplus mean when guests are invited to bring garden food?","options":["Extra produce beyond what they need to keep","A formal speech thanking the chair","A prize awarded for the best apples","A ticket that must be bought at the door"],"correctAnswer":"Extra produce beyond what they need to keep","explanation":"A surplus from a garden is food people can share because they do not need all of it themselves.","supportingSentence":"Guests could bring a surplus from their gardens if it was labelled and easy to share.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":337,"questionCount":5}$rbq99197$::jsonb,
  'ad093cd31db62c77b14704c27b61f8f7c76844133a9b688a45265a6aad10dd4c',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '998408f3-cd36-4a7b-a034-734dcd8172c8'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5382${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq5382$::jsonb,
  $rbq96212${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Lab Partner","story":"Sofia had hoped to choose her own partner for the chemistry practical, but the tutor read names from a list and paired her with Daniel. Although they had been in the same class for months, they had barely spoken. Their task was to measure how temperature changed the speed of a reaction and to record the results in a shared table, and the report was due in a week.\n\nAt the first session Daniel arrived early and had already labelled the tubes. Sofia was relieved, then slightly annoyed, because he began the experiment before they had agreed on a method. She stopped him and pointed at the instructions, which said that one step had to be repeated three times and that the solutions had to be kept at fixed temperatures. Daniel said he was trying to save time. Sofia replied that saved time was useless if the data could not be trusted, and the exchange was tense, yet neither of them walked away.\n\nThey divided the work more carefully after that. Sofia controlled the timer and wrote each measurement as it happened, while Daniel handled the equipment and watched for spills. When a result looked strange, they repeated it instead of hiding it. However, a new problem appeared outside the lab. Daniel missed a planned meeting and sent a brief message two hours later, saying he had been at work. Sofia had waited in the library with the unfinished graph. She told him, calmly, that she would not complete his section for him, although she was willing to meet later that evening.\n\nHe came, and they finished the graph, checked the units, and wrote a conclusion that matched the evidence rather than the result they had expected. The tutor later praised the report for explaining an odd measurement instead of ignoring it. Sofia and Daniel were not close friends afterwards, but they trusted each other in the lab. A good partner, she decided, was someone who would correct a mistake and then help to repair it.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"paired","definition":"Put with another person to work as partners."},{"word":"measurement","definition":"A figure recorded while a test is being done."},{"word":"tense","definition":"Uneasy and full of strain."},{"word":"conclusion","definition":"A final judgement based on what has been found."},{"word":"evidence","definition":"Information used to show whether something is true."},{"word":"trusted","definition":"Felt able to rely on someone."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How was Daniel chosen to work with Sofia?","options":["They had shared every practical that year","She invited him because they were already friends","The tutor assigned him from a list of names","He was the only student who volunteered"],"correctAnswer":"The tutor assigned him from a list of names","explanation":"Sofia had wanted to choose, but the tutor read names from a list and paired her with Daniel.","supportingSentence":"Sofia had hoped to choose her own partner for the chemistry practical, but the tutor read names from a list and paired her with Daniel.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did they do when a result looked strange?","options":["They carried out the test again","They copied a figure from another pair","They removed it from the shared table","They stopped the practical immediately"],"correctAnswer":"They carried out the test again","explanation":"Instead of hiding a strange result, they repeated it.","supportingSentence":"When a result looked strange, they repeated it instead of hiding it.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What can a reader tell about Sofia when Daniel starts the experiment?","options":["A quick start did not make up for the lack of an agreed method","She was annoyed because he had come in late","She had finished the practical before he arrived","She wanted the tutor to cancel the session"],"correctAnswer":"A quick start did not make up for the lack of an agreed method","explanation":"She becomes annoyed because he begins before they have agreed how to work, so speed alone does not satisfy her.","supportingSentence":"Sofia was relieved, then slightly annoyed, because he began the experiment before they had agreed on a method.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does tense mean in this story?","options":["Written down in the results table","Too quick to follow","Uneasy and strained","Quiet, short, and friendly"],"correctAnswer":"Uneasy and strained","explanation":"The word describes their sharp exchange about whether speed mattered more than data they could trust.","supportingSentence":"Sofia replied that saved time was useless if the data could not be trusted, and the exchange was tense, yet neither of them walked away.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Which conclusion about their partnership is best supported?","options":["They agreed never to speak after the report","Working trust in the lab did not require a close friendship","Being friends mattered more to them than the measurements","The tutor separated them because of the odd result"],"correctAnswer":"Working trust in the lab did not require a close friendship","explanation":"They are not close friends afterwards, but they trust each other in the lab, so the trust is about the work.","supportingSentence":"Sofia and Daniel were not close friends afterwards, but they trusted each other in the lab.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq96212$::jsonb,
  '998408f3cd36ca7b2034734dcd8172c8b4dccf84f3bc98cc5d10aec9e7e31d50',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '7dc41dc4-e469-4a76-a78b-8e628f658c4c'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4666${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq4666$::jsonb,
  $rbq97360${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The Library That Moved","story":"The library had been on Market Street on Monday. On Tuesday Neve found an empty shop and a note in the librarian's small handwriting. The shelves had gone to sit by the canal, and anyone who truly needed a book should follow before dusk. Neve did need one. Her apprentice test, set for the next morning, included a repair on a canal lock, and the only clear manual was in the library she had postponed visiting.\n\nShe reached the canal as the lamps were being lit. The library stood where a boathouse had been, its windows fogged from the damp. Oster, the librarian, nodded at Neve's oil-stained notebook and guessed her errand before she explained it.\n\nHe stated the condition before he fetched the manual. A borrower had to return a finished book, because the library traveled more easily when it was not carrying stories nobody was reading. Unfinished books made it stubborn. Neve had a novel in her bag, one she had loved and then kept because she was not ready to miss it. She returned it to a shelf. The shelves gave a small, settling sound, like a boat easing against a wall.\n\nOster let her read by the window while he mended a torn map. The diagrams were plain, and the warnings were plainer: never force a gate against the water, and never trust a dry step that had been wet a minute earlier. Neve copied the pages she would not remember under pressure. The library, Oster said, did not move to impress anyone. It moved because a needed book left too long in one place began to feel dishonest.\n\nIn the morning the canal bank was only a canal bank. A fresh note on Market Street said her novel would be waiting when she wanted it for its own sake. She passed the test with wet sleeves and steady hands. That evening she went back to Market Street, because a promise kept by a building deserved a reader who returned on time.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"postponed","definition":"Delayed until a later time."},{"word":"errand","definition":"A short trip made in order to do a task."},{"word":"borrower","definition":"A person who takes something and is expected to return it."},{"word":"stubborn","definition":"Unwilling to move or change."},{"word":"diagrams","definition":"Simple drawings that show how something works."},{"word":"dishonest","definition":"Not truthful or not right in the circumstances."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How could Oster guess Neve's errand before she explained it?","options":["The oil on her notebook suggested hands-on repair work","The note on the empty shop described her by name","Every visitor that day asked for the same novel","She was already holding the canal manual open"],"correctAnswer":"The oil on her notebook suggested hands-on repair work","explanation":"He looks at her oil-stained notebook and guesses, so the stains are the clue that her work involves machinery.","supportingSentence":"Oster, the librarian, nodded at Neve's oil-stained notebook and guessed her errand before she explained it.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did a borrower have to give back before taking the manual?","options":["A promise to take the test without any notes","A torn map in exchange for a novel","A book that the borrower had already finished","Payment for moving the shelves to the canal"],"correctAnswer":"A book that the borrower had already finished","explanation":"A borrower had to return a finished book before the library would travel more easily.","supportingSentence":"A borrower had to return a finished book, because the library traveled more easily when it was not carrying stories nobody was reading.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Neve copy pages instead of only reading them?","options":["The window table was not meant for reading","She still needed to return an unfinished story","She thought pressure might make her forget the instructions","The drawings were too plain to read even once"],"correctAnswer":"She thought pressure might make her forget the instructions","explanation":"She copied the pages she would not remember under pressure, so she was preparing for a stressful test.","supportingSentence":"Neve copied the pages she would not remember under pressure.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does stubborn mean when unfinished books affect the library?","options":["Already finished and ready to lend","Unwilling to move or give way","Eager to travel at a moment's notice","Closed to every visitor after dusk"],"correctAnswer":"Unwilling to move or give way","explanation":"Unfinished books make the library stubborn, while returning a finished book helps it travel, so stubborn means unwilling to move.","supportingSentence":"Unfinished books made it stubborn.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why did the library change streets, according to Oster?","options":["A needed book began to feel wrong if it was left too long in one place","Neve asked the shelves to follow her to the test","Rain had ruined the shop on Market Street","The boathouse owner charged rent every Monday"],"correctAnswer":"A needed book began to feel wrong if it was left too long in one place","explanation":"Oster said it moved because a needed book left too long in one place began to feel dishonest.","supportingSentence":"It moved because a needed book left too long in one place began to feel dishonest.","skill":"detail"}],"wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq97360$::jsonb,
  '7dc41dc4e4697a76478b8e628f658c4c49c9eb3b0c730147fda3311016c1b135',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '2f75b163-30f4-4130-ac31-f143a87841af'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4699${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":321,"questionCount":5}$rbq4699$::jsonb,
  $rbq60733${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"The Locked Shed","story":"The community garden shed was locked when Rosa arrived for her Tuesday morning, and the tool board outside told her the wrong story. The hooks for the spades were empty on the painted list, yet the padlock was the old one, shut properly, with no scratch around the screws. Wet footprints led across the path and stopped at the door. The watering can was gone from its nail. Rosa checked the usual place for the key, a hook inside the cafe, and found only a bare nail. For a few minutes she was sure somebody had cleared the shed in the night.\n\nShe stood in the doorway of the cafe and made herself list what she actually knew. The lock was not broken. The gate had been mentioned in a message she had only half read. She opened that message again. The treasurer, who kept the garden's money and the spare keys, had written that a gate was found open on Sunday. The newer tools would stay locked inside until the committee met on Thursday. The key was no longer on the hook. It was in the tin labelled tea, on the high shelf, because a hook is the first place a stranger looks.\n\nRosa climbed onto a stool, opened the tin, and fitted the key. The shed smelled of damp wood and oil, and every spade was there, dry and hung in a row. The watering can sat just inside, full, as if someone had meant to use it and had found the door already shut. Rosa pinned a fresh note under the tool board, saying where the key lived now. She copied the treasurer's instruction into the garden book so the next volunteer would not repeat her scare. Between sessions the shed would stay locked. Rosa locked the shed again, returned the key to the tea tin, and texted the treasurer that every spade was still on its hook.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"padlock","definition":"The lock that fastened the shed door."},{"word":"footprints","definition":"Marks left by shoes on the wet path."},{"word":"treasurer","definition":"The person who kept the garden's money and spare keys."},{"word":"committee","definition":"The group that meets to decide garden matters."},{"word":"volunteer","definition":"A person who helps without being paid."},{"word":"instruction","definition":"A written direction about what to do."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where did the message say the shed key had been put?","options":["Under the painted tool board","In a lock that had been scratched","In the tin labelled tea","On the hook inside the cafe"],"correctAnswer":"In the tin labelled tea","explanation":"The key was no longer on the hook. It was in the tin labelled tea on the high shelf.","supportingSentence":"It was in the tin labelled tea, on the high shelf, because a hook is the first place a stranger looks.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"When was the committee due to meet?","options":["On Thursday","On Sunday, after the open gate","Before every garden session","On Tuesday morning"],"correctAnswer":"On Thursday","explanation":"The newer tools were to stay locked inside until the committee met on Thursday.","supportingSentence":"The newer tools would stay locked inside until the committee met on Thursday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Who most likely left the wet marks that stopped at the door?","options":["The treasurer, moving tools into the cafe","Someone who came to water and found the door shut","A thief who carried every spade away at night","A stranger who broke the lock off the door"],"correctAnswer":"Someone who came to water and found the door shut","explanation":"A full watering can was just inside, as if someone had meant to water and had found the door already shut.","supportingSentence":"The watering can sat just inside, full, as if someone had meant to use it and had found the door already shut.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What was misleading about the empty hooks on the outside board?","options":["They did not mean the spades had been stolen","They showed that the lock on the door was new","They proved the painted list had been taken on Sunday","They meant the spades were kept in the cafe each night"],"correctAnswer":"They did not mean the spades had been stolen","explanation":"The outside hooks were empty, but the lock was undamaged and every spade was hanging inside.","supportingSentence":"The hooks for the spades were empty on the painted list, yet the padlock was the old one, shut properly, with no scratch around the screws.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the word padlock mean in this story?","options":["The lock that held the shed door shut","A mark scratched around the screws","The tin in which the tea was stored","A painted list of the garden tools"],"correctAnswer":"The lock that held the shed door shut","explanation":"The padlock is the shed's lock, and it was properly shut with no scratch around the screws.","supportingSentence":"The hooks for the spades were empty on the painted list, yet the padlock was the old one, shut properly, with no scratch around the screws.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":321,"questionCount":5}$rbq60733$::jsonb,
  '2f75b16330f491306c31f143a87841af88ab05ba55e3a65be37626d6439ef853',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '41cbe5b8-ea55-432e-ad9d-66baef28935e'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5492${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":339,"questionCount":5}$rbq5492$::jsonb,
  $rbq91930${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"The Market Stall Accounts","story":"On wet Saturdays the fruit stall at the east end of the town market still opened at eight. When her uncle went into hospital, Amina agreed to run it for four weeks. He left her a tin of coins, a handwritten price card, and a notebook with columns. Each evening she was supposed to record every sale and then count what remained in the tin. By the second Saturday the notebook looked healthy, yet the tin felt light in her hands.\n\nThat night Amina spread the notebook and the tin on the kitchen table. The book said she had taken three hundred and twelve pounds. She counted the notes and coins twice and reached only two hundred and seventy-four. A shortfall of that size could not be a single coin dropped between the crates. At about eleven she had begun selling bruised apples for less than the card, because the regulars expected that favour when the fruit was past its best. She had copied the full price into the notebook and accepted the lower sum. The same mistake had happened with the soft pears after lunch. Generosity, written down as if it were the listed price, had invented money that never entered the tin.\n\nOn Sunday she drew a second column headed money received and copied the week from memory and from the few receipts in her coat. The new total matched the cash exactly. While she was smoothing the pages she found a supplier's bill folded into the back cover, still unpaid from her uncle's last Saturday. Once that bill was included, the stall had made only a modest profit, not the comfortable surplus suggested by the old column. Amina telephoned the ward, explained the two columns, and promised not to hide a discount inside a hopeful figure. Her uncle said the stall had always survived on careful notes rather than cheerful ones. For the remaining Saturdays she wrote the real price as she handed over the fruit, and each evening the tin and the book finally agreed.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"shortfall","definition":"The amount by which the cash was less than the written total."},{"word":"handwritten","definition":"Written by hand rather than printed."},{"word":"receipts","definition":"Small papers that show what was paid or sold."},{"word":"surplus","definition":"Extra money left after costs, more than enough."},{"word":"discount","definition":"A lower price than the one shown on the card."},{"word":"modest","definition":"Fairly small, and not large or showy."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How much did the notebook say Amina had taken?","options":["Three hundred and twelve pounds","The cost of the supplier's bill","Four weeks of market takings","Two hundred and seventy-four pounds"],"correctAnswer":"Three hundred and twelve pounds","explanation":"The notebook said she had taken three hundred and twelve pounds, which was more than the cash.","supportingSentence":"The book said she had taken three hundred and twelve pounds.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did Amina find folded in the back cover?","options":["A second handwritten price card","A list of the regular customers","An unpaid bill from a supplier","The hospital's visiting hours"],"correctAnswer":"An unpaid bill from a supplier","explanation":"Inside the back cover was a supplier's bill from her uncle's last Saturday, and it was still unpaid.","supportingSentence":"While she was smoothing the pages she found a supplier's bill folded into the back cover, still unpaid from her uncle's last Saturday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the exact match on Sunday tell the reader about the missing pounds?","options":["They had never been received, so the book was ahead of the tin","They were hidden inside the supplier's unpaid bill","They had been carried to the hospital that morning","She had counted the notes once and skipped the coins"],"correctAnswer":"They had never been received, so the book was ahead of the tin","explanation":"When she recorded the money she had really received, the book matched the tin, so the gap was imaginary.","supportingSentence":"The new total matched the cash exactly.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What would her uncle most likely have praised on the later Saturdays?","options":["Writing the price customers actually paid","Giving bruised fruit away without a note","Keeping the old column and reporting the higher total","Leaving the supplier's bill out of the sum"],"correctAnswer":"Writing the price customers actually paid","explanation":"He trusted careful notes rather than cheerful ones, and she began writing the real price at each sale.","supportingSentence":"Her uncle said the stall had always survived on careful notes rather than cheerful ones.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the word shortfall mean in this story?","options":["The amount by which the cash was below the written total","The profit that remains after a bill is paid","A lower price offered to regular customers","A coin that fell between the fruit crates"],"correctAnswer":"The amount by which the cash was below the written total","explanation":"The shortfall is the gap between the higher notebook total and the smaller amount in the tin.","supportingSentence":"A shortfall of that size could not be a single coin dropped between the crates.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":339,"questionCount":5}$rbq91930$::jsonb,
  '41cbe5b8ea55f32e5d9d66baef28935e5b42fb4febb4ef1021eb7bac21b6167b',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '26aef543-033a-4dfc-a2d4-988013b86ff5'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4639${"topic":"science","wordCount":328,"questionCount":5}$rbq4639$::jsonb,
  $rbq70247${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"The Museum Workshop","story":"On Saturday the city museum opened its workshop bench for a session called Slow Machines. Families could test a simple ramp, a pulley, and a set of gears, using wooden blocks rather than a screen. The guide, Marta, did not begin with a speech about famous discoveries. She began with a prediction. Each pair had to write what they thought would happen before they touched the equipment.\n\nNoor and her father chose the pulley. They predicted that two wheels would make the block feel twice as heavy. The first lift surprised them. The block rose more easily, although the string they pulled was longer than the distance the block travelled. Marta asked them not to change their written prediction. The point of the sheet was to keep the first idea visible, so they could compare it with the evidence. In addition, she asked them to repeat the lift three times and to note whether the result stayed the same.\n\nAt the next bench the gears produced a different lesson. A large gear turned a small one quickly, whereas the small gear turned the large one with less speed and more force. A boy called Eli had expected the opposite, because the large gear looked stronger. He was not wrong to notice its size. He was wrong about what that size did. Marta treated the mistake as useful. A clear wrong prediction is easier to correct than a guess that was never written down.\n\nBefore leaving, every pair completed one sentence about what they would change in their prediction, and which observation had forced the change. Noor wrote that the pulley traded a longer pull for an easier lift. Eli wrote that size in a gear can increase force or speed, but not both at once. The workshop had stayed small and concrete. Nevertheless, it had trained a habit that careful investigators rely on, which is to predict, test, and revise in public, even when the first idea fails.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"prediction","definition":"An idea, written first, of what a test will do."},{"word":"evidence","definition":"What people observe when they test an idea."},{"word":"pulley","definition":"A wheel and string used to lift a weight."},{"word":"force","definition":"A push or pull that can move something."},{"word":"observation","definition":"Something noticed carefully during a test."},{"word":"revise","definition":"Change an idea after looking at the results."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does \"prediction\" mean in this story?","options":["An idea, written first, of what a test will do","A speech about famous discoveries","A wooden block used instead of a screen","A gear that looks larger than the others"],"correctAnswer":"An idea, written first, of what a test will do","explanation":"Each pair wrote what they thought would happen before they touched the equipment, and that first idea was their prediction.","supportingSentence":"She began with a prediction.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What bargain did the pulley test reveal?","options":["Two wheels that stopped the block from rising","A screen that replaced the wooden blocks","An easier lift in return for more string to pull","A heavier block in return for a shorter string"],"correctAnswer":"An easier lift in return for more string to pull","explanation":"The block rose more easily, but the string pulled was longer than the distance the block travelled.","supportingSentence":"The block rose more easily, although the string they pulled was longer than the distance the block travelled.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did Noor and her father expect the two wheels to do?","options":["Make the block feel twice as heavy","Stop the block from moving at all","Make the string shorter than the lift","Turn the large gear with less force"],"correctAnswer":"Make the block feel twice as heavy","explanation":"They predicted that two wheels would make the block feel twice as heavy.","supportingSentence":"They predicted that two wheels would make the block feel twice as heavy.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did Eli misunderstand about size?","options":["He believed the pulley would feel twice as light","He refused to write anything before the test","He thought a bigger gear was simply the stronger one","He said size changes neither speed nor force"],"correctAnswer":"He thought a bigger gear was simply the stronger one","explanation":"Eli expected the opposite because the large gear looked stronger, but he was wrong about what that size actually did.","supportingSentence":"A boy called Eli had expected the opposite, because the large gear looked stronger.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How many times was the pulley lift supposed to be repeated?","options":["Three times","Until the first idea was erased","Two times","One time"],"correctAnswer":"Three times","explanation":"Marta asked them to repeat the lift three times and note whether the result stayed the same.","supportingSentence":"In addition, she asked them to repeat the lift three times and to note whether the result stayed the same.","skill":"detail"}],"wordCount":328,"questionCount":5}$rbq70247$::jsonb,
  '26aef543033a8dfc42d4988013b86ff5db1cccb0de084550bcb5b12d6c95bc36',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '5fccf3c1-5c6e-4e78-af90-c3ce9f905102'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4883${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq4883$::jsonb,
  $rbq81630${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"The Night Bus Home","story":"The last train had already left when Nico reached the station, so he walked to the stop where the night bus picked up passengers after midnight. The street was almost deserted, although a bakery on the corner still had its lights on. He checked the timetable twice and saw that the bus was due at twenty past twelve, yet the minutes passed and nothing came. He thought about calling a taxi, but the fare across the city would have used most of his weekly travel money.\n\nA woman in a red coat arrived and stood a short distance away. She looked weary, and she kept glancing down the road. Nico hesitated before speaking, because he did not want to alarm her, but at last he asked whether she was waiting for the same service. She said she was, and that this route was often late after concerts finished in the centre. Her name was Farah. She had missed her earlier bus after a long shift at a hospital, and she still had a twenty-minute walk at the other end, which made Nico feel less foolish for waiting beside her.\n\nHowever, another half hour went by. Farah suggested they check the transport notice on her phone, and a short message said the bus had been held up by a road closure and would arrive soon. They stayed under the shelter and talked quietly about the kind of work that ends when everyone else is asleep. Nico packed boxes in a warehouse, whereas Farah cleaned operating theatres. Neither job was glamorous, but both required them to be punctual the next day, which made a cancelled bus more than an inconvenience.\n\nWhen the vehicle finally appeared, they boarded and found a few free seats. Farah got off first and wished him a safe rest of the journey. Nico watched the empty streets slide past and felt a quiet relief, because the night had been inconvenient, yet a stranger's company had made it easier to bear.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"deserted","definition":"Empty of people."},{"word":"weary","definition":"Very tired."},{"word":"hesitated","definition":"Paused before acting, because of uncertainty."},{"word":"punctual","definition":"On time, at the hour you are expected."},{"word":"relief","definition":"The easing of a worry."},{"word":"inconvenient","definition":"Causing trouble or making a situation difficult."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How had Nico felt about waiting before he heard Farah's story?","options":["Sure that a taxi would be the cheaper choice","Ready to walk across the city by himself","A little foolish for standing at the stop","Proud that he had missed the train"],"correctAnswer":"A little foolish for standing at the stop","explanation":"Hearing about her long shift and her walk made him feel less foolish, so the wait had embarrassed him before that.","supportingSentence":"She had missed her earlier bus after a long shift at a hospital, and she still had a twenty-minute walk at the other end, which made Nico feel less foolish for waiting beside her.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Nico decide against a taxi?","options":["The fare would have taken most of the money he kept for weekly travel","Farah had offered him a seat in her car","He had left his wallet at the warehouse","Taxis were not allowed to stop near the station"],"correctAnswer":"The fare would have taken most of the money he kept for weekly travel","explanation":"He thought about a taxi, then realised the fare across the city would use most of his weekly travel money.","supportingSentence":"He thought about calling a taxi, but the fare across the city would have used most of his weekly travel money.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What reason did the phone message give for the delay?","options":["Concert crowds had cancelled the service","A road closure had held the bus up","The driver had already finished work","The printed timetable was for another day"],"correctAnswer":"A road closure had held the bus up","explanation":"The notice said the bus had been held up by a road closure and would arrive soon.","supportingSentence":"Farah suggested they check the transport notice on her phone, and a short message said the bus had been held up by a road closure and would arrive soon.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does weary mean in this story?","options":["Extremely tired","Newly arrived","Dressed for cold weather","Unsure of the route"],"correctAnswer":"Extremely tired","explanation":"It describes Farah after a long hospital shift, while she is still waiting at the stop.","supportingSentence":"She looked weary, and she kept glancing down the road.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What does Nico's pause before speaking show?","options":["He was careful not to frighten someone he did not know","He had decided to give up on the journey","He hoped Farah would pay the taxi fare","He could not remember which bus he needed"],"correctAnswer":"He was careful not to frighten someone he did not know","explanation":"He waited before talking because he did not want to alarm her, late at night and at an empty stop.","supportingSentence":"Nico hesitated before speaking, because he did not want to alarm her, but at last he asked whether she was waiting for the same service.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq81630$::jsonb,
  '5fccf3c15c6e5e789f90c3ce9f9051022ce917c3e9315e5d4e7317be70ce29c3',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '7579d7a5-93fa-4ac0-aee5-f141d659df6d'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4990${"topic":"technology","wordCount":334,"questionCount":5}$rbq4990$::jsonb,
  $rbq68808${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"The Office Backup","story":"When Priya's laptop stayed black on Monday morning, the newest posters for the weekend market were trapped inside it. She had meant to copy them onto the shared drive, although the latest versions existed only on that machine. A local repair shop warned that the damaged drive might take several days to examine. The market, however, was on Saturday, so the team could not wait for the repair. They therefore searched old messages and printed drafts, rebuilding the posters from memory. The new files were usable. Even so, two prices were wrong, and one illustration was an earlier version.\n\nAfter the market, the office manager, Elena, introduced a backup procedure that everyone had to follow. Each project file would be stored in two places before the end of the working day. In addition, the copy had to be automatic, because busy people often skip a task that depends on memory. Luis, who maintained the office computers, set every laptop to send its project folder to the shared drive at half past five. He also kept a small external drive in a locked cupboard, in case the office network failed. The first week was imperfect. One transfer stopped because a folder was too large, and another laptop reported that its disk was full. Nevertheless, the staff repeated the routine until it felt ordinary.\n\nThe procedure proved useful during a sudden power cut. Nora had been editing a client brochure when the lights went out, and the latest paragraph vanished. She did not have to begin the whole document again. The afternoon copy was still on the shared drive, so she restored it and rewrote only the missing lines. As a result, the brochure reached the client that evening rather than the next morning. Priya admitted that the daily check felt dull, whereas rebuilding a whole project had been stressful. The team now reads the backup log each morning in order to confirm that every copy has finished. It is quiet work, but it is reliable.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"procedure","definition":"A set of steps that people agree to follow."},{"word":"automatic","definition":"Done by a machine without a person starting it each time."},{"word":"confirm","definition":"To check that something has been completed."},{"word":"restored","definition":"Put back from an earlier saved copy."},{"word":"vanished","definition":"Disappeared suddenly."},{"word":"reliable","definition":"Able to be trusted to work properly."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When was the market that the posters were for?","options":["During the power cut","On Monday morning","On Saturday","At half past five"],"correctAnswer":"On Saturday","explanation":"The market was on Saturday, so the team could not wait for the slow repair.","supportingSentence":"The market, however, was on Saturday, so the team could not wait for the repair.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does keeping the morning log suggest about the team?","options":["They prefer printed drafts to any digital copy","They trust only the repair shop with client work","They plan to stop copying files after the market","They accept a dull task because lost work is worse"],"correctAnswer":"They accept a dull task because lost work is worse","explanation":"Priya contrasts the dull check with the stress of rebuilding a project, and the team still reads the log.","supportingSentence":"Priya admitted that the daily check felt dull, whereas rebuilding a whole project had been stressful.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Where was the second drive stored if the network failed?","options":["At the local repair shop","On the client brochure","Inside a printed draft","In a locked cupboard"],"correctAnswer":"In a locked cupboard","explanation":"Luis kept a small external drive in a locked cupboard in case the office network failed.","supportingSentence":"He also kept a small external drive in a locked cupboard, in case the office network failed.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the word \"reliable\" mean in the final sentence?","options":["Too dull to repeat each morning","Trusted to do the job properly","Impossible to bring back after a cut","Full of the wrong prices"],"correctAnswer":"Trusted to do the job properly","explanation":"Calling the quiet routine reliable means the team can trust it to work.","supportingSentence":"It is quiet work, but it is reliable.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What risk remains if people check a copy only after a failure?","options":["A missed copy might stay hidden until work is lost","Clients would receive posters before they were made","The shared drive would empty itself every evening","The repair shop would refuse to examine a laptop"],"correctAnswer":"A missed copy might stay hidden until work is lost","explanation":"The morning log exists to confirm that each copy has finished, so a failure can be seen before a file is needed.","supportingSentence":"The team now reads the backup log each morning in order to confirm that every copy has finished.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":334,"questionCount":5}$rbq68808$::jsonb,
  '7579d7a593fa7ac0dee5f141d659df6d4c896dac180af41039794292e1cd4074',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '8b15c0d2-f9c8-4d3f-a530-dc0a2e4e980e'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5384${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq5384$::jsonb,
  $rbq58565${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Open Day Tour","story":"Leila arrived at Northbridge College an hour early, although the open day tour was not due to start until ten. She had read the prospectus twice on the bus, marking courses that might suit her, yet the campus still felt larger than the photographs suggested. Her father waited by the entrance while she collected a map from a student guide called Samir, who gathered the group and warned them that the route included stairs. Anyone who preferred a lift could tell him at once, and nobody would be left behind for asking.\n\nThe first stop was the library. Samir explained that students could borrow laptops as well as books, and that quiet floors were strictly for silent study. However, the workshop next door was anything but quiet, because engineering students were testing a small bridge made of thin wood, and a tutor invited the visitors to watch rather than touch. Leila asked how much independent study the course required. The tutor said the timetable looked light only until the project weeks began, when workshops stayed open late.\n\nOver lunch Leila sat with two applicants who were equally unsure. One wanted nursing, and the other was choosing between business and design. Their questions were practical and covered travel time, the cost of materials, and whether support was available for students who worked evenings. Samir answered honestly. He admitted that the first term had overwhelmed him, especially the number of deadlines, but that tutors noticed if someone stopped attending. He also pointed out a small advice centre beside the cafeteria.\n\nIn the afternoon Leila visited a language classroom and sat at the back of a sample lesson. The teacher asked the visitors to describe their towns in three sentences. Leila's voice shook at first, but she finished, and another student smiled at her. On the way out she told her father that the college no longer felt impossible. She had not made a final choice, but she had enough information to compare this campus with the others on her list.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"prospectus","definition":"A printed guide that describes a college and its courses."},{"word":"independent","definition":"Done on your own, without someone directing every step."},{"word":"applicants","definition":"People who are asking to be considered for a place."},{"word":"overwhelmed","definition":"Feeling that there is more to deal with than you can manage."},{"word":"sample","definition":"A short example of what something is usually like."},{"word":"compare","definition":"To look at things together in order to judge the differences."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How does Leila seem to feel when she first reaches the college?","options":["Interested in the courses, but surprised by how large the campus feels","Angry that her father has stayed outside","Certain that she will sign up before lunch","Ready to reject the place before the tour begins"],"correctAnswer":"Interested in the courses, but surprised by how large the campus feels","explanation":"She has already marked possible courses, yet the real campus feels larger than the photographs, so she is keen and slightly daunted.","supportingSentence":"She had read the prospectus twice on the bus, marking courses that might suit her, yet the campus still felt larger than the photographs suggested.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What rule did Samir give for the quiet library floors?","options":["Visitors could eat beside the computers","They were strictly for silent study","Group discussion was encouraged there","Laptops had to be left at the entrance"],"correctAnswer":"They were strictly for silent study","explanation":"He said students could borrow books and laptops, and that the quiet floors were strictly for silent study.","supportingSentence":"Samir explained that students could borrow laptops as well as books, and that quiet floors were strictly for silent study.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What should a possible student take from Samir's account of his first term?","options":["The start of a course can feel like too much because deadlines build up","Guides are forbidden to mention any difficulty","The first term contains almost no deadlines","Tutors never notice whether a student attends"],"correctAnswer":"The start of a course can feel like too much because deadlines build up","explanation":"Samir says the number of deadlines overwhelmed him, so a new student can expect the opening term to feel heavy.","supportingSentence":"He admitted that the first term had overwhelmed him, especially the number of deadlines, but that tutors noticed if someone stopped attending.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does overwhelmed mean in this story?","options":["Feeling that there is more to manage than you can handle","Leaving the college before the first class","Receiving the highest result in the year","Having no work left to do"],"correctAnswer":"Feeling that there is more to manage than you can handle","explanation":"Samir uses it for the feeling created by the number of deadlines in his first term.","supportingSentence":"He admitted that the first term had overwhelmed him, especially the number of deadlines, but that tutors noticed if someone stopped attending.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What warning did the engineering tutor give about the timetable?","options":["Students did no practical work at all","The course was taught only to visitors","It looked light only until project weeks kept the workshops open late","Lectures finished at lunch every day"],"correctAnswer":"It looked light only until project weeks kept the workshops open late","explanation":"The tutor said the timetable seemed light only until the project weeks, when the workshops stayed open late.","supportingSentence":"The tutor said the timetable looked light only until the project weeks began, when workshops stayed open late.","skill":"detail"}],"wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq58565$::jsonb,
  '8b15c0d2f9c84d3ff530dc0a2e4e980e336b7bac77c043b91239fc52d11a5a4f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'd7d21c93-a648-4505-a11d-322200559298'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5312${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq5312$::jsonb,
  $rbq10131${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"The Physiotherapy Plan","story":"Mara had expected the sprain to settle if she stayed off her feet, yet three weeks later her ankle still protested on the stairs. At the community clinic, the physiotherapist Lena refused to promise a miracle. She handed over a written plan, explained every line, and told Mara to pin it where it could not be ignored. Each morning Mara circled the foot slowly, then rose onto her toes until the calf began to tremble. A short evening walk on flat ground was allowed only when the swelling had not returned.\n\nLena described pain as a signal rather than a target, and she warned that a sharp stab would set the recovery back by days. Mara coached a junior netball side and hated the thought of another Saturday spent on a plastic chair. She asked whether she could demonstrate a chest pass while seated. Lena agreed, provided the injured foot stayed off the floor and Mara stopped if the ache turned from dull to bright. That permission mattered, because coaching was the part of the week that made her feel useful.\n\nBy the second week the exercises felt less like a punishment and more like a private routine. Mara marked each one in a notebook, not because anyone checked, but because the ticks made slow progress visible. One Thursday the swelling stayed down, and she climbed the stairs without gripping the rail. She still wore a support bandage to training and sat out the sprints, which annoyed players who wanted her voice beside them. Lena said a coach who limped through a session taught the wrong lesson about courage.\n\nThe plan would continue for six more weeks, with a review if the ankle stiffened after rain. Mara taped a fresh page beside the first and wrote the date she hoped to jog. It was a direction rather than a promise, and the injury began to feel like a problem she could manage. She learned to tell tired muscle from a warning pain.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"sprain","definition":"An injury caused by twisting a joint."},{"word":"physiotherapist","definition":"A person who treats injuries with movement and exercise."},{"word":"swelling","definition":"An enlarged area caused by fluid under the skin."},{"word":"signal","definition":"A sign that gives information and should guide action."},{"word":"bandage","definition":"A strip of material used to support an injured part."},{"word":"routine","definition":"A set of actions that someone repeats regularly."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When was Mara allowed to take her evening walk?","options":["On the stairs, as many times as she wished","Only while she was coaching the Saturday session","After a sharp pain, so she could test the ankle","Only on flat ground, and only if the swelling had stayed away"],"correctAnswer":"Only on flat ground, and only if the swelling had stayed away","explanation":"The plan allowed a short walk on flat ground only when the swelling had not come back.","supportingSentence":"A short evening walk on flat ground was allowed only when the swelling had not returned.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How long did Lena expect the rest of the plan to last?","options":["Six more weeks, with a check if rain made the ankle stiff","A year, with no further visit to the clinic","Until the next Saturday, when a match was due","Two days of total rest and then full sprints"],"correctAnswer":"Six more weeks, with a check if rain made the ankle stiff","explanation":"Lena set six further weeks and a review if the ankle stiffened after rain.","supportingSentence":"The plan would continue for six more weeks, with a review if the ankle stiffened after rain.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What seems to weigh on Mara more than the exercises themselves?","options":["Being kept away from the coaching that made her feel useful","Having to copy the plan into her own handwriting","The cost of each visit to the community clinic","A race she had already entered for the spring"],"correctAnswer":"Being kept away from the coaching that made her feel useful","explanation":"The seated pass mattered because coaching was the part of the week that made her feel useful.","supportingSentence":"That permission mattered, because coaching was the part of the week that made her feel useful.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is Lena's view of Mara limping through a training session?","options":["It would be fine if Mara hid the bandage","It would show that a sharp pain can be ignored","It would make the ankle heal more quickly","It would teach the players an unhelpful idea of bravery"],"correctAnswer":"It would teach the players an unhelpful idea of bravery","explanation":"Lena says a limping coach teaches the wrong lesson about courage, so a hasty return is not bravery.","supportingSentence":"Lena said a coach who limped through a session taught the wrong lesson about courage.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In Lena's advice, what does the word signal suggest about pain?","options":["A score she is supposed to beat each morning","Information that should guide what Mara does next","Proof that the written plan has already failed","A sign that the ankle is ready for sprints"],"correctAnswer":"Information that should guide what Mara does next","explanation":"Lena calls pain a signal rather than a target, so it is information to act on, not a goal.","supportingSentence":"Lena described pain as a signal rather than a target, and she warned that a sharp stab would set the recovery back by days.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq10131$::jsonb,
  'd7d21c93a648b505711d322200559298943cab78181f4276dcda1421fa7319ac',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '2fb9193e-c1c7-4732-ac10-9c7697c01ee6'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5201${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq5201$::jsonb,
  $rbq10510${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"The Regional Food Fair","story":"The regional food fair took over the old cattle market for a windy weekend, and Amina arrived before the gates with a borrowed table and three crates of pickled vegetables. She had spent the summer testing jars, changing the vinegar until the carrots tasted sharp rather than harsh. A small committee, not a visiting celebrity, would taste a short list of entries and give a paper award to the preserve that best showed a home method. Amina wanted the written notes more than the ribbon.\n\nHer stall stood between a cheese maker from the hills and a teenager selling herb oil. The rules were pinned to a post. Samples had to be small, allergens had to be written in clear letters, and nobody was to speak badly of a neighbour's recipe. Amina had printed cards that named beetroot, dill, and apple vinegar, and she kept the prices modest so people might finish a jar at home. By late morning she had learned to offer a taste before a speech. The visitors who stayed longest asked about salt, not about her family history.\n\nAfter lunch a juror in a plain coat tried the beetroot and the carrot, then asked why the carrot was cut thick. Amina explained that a thin slice turned soft in the jar and that she preferred a bite which stayed firm. The juror wrote a line, thanked her, and moved on without a smile. For the next hour Amina was sure she had sounded defensive. The cheese maker leaned over and said a direct answer was exactly what the tasting panel had requested.\n\nWhen the award list went up on the office door, Amina's beetroot received a comment for clear flavour and no prize. The disappointment lasted a moment, and then a steadier feeling replaced it. The fair had shown her which jar people finished and which they only praised. She packed the remaining carrot for Monday and rewrote one line on the card so it read that the pieces were cut thick on purpose.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"regional","definition":"Belonging to one area of a country."},{"word":"stall","definition":"A table or stand where food is shown and sold."},{"word":"samples","definition":"Small tastes offered to visitors."},{"word":"juror","definition":"A person asked to taste entries and help judge them."},{"word":"award","definition":"A prize or public recognition for a piece of work."},{"word":"preserve","definition":"Food kept with vinegar or sugar, such as a pickle."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Who was responsible for tasting the entries?","options":["The teenager selling bottles of herb oil","A small committee rather than a famous guest","Every visitor who stopped at the stall","A celebrity cook invited from the capital"],"correctAnswer":"A small committee rather than a famous guest","explanation":"A small committee, not a visiting celebrity, would taste a short list and give the paper award.","supportingSentence":"A small committee, not a visiting celebrity, would taste a short list of entries and give a paper award to the preserve that best showed a home method.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did Amina's beetroot fare when the list was posted?","options":["It was noted for clear flavour but did not win","It was banned from the Monday market","It was ordered to be thrown away","It received the main paper award"],"correctAnswer":"It was noted for clear flavour but did not win","explanation":"The award list gave the beetroot a comment for clear flavour and no prize.","supportingSentence":"When the award list went up on the office door, Amina's beetroot received a comment for clear flavour and no prize.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the juror's departure leave Amina uneasy?","options":["A polite exit with no warmth felt like a poor judgement","The panel had closed her stall before lunch","The cheese maker had mocked her prices in front of the crowd","She had forgotten to write the allergens on the card"],"correctAnswer":"A polite exit with no warmth felt like a poor judgement","explanation":"The juror thanked her and left without a smile, after which she spent an hour sure she had sounded defensive.","supportingSentence":"The juror wrote a line, thanked her, and moved on without a smile.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the new line on the carrot card suggest about Amina?","options":["She wanted buyers not to notice the beetroot comment","She chose to explain her method instead of sounding unsure about it","She decided to cut the carrot thin for the Monday sale","She removed the price so the jars would be given away"],"correctAnswer":"She chose to explain her method instead of sounding unsure about it","explanation":"She rewrote the card to say the pieces were cut thick on purpose, so she was owning the choice.","supportingSentence":"She packed the remaining carrot for Monday and rewrote one line on the card so it read that the pieces were cut thick on purpose.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In the rules of the fair, what are samples?","options":["Written attacks on a neighbour's recipe","Paper prizes pinned up on the office door","Tickets required to enter the old market","Small tastes of food given to people at a stall"],"correctAnswer":"Small tastes of food given to people at a stall","explanation":"The rules require samples to be small, and Amina was offering visitors a taste before she talked.","supportingSentence":"Samples had to be small, allergens had to be written in clear letters, and nobody was to speak badly of a neighbour's recipe.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":338,"questionCount":5}$rbq10510$::jsonb,
  '2fb9193ec1c727325c109c7697c01ee62f52655f411937256ca8cd801f322073',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'a193e10f-3d16-453d-a18e-5ed4efbbe451'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4879${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":324,"questionCount":5}$rbq4879$::jsonb,
  $rbq77903${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"The Ridge in the Fog","story":"The ridge had been clear at dawn, but by late morning a thick fog had closed over the path and turned every rock into a guess. Lena stopped where the ground narrowed and waited for Tomas and Iria to catch up. Their map showed a line of small stone piles, yet the nearest cairn was only a pale shape, and the next one had vanished completely.\n\nTomas wanted to keep walking while they could still feel the path under their boots. He argued that the hut lay less than an hour ahead and that turning around would leave them on the open slope after dark. Lena had crossed this ridge before, though never in weather as poor as this, and she remembered a place where the path bent sharply toward a cliff. If they missed that bend, the fog would not warn them in time.\n\nIria, who had twisted an ankle on the way up, settled the argument in a practical way. She pointed out that her pace was already slow and that a rescue in this weather would be harder than a careful retreat. They agreed to descend by the western gully, which was longer but sheltered from the worst of the wind. Before they left the ridge, Lena tied a bright strip of cloth to the last cairn she could see, so that any walker coming later would know the forward route had been abandoned.\n\nThe gully was steep and wet. Loose stones slid underfoot, and the three of them moved one at a time, with Tomas testing each step before the others followed. Halfway down, the fog thinned enough to reveal a stream they recognized from the map. That glimpse of running water changed the mood at once. They filled their bottles, checked Iria's ankle, and continued with shorter rests than before.\n\nWhen the trees closed around them, Lena felt the worry leave her shoulders. The hut could wait until morning.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"cairn","definition":"A pile of stones used to mark a route."},{"word":"gully","definition":"A narrow channel cut into a hillside."},{"word":"retreat","definition":"A movement back, away from danger."},{"word":"sheltered","definition":"Protected from wind or bad weather."},{"word":"abandoned","definition":"Left behind on purpose."},{"word":"glimpse","definition":"A very brief look at something."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Which way did the group decide to leave the ridge?","options":["Across the open slope so they could arrive before dark","Down the western gully, which was longer but out of the worst wind","Along the cliff bend, which was the shortest way to the hut","Back up the path they had already climbed that morning"],"correctAnswer":"Down the western gully, which was longer but out of the worst wind","explanation":"They agreed to descend by the western gully because it was longer but sheltered from the worst of the wind.","supportingSentence":"They agreed to descend by the western gully, which was longer but sheltered from the worst of the wind.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did Lena attach to the last stone marker she could see?","options":["A bright strip of cloth","A bottle filled from the stream","A copy of their map","A note asking for a rescue"],"correctAnswer":"A bright strip of cloth","explanation":"Before leaving the ridge, Lena tied a bright strip of cloth to the last cairn she could see.","supportingSentence":"Before they left the ridge, Lena tied a bright strip of cloth to the last cairn she could see, so that any walker coming later would know the forward route had been abandoned.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did Lena feel once the group reached the trees?","options":["Less worried than she had been on the ridge","Sure the hut was only a few steps ahead","Ready to climb back and fetch the cloth","Angry that the shorter route had been given up"],"correctAnswer":"Less worried than she had been on the ridge","explanation":"The worry leaving her shoulders shows that she felt safer once the trees closed around them.","supportingSentence":"When the trees closed around them, Lena felt the worry leave her shoulders.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the ending suggest about the group's priorities?","options":["They wanted to photograph the ridge at sunrise","A safe descent mattered more than reaching the hut that night","They planned to try the cliff bend at dawn","They hoped Tomas would go back for the packs"],"correctAnswer":"A safe descent mattered more than reaching the hut that night","explanation":"Saying the hut could wait shows that getting down safely mattered more than arriving that night.","supportingSentence":"The hut could wait until morning.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is a cairn in this story?","options":["A brief sight of a familiar stream","A steep wet channel in the hillside","A pile of stones used to mark the route","A cloth left as a message for walkers"],"correctAnswer":"A pile of stones used to mark the route","explanation":"The same sentence calls the map markers small stone piles and then calls the nearest one a cairn.","supportingSentence":"Their map showed a line of small stone piles, yet the nearest cairn was only a pale shape, and the next one had vanished completely.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":324,"questionCount":5}$rbq77903$::jsonb,
  'a193e10f3d16753d918e5ed4efbbe451eeeb2b545bd9f543b1ce73df80fedd02',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

