  'text',
  $rbq4640${"topic":"science","wordCount":329,"questionCount":5}$rbq4640$::jsonb,
  $rbq76185${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"The River Sample","story":"The science club at Kedley School wanted to know whether the stream behind the playing field was healthy enough for the spring pond dip. A single look at the water was not evidence. On Thursday after class, their teacher, Idris, took the group to two points. The first was upstream of the school drain, and the second was just below it. At each point they rinsed a bottle three times, filled it from the middle of the flow, and sealed it before anyone touched the inside of the cap. They also recorded the time, the air temperature, and whether it had rained that day. Although the water looked clear in both places, the club had agreed not to trust appearance alone.\n\nBack in the lab they measured temperature and cloudiness and noted a faint smell in the lower sample. The upstream sample stayed clear in the tube. Idris warned them not to claim that the drain had caused the difference. One pair of bottles, taken on one afternoon, could not prove a cause. A bird, a passing walker, or yesterday's rain might have disturbed the lower site. Consequently, the result was a clue, not a conclusion.\n\nThey repeated the collection the following Thursday, after a dry week, and again on a morning after heavy rain. The pattern was similar on the dry day and stronger after the rain, when the lower sample carried more silt. Meanwhile, they kept the method identical, so the results would be comparable. Gloves, the same rinse, the middle of the flow, and notes on the same sheet were all required. Only then did the club write a cautious report for the head teacher. The stream was probably affected by the drain during wet weather, yet they still needed the town's official test before any pond dip. Careful sampling had narrowed the question. It had not answered every part of it. The club planned one more wet morning before they spoke to the town office.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"evidence","definition":"Information that helps show whether something is true."},{"word":"upstream","definition":"In the direction the water comes from."},{"word":"conclusion","definition":"A final claim about what a result proves."},{"word":"silt","definition":"Fine soil carried along by moving water."},{"word":"comparable","definition":"Able to be compared fairly because the method matches."},{"word":"sampling","definition":"Collecting small amounts to study a larger whole."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where were the two bottles filled?","options":["Inside the lab and the town office","From the tap beside the playing field","Upstream of the drain and just below it","Only at the pond used for the dip"],"correctAnswer":"Upstream of the drain and just below it","explanation":"One point was upstream of the school drain, and the other was just below it.","supportingSentence":"The first was upstream of the school drain, and the second was just below it.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did the club still need before a pond dip?","options":["Permission to skip the rainy visit","An official test from the town","A smell test done by eye only","A new bottle for every student"],"correctAnswer":"An official test from the town","explanation":"They still needed the town's official test before any pond dip.","supportingSentence":"The stream was probably affected by the drain during wet weather, yet they still needed the town's official test before any pond dip.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was a clear look at the stream a weak test?","options":["The lab could not measure temperature","A problem can exist even when the water looks clean","Clear water is always unsafe for a pond dip","Rain never changes what a bottle contains"],"correctAnswer":"A problem can exist even when the water looks clean","explanation":"Both samples looked clear, but the club would not trust appearance, and the lower sample later had a faint smell.","supportingSentence":"Although the water looked clear in both places, the club had agreed not to trust appearance alone.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"silt\" mean in this story?","options":["A bottle rinsed three times","Fine soil carried along by the water","A chemical sent by the town office","The air temperature beside the stream"],"correctAnswer":"Fine soil carried along by the water","explanation":"After heavy rain the lower sample carried more silt, meaning more fine soil in the water.","supportingSentence":"The pattern was similar on the dry day and stronger after the rain, when the lower sample carried more silt.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why wait for another wet morning before calling the town?","options":["The head teacher had banned every report","Dry weather had already closed the stream","They wanted that pattern confirmed once more","The upstream bottle had never been sealed"],"correctAnswer":"They wanted that pattern confirmed once more","explanation":"They had a cautious pattern, not a finished answer, so they planned one more wet morning before speaking to the town.","supportingSentence":"The club planned one more wet morning before they spoke to the town office.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":329,"questionCount":5}$rbq76185$::jsonb,
  '8c274fbe4b957a0c996566f24501a88d591f6994950f1c1543f431e00ccb82a3',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '3b49e426-a2ca-4355-ac2e-1f2af8d83784'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4662${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq4662$::jsonb,
  $rbq71454${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The River That Remembered","story":"The river kept promises in its quieter pools. People still made them on the banks, because some words feel truer when water is listening. Dafne had spoken hers at seventeen, on the evening her brother left to work in the city. She had promised she would never leave the village. The sentence had seemed like loyalty then. Years later it sat in a pool below the ferry and would not thin, although the life around it had changed.\n\nA college had offered her a place to study bridge design, and the term began in ten days. She also knew that her brother, who now built river walls, had never asked her to stay frozen so that he could leave. She went to the ferry at dusk with the letter folded in her coat.\n\nOsip, the ferryman, was oiling the chain. He did not pretend to be surprised. The pool near his landing repeated her old sentence every spring, softly enough that strangers missed it and clearly enough that she never could. He said the river would hold a promise until she obeyed it or replaced it with one she could carry without becoming smaller. The water had no interest in clever wording.\n\nDafne thought of the ferry steps, which crumbled a little more each winter. She spoke a new promise in a voice the pool could keep. She would return each spring to repair those steps, and she would write to her brother whenever the river ran high enough to worry the village. The old sentence loosened like a knot that had been given the right end.\n\nOsip swung the gate open. He did not congratulate her, because the river had done the difficult listening. As the boat moved off, Dafne heard the new words settle, shorter than the old ones and harder to betray. She was leaving, but she was not breaking faith. She had given the water a promise that could travel with her and still find its way home.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"loyalty","definition":"Faithful support for a person or a place."},{"word":"ferry","definition":"A boat that carries people across a river."},{"word":"term","definition":"One of the periods into which a college year is divided."},{"word":"wording","definition":"The particular words chosen to say something."},{"word":"crumbled","definition":"Broke slowly into small pieces."},{"word":"betray","definition":"To break faith with a promise or a person."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why had Dafne's old promise become a problem?","options":["Her brother had ordered her to break it immediately","The ferryman had forgotten how to open the gate","The college had taken the offer away","The promise stayed fixed while the life around it changed"],"correctAnswer":"The promise stayed fixed while the life around it changed","explanation":"The old sentence would not thin, although life around it had changed, so the promise no longer fitted her life.","supportingSentence":"Years later it sat in a pool below the ferry and would not thin, although the life around it had changed.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What had she promised on the evening her brother left?","options":["To study bridge design before the new term","To rebuild the ferry steps every winter","To write whenever the river ran high","That she would never leave the village"],"correctAnswer":"That she would never leave the village","explanation":"She had promised she would never leave the village.","supportingSentence":"She had promised she would never leave the village.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does wording mean in the warning about clever speech?","options":["The sound of a pool repeating a sentence","The pocket where a letter is hidden","The oil used on a ferry chain","The exact words someone chooses"],"correctAnswer":"The exact words someone chooses","explanation":"Clever wording is a tricky way of phrasing a promise, so wording means the words that are chosen.","supportingSentence":"The water had no interest in clever wording.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What was importantly different about the promise she spoke at the pool?","options":["It ended her chance to study bridges","It was so weak that the water refused it","It forced her brother to give up his work","It would be harder to break than the old one"],"correctAnswer":"It would be harder to break than the old one","explanation":"The new words were harder to betray, which means they would be more difficult to break.","supportingSentence":"As the boat moved off, Dafne heard the new words settle, shorter than the old ones and harder to betray.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did she promise to do about the ferry steps?","options":["Stay in the village and refuse the college","Let the old sentence be forgotten at once","Oil the chain so the ferryman could rest","Return each spring and repair them"],"correctAnswer":"Return each spring and repair them","explanation":"She said she would return each spring to repair those steps.","supportingSentence":"She would return each spring to repair those steps, and she would write to her brother whenever the river ran high enough to worry the village.","skill":"detail"}],"wordCount":332,"questionCount":5}$rbq71454$::jsonb,
  '3b49e426a2ca13557c2e1f2af8d837842cc7181004b12a09dafa1396205199f2',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '6a561506-4d8e-4403-aff1-1ca5b9a4db26'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4887${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq4887$::jsonb,
  $rbq20592${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"The Shared Flat Rota","story":"When Maya moved into the third-floor flat, her new housemates promised that the housework would be shared fairly. A handwritten rota was already stuck to the fridge, although so many names had been crossed out that the paper looked exhausted. For the first fortnight she washed up without complaint. However, by the third week the sink was often full when she returned from her evening shift, and the bathroom mirror was spotted with toothpaste.\n\nOn Sunday she asked everyone to sit down for ten minutes. Jonah arrived late and Priya was still wearing her coat, while Luis kept checking his phone until Maya spread a clean sheet of paper on the table and suggested a simpler system. One person would take the kitchen, one the bathroom, one the floors, and one the rubbish, with the jobs rotating every Monday. Priya explained that she travelled for work and could not always be home on bin day. Luis argued that he already cooked most evenings, so cleaning on top of that felt unfair. Jonah admitted, rather reluctantly, that he forgot tasks once they were out of sight.\n\nWithout raising her voice, Maya said the resentment in the flat was already affecting the atmosphere, and that a rota would work only if people swapped duties before a problem appeared. After a long discussion they agreed on three changes. Anyone who was going away had to arrange a swap in advance; unfinished jobs would be mentioned calmly in the group chat rather than shouted about in the corridor; and they would review the plan after one month instead of dropping it at the first difficulty. They also started a small household fund: each missed task meant a modest contribution towards shared items such as washing-up liquid and light bulbs.\n\nThe new rota went up that night. It was not perfect, and Maya still found a dirty mug on Tuesday morning. Nevertheless, the bathroom was clean, the bins went out on time, and nobody pretended the problem belonged to somebody else.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"fortnight","definition":"A period of two weeks."},{"word":"reluctantly","definition":"In a way that shows you do not really want to do something."},{"word":"resentment","definition":"A bitter feeling that a situation is unfair."},{"word":"atmosphere","definition":"The mood shared by the people in a place."},{"word":"modest","definition":"Fairly small rather than large."},{"word":"nevertheless","definition":"In spite of what has just been said."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How often would the household jobs change hands?","options":["They would pass to a different person every Monday","Each person would keep the same job all year","Only the bins would move to someone new","The group would choose new jobs every morning"],"correctAnswer":"They would pass to a different person every Monday","explanation":"Maya's plan gave each person one area, and those jobs rotated every Monday.","supportingSentence":"One person would take the kitchen, one the bathroom, one the floors, and one the rubbish, with the jobs rotating every Monday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What happened if somebody missed an assigned job?","options":["They had to leave the flat within a week","They added a small sum to a fund for shared goods","They lost the right to cook in the kitchen","The others cancelled the rota that night"],"correctAnswer":"They added a small sum to a fund for shared goods","explanation":"A missed task meant a modest contribution towards things the flat shared, such as light bulbs.","supportingSentence":"They also started a small household fund: each missed task meant a modest contribution towards shared items such as washing-up liquid and light bulbs.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the state of the paper on the fridge suggest?","options":["Maya had brought it from her previous home","It was a menu for Sunday dinner","Earlier versions of the plan had been changed again and again","The list had been written once and never altered"],"correctAnswer":"Earlier versions of the plan had been changed again and again","explanation":"So many names had been crossed out that the paper looked worn out, which points to repeated changes.","supportingSentence":"A handwritten rota was already stuck to the fridge, although so many names had been crossed out that the paper looked exhausted.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What point is Luis making about his place in the flat?","options":["He is planning to move out before Monday","He would rather clean every room than cook at all","He feels that cooking most evenings is already a heavy share of the work","He wants the others to stop eating meals at home"],"correctAnswer":"He feels that cooking most evenings is already a heavy share of the work","explanation":"He says extra cleaning feels unfair because he already cooks on most evenings.","supportingSentence":"Luis argued that he already cooked most evenings, so cleaning on top of that felt unfair.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does resentment mean in this story?","options":["A written list of household jobs","A bitter feeling caused by unfairness","A calm discussion in the kitchen","A payment into a shared fund"],"correctAnswer":"A bitter feeling caused by unfairness","explanation":"Maya uses the word for the bad feeling that is already changing the mood of the flat.","supportingSentence":"Without raising her voice, Maya said the resentment in the flat was already affecting the atmosphere, and that a rota would work only if people swapped duties before a problem appeared.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq20592$::jsonb,
  '6a5615064d8e84031ff11ca5b9a4db2643eda62d389d51cdcec8edbbe1bb7c33',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'b6435282-8a90-481b-adfe-5c95525dcde4'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5484${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":322,"questionCount":5}$rbq5484$::jsonb,
  $rbq69010${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"The Staff Collection","story":"When Paul, the bookshop manager, announced that he was leaving, the staff decided to collect for a gift. Somebody suggested that each person put ten pounds into an envelope. It sounded simple until Idris, who worked only on Wednesdays, said he would be paying the same as colleagues who worked the whole week. The room went quiet. Nobody wanted a mean argument, but nobody wanted a collection that felt unfair either.\n\nLeila, who kept the till, turned over a used paper bag and listed the days each person would work in Paul's last week. The full-time names each showed five days, and Idris's name showed one. A flat ten pounds would take a much larger share of his pay. She proposed two pounds for each day worked, written down so anyone could check the sum. The idea was still a contribution, not a tax, and anyone in real difficulty could speak to her privately. People nodded, relieved to have a rule instead of a guess. Nobody was asked to give more than the list required.\n\nThey filled the list that afternoon. Most of the full-time staff put in ten pounds, matching five days. Idris put in two and looked less cornered. One colleague who had already dropped in ten pounds, then realised she had been away for two days, took four pounds back and initialled the change. The envelope reached forty-six pounds. Before they chose the print, Leila checked the prices in two shops and refused a frame that would have swallowed the whole envelope. The card cost four pounds, and the print cost forty-two. Leila kept the paper bag in the till drawer until the gift was bought, because a collection without a record is only a story about who remembers what. Paul was surprised by the print of the old market. He was more surprised, when Leila showed him the list later, by how carefully they had shared the cost.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"collection","definition":"Money gathered from a group for one shared purpose."},{"word":"envelope","definition":"The paper cover in which the collected money was kept."},{"word":"colleagues","definition":"People who work in the same place."},{"word":"contribution","definition":"Money a person chooses to give toward a shared cost."},{"word":"initialled","definition":"Marked with the first letters of someone's name."},{"word":"record","definition":"A written note that other people can check later."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How much did Leila propose for each day someone worked?","options":["Ten pounds","Forty-two pounds","Four pounds","Two pounds"],"correctAnswer":"Two pounds","explanation":"Leila proposed two pounds for each day worked in Paul's last week.","supportingSentence":"She proposed two pounds for each day worked, written down so anyone could check the sum.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What price did Leila find for the print?","options":["Four pounds","Forty-two pounds","Ten pounds","Forty-six pounds"],"correctAnswer":"Forty-two pounds","explanation":"The print cost forty-two pounds, and the card cost four pounds.","supportingSentence":"The card cost four pounds, and the print cost forty-two.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What can the reader conclude about the money one colleague took back?","options":["She changed her share so it matched the days she had worked","She asked for her name to be removed from the card","She wanted the gift itself to cost less","Leila had charged her for the frame that was refused"],"correctAnswer":"She changed her share so it matched the days she had worked","explanation":"She had been away for two days, and at two pounds a day the extra she returned was four pounds.","supportingSentence":"One colleague who had already dropped in ten pounds, then realised she had been away for two days, took four pounds back and initialled the change.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Paul's greater surprise suggest?","options":["The list proved that Idris had paid nothing at all","He was angry that a dearer frame had been refused","The fair sharing stood out more than the gift itself","He had wanted the staff to keep the money for the shop"],"correctAnswer":"The fair sharing stood out more than the gift itself","explanation":"The print surprised him, but he was more surprised by how carefully the staff had shared the cost.","supportingSentence":"He was more surprised, when Leila showed him the list later, by how carefully they had shared the cost.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the word contribution mean in this story?","options":["A tax that every worker has to pay","The frame that would have used the whole sum","Money someone chooses to give toward a shared cost","The list of working days kept in the till"],"correctAnswer":"Money someone chooses to give toward a shared cost","explanation":"Leila calls the payment a contribution, not a tax, and someone in difficulty could speak to her privately.","supportingSentence":"The idea was still a contribution, not a tax, and anyone in real difficulty could speak to her privately.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":322,"questionCount":5}$rbq69010$::jsonb,
  'b64352828a90281bedfe5c95525dcde469c29e6aa9e1ee1db2b1c980af5735f6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '2ca18687-63cc-46e2-af06-8e4df1567930'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4704${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":335,"questionCount":5}$rbq4704$::jsonb,
  $rbq7475${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"The Wrong Parcel","story":"The parcel for Harbour Lane Books arrived just after nine, addressed in full and tied with the usual depot tape. Priya signed, carried it to the counter, and cut the tape. Inside, packed in shredded paper, were metal parts for a sewing machine, with a delivery note that mentioned bobbins and a motor. She had ordered paperbacks. Nothing in the box was a book, and the customer who wanted the new novel was already waiting by the window.\n\nPriya compared the note with the label on the outside. Her shop's name was on the label, but the note named Harbour Lane Repairs, and the last two digits of the postcode were swapped. She rang the number printed on the note and reached a woman there, two streets away, who had just opened a box of paperbacks and was equally confused. The driver was already on the next round and could not come back before noon. Priya had paid the invoice for the paperbacks the week before, so a missing box would have been a loss, not a delay. The repair shop, in turn, had a customer coming at one to collect the motor.\n\nWaiting would have left both shops with the wrong stock on a busy morning, so they agreed to walk the parcels across themselves. Neither woman wanted the other to be short of stock while a customer stood waiting. Priya closed the bookshop for ten minutes, which she rarely did, and met the repairer on the corner by the bakery. They checked the notes aloud, exchanged the boxes, and each kept the copy that named her own shop. They swapped phone numbers beside the bakery before they walked back. The novels were intact, and the sewing parts were still wrapped. Back at the counter, the customer laughed once the story was told and left with the book she had ordered. Priya wrote both postcodes into the order book, so the next delivery would be checked against the door before anyone cut the tape.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"parcel","definition":"A packed box of goods delivered to a shop."},{"word":"depot","definition":"The place from which the deliveries were sent out."},{"word":"postcode","definition":"The letters and numbers that identify an address."},{"word":"swapped","definition":"Exchanged, so each was put in the other's place."},{"word":"invoice","definition":"A bill for goods that have been ordered."},{"word":"delivery","definition":"The bringing of ordered goods to a shop or person."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What was packed inside the box Priya opened?","options":["The paperbacks she had ordered that month","A novel the customer was waiting to buy","Both postcodes copied into an order book","Metal parts for a sewing machine"],"correctAnswer":"Metal parts for a sewing machine","explanation":"Priya's box held metal parts for a sewing machine, not the paperbacks she had ordered.","supportingSentence":"Inside, packed in shredded paper, were metal parts for a sewing machine, with a delivery note that mentioned bobbins and a motor.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What was wrong with the postcode when Priya compared the papers?","options":["The street name belonged to a different town","The depot tape had hidden the whole address","The shop name was missing from both papers","The last two digits were swapped"],"correctAnswer":"The last two digits were swapped","explanation":"The label and the note did not agree, because the last two digits of the postcode were swapped.","supportingSentence":"Her shop's name was on the label, but the note named Harbour Lane Repairs, and the last two digits of the postcode were swapped.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did each shop open goods that belonged to the other?","options":["The customer at the window had carried the books away","Priya had ordered sewing parts and books under one name","A swapped postcode had mixed up two very similar addresses","The driver had refused to read any address at all"],"correctAnswer":"A swapped postcode had mixed up two very similar addresses","explanation":"The bookshop label and the repair-shop note differed by swapped postcode digits, and each shop had the other's goods.","supportingSentence":"Her shop's name was on the label, but the note named Harbour Lane Repairs, and the last two digits of the postcode were swapped.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why was closing the bookshop for ten minutes a reasonable choice?","options":["The mix-up was too urgent to leave until the driver returned","The repairer had asked her to shut for the rest of the day","Priya preferred a walk to the bakery over a sale","Ten minutes was the lunch break she took every morning"],"correctAnswer":"The mix-up was too urgent to leave until the driver returned","explanation":"Priya rarely closed, but the driver could not return before noon and both shops had customers waiting.","supportingSentence":"The driver was already on the next round and could not come back before noon.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the word invoice mean in this story?","options":["A bill for the books she had already ordered","The phone number printed on the label","The motor the repair shop's customer wanted","The tape the depot used to close the box"],"correctAnswer":"A bill for the books she had already ordered","explanation":"The invoice was the bill for the paperbacks, and Priya had paid it the week before.","supportingSentence":"Priya had paid the invoice for the paperbacks the week before, so a missing box would have been a loss, not a delay.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":335,"questionCount":5}$rbq7475$::jsonb,
  '2ca1868763cc76e2df068e4df15679302eb6d8b0b335a56369b9a7a14c84d92b',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '28df9f0d-d769-4c04-aaf8-271dbac8025c'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq5316${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq5316$::jsonb,
  $rbq10641${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"Training in the Heat","story":"The forecast promised a heavy, bright afternoon, so coach Priya moved the track session earlier and hoped to finish before the worst of the heat. Even at nine the air was thick with humidity, and the warm-up left shirts dark with sweat. Priya gathered the runners by the stand and set out a precaution that was not open to debate. Anyone whose face went pale, or who stopped sweating, had to leave the track and sit with a cold drink. Pride, she told them, was a poor partner on a day like this.\n\nJonah had hoped to chase a fast time over one lap. Priya cut that idea before he could argue. The workout became repetitions at a comfortable pace, with a full walk between them and a drink that replaced more than water. She called the bottles an electrolyte mix, a salty drink to replace what sweat removed, and made every runner taste one. Jonah found the flavour salty, yet he finished the bottle because the alternative was a seat in the shade.\n\nHalfway through the set the far straight began to shimmer. Jonah's legs felt heavier than the pace deserved, which Priya had said might happen when the body spent effort on cooling itself. She shortened the last two runs and moved the group onto the grass beneath the trees. A younger runner wanted extra sprints in the sun to prove he was fine. Priya refused, and she asked him to explain why the idea was unsafe. He mentioned dehydration and then went quiet, which was answer enough.\n\nJonah cooled down with easy strides where the shade was deepest, then read the card Priya had scribbled for him. It told him to weigh himself before and after hot sessions and to sleep earlier before a warm race. He was disappointed to leave without a sharp time, but he understood that a collapsed runner helped nobody. On the bus he wrote that the session had been slow and that the slowness had been the point.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"humidity","definition":"Dampness held in the air."},{"word":"precaution","definition":"An action taken in advance to stop harm."},{"word":"pace","definition":"The speed at which a person runs."},{"word":"electrolyte","definition":"A salt, in a drink, that replaces what sweat takes from the body."},{"word":"dehydration","definition":"A dangerous lack of water in the body."},{"word":"shade","definition":"An area that is out of direct sun."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why did Priya bring the track session forward?","options":["She hoped to finish before the hottest part of the day","Jonah had asked to race that same afternoon","The clubhouse clock was running an hour fast","Another sport had booked the track after nine"],"correctAnswer":"She hoped to finish before the hottest part of the day","explanation":"A hot afternoon was forecast, so she moved the session in order to finish before the worst heat.","supportingSentence":"The forecast promised a heavy, bright afternoon, so coach Priya moved the track session earlier and hoped to finish before the worst of the heat.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Which habits did the card tell Jonah to take up?","options":["Training at midday to get used to collapsing","Adding sprints in full sun after every easy run","Skipping drinks so that he would feel lighter","Checking his weight around hot sessions and sleeping earlier before a warm race"],"correctAnswer":"Checking his weight around hot sessions and sleeping earlier before a warm race","explanation":"The card told him to weigh himself before and after hot sessions and to sleep earlier before a warm race.","supportingSentence":"It told him to weigh himself before and after hot sessions and to sleep earlier before a warm race.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What matters most to Priya once the day turns hot?","options":["Giving the people in the stand something exciting to watch","Making the drinks taste less salty for Jonah","Proving that her group can beat every neighbouring club","Stopping pride from pushing anyone into a risky effort"],"correctAnswer":"Stopping pride from pushing anyone into a risky effort","explanation":"She calls pride a poor partner and builds the session around rules that keep runners from overreaching.","supportingSentence":"Pride, she told them, was a poor partner on a day like this.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Priya ask the younger runner to explain his idea?","options":["She needed a volunteer to lead sprints in the sun","She wanted him to see the danger, not only to follow an order","She hoped the group would vote to keep running","She was unsure of the workout and wanted him to redesign it"],"correctAnswer":"She wanted him to see the danger, not only to follow an order","explanation":"Making him say why the extra sprints were unsafe pushed him to understand the risk himself.","supportingSentence":"Priya refused, and she asked him to explain why the idea was unsafe.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does humidity describe at the start of this session?","options":["A running shoe designed for wet weather","Dampness in the air that made the morning feel heavy","A cold wind blowing across the track","The covered stand beside the finishing line"],"correctAnswer":"Dampness in the air that made the morning feel heavy","explanation":"The air was thick with humidity, so the word refers to the moisture that made the morning feel heavy.","supportingSentence":"Even at nine the air was thick with humidity, and the warm-up left shirts dark with sweat.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":336,"questionCount":5}$rbq10641$::jsonb,
  '28df9f0dd7698c04faf8271dbac8025c8530e8d220bd52333128f744234c0514',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '00897f32-5550-4e3e-a16c-c80f168efda3'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $rbq4884${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":329,"questionCount":5}$rbq4884$::jsonb,
  $rbq82459${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"Waiting for the Tide","story":"The causeway disappeared faster than Jon had expected. One minute the stone line to the mainland was a wet road, and the next it was a chain of dark shapes under shallow waves. He and his sister Priya stood on the island beach with their kayaks above the weed, watching the gap widen. A posted sign gave the evening tide times in plain numbers. They had spent those minutes photographing the abbey ruins, and the sea had used the same minutes more seriously.\n\nWaiting was now the whole task. The next safe crossing would not come until after nightfall, and the wind might keep the water restless even then. Priya checked the hatches and moved the food bag into the lee of a wall. Jon found a higher shelf of rock that would make a drier camp, and they carried the boats up in two tired trips.\n\nThe only light came from a cottage whose owner, an older man in a salt-stiff coat, came down to ask whether they were equipped to stay. He did not offer a rescue, because nothing sensible could be attempted until the tide turned. He lent them a dry blanket and told them which part of the beach the water never reached in ordinary weather.\n\nThey ate slowly and took turns watching the causeway. Priya kept the tide table under a stone so the wind could not take it, as if the printed hours were a promise the sea had to keep. Jon could see that she was angry with the delay and angrier with herself.\n\nWhen the moon rose, dark stone returned in patches, then in a broken line. The cottage owner called down that they should wait for the full width, since a shining strip might be an inch deep or a foot. They waited until the road was a road. Only then did they drag the kayaks toward the mainland, more interested in the lesson than in the ruins behind them.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"causeway","definition":"A raised road that crosses water or wet ground."},{"word":"tide","definition":"The regular rise and fall of the sea."},{"word":"lee","definition":"The side of something that is sheltered from the wind."},{"word":"equipped","definition":"Having the things needed for a situation."},{"word":"restless","definition":"Moving or uneasy, and not calm."},{"word":"width","definition":"The distance from one side of something to the other."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What information did the posted sign give?","options":["The price of a room in the cottage","A list of boats that had already left","The name of every person on the bus","The evening times of the tide, written plainly"],"correctAnswer":"The evening times of the tide, written plainly","explanation":"The posted sign gave the evening tide times in plain numbers.","supportingSentence":"A posted sign gave the evening tide times in plain numbers.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the time at the ruins become a problem?","options":["The photographs proved the road was still open","The abbey doors closed and trapped them inside","Priya used them to repair a broken hatch","Those were the minutes they needed for the crossing"],"correctAnswer":"Those were the minutes they needed for the crossing","explanation":"They spent the minutes photographing the ruins while the sea used the same minutes, so the tide came in during the delay.","supportingSentence":"They had spent those minutes photographing the abbey ruins, and the sea had used the same minutes more seriously.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What help did the cottage owner give them?","options":["A new set of numbers for the posted sign","A boat ride back to the mainland","A hot meal from the closed cafe","A dry blanket and advice about where the water would not reach"],"correctAnswer":"A dry blanket and advice about where the water would not reach","explanation":"He lent them a dry blanket and told them which part of the beach the water never reached in ordinary weather.","supportingSentence":"He lent them a dry blanket and told them which part of the beach the water never reached in ordinary weather.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why should they avoid the first shining strip of stone?","options":["The boats would scratch the light of the moon","The ruins were more important than the road","The blanket had to be returned before they moved","That strip might still be deep enough to be unsafe"],"correctAnswer":"That strip might still be deep enough to be unsafe","explanation":"A shining strip might be an inch deep or a foot, so it was not yet proof of a safe road.","supportingSentence":"The cottage owner called down that they should wait for the full width, since a shining strip might be an inch deep or a foot.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does restless mean about the water after nightfall?","options":["Low enough for an easy crossing","Frozen along the edge of the beach","Written in plain numbers on a sign","Still moving instead of becoming calm"],"correctAnswer":"Still moving instead of becoming calm","explanation":"The wind might keep the water restless even after nightfall, so the sea may still be unsettled.","supportingSentence":"The next safe crossing would not come until after nightfall, and the wind might keep the water restless even then.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":329,"questionCount":5}$rbq82459$::jsonb,
  '00897f3255500e3e316cc80f168efda3b0abb68eefbe0cc82ef5237c741b43d9',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '7e66e12b-61a8-42a5-a795-b9cd49ecd454'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4704${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":425,"questionCount":5}$rbq4704$::jsonb,
  $rbq80908${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"A Badge That Still Opened","story":"The badge should have died on the last Friday of the contract. Facilities had a checklist for that: collect the card, revoke it on the access system, and file the signed leaver form before the person reached the lift. Three weeks later, a clerk named Yusuf heard the side door of the records office open at a time when he was supposed to be alone. He stepped into the corridor and saw nobody. The door had already closed. He checked the leaver form in the shared drawer and found it signed, dated, and apparently complete. The card itself had never been returned. On the floor inside the threshold lay a tender folder he had tied and shelved the previous afternoon.\n\nHe pulled the access log before he touched the folder again. The door had been opened with badge 4418, assigned to a contractor who had designed the shelving and whose contract had ended twenty-two days earlier. The log showed a clean deactivation on the final afternoon, a gap of twenty-one days, and then a reactivation just after midnight on the Sunday before last. The second change had been made with a supervisor code. The code belonged to the facilities manager. She was on leave, and had been on leave since the Friday, a fact Yusuf confirmed with the shared calendar before he allowed himself a worse thought.\n\nThe folder's tie was intact, which puzzled him until he compared it with the photograph he had taken for the handover note. The knot sat a finger's width lower than in the picture. Someone had untied it, read or removed a page, and retied it with care that was almost good enough. The page count on the cover said forty-eight. Yusuf counted forty-seven. The missing sheet was the pricing schedule, the one page a rival firm would have been glad to see before the submission deadline on Thursday.\n\nHe locked the office from the inside, left the folder where it was, and called the duty security officer rather than the number stored against the supervisor code. Then he wrote down the times from the log in his own notebook, because screens can be altered by the same people who alter doors. When security arrived, he did not offer a theory about who had stood in the corridor. He offered the badge number, the midnight change, and the gap in the page count. The badge still opened a door it should have forgotten. That single fact was enough to stop the building from treating the evening as a glitch.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"revoke","definition":"To cancel a permission so that it no longer works."},{"word":"deactivation","definition":"The act of switching an access card off."},{"word":"reactivation","definition":"The act of switching an access card back on."},{"word":"tender","definition":"A formal bid for a piece of work, here kept in a folder."},{"word":"schedule","definition":"A list that sets out items, here the prices in the bid."},{"word":"glitch","definition":"A small technical fault, not a serious breach."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Which sheet was no longer inside the tender folder?","options":["The pricing schedule","The facilities checklist","The handover photograph","The signed leaver form"],"correctAnswer":"The pricing schedule","explanation":"Yusuf counted one sheet fewer than the cover promised, and the missing sheet was the pricing schedule.","supportingSentence":"The missing sheet was the pricing schedule, the one page a rival firm would have been glad to see before the submission deadline on Thursday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What can a reader be sure of about the person who came through the side door?","options":["The facilities manager was standing in the threshold.","A rival firm signed the visitor book at that door.","The log identifies the badge that opened the door, but not who was holding it.","Yusuf recognised the contractor and followed him to the lift."],"correctAnswer":"The log identifies the badge that opened the door, but not who was holding it.","explanation":"Yusuf saw nobody, and when security arrived he offered the badge number rather than a name. The record shows a card, not a face.","supportingSentence":"When security arrived, he did not offer a theory about who had stood in the corridor.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Yusuf's choice of phone call suggest?","options":["He wants the facilities manager to unlock the door from home.","He does not want the warning to go through the code that was just misused.","He thinks the duty officer removed the missing page.","He already knows which colleague was in the corridor."],"correctAnswer":"He does not want the warning to go through the code that was just misused.","explanation":"He calls the duty security officer instead of the number stored against the supervisor code used to switch the badge back on.","supportingSentence":"He locked the office from the inside, left the folder where it was, and called the duty security officer rather than the number stored against the supervisor code.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does revoke mean in the facilities checklist?","options":["To print a fresh copy of a tender folder","To cancel a card on the access system","To lend a badge to the next contractor","To count the sheets in a file"],"correctAnswer":"To cancel a card on the access system","explanation":"The checklist says to collect the card and revoke it on the access system, meaning to switch that permission off.","supportingSentence":"Facilities had a checklist for that: collect the card, revoke it on the access system, and file the signed leaver form before the person reached the lift.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What does the writer want the reader to reject in the closing lines?","options":["The idea that contractors may design shelving","The idea that security officers keep notebooks","The idea that tender folders should be tied with string","The idea that a badge which still opened was only a minor fault"],"correctAnswer":"The idea that a badge which still opened was only a minor fault","explanation":"The last lines say that one fact, the badge still opening, was enough to stop the evening being treated as a glitch.","supportingSentence":"That single fact was enough to stop the building from treating the evening as a glitch.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":425,"questionCount":5}$rbq80908$::jsonb,
  '7e66e12b61a8e2a58795b9cd49ecd4548c83cbafc17ab9845eec9ab8cdfcb0b5',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '15d29c8c-ab61-4be9-a2f7-5f62d61668bb'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4641${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq4641$::jsonb,
  $rbq62990${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"A Choir on Tour","story":"The choir from Ostel spent four days in a borrowed minibus, singing in rooms that had never been asked to look after music. Nia Hesk, who kept both the scores and the petrol money, had accepted the tour because a string of small libraries wanted something live between lectures. Their usual hall was under a scaffold, and waiting for the roof would have meant a silent season. Illness had reduced twenty booked voices to sixteen, so they learned a quicker way to arrive, take a note from a pitch pipe, and begin while the audience was still deciding whether to sit.\n\nThe repertoire was deliberately narrow. Four pieces could be held in tune by tired people, and one newer work still made the tenors glance sideways at one another. In the first library the ceiling was low, and the sound came back at them almost before it had left. Nia reduced the volume and asked for consonants that carried the words without giving the room a shove. The second venue, a converted grain store, produced the opposite trouble. Tone thinned before it reached the back chairs. They stood closer, which improved the blend and irritated the bass who guarded his space as if it were part of the score. Nia treated each adjustment as musical work, not as a compromise with an inferior room. A piece that could prosper in only one acoustic was, for this week, unfinished.\n\nOn the last afternoon a lecture overran, and the reading room held half the expected listeners and, by the singers' own later admission, twice the attention. During the new work a tenor missed an entry, found the next phrase, and did not apologise with his eyebrows. Nia counted that restraint as professionalism, because a face that confesses a mistake can persuade an audience that the piece has failed. Afterwards a librarian asked about a return in spring. Nia said she would look at the roof and at the voices, and she would not promise the same programme merely to be agreeable. Walking to the bus, she heard two singers quietly repairing the missed entry until it sat cleanly. The tour had shown them how much of a performance is fitting: altering the sound to the room without sanding the piece down to something easier. In the minibus Nia wrote the adjustments in the margin of the score, not as apologies, but as instructions for the next unsuitable room. The bass, who had given up a little space and survived, asked whether the grain store had been the best of them. Nia said it had been the clearest teacher.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"repertoire","definition":"The set of pieces a performer or group is ready to present."},{"word":"consonants","definition":"Speech sounds such as t or k that shape words clearly."},{"word":"blend","definition":"The way separate voices mix into one sound."},{"word":"acoustic","definition":"The way a particular room carries and changes sound."},{"word":"phrase","definition":"A short musical passage within a longer piece."},{"word":"professionalism","definition":"Skilled, controlled behaviour suitable to the work."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why could the choir not use its usual hall?","options":["A lecture series had booked it for four days","Illness had closed every room in Ostel","The grain store had replaced it permanently","The roof work had put the hall under scaffold"],"correctAnswer":"The roof work had put the hall under scaffold","explanation":"Their usual hall was under a scaffold, and waiting for the roof would have meant a silent season.","supportingSentence":"Their usual hall was under a scaffold, and waiting for the roof would have meant a silent season.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What approach to mistakes do Nia and the singers seem to share?","options":["Hold steady in front of listeners, then correct the fault in private","Sing louder whenever a ceiling feels low","Drop any piece that still worries the tenors","Apologise with a look so the room can share the error"],"correctAnswer":"Hold steady in front of listeners, then correct the fault in private","explanation":"The tenor does not show the miss on his face, and later two singers quietly repair the entry on the way to the bus.","supportingSentence":"Walking to the bus, she heard two singers quietly repairing the missed entry until it sat cleanly.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What do the low library and the grain store together imply?","options":["Listeners at the back should be ignored","A piece must be adapted to each room rather than sung one fixed way","A performance succeeds only under a low ceiling","Sixteen voices are too few for any library"],"correctAnswer":"A piece must be adapted to each room rather than sung one fixed way","explanation":"One room threw the sound back and the other swallowed it, and Nia treats a piece that can live in only one room as unfinished.","supportingSentence":"A piece that could prosper in only one acoustic was, for this week, unfinished.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does repertoire refer to in this story?","options":["The petrol money Nia kept for the tour","The route the minibus took between towns","The limited set of music the choir brought","The scaffold covering their usual hall"],"correctAnswer":"The limited set of music the choir brought","explanation":"The repertoire is described as narrow, and the following lines name the few pieces they could hold when tired.","supportingSentence":"The repertoire was deliberately narrow.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer end on the grain store as a teacher?","options":["To announce that the choir plans to buy the building","To blame the bass for the missed entry","To claim the lecture was better than the music","To value the difficult room for the lesson it gave"],"correctAnswer":"To value the difficult room for the lesson it gave","explanation":"The bass had disliked standing close there, yet Nia calls that room the clearest teacher, judging it by what the singers learned.","supportingSentence":"Nia said it had been the clearest teacher.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq62990$::jsonb,
  '15d29c8cab614be9f2f75f62d61668bbfc3d58f6a3af5ca5d414ab65b93d6209',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '139c4369-de09-4b6f-a0d6-a55b9d87d9da'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5384${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq5384$::jsonb,
  $rbq93002${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"A Contested Grade","story":"Amir collected the essay from the office with the mark he had feared and a comment he had not. The script sat at the lower edge of a merit, and the second page of feedback praised his reading while judging the central claim unsubstantiated. He had spent weeks on the assignment, which asked whether a city's night-bus cuts fell hardest on workers who started before dawn. What the marker, Professor Adeyemi, had not accepted was the leap from three interviews to a sentence about the city as a whole.\n\nHe requested a review the same afternoon, using the faculty form rather than a personal email. The form asked him to name the criterion he believed had been misapplied, and to avoid a general statement that the mark felt low. Amir wrote that the brief had invited a limited study, and that he had described his interviews as illustrative rather than representative. If the marker had wanted city-wide proof, the word count would not have allowed it. He attached the briefing sheet and underlined explore, which he thought signalled a narrower task than prove. He also conceded, because the rules asked students to be specific, that one paragraph stated the pattern more firmly than his sample could support.\n\nThe review took ten days. A second marker read the script without the earlier comments, then the two markers met. Their notes, which Amir was allowed to see afterwards, showed a discrepancy on a single descriptor: how far a conclusion may go beyond its evidence. Professor Adeyemi had treated the firm paragraph as a failure of method. The second marker called it a local overstatement in an otherwise controlled essay, and argued that the comment should have asked for a hedge rather than pulled the whole mark down. They agreed a modest revision. The grade moved to the middle of the same band, and a new sentence was added to the feedback, explaining that the interviews could support a claim about those workers but not about every route in the city.\n\nAmir read the revised sheet twice. He was glad the process had been documented, and dissatisfied that the original comment still called his tone assertive. The faculty letter made plain that a review could correct the application of a criterion, not rewrite the marker's taste. If he submitted a similar essay again, he would fence the final claim with the limits of the sample before anyone asked him to. The mark was fairer than it had been. It was not the mark he had imagined when he chose a question that was harder to evidence than to care about.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"unsubstantiated","definition":"Not supported with enough evidence"},{"word":"criterion","definition":"A standard used when judging work"},{"word":"illustrative","definition":"Serving as an example, not as proof about everyone"},{"word":"discrepancy","definition":"A difference between two accounts or judgements"},{"word":"overstatement","definition":"A claim that goes further than the evidence"},{"word":"hedge","definition":"Wording that makes a claim less absolute"}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where did the mark sit after the two markers had met?","options":["At the bottom of a lower band","Off the record until the essay was rewritten","In exactly the place first given by the marker","In the middle of the same band"],"correctAnswer":"In the middle of the same band","explanation":"The grade moved to the middle of the same band after a modest revision.","supportingSentence":"The grade moved to the middle of the same band, and a new sentence was added to the feedback, explaining that the interviews could support a claim about those workers but not about every route in the city.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was the firm paragraph a problem for the first marker?","options":["It treated three interviews as if they described the whole city","It attacked the marker in a closing footnote","It ignored every limit stated on the briefing sheet","It quoted sources that did not match the reading list"],"correctAnswer":"It treated three interviews as if they described the whole city","explanation":"He rejected the leap from three interviews to a claim about the city as a whole.","supportingSentence":"What the marker, Professor Adeyemi, had not accepted was the leap from three interviews to a sentence about the city as a whole.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is a hedge in the comment from the second marker?","options":["A private message sent instead of the form","Wording that stops a claim from going too far","A clean copy with the earlier mark hidden","A demand that every merit be raised"],"correctAnswer":"Wording that stops a claim from going too far","explanation":"The second marker wanted a limit on the claim rather than a lower mark for the whole essay.","supportingSentence":"The second marker called it a local overstatement in an otherwise controlled essay, and argued that the comment should have asked for a hedge rather than pulled the whole mark down.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include the faculty letter about taste?","options":["To show that a review can correct a standard but not every opinion","To suggest the first marker refused to meet the second","To prove that tone was the only reason for the original mark","To argue that interviews should be banned from essays"],"correctAnswer":"To show that a review can correct a standard but not every opinion","explanation":"The letter says a review can correct how a criterion was applied, not a marker's private taste.","supportingSentence":"The faculty letter made plain that a review could correct the application of a criterion, not rewrite the marker's taste.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"Why is Amir still uneasy after the mark improves?","options":["The second reader refused to look at the script","The new feedback pushed the grade down again","The faculty hid the notes from him","A remark about his tone remains, even though the standard was adjusted"],"correctAnswer":"A remark about his tone remains, even though the standard was adjusted","explanation":"He is glad the process was recorded, but the original comment still calls his tone assertive.","supportingSentence":"He was glad the process had been documented, and dissatisfied that the original comment still called his tone assertive.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq93002$::jsonb,
  '139c4369de09fb6fc0d6a55b9d87d9da382a187f1ac37952c9867f8c44ca9743',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ec1dcece-1012-4aa5-ac91-3c3ae8838e39'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5490${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq5490$::jsonb,
  $rbq80780${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"A Disputed Invoice","story":"The invoice arrived on a Tuesday, clipped to an envelope that still smelled faintly of the print shop downstairs. It asked the design studio for six hundred and eighty pounds, most of it listed as rush hours on a poster series the client had already approved. Petra, who managed the books from a desk beside the kettle, remembered authorizing a flat fee for three illustrations and a single round of changes. She did not remember authorizing nights, weekends, or a rate that doubled after six. The studio's standard terms, pinned above her desk, named the flat fee and forbade overtime unless she had confirmed it in writing the day before the work.\n\nShe laid the purchase order, the email thread, and the illustrator's timesheet in a row. The purchase order named the fee and the deadline. The emails showed that the client had requested a different typeface on a Thursday afternoon, and that Petra had replied, before the end of the day, agreeing to the change inside the original fee. The timesheet told another story. It recorded nine additional hours on Friday evening and four on Saturday, each marked urgent, none of them mentioned in any message she could find. The discrepancy was not a matter of arithmetic. The hours might well have been worked. The question was whether anyone at the studio had asked for them.\n\nPetra rang the illustrator and kept her voice level. He said the client had telephoned him directly, anxious about a board meeting, and that he had assumed the studio would stand behind the extra work because the relationship mattered. He had not written that assumption down. Petra listened, then read him the clause in their short contract that required written approval before any charge above the agreed fee. She did not call him careless. She said the studio could not pay, in full, for work it had not been given the chance to refuse.\n\nThey settled that afternoon. The studio would pay the original fee at once, plus a smaller sum for the Friday hours, which had at least kept the project on the client's timetable. The Saturday hours would be dropped. In return, the illustrator would send future variations only after a short written note, even if the note was a single sentence on his phone. Petra amended the invoice by hand, initialled the change, and filed the emails behind it. The amount that left the account was smaller than the figure on the first page. The more useful result was the habit they had both agreed to keep.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"invoice","definition":"A bill asking to be paid for goods or work."},{"word":"authorizing","definition":"Officially allowing something to happen."},{"word":"timesheet","definition":"A record of the hours someone says they worked."},{"word":"discrepancy","definition":"A difference between two accounts that should agree."},{"word":"clause","definition":"A particular term in a contract."},{"word":"amended","definition":"Changed a document so that it shows a new agreement."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How much did the invoice ask the studio to pay?","options":["The original flat fee and nothing more","A doubled rate for the whole poster series","Three hundred pounds for a single illustration","Six hundred and eighty pounds"],"correctAnswer":"Six hundred and eighty pounds","explanation":"The opening of the story gives the total on the invoice as six hundred and eighty pounds.","supportingSentence":"It asked the design studio for six hundred and eighty pounds, most of it listed as rush hours on a poster series the client had already approved.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What is Petra's view of the extra hours on the timesheet?","options":["The illustrator invented every hour, so none of the work was real.","He may have worked them, but the studio never had a chance to refuse them.","The client should pay the doubled rate straight to him.","The original flat fee should be cancelled as well."],"correctAnswer":"He may have worked them, but the studio never had a chance to refuse them.","explanation":"Petra allows that the hours might have been worked, and she objects because the studio could not refuse them first.","supportingSentence":"She said the studio could not pay, in full, for work it had not been given the chance to refuse.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Where did the charge the studio would not meet in full come from?","options":["Overtime that Petra had confirmed in writing the day before","Extra work after a direct client call that had not been approved in writing","A second poster series the client had never seen","A mistake in adding up the original flat fee"],"correctAnswer":"Extra work after a direct client call that had not been approved in writing","explanation":"The illustrator acted on a private call from the client and only assumed the studio would pay. The contract required written approval first.","supportingSentence":"He said the client had telephoned him directly, anxious about a board meeting, and that he had assumed the studio would stand behind the extra work because the relationship mattered.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what is a discrepancy?","options":["A gap between two records of the same job","A late payment of a flat fee","A type of envelope used for bills","A discount given to a loyal client"],"correctAnswer":"A gap between two records of the same job","explanation":"Petra uses the word for the clash between the timesheet and the messages, and she says it is not just a sums problem.","supportingSentence":"The discrepancy was not a matter of arithmetic.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What point is the writer making by ending with the new habit?","options":["The illustrator should stop working with studios","The client ought to design the posters alone","A clear habit of written approval mattered more than the exact sum paid","Handwritten changes on a bill are never valid"],"correctAnswer":"A clear habit of written approval mattered more than the exact sum paid","explanation":"The story calls the shared habit more useful than the smaller amount that left the account.","supportingSentence":"The more useful result was the habit they had both agreed to keep.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq80780$::jsonb,
  'ec1dcece10129aa55c913c3ae8838e39dbbc9f3d51d6feea605b61550a009ae8',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'd577295d-d983-423e-a93d-c0fcb2dd2864'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5312${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":440,"questionCount":5}$rbq5312$::jsonb,
  $rbq2427${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"A Disputed Offside","story":"The goal that would have tied the semi-final arrived in the eighty-seventh minute, and it was disallowed before the scorer had finished celebrating. From the far touchline the assistant referee had raised her flag, judging that the winger, Tomas Adler, had been beyond the last defender when the pass left his teammate's foot. Half the ground saw a clear offence. The other half saw a shoulder that had never quite crossed the line. Captain Nia Rahman, who had played the ball, stood with her hands on her hips and did not chase the official, which surprised the substitutes more than the decision itself.\n\nIn the tunnel afterwards the argument changed shape. The club secretary wanted a formal complaint, complete with a still from a supporter's phone, cropped and brightened until the line looked friendly to their case. Nia asked to see the image before anyone sent it. The winger's trailing arm was level with the defender, and the boot that mattered was ambiguous in the blur. She handed the phone back and said a protest built on an unclear picture would sound like a refusal to lose. The secretary replied that silence would be read as consent, and that consent would travel into the next committee meeting, where disciplinary habits were formed.\n\nThe referee's written report, delivered the next morning, was drier than the night had been. She stated that the flag had gone up late, that she had considered overruling it, and that she had kept the decision because the assistant's angle was better than her own. She also noted that both captains had kept their players at a distance. That last line pleased the league officer who called the club, though he was careful not to call the decision correct. He called it finished. Under the competition rules a factual error could be reviewed only if the club produced an angle the officials had not had. A supporter's enlarged still, he said, was not such an angle.\n\nNia told the squad before Thursday's training. She described the picture, the rule, and the choice not to appeal. Two younger players wanted the grievance kept alive, because a shared grievance can feel like loyalty. She answered that loyalty also included protecting the next match from a story that would make every tackle personal. They could be angry on the walk home, she said, and precise on the pitch. The secretary filed no complaint. In the match report the club recorded the score, the disallowed goal, and a single sentence of thanks to the players for accepting a decision they still disliked. The sentence was political, and it was also accurate.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"disallowed","definition":"Ruled not to count."},{"word":"cropped","definition":"Cut so that part of a picture is removed."},{"word":"ambiguous","definition":"Unclear, and able to be read in more than one way."},{"word":"overruling","definition":"Using a higher authority to cancel a decision."},{"word":"disciplinary","definition":"Concerned with how rules are enforced."},{"word":"grievance","definition":"A complaint about unfair treatment."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What kind of evidence would have allowed the club to seek a review?","options":["A new angle the match officials had not seen","A written protest from the team captain","Any photograph taken by a supporter","A second look at the assistant's flag"],"correctAnswer":"A new angle the match officials had not seen","explanation":"The rules allowed a review only if the club produced an angle the officials had not had.","supportingSentence":"Under the competition rules a factual error could be reviewed only if the club produced an angle the officials had not had.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Nia's stillness after the flag surprise the substitutes?","options":["They thought the goal had been scored by the other team","They wanted Nia to leave the pitch at once","They had expected the captain to argue with the official","They believed the match had already been won"],"correctAnswer":"They had expected the captain to argue with the official","explanation":"She did not chase the official, and that restraint surprised the substitutes more than the decision itself.","supportingSentence":"Captain Nia Rahman, who had played the ball, stood with her hands on her hips and did not chase the official, which surprised the substitutes more than the decision itself.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did the league officer's choice of words show about the offside decision?","options":["He was ending the case without saying the flag was certainly right","He blamed both captains for standing too far away","He accepted the supporter's picture as a new official angle","He promised to restore the goal before Thursday"],"correctAnswer":"He was ending the case without saying the flag was certainly right","explanation":"He avoided calling the decision correct, and the word he used for it was finished.","supportingSentence":"That last line pleased the league officer who called the club, though he was careful not to call the decision correct.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does ambiguous say about the boot in the phone picture?","options":["Its position could not be settled from that blur","It had been removed from the image","It belonged to the assistant referee","It was clearly behind the last defender"],"correctAnswer":"Its position could not be settled from that blur","explanation":"The boot that mattered could not be read clearly, so the picture did not prove where the player had been.","supportingSentence":"The winger's trailing arm was level with the defender, and the boot that mattered was ambiguous in the blur.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include the club's single sentence of thanks in the match report?","options":["To announce that the next match would be cancelled","To blame the players for the secretary's complaint","To show that the score had been changed overnight","To show a public acceptance that still admitted disagreement"],"correctAnswer":"To show a public acceptance that still admitted disagreement","explanation":"The report thanks the players for accepting a decision they still disliked, which is both a public stance and a true description.","supportingSentence":"In the match report the club recorded the score, the disallowed goal, and a single sentence of thanks to the players for accepting a decision they still disliked.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":440,"questionCount":5}$rbq2427$::jsonb,
  'd577295dd983823e493dc0fcb2dd286496093dad8018e3573123890a846d2bd0',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'b16e3687-90d4-41a0-a679-1b478c3a1168'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4994${"topic":"technology","wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq4994$::jsonb,
  $rbq98441${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"A Failure in the Backup","story":"The night log had been green for forty-one days. Each morning the archive desk received the same line: the copy job had finished, the file count matched the source, and the tape set had been marked complete. Nobody had restored a sample in that time. The check compared names and sizes, then stopped. When a visiting fellow asked for a set of field notes from March, the restored folder opened, and the last third of every long file was simply absent. The job had ended. The bytes had not all arrived.\n\nLina Voss, who kept the archive, pulled the quota notice that the storage group had sent in late February and then forgotten. A shared volume had been capped so that one project could not fill the disk. The copy program, meeting that cap, wrote what would fit, closed the file, and reported success because the close itself had not returned an error. Truncation left a shorter file with a normal ending, which is why a count of files could stay perfect while the text inside them was cut. The failure was quiet. The dashboard had no column for the missing pages, only a lamp that meant the process had stopped running.\n\nVoss refused to treat the green weeks as safe. She froze deletions on the source shelves and chose a restore test that did not trust the log. For a sample of thirty files she compared a checksum made on the source with a checksum made after the copy, and she did the same for the week before the quota notice, when the lengths still agreed. The earlier week matched. Every week after it showed the same pattern: small files intact, long files shortened near the cap. That boundary, not the calendar date alone, told her where the damage began. The green log stayed useful for one limited purpose. It showed which nights a job had started, so the search had edges.\n\nThe repair was dull on purpose. Staff would recopy the damaged weeks from the source, which was still complete, onto new media, and they would not reuse the tapes that had passed only a count. A backup would now count as finished only when a sample of checksums matched, including at least one file large enough to have hit the cap. Voss wrote the rule on the job sheet in plain sentences. A completed run is a fact about the program. It is not a fact about the documents. Until those two facts are the same, the lamp on the desk is only a lamp.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"truncation","definition":"The cutting short of a file so that part of its contents is lost."},{"word":"quota","definition":"A limit on how much storage a project is allowed to use."},{"word":"checksum","definition":"A calculated value used to test whether a file’s contents still match."},{"word":"restore","definition":"To bring a stored copy back so that someone can open it."},{"word":"recopy","definition":"To copy the material again from the complete source."},{"word":"boundary","definition":"The point that marks where one condition ends and another begins."}],"quiz":[{"question":"For how long had the night log stayed green before the missing text was found?","options":["Forty-one days","Since late February","One earlier week","Thirty files"],"correctAnswer":"Forty-one days","explanation":"The night log had been green for forty-one days.","supportingSentence":"The night log had been green for forty-one days.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Voss stop deletions on the source shelves while she tested the copies?","options":["The source files were already shorter than the copies on tape.","The visiting fellow had asked her to delete the March notes.","The green log could be kept only by emptying those shelves.","The source was the complete set she might still need to copy from."],"correctAnswer":"The source was the complete set she might still need to copy from.","explanation":"The damaged copies had to be made again from the source, which was still complete.","supportingSentence":"She froze deletions on the source shelves and chose a restore test that did not trust the log.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the new rule refuse to call a backup finished unless the sample includes a large file?","options":["Smaller files alone could match even while longer files were still being cut.","The tape can store a checksum for one file and then stops.","The visiting fellow is allowed to read large files and no others.","The size limit had applied only to the notes written in March."],"correctAnswer":"Smaller files alone could match even while longer files were still being cut.","explanation":"Small files had stayed intact while long files were shortened near the cap, so a small sample could hide the fault.","supportingSentence":"A backup would now count as finished only when a sample of checksums matched, including at least one file large enough to have hit the cap.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what does “truncation” describe?","options":["A checksum that matched the source exactly.","A file cut short that still looked as if it had closed normally.","A file count that the night log had already rejected.","A tape the archive refused to mark as complete."],"correctAnswer":"A file cut short that still looked as if it had closed normally.","explanation":"Truncation left a shorter file with a normal ending, so the text was cut even though the file looked closed.","supportingSentence":"Truncation left a shorter file with a normal ending, which is why a count of files could stay perfect while the text inside them was cut.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage separate a fact about the program from a fact about the documents?","options":["To show that a finished job status can hide an incomplete copy.","To explain why visitors should stop asking for old notes.","To praise the dashboard lamp as a full check of every page.","To argue that field notes should be kept on paper only."],"correctAnswer":"To show that a finished job status can hide an incomplete copy.","explanation":"The writer wants the reader to see that a completed run says the program finished, not that the documents are whole.","supportingSentence":"A completed run is a fact about the program.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq98441$::jsonb,
  'b16e368790d411a016791b478c3a116874c47393c7579eff727bbe1fb4d59166',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '39a14e53-9e7d-43e3-a50f-bae2d1d2a581'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4886${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":439,"questionCount":5}$rbq4886$::jsonb,
  $rbq42032${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"A Hut above the Tree Line","story":"The last trees had given up an hour below them, and the slope above was only grass, stone, and a wind that had somewhere else to be. Iria saw the hut before Ansel did, a dark square tucked under a rib of rock, its roof held down by wires and by stones that looked as if they had been chosen in a hurry. The door faced away from the prevailing weather, which was the first sensible thing she had seen all afternoon. They had left the valley at dawn with light packs and a forecast that spoke of high cloud and little else.\n\nInside, the hut smelled of damp wool and cold ash. A ledger lay open on the table, its pages swollen by old moisture, and the last entry was three days old. Someone named Peder had written that the ridge was in poor condition and that he was going down by the long valley rather than over the top. He had left a half packet of tea and a note asking the next party to tell the warden if the north shutter was still loose. Iria read the lines twice. A warning from a stranger was not a command, but it was not nothing, and the light outside was already turning the colour of tin.\n\nAnsel wanted to push on. The next marked shelter was four hours away if the path stayed where the map said it stayed, and he disliked sleeping in a place that rattled. Iria opened the shutter and put her hand into the wind. It had backed since noon, and it carried the dry smell that often came before snow on this side of the range. She thought of the exposed shoulder above the hut, of how quickly a simple walk became a navigation problem when the ground and the sky turned the same colour. Ambition could be packed and carried. A mistake at this height had to be worn.\n\nThey stayed. Iria boiled the stranger's tea and wrote their names under his, adding the time and the fact of the wind. Ansel wedged the loose shutter with a spoon and a folded map he no longer trusted, then laughed once at the smallness of the repair. Night came without ceremony. Snow began as single hard ticks against the metal roof, then thickened until the sound was continuous. In the morning the ridge had vanished into cloud, and their decision had become obvious. They ate slowly, left the rest of the tea, and started down the way they had come, keeping the hut in sight until the first twisted trees took it away.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"prevailing","definition":"Most usual in that place, especially of wind or weather."},{"word":"ledger","definition":"A book in which records or notes are written."},{"word":"backed","definition":"Changed direction, said here of the wind."},{"word":"navigation","definition":"The work of finding and holding a safe route."},{"word":"ambition","definition":"A strong wish to push on and achieve a plan."},{"word":"continuous","definition":"Going on without a break."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What makes the stranger's note hard to dismiss?","options":["The failing light makes his caution feel serious","The ledger says the valley path has collapsed","The warden is already climbing up to fetch them","Peder has ordered them to guard the hut all night"],"correctAnswer":"The failing light makes his caution feel serious","explanation":"Iria reads the warning twice as the light turns the colour of tin, so it is more than a stranger's fuss.","supportingSentence":"A warning from a stranger was not a command, but it was not nothing, and the light outside was already turning the colour of tin.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Which way did Peder decide to leave?","options":["Over the top before the wind rose","Along the roof wires toward the nearest trees","Down the long valley instead of over the ridge","Back to the valley to wait for three days"],"correctAnswer":"Down the long valley instead of over the ridge","explanation":"His entry says the ridge is in poor condition and that he is going down by the long valley.","supportingSentence":"Someone named Peder had written that the ridge was in poor condition and that he was going down by the long valley rather than over the top.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention the direction of the door?","options":["To show that the hut was built against the usual wind","To explain why the ledger was too wet to read","To prove that Ansel preferred to sleep outside","To suggest that the morning forecast had been exact"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the hut was built against the usual wind","explanation":"A door turned away from the usual weather is the first sign that the hut was placed with care.","supportingSentence":"The door faced away from the prevailing weather, which was the first sensible thing she had seen all afternoon.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What does \"ledger\" mean in this story?","options":["A packet of tea left for the next party","A book in which travellers write their notes","A shutter that keeps the snow outside","A map folded up to wedge a window"],"correctAnswer":"A book in which travellers write their notes","explanation":"The ledger is the open book that holds written entries, including Peder's warning.","supportingSentence":"A ledger lay open on the table, its pages swollen by old moisture, and the last entry was three days old.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What does the clouded morning show about their choice?","options":["The warden has marked a new path over the top","The ridge they might have crossed can no longer be seen","The loose shutter has blown open and ended the debate","The stranger's tea has left them too tired to walk"],"correctAnswer":"The ridge they might have crossed can no longer be seen","explanation":"Once the ridge has vanished into cloud, going back down no longer needs an argument.","supportingSentence":"In the morning the ridge had vanished into cloud, and their decision had become obvious.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":439,"questionCount":5}$rbq42032$::jsonb,
  '39a14e539e7d53e3f50fbae2d1d2a58175bace4e3fe442bd382c4fd3f68b9aee',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'a61c0c98-2b95-49a0-ab9a-6bee75877983'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5199${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq5199$::jsonb,
  $rbq93963${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"A Kitchen during Service","story":"By half past seven the pass was a narrow country with its own weather. Chef Anouk Pelletier called tickets in a voice that carried, and the cooks answered with times rather than opinions. A sauce could be thirty seconds away. A table could not. On the board, the first seating had ordered more baked hake than the morning delivery could support, because a crate of fish had been left on the wrong quay and was warming in a van. Anouk heard the news from the porter and did not spend a sentence on blame.\n\nShe changed the menu with small corrections announced before anyone could invent a larger one. Two portions of hake would be finished for guests already promised the dish. The remaining tickets would be offered a mussel stew that the kitchen could make well, or a chicken braise intended for the next day and already ready. Server Elias Kwon was to say that the fish had not arrived, not that the kitchen had run out, because the second phrase sounded like carelessness and the first sounded like weather. He repeated the line once, then took it into the dining room.\n\nWhat tested the plan was not the stew. It was a birthday table, whose host had announced the hake to his guests as if he had caught it. Elias came back with the refusal and with the temperature of the man's pride. Anouk stepped to the pass, wiped her hands, and gave him a better offer: the two finished portions for the host and the guest of honour, and the stew presented as a kitchen choice rather than as a shortage. She plated them herself, checked the salt, and sent the stew in wide bowls that did not look like apologies. The host accepted, partly because the bowls arrived quickly and partly because no one was handed a speech.\n\nHalfway through service the missing crate appeared, still cold enough to use, which was its own kind of insult. Anouk refused to throw the new plan away for the sake of being right. The hake would return on the late tickets, she said, and the stew would stay for anyone who had already agreed to it. A junior cook called the decision stubborn. The sous-chef, Marta Beltran, called it coherent. Coherent meant the room would not watch one table eat a public correction of another table's meal. When the board finally emptied, Anouk ate a spoon of the stew over the pass and salted the next batch. The service had been held together by a sentence the diners were never asked to admire.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"porter","definition":"The worker who brought news of the missing crate."},{"word":"braise","definition":"A dish of meat cooked slowly in liquid."},{"word":"carelessness","definition":"A failure that looks like the kitchen's own fault."},{"word":"shortage","definition":"Not having enough of something that was expected."},{"word":"coherent","definition":"Fitting together so that the room tells one story."},{"word":"insult","definition":"Here, a late arrival that mocked the trouble already taken."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why was there not enough baked hake at the start of service?","options":["The porter had cooked it for the staff breakfast","A crate of fish had been left on the wrong quay","The guests had all ordered mussel stew","Anouk had sold the morning fish to another kitchen"],"correctAnswer":"A crate of fish had been left on the wrong quay","explanation":"The first seating ordered more hake than the delivery could support because the crate was left on the wrong quay and was warming in a van.","supportingSentence":"On the board, the first seating had ordered more baked hake than the morning delivery could support, because a crate of fish had been left on the wrong quay and was warming in a van.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was the birthday table harder to move off the hake than the other tickets?","options":["Elias refused to speak to anyone in the room","The chicken braise was reserved for the staff","The stew had burned before it left the kitchen","The host had already presented the missing fish as his own promise"],"correctAnswer":"The host had already presented the missing fish as his own promise","explanation":"He had announced the hake to his guests as if he had caught it, so a plain substitution would expose that promise.","supportingSentence":"It was a birthday table, whose host had announced the hake to his guests as if he had caught it.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Once the fish was cold enough to use, what did Anouk refuse to give up?","options":["The chance to blame the porter in front of the cooks","The birthday host's request for a speech","A steady plan for guests who had already accepted the stew","Marta's wish to close the kitchen early"],"correctAnswer":"A steady plan for guests who had already accepted the stew","explanation":"She would not throw away the new plan merely to be right, and the stew stayed for anyone who had already agreed to it.","supportingSentence":"Anouk refused to throw the new plan away for the sake of being right.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does carelessness describe in the line Anouk gives Elias?","options":["A sauce that needed thirty more seconds","The weather that delayed the van","The wide bowls used for the stew","Wording that would make the kitchen look at fault"],"correctAnswer":"Wording that would make the kitchen look at fault","explanation":"Saying the kitchen had run out would sound like carelessness, while saying the fish had not arrived would sound like weather.","supportingSentence":"Server Elias Kwon was to say that the fish had not arrived, not that the kitchen had run out, because the second phrase sounded like carelessness and the first sounded like weather.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer end on a sentence the diners were never asked to admire?","options":["To argue that servers should never speak to tables","To show that the rescue depended on wording the guests did not need to notice","To admit the stew was too poor to praise","To suggest the birthday host had written the menu"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the rescue depended on wording the guests did not need to notice","explanation":"The service holds together because of a sentence spoken in the room, and the diners are not asked to admire the trick of it.","supportingSentence":"The service had been held together by a sentence the diners were never asked to admire.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq93963$::jsonb,
  'a61c0c982b9519a02b9a6bee758779837d77bcc1506a891e69db1521ea90275c',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

