  'text',
  $rbq4668${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq4668$::jsonb,
  $rbq63829${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"A Market That Opened at Dusk","story":"The market arrived with the first honest shadow. Stalls unfolded out of what had been, a moment earlier, an ordinary square used for nothing more romantic than parking carts. Canvas took the colour of the sky it had stolen from, and lamps were lit only halfway, because a full flame made the goods shy. People who knew the custom came without baskets. What was sold there could not be carried in the usual sense, and what was bought had to be paid for with something the buyer would not entirely miss.\n\nIren walked the rows with her hands in her coat. At one stall a woman sold the last warm afternoon of a childhood street, corked in a jar that fogged when it was shaken. At another, a man with careful fingers offered the name of a song without the song itself, useful to anyone who had been humming the shape of a tune for years and waking annoyed. Prices were written on slate and then wiped, not to cheat, but because a price that stayed still became a different kind of object and could no longer be traded. Iren had come for none of these. She had come because her brother had forgotten the way her voice sounded when she was not being brave.\n\nShe found the stall at the end of the second row, where a clerk kept small tins labelled in pencil. He did not ask what she wanted. He asked what she could spare. Iren considered the afternoon she had wasted being angry at a neighbour, and the clerk accepted it with a nod that was almost kind. In return he gave her a tin no larger than a coin. When she opened it, later, in her own kitchen, the room held for a few breaths the exact pitch of her laugh at seventeen, aimed at her brother across a table that no longer existed. She did not try to keep the sound. Kept sounds went thin. She let it cross the room, and she watched her brother's face change as he recognised a person he had not known he was missing.\n\nThe market closed when the first lamp in the square was turned up too high. Canvas slackened. Jars became jars. The clerk was suddenly a man packing pencils into an ordinary case, and he would not meet anyone's eye, because eye contact after closing was a kind of debt. Iren bought nothing else. She walked her brother home by the long way, and neither of them hurried, in case the evening still had one more thing it was willing to return.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"romantic","definition":"Full of imagined charm, beyond plain everyday use."},{"word":"shy","definition":"Quick to draw back when conditions are too strong."},{"word":"spare","definition":"To give something up without a serious loss."},{"word":"recognised","definition":"Knew someone or something again after a gap."},{"word":"slackened","definition":"Became loose after having been stretched tight."},{"word":"debt","definition":"Something still owed after a deal should have ended."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What had Iren's brother forgotten?","options":["How her voice sounded when she was not acting brave","The price last written on the clerk's slate","The name of a tune he had hummed for years","The road into the square where the carts waited"],"correctAnswer":"How her voice sounded when she was not acting brave","explanation":"She came because he had forgotten the sound of her voice when she was not being brave.","supportingSentence":"She had come because her brother had forgotten the way her voice sounded when she was not being brave.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why is the wasted angry afternoon a suitable payment?","options":["The neighbour has asked her to sell the quarrel","The little tin is worth only one kitchen coin","The clerk collects anger in order to light the lamps","It is a piece of the day she can lose without real harm"],"correctAnswer":"It is a piece of the day she can lose without real harm","explanation":"The market takes what a buyer will not entirely miss, and a wasted quarrel is exactly that kind of offering.","supportingSentence":"Iren considered the afternoon she had wasted being angry at a neighbour, and the clerk accepted it with a nod that was almost kind.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What happens to the clerk when the market ends?","options":["He packs the unsold jars so he can open again at dawn","He becomes an ordinary man and will not continue the trade","He follows Iren in order to ask for a second payment","He turns the square lamp down until the goods return"],"correctAnswer":"He becomes an ordinary man and will not continue the trade","explanation":"The ordinary pencil case and the avoided look show that he has stepped out of the market's bargain.","supportingSentence":"The clerk was suddenly a man packing pencils into an ordinary case, and he would not meet anyone's eye, because eye contact after closing was a kind of debt.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer send the pair home slowly?","options":["To keep open the hope that the evening might return one more thing","To show that they can no longer find the way out","To prove that the stalls have followed them into the street","To explain that the brother refused to hear the laugh"],"correctAnswer":"To keep open the hope that the evening might return one more thing","explanation":"They do not hurry, in case the evening still has something left that it is willing to give back.","supportingSentence":"She walked her brother home by the long way, and neither of them hurried, in case the evening still had one more thing it was willing to return.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What does \"shy\" mean about the goods?","options":["They cost more than a buyer in a plain coat can pay","They draw back when the flame is too bright","They are offered only to children at dusk","They can be carried home in an ordinary basket"],"correctAnswer":"They draw back when the flame is too bright","explanation":"A full flame makes the goods shy, which is why the lamps are kept only halfway lit.","supportingSentence":"Canvas took the colour of the sky it had stolen from, and lamps were lit only halfway, because a full flame made the goods shy.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq63829$::jsonb,
  'e81e9669084f04d220ee6cdec82c26b7c57c56baff23801c9d147d4946531c9a',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '1443e713-3476-4a48-a394-ca34ea518377'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5200${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq5200$::jsonb,
  $rbq92395${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"A Menu for the Delegation","story":"The delegation would arrive on Thursday, twelve people from a partner town, and the town hall had given Chef Carlo Benedetti a brief that said local, generous, and mindful. Mindful was the word that did the most work. One guest did not eat meat. Two did not eat pork. One carried a serious allergy to nuts, and another would not drink alcohol. Carlo could have treated the list as a set of obstacles. He pinned it above the bench and treated it as the first true description of his guests.\n\nThe protocol officer, Sandra Vogel, wanted a single menu, served to everyone, so that no plate would announce a category. Carlo agreed, then protected the agreement from becoming bland. A beetroot tart could be finished without goat cheese at the last moment, so the table would still look shared. The main course would be a barley pilaf with roasted vegetables and a herb sauce, with slow-cooked lamb offered from a separate dish for those who wanted it, not imposed on those who did not. Nuts were removed from the whole kitchen for the day, including the pastry corner, because a serious allergy is not a preference and a stray crumb is not a small thing. Dessert would be poached pears and a citrus cake whose recipe had never relied on almond flour.\n\nSandra worried that the visitors would think the town had hidden its traditions. Carlo took her to the storeroom and showed her the tradition he thought worth serving: late pears, barley from the cooperative upriver, herbs cut that morning, and cheese from a nearby dairy. A dish did not become less local because the whole table could eat it. It became more hospitable. He would print a short menu naming the producers and the main ingredients, and he would not print the medical reasons, which belonged to the guests.\n\nOn Wednesday they cooked a trial for the staff who would serve. The lamb sat in its own dish, and nobody was asked to explain a choice. The pear dessert tasted like the week it was, not like a bargain negotiated by email. Sandra, who had expected to apologise for something, found she had nothing to apologise for. Carlo adjusted the salt and moved the tarts apart so the version without cheese was not exiled to the edge. He told the servers that care would show in the pacing and the plates, not in a speech about the brief. If the dinner succeeded, the guests would remember that they had been expected, exactly as they were.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"delegation","definition":"A group visiting on behalf of another town."},{"word":"mindful","definition":"Careful about the different needs of the guests."},{"word":"protocol","definition":"The official arrangements for a formal visit."},{"word":"imposed","definition":"Forced on someone who has not chosen it."},{"word":"hospitable","definition":"Welcoming to the people who are actually present."},{"word":"exiled","definition":"Pushed aside, as if it did not belong with the others."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did Carlo do in the kitchen because a guest had a serious nut allergy?","options":["He asked that guest to bring a separate dessert","He served nuts only with the citrus cake","He removed nuts from the whole kitchen, including the pastry corner","He left nuts out of the lamb and nowhere else"],"correctAnswer":"He removed nuts from the whole kitchen, including the pastry corner","explanation":"Nuts were taken out of the whole kitchen for the day, because a serious allergy is not a preference and a stray crumb is not small.","supportingSentence":"Nuts were removed from the whole kitchen for the day, including the pastry corner, because a serious allergy is not a preference and a stray crumb is not a small thing.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How is Sandra's worry about hidden traditions answered by the trial?","options":["Carlo agreed to print every allergy on the bench","The guests cancelled, so no menu was needed","The meal tasted local and complete, so she had nothing to apologise for","The lamb was removed and only pears remained"],"correctAnswer":"The meal tasted local and complete, so she had nothing to apologise for","explanation":"She had feared the town would seem to be hiding its food, but the pear dessert tasted of the week itself and she found nothing to apologise for.","supportingSentence":"Sandra, who had expected to apologise for something, found she had nothing to apologise for.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What kind of welcome do Carlo's serving choices aim to give?","options":["Each person will order a separate menu at the door","The servers will describe the brief in a speech before the pears","Guests can follow their needs without having to explain themselves in public","Guests must all eat lamb in order to honour the town"],"correctAnswer":"Guests can follow their needs without having to explain themselves in public","explanation":"The lamb sits apart, nobody is asked to explain a choice, and medical reasons are kept off the printed menu.","supportingSentence":"The lamb sat in its own dish, and nobody was asked to explain a choice.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does exiled describe in the way the tarts are arranged?","options":["Pushed to the edge so that it looks unwelcome","Stored for the staff meal the next day","Named on the menu as a medical warning","Salted more heavily than the other tarts"],"correctAnswer":"Pushed to the edge so that it looks unwelcome","explanation":"Carlo moves the tarts apart so the version without cheese is not exiled to the edge.","supportingSentence":"Carlo adjusted the salt and moved the tarts apart so the version without cheese was not exiled to the edge.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer have Carlo pin the dietary list above the bench?","options":["To show that the guests' needs were the start of the meal, not a late obstacle","To argue that one shared menu must be bland","To suggest Sandra had hidden the list from the cooks","To prove the visitors would refuse pears"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the guests' needs were the start of the meal, not a late obstacle","explanation":"He could have treated the list as obstacles, but he pins it up as the first true description of the people he is cooking for.","supportingSentence":"He pinned it above the bench and treated it as the first true description of his guests.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq92395$::jsonb,
  '1443e7133476da488394ca34ea518377148c6f710b5ae652f53e4f64a58f2680',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '76707b35-122b-41f8-a81f-3e9a1b7bead3'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4890${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":429,"questionCount":5}$rbq4890$::jsonb,
  $rbq91784${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"A Neighbourhood Petition","story":"The petition appeared on a Tuesday, pinned outside the corner shop and again on the community-room door. It asked the council to refuse a late-night takeaway on the parade, on the grounds that the street already had litter, idling cars, and noise after the buses thinned. Ruth read it twice before she signed. She lived above the empty unit, and she had spent the previous summer shutting her windows against a different shop's extractor. The phrasing, which described the whole neighbourhood as united, still troubled her. She knew at least three households who wanted any legal business rather than another boarded front.\n\nPatrick, who had drafted the text, came round that evening to collect paper copies from people who avoided the online form. He was proud of the speed. Forty names had appeared by lunch. Ruth told him she had signed, then asked whether the wording overreached. If the council tested the claim that residents spoke with one voice, a handful of dissenting letters could make the petition look careless. Patrick replied that campaigns needed warmth, and that a document full of caveats would persuade nobody. He offered to add her name to the covering note as a resident who supported refusal but wanted the evidence kept separate from the adjectives.\n\nBy Thursday the dispute had left the doorstep. Someone had photographed the sheet and posted it with a comment that the street was being spoken for by its most anxious minority. Two people asked for their names to be removed. Ruth did not. She wrote a short addition, which Patrick agreed to staple behind the petition, stating that she objected to a late licence and overnight deliveries, not to a daytime counter. The distinction mattered to her because a blanket refusal might be harder to defend if the applicant accepted earlier hours. Patrick thought the extra page weakened the impact. He attached it anyway, partly because Ruth's flat faced the unit and her account of the previous extractor was the most concrete evidence they had.\n\nThey handed the papers in on Friday morning, together with a log of evening noise and a map of the flats whose windows opened onto the parade. Walking home, Patrick admitted that the softer page might survive a challenge that the original sentence would have invited. Ruth was less interested in the tactics than in the record. If a licence were granted with conditions, she wanted those conditions to reflect what she had actually requested. A neighbourhood could ask for limits without pretending that every household on the street wanted the same outcome.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"phrasing","definition":"The particular way something is worded"},{"word":"dissenting","definition":"Saying that you disagree"},{"word":"caveats","definition":"Warnings or limits added to a statement"},{"word":"concrete","definition":"Based on specific facts, not general claims"},{"word":"blanket","definition":"Covering every case, with no exceptions"},{"word":"conditions","definition":"Limits that control how something is allowed"}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why did the wording trouble Ruth even after she signed?","options":["It asked the council to approve a late licence","It claimed a unity the street did not really have","It described her flat as the source of the litter","It left her name off the list on purpose"],"correctAnswer":"It claimed a unity the street did not really have","explanation":"The petition described the whole neighbourhood as united, though she knew some households disagreed.","supportingSentence":"The phrasing, which described the whole neighbourhood as united, still troubled her.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did Ruth say she was objecting to?","options":["A late licence and deliveries in the night","Any business of any kind on the parade","The corner shop that displayed the sheet","Patrick collecting copies from neighbours"],"correctAnswer":"A late licence and deliveries in the night","explanation":"Her added page objected to a late licence and overnight deliveries, not to a daytime counter.","supportingSentence":"She wrote a short addition, which Patrick agreed to staple behind the petition, stating that she objected to a late licence and overnight deliveries, not to a daytime counter.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Patrick keep a page he thought weakened the petition?","options":["He wanted the online comments to remove more names","He had stopped wanting the late opening to be refused","The council had ordered every signer to add a second page","Her account of the previous extractor was their firmest evidence"],"correctAnswer":"Her account of the previous extractor was their firmest evidence","explanation":"He attached it because her flat faced the unit and her account was the most concrete evidence.","supportingSentence":"He attached it anyway, partly because Ruth's flat faced the unit and her account of the previous extractor was the most concrete evidence they had.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer include her hope about a licence that comes with limits?","options":["To prove that Patrick now welcomes a late takeaway","To argue that daytime trade is noisier than the night","To show she wants the official record to match her real request","To suggest the council should ignore this street"],"correctAnswer":"To show she wants the official record to match her real request","explanation":"If a licence were granted with conditions, she wanted those conditions to reflect what she had actually asked for.","supportingSentence":"If a licence were granted with conditions, she wanted those conditions to reflect what she had actually requested.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What are caveats in the reply about campaigns?","options":["Names gathered on paper before lunch","Drawings of the flats along the parade","Careful limits that would make the text less forceful","Photographs posted with critical comments"],"correctAnswer":"Careful limits that would make the text less forceful","explanation":"He believes a document full of limits would persuade nobody, unlike a warmer campaign.","supportingSentence":"Patrick replied that campaigns needed warmth, and that a document full of caveats would persuade nobody.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":429,"questionCount":5}$rbq91784$::jsonb,
  '76707b35122bf1f8481f3e9a1b7bead31a0ee645194920214b6d4af89fbc285b',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '0c193a78-7b18-4220-a563-cdf601e0184b'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4633${"topic":"science","wordCount":430,"questionCount":5}$rbq4633$::jsonb,
  $rbq71105${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"A Trial in the Glasshouse","story":"The glasshouse trial compared three irrigation regimes on a single leafy crop. Every bench received the same compost, the same sowing date, and the same nutrient feed. What no bench received was the same heat. The south wall ran warmer on clear afternoons, and plants there dried faster between waterings, whatever the regime. The team had expected that gradient. They did not pretend it was absent. Once a week they rotated the trays, so that each regime spent the same number of days on the hot side, the middle, and the cool north bench. Rotation does not delete a confound. It stops the confound from sitting permanently on one treatment.\n\nAt harvest the mid-frequency regime produced a detectable difference in fresh leaf mass. The difference was real in the narrow sense that the statistical test cleared the threshold the protocol had set. It was also small: a few grams a plant, well inside the range a grower would struggle to notice in a crate. When the analyst removed the weeks a tray had spent on the south bench and ran the test again, the difference shrank below the threshold. Watering still mattered, but part of what the first test had attributed to water had belonged to the glass. Counters at harvest worked without being told which regime a tray had followed. The labels were coded, and the code sheet stayed with Helen Okoye, the lead researcher, until the masses were written down. A detectable gap that survives a blind count is harder to dismiss as enthusiasm for a favored schedule.\n\nThe funder wanted a sentence that growers could take outdoors. Okoye would not write it. The trial had no wind, no soil profile, and no rain on the leaves. A result that appears under glass, and that weakens when the hottest bench is set aside, supports a cautious preference among these regimes in this house. It does not support an instruction for a field, where drying and cooling are arranged by weather the team did not vary on purpose. To extrapolate that far would treat the walls as irrelevant, after a season spent measuring how much the walls mattered.\n\nOkoye published the full table, including the run that excluded the south bench, and she described the rotation in the methods rather than in a footnote. Readers could see both the detectable gap and the condition under which it faded. The trial had done its job when those two facts remained visible together. A recommendation that kept only the first would have been smoother, and it would have been less true.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"regime","definition":"A planned pattern of watering used as one treatment in the trial."},{"word":"gradient","definition":"A gradual difference, here in heat from one side of the house to the other."},{"word":"confound","definition":"A second factor that can be mistaken for the effect being tested."},{"word":"rotation","definition":"Moving the trays so each treatment spends time in each position."},{"word":"detectable","definition":"Large enough for the chosen test to pick it out from chance variation."},{"word":"extrapolate","definition":"To extend a result to a situation the test did not cover."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What happened to the leaf-mass difference when weeks on the hottest bench were left out of the test?","options":["It fell below the threshold the protocol had set.","It showed that the north bench had received no water.","It stayed the same because the weekly moves had removed all heat.","It grew large enough for a grower to notice in a crate."],"correctAnswer":"It fell below the threshold the protocol had set.","explanation":"When the south-bench weeks were removed, the difference shrank below the protocol’s threshold.","supportingSentence":"When the analyst removed the weeks a tray had spent on the south bench and ran the test again, the difference shrank below the threshold.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What doubt is a coded, blind harvest meant to reduce?","options":["That the funder had already printed an outdoor instruction.","That fresh leaves cannot be weighed on a scale at all.","That the south wall was cooler than the north bench on clear days.","That the people weighing the leaves favored a schedule they hoped would win."],"correctAnswer":"That the people weighing the leaves favored a schedule they hoped would win.","explanation":"The counters were not told the regime, so a gap is harder to dismiss as enthusiasm for a favored schedule.","supportingSentence":"A detectable gap that survives a blind count is harder to dismiss as enthusiasm for a favored schedule.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What two limits, taken together, block a simple recommendation for outdoor growers?","options":["The gap shrinks without the hot bench, and the house never included wind, soil, or rain.","The compost differed on every bench, and the sowing dates were chosen at random.","The funder banned tables, and the counters lost the code sheet.","The weekly moves kept every tray on the south wall for the whole season."],"correctAnswer":"The gap shrinks without the hot bench, and the house never included wind, soil, or rain.","explanation":"The result weakens when the hot bench is removed, and the house never tested the weather of a field.","supportingSentence":"It does not support an instruction for a field, where drying and cooling are arranged by weather the team did not vary on purpose.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What would it mean to “extrapolate” in the way the researcher refuses?","options":["To move trays so each pattern of watering spends equal time on each bench.","To weigh fresh leaves without telling the counters which treatment they have.","To treat a glasshouse result as if it were an instruction for a field.","To publish the table that leaves out the south bench."],"correctAnswer":"To treat a glasshouse result as if it were an instruction for a field.","explanation":"Extrapolating that far would ignore the walls and turn a glasshouse result into field advice.","supportingSentence":"To extrapolate that far would treat the walls as irrelevant, after a season spent measuring how much the walls mattered.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage keep both the full result and the result without the hot bench in view?","options":["To let the reader see that the gap depends on a condition, not only that a gap existed.","To show that moving the trays had failed and should be abandoned.","To argue that the cool north bench should be closed.","To hide the fact that the statistical test had cleared its threshold."],"correctAnswer":"To let the reader see that the gap depends on a condition, not only that a gap existed.","explanation":"The writer wants both facts visible so a smoother recommendation cannot hide the condition that makes the gap fade.","supportingSentence":"The trial had done its job when those two facts remained visible together.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":430,"questionCount":5}$rbq71105$::jsonb,
  '0c193a787b185220a563cdf601e0184b24d47e23ba97485acd8f1ff86fbdbfa6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '5fab2a81-641c-47b8-abd0-3d6cbe9e200a'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4559${"topic":"travel","wordCount":423,"questionCount":5}$rbq4559$::jsonb,
  $rbq85367${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"A Visa at the Consulate","story":"Lena Morat reached the consulate of Orsena with a folder she had checked three times on the tram. The appointment slip allowed her twelve minutes with a consular officer, and the list of required papers ran onto a second page. She carried a letter from her employer, a bank statement, a photograph of the stated size, and a short account of the archive she hoped to consult in the city of Pell. What she lacked was a paper the website had added over the weekend, a signed note from her landlord confirming that she intended to return to her flat. She had printed the older list and had trusted it.\n\nThe waiting room was quiet in the particular way of places where people rehearse answers under their breath. A clerk called numbers without looking up from the screen. When Lena's turn came, the officer, a woman with a pencil parked behind one ear, turned each page as if the order of the papers were itself a kind of evidence. She stopped at the end and tapped the blank place where the landlord's note should have sat. The requirement, she said, was not meant to be hostile. It existed because some applicants described a short research visit as complete in itself when they were in fact arranging a longer stay, and the office had been instructed to look for ties that pulled a person home. Lena explained that her landlord was away and could not sign until Thursday. The officer listened without sympathy and without impatience, a neutrality that was its own form of courtesy.\n\nLena could submit the file as it stood and risk a discretionary refusal, or she could return with the missing page and keep her place on the week's list. There was no stamp that meant almost. She chose to return. In the corridor she passed a man arguing, softly, that a wedding invitation ought to count as a tie. On the steps she telephoned the neighbour who held a spare key and asked him to slide a note under the landlord's door, worded plainly and limited to the sentence the form required. Walking back towards the tram, she felt the particular humiliation of having been careful according to the wrong version of the instructions. The consulate had not questioned her purpose. It had questioned whether purpose, however sincere, could stand in for proof. She could supply the page on Thursday. What she could not supply was a version of the truth the form had not thought to ask for.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"consular","definition":"Related to the work of a consul or a consulate."},{"word":"archive","definition":"A collection of historical papers kept for study."},{"word":"rehearse","definition":"To practise what you are going to say."},{"word":"discretionary","definition":"Decided by a person's judgment rather than by a fixed rule."},{"word":"neutrality","definition":"A manner that takes neither side and shows little emotion."},{"word":"humiliation","definition":"A painful feeling of having been made to look foolish."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How long was Lena's meeting with the officer supposed to last?","options":["Twelve minutes","Until Thursday","A full afternoon","Half an hour"],"correctAnswer":"Twelve minutes","explanation":"The appointment slip allowed her twelve minutes with the officer.","supportingSentence":"The appointment slip allowed her twelve minutes with a consular officer, and the list of required papers ran onto a second page.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How could the incomplete file have made Lena look to the officer?","options":["Like a clerk who had called the wrong number","Like a visitor who already held a long-term permit","Like a landlord signing forms for other applicants","Like someone whose return home was not yet on paper"],"correctAnswer":"Like someone whose return home was not yet on paper","explanation":"The missing note was supposed to show a tie that would bring her home, so its absence left that tie unproven.","supportingSentence":"It existed because some applicants described a short research visit as complete in itself when they were in fact arranging a longer stay, and the office had been instructed to look for ties that pulled a person home.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Lena's telephone call show about the choice she has made?","options":["She will ask the officer to accept a wedding invitation","She will submit the file and hope for a soft answer","She will try to meet the rule in the exact form it demands","She will send her employer to sign in the landlord's place"],"correctAnswer":"She will try to meet the rule in the exact form it demands","explanation":"She asks for a plain note limited to the sentence the form requires, which shows she means to comply rather than argue.","supportingSentence":"On the steps she telephoned the neighbour who held a spare key and asked him to slide a note under the landlord's door, worded plainly and limited to the sentence the form required.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does discretionary suggest about the refusal she might receive?","options":["It would refund the cost of her tram","It would depend on the officer's judgment","It had already been stamped before she arrived","It would count as a short approval"],"correctAnswer":"It would depend on the officer's judgment","explanation":"Submitting the incomplete file would risk a refusal the officer could choose to make, not one already fixed in advance.","supportingSentence":"Lena could submit the file as it stood and risk a discretionary refusal, or she could return with the missing page and keep her place on the week's list.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include the man with the wedding invitation?","options":["To show another applicant offering a substitute for the required proof","To explain why the clerk never looked up","To mark Thursday as a public holiday","To reveal that Lena is travelling to a wedding in Pell"],"correctAnswer":"To show another applicant offering a substitute for the required proof","explanation":"He wants a personal paper to count as a tie, which sets a persuasive argument beside the plain document Lena decides to obtain.","supportingSentence":"In the corridor she passed a man arguing, softly, that a wedding invitation ought to count as a tie.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":423,"questionCount":5}$rbq85367$::jsonb,
  '5fab2a81641cc7b8cbd03d6cbe9e200abdb82665e40d528fbe705b7c6d6080bb',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

