  'text',
  $rbq5312${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":440,"questionCount":5}$rbq5312$::jsonb,
  $rbq67375${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"Altitude and the Timetable","story":"The training camp had been planned at the coast, where the mornings were heavy and the roads were familiar, but a late invitation moved the squad to a rented lodge at two thousand metres. The sports director, Helen Varga, liked the promise of thin air and disliked the way the promise had arrived, six days before departure, with the old timetable still printed. Riders who had expected sea-level work were now being asked to sleep, eat, and climb in air that would make an easy effort feel dishonest.\n\nDoctor Paulo Serrato, who travelled with the team, walked the first afternoon with a pulse oximeter and a notebook. Two climbers, Nessa Holde and Chris Amaro, dropped more than the others overnight. They were not ill, he said at breakfast, but they were not adapted, and adaptation is a bodily vote that cannot be hurried by a schedule pinned to a door. He wanted the time trial, set for the third morning, delayed by forty-eight hours. The mechanic objected that the closed-road permit would not move. The permit, Helen said, was a piece of paper, and paper was cheaper than a headache the team had been told to respect.\n\nThey rewrote the week on the long table in the lodge kitchen. Hard efforts slid toward the final days. The early rides became conversational, with heart rate capped and the summit saved for the bus. Nessa hated the cap. She had come to test her climbing, and a limit felt like a rumour she could not check. Paulo answered with the numbers from the night, then with a plainer sentence: if she forced the issue, the test would measure distress, not talent. Chris, who hid his fatigue behind jokes, admitted that the stairs to the bunk room had made his ears ring. That admission did more than the lecture. The room stopped treating the delay as an insult.\n\nHelen still had a sponsor's filming window to protect, a two-hour block on the fourth afternoon that the contract called flexible and that the producer called fixed. She moved the filming to the lodge balcony and to a spin on the indoor trainers, and she told the producer the mountain would be a backdrop rather than a performance. Seeing the riders' faces, he became practical. The time trial eventually ran on the fifth morning, in cold sun, after Helen herself obtained an extension of the permit. Nessa finished strongly and did not pretend she would have finished the same way on the original day. In the report Helen wrote that altitude had been useful only after the timetable admitted what the bodies were already saying.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"oximeter","definition":"A device that measures how much oxygen is in the blood."},{"word":"adapted","definition":"Adjusted to new conditions, here the thin air."},{"word":"permit","definition":"Official permission to use the closed road."},{"word":"capped","definition":"Kept from rising above a set limit."},{"word":"distress","definition":"Serious physical strain, rather than a fair test of skill."},{"word":"backdrop","definition":"A background, not the event the camera is there to judge."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How long did Paulo want the time trial delayed?","options":["By six days before departure","Until the following season","Until the squad returned to the coast","By forty-eight hours"],"correctAnswer":"By forty-eight hours","explanation":"He asked for the trial planned on the third morning to be delayed by forty-eight hours.","supportingSentence":"He wanted the time trial, set for the third morning, delayed by forty-eight hours.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What disagreement sat between Nessa and Paulo about the early rides?","options":["She trusted the coastal timetable, while he cancelled the whole camp","She asked to ride the bus, while he told her to climb alone","She wanted to leave the mountain, while he ordered a night race","She wanted a real test of her climbing, while he thought an early effort would not be one"],"correctAnswer":"She wanted a real test of her climbing, while he thought an early effort would not be one","explanation":"Nessa had come to test her climbing, but Paulo said a forced effort would measure distress rather than talent.","supportingSentence":"She had come to test her climbing, and a limit felt like a rumour she could not check.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did the producer give up once he had seen the riders?","options":["The closed-road permit for the fifth morning","A filmed performance on the climb","Chris's place in the squad","The lodge kitchen as a meeting room"],"correctAnswer":"A filmed performance on the climb","explanation":"He had called the filming window fixed, but after seeing the riders he accepted a shoot in which the mountain was only a backdrop.","supportingSentence":"Seeing the riders' faces, he became practical.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does capped mean for the heart rate on the early rides?","options":["It was ignored so the riders could sprint","It was not allowed to rise above a chosen level","It replaced the need for a road permit","It was measured only during the time trial"],"correctAnswer":"It was not allowed to rise above a chosen level","explanation":"The early rides stayed conversational because heart rate had an upper limit, and the summit was left for the bus.","supportingSentence":"The early rides became conversational, with heart rate capped and the summit saved for the bus.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include Helen's remark that the permit was only paper?","options":["To argue that thin air has no effect on climbers","To blame Paulo for losing the closed road","To suggest the mechanic had forged the document","To show she put the riders' health ahead of the original paperwork"],"correctAnswer":"To show she put the riders' health ahead of the original paperwork","explanation":"She treats the permit as cheaper than ignoring a headache the team had already been told to respect.","supportingSentence":"The permit, Helen said, was a piece of paper, and paper was cheaper than a headache the team had been told to respect.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":440,"questionCount":5}$rbq67375$::jsonb,
  '3156d8bdd624dd31e4257d0fba6f810703d0bf99dfd1a07077932d4a50d13268',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'fef7073d-f3cd-43a4-a1ed-657e592fddd5'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4638${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq4638$::jsonb,
  $rbq77325${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"Binding a Notebook by Hand","story":"Clare Vedel cleared the kitchen table with a care that made the ordinary room look temporarily like a workshop. She bound notebooks by hand, a practice she had learned from a manual with muddy photographs and from a retired printer who corrected the position of her fingers more often than her terms. A signature, in this work, was a nest of folded sheets, and each nest had to be punched with an awl so the holes sat in one straight line a short width from the fold. Haste showed itself later, when the book opened as if it remembered being forced.\n\nShe was making a gift for her brother, who kept notes on the bridges he inspected and complained that shop books came apart in a coat pocket. Clare chose a paper with enough tooth to hold a hard pencil, pale enough not to tint his drawings. The endpapers were a dull green that would frame the pages without competing with them. She waxed a linen thread until it slid, then sewed from the middle of each signature outward, keeping the tension even so that no section puffed above its neighbour. At the head and tail she tied the stitches that lock a binding, small knots that can announce a mistake only after the covers are on. Adhesive came last and sparingly. Too much of it would turn the spine into a plank and betray the flexibility she had spent the evening protecting.\n\nWhen the boards had dried under a stack of dictionaries, she opened the book once. The first page lay flat, with a slight resistance she trusted more than a lazy ease. She wrote nothing on the flyleaf. Her brother disliked dedications, and she had come to think of the blank leaf as part of the gift, a decision not to occupy the very space she had made usable. In the morning she wrapped the notebook in plain paper and tied it with the thread that remained. Seen from the outside, the object was modest to the point of being easy to underestimate. Its argument was structural. A thing consulted every day, often with dirty hands and in a wind off the bridges, deserves a spine that survives the consultation. Clare turned the parcel once, checking that the knot sat flat, and felt the small private pleasure of a standard met rather than a decoration added. The printer would have found something to adjust, because he always did, and she would have agreed with him. That expectation of a further correction was, she thought, a healthy way to love a craft.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"signature","definition":"In bookbinding, a group of folded sheets sewn as one section."},{"word":"awl","definition":"A small pointed tool used to pierce holes."},{"word":"endpapers","definition":"The sheets fixed inside the covers at each end of a book."},{"word":"tension","definition":"How tight something is pulled."},{"word":"adhesive","definition":"A substance such as glue that makes surfaces stick."},{"word":"flyleaf","definition":"A blank page at the beginning or end of a book."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Who was meant to use the notebook Clare was making?","options":["A neighbour who collects dull green paper","Her brother, for notes he takes on bridges","The retired printer, as a sample of her terms","A shop that sells books for coat pockets"],"correctAnswer":"Her brother, for notes he takes on bridges","explanation":"She was making the book as a gift for her brother, who keeps notes on the bridges he inspects.","supportingSentence":"She was making a gift for her brother, who kept notes on the bridges he inspected and complained that shop books came apart in a coat pocket.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the empty flyleaf suggest about Clare's idea of the gift?","options":["She wanted the notebook to be sold without being opened","She forgot the sentence the printer told her to copy","The book should be ready for his work, not marked by her","A written greeting would have weakened the linen thread"],"correctAnswer":"The book should be ready for his work, not marked by her","explanation":"She leaves the leaf blank because he dislikes dedications, and she treats that emptiness as part of making the book usable for him.","supportingSentence":"Her brother disliked dedications, and she had come to think of the blank leaf as part of the gift, a decision not to occupy the very space she had made usable.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What failure does Clare seem most anxious to prevent?","options":["A cover that is greener than her brother's drawings","A dedication written in the wind off the bridges","A kitchen that stays looking like a workshop","A handsome book that cannot survive ordinary daily use"],"correctAnswer":"A handsome book that cannot survive ordinary daily use","explanation":"She limits the glue so the spine stays flexible, and she wants a book used every day to keep its strength.","supportingSentence":"A thing consulted every day, often with dirty hands and in a wind off the bridges, deserves a spine that survives the consultation.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what is a signature?","options":["A group of folded sheets forming part of the book","A printed message from the retired printer","Wax rubbed into the linen thread","Clare's name written on the flyleaf"],"correctAnswer":"A group of folded sheets forming part of the book","explanation":"Here a signature is not a name. It is a nest of folded sheets that she punches and sews.","supportingSentence":"A signature, in this work, was a nest of folded sheets, and each nest had to be punched with an awl so the holes sat in one straight line a short width from the fold.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention the stack of dictionaries?","options":["To replace the manual's muddy photographs","To show she pressed the boards with ordinary household weight","To list the reading her brother was expected to finish","To explain why the endpapers were dull green"],"correctAnswer":"To show she pressed the boards with ordinary household weight","explanation":"The dictionaries are what she uses to dry the boards, which keeps the craft inside an ordinary kitchen.","supportingSentence":"When the boards had dried under a stack of dictionaries, she opened the book once.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq77325$::jsonb,
  'fef7073df3cd53a4e1ed657e592fddd5c17ee8d5b3a716a76295286320864da6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '9ada5ddd-7e3d-4237-a76c-75f4d3717ff2'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4637${"topic":"science","wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq4637$::jsonb,
  $rbq50128${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"Calibrating the Rain Gauge","story":"The rain gauge on the east slope had been under-reading since the stand was moved in March. The new site was more open, which the crew had wanted, because the old hedge had sheltered the funnel and had also dropped debris into it. Open ground brought a different error. In wind, rain crossed the mouth of the gauge at an angle, and the tipping bucket caught less than a cylinder standing at ground level in the lee of the equipment box. The instrument was not broken. Its exposure had changed, and the change had a direction: the recorded catch was low, not scattered at random.\n\nFor ten storms the technician, Nia Berent, set a manual cylinder beside the gauge and read both after the rain had stopped. She excluded two storms in which the cylinder had overflowed, because an overflow is not a measurement of the difference. In the remaining eight, the bucket recorded between four and eleven percent less than the cylinder, with the larger gaps on the windier days. The ground-level cylinder was not a perfect truth. Splash and evaporation could touch it too. Berent chose it as a reference because those errors were smaller, in these storms, than the wind loss at the raised mouth, and because she could see the water she was counting. A cup anemometer at the same post gave her the wind, so the comparison was not a guess about the weather taken from memory. The bias was consistent enough to describe, and too dependent on wind to treat as a single magic number for every future shower.\n\nA colleague suggested multiplying the whole season by 1.07 and reissuing the file. Berent refused. The gauge had not stood in the same exposure before the move, and applying a factor derived from spring storms to January would invent a precision the comparison had never earned. She also refused to overwrite the original readings. The bucket's numbers were what the instrument had recorded. The correction was a later claim about those numbers, valid, if at all, for the interval in which the paired readings had been made.\n\nShe attached an adjusted column to the days from the move through the last paired storm, flagged each adjusted value, and wrote the rule beside the column: the factor may be used for that interval when wind at the post resembles the storms in the comparison, and it must not be copied to other months or other gauges. A correction is a statement with edges. Outside those edges it stops being a correction and becomes a story the rain did not tell.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"exposure","definition":"How open an instrument is to wind and weather at its site."},{"word":"catch","definition":"The rain a gauge actually collects."},{"word":"anemometer","definition":"An instrument that measures how hard the wind is blowing."},{"word":"bias","definition":"A consistent error in one direction, not a random scatter."},{"word":"interval","definition":"The stretch of time for which a stated claim is valid."},{"word":"flagged","definition":"Marked so a reader can see that a value was adjusted."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How many storms remained in the paired comparison after the overflows were set aside?","options":["Two","Eleven","Eight","Ten"],"correctAnswer":"Eight","explanation":"Two of the ten storms were excluded because the cylinder overflowed, leaving eight.","supportingSentence":"In the remaining eight, the bucket recorded between four and eleven percent less than the cylinder, with the larger gaps on the windier days.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What would have been lost if the bucket’s original numbers had been replaced by adjusted ones?","options":["The wind readings from the windier days.","The hedge that used to shelter the earlier site.","The decision to move the stand in March.","The record of what the instrument itself had registered."],"correctAnswer":"The record of what the instrument itself had registered.","explanation":"The bucket’s numbers were the instrument’s own record, so replacing them would have erased that record.","supportingSentence":"She also refused to overwrite the original readings.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why would copying this factor onto a different gauge go beyond the evidence?","options":["The comparison describes one moved instrument in stated winds, not every gauge.","The manual cylinder overflowed at every other site.","Other gauges are unable to record rain as a percent.","Only the January file still contains any readings."],"correctAnswer":"The comparison describes one moved instrument in stated winds, not every gauge.","explanation":"The factor comes from this gauge, after its move, in particular winds, so another gauge is a new claim.","supportingSentence":"She attached an adjusted column to the days from the move through the last paired storm, flagged each adjusted value, and wrote the rule beside the column: the factor may be used for that interval when wind at the post resembles the storms in the comparison, and it must not be copied to other months or other gauges.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what does “exposure” refer to?","options":["The percent written into the adjusted column.","How open the gauge is to wind and weather at its site.","The proposal to reissue a whole season of figures.","The overflow line painted on the manual cylinder."],"correctAnswer":"How open the gauge is to wind and weather at its site.","explanation":"The gauge’s exposure changed when it was moved to a more open site, and that change lowered the catch in wind.","supportingSentence":"Its exposure had changed, and the change had a direction: the recorded catch was low, not scattered at random.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage say that a correction outside its edges becomes a story the rain did not tell?","options":["To warn that extending the factor past the tested conditions invents a result.","To show that the manual cylinder was less accurate than the bucket.","To argue that wind should be deleted from every weather record.","To admit that the gauge was broken and should be thrown away."],"correctAnswer":"To warn that extending the factor past the tested conditions invents a result.","explanation":"The writer wants the reader to keep the correction inside the conditions that were actually compared.","supportingSentence":"Outside those edges it stops being a correction and becomes a story the rain did not tell.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq50128$::jsonb,
  '9ada5ddd7e3d1237776c75f4d3717ff2eab4d9ca638b39e804df6fec5005ec92',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '0e2e0374-55fc-4092-a6b6-bf164c151456'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5312${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq5312$::jsonb,
  $rbq1535${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"Coaching without Shouting","story":"The first time Coach Renata Solano stopped shouting, it was not yet a philosophy. It was a Thursday, and the point guard, Mina Darroch, had missed a layup, heard her name across the hall, and played the next twelve minutes as if the basket were a rumour. Renata saw the shutdown and heard her own voice still hanging in the rafters. After practice she apologised, which startled the assistant, Pavel Romic, more than the missed shots had. Pavel had learned the game in a gym where volume meant care. He was not cruel. He was unconvinced that quiet could carry across thirteen teenagers and a wooden floor.\n\nRenata spent the weekend rewriting the session rather than the slogans on the wall. She divided the squad into pairs, moved the corrective talk to the sideline, and gave the whole group one public target at a time. If the defence arrived late, she stopped the play, asked the pair what they had seen, and restarted. Praise, when she offered it, named an action. Criticism, when it was required, waited until the player could answer. Mina's shoulders dropped by the second drill. She missed another layup and stayed in the action, which Renata privately counted as the better result.\n\nPavel kept a tally because he did not trust impressions. At the end of the week the same press that had collapsed under noise was holding for three more seconds, and hurried passes were turning the ball over less often. He admitted the numbers, then asked what they would do when a final was loud and a parent was already shouting from the rail. Renata did not promise a silent career. She said the hall would sometimes need a raised voice for safety or for a call, and that raised was not the same as humiliating. The difference, she told him, was whether the player could use the sentence in the next possession.\n\nThey tested the distinction in a messy scrimmage on Sunday, with a crowd of siblings and a father who believed encouragement should be audible from the car park. Renata met him at the rail once, asked him to help by keeping still, and returned to the floor before the request became a speech. Mina turned the ball over, looked across, and received a signal rather than a verdict. After the buzzer Pavel said the gym had not become soft. It had become specific. Renata wrote that word in the margin of her plan, because specificity gave the players somewhere to put their effort, whereas volume had only given them somewhere to hide.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"shutdown","definition":"A sudden withdrawal from the game after public criticism."},{"word":"corrective","definition":"Meant to put a mistake right."},{"word":"impressions","definition":"General feelings, rather than counted results."},{"word":"humiliating","definition":"Shaming a person, especially in front of others."},{"word":"scrimmage","definition":"A practice match that is not a formal game."},{"word":"specificity","definition":"Exactness about what a player should do next."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did Mina respond after her name was called across the hall?","options":["She left the gym before the session ended","She scored on every possession after that","She played as if the basket were no longer quite real","She took over the instructions from Renata"],"correctAnswer":"She played as if the basket were no longer quite real","explanation":"After missing a layup and hearing her name, she played the next twelve minutes as if the basket were a rumour.","supportingSentence":"It was a Thursday, and the point guard, Mina Darroch, had missed a layup, heard her name across the hall, and played the next twelve minutes as if the basket were a rumour.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Renata count Mina's second missed layup as the better result?","options":["The slogans on the wall had finally been memorised","Mina stayed in the action instead of disappearing after the mistake","Pavel had told the squad that the score no longer mattered","The defence had been removed from the drill"],"correctAnswer":"Mina stayed in the action instead of disappearing after the mistake","explanation":"Earlier criticism had made Mina withdraw, so remaining in the play after another miss mattered more than the miss itself.","supportingSentence":"She missed another layup and stayed in the action, which Renata privately counted as the better result.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did Pavel's tally matter to the argument about the quieter sessions?","options":["It proved that parents should shout from the rail","It measured how far Mina could throw the ball","It showed that nobody missed another shot all week","It gave a doubtful assistant evidence he would not dismiss as a mood"],"correctAnswer":"It gave a doubtful assistant evidence he would not dismiss as a mood","explanation":"Pavel did not trust impressions, and the count later showed the press lasting longer and hurried passes failing less often.","supportingSentence":"Pavel kept a tally because he did not trust impressions.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does humiliating mean in Renata's contrast with a raised voice?","options":["Asking a pair what they have just seen","Writing one word in the margin of a plan","Speaking loudly enough to stop a dangerous play","Shaming a player instead of giving a sentence she can use"],"correctAnswer":"Shaming a player instead of giving a sentence she can use","explanation":"A voice may be raised for safety, but humiliating is different: it is shame rather than words a player can use.","supportingSentence":"She said the hall would sometimes need a raised voice for safety or for a call, and that raised was not the same as humiliating.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer end with the word Renata writes in the margin?","options":["To admit the quieter method failed in the scrimmage","To show that Pavel had taken over the session plan","To suggest Mina should stop playing in the team","To leave a practical standard that could replace mere volume"],"correctAnswer":"To leave a practical standard that could replace mere volume","explanation":"Specificity gives the players a place to put their effort, whereas volume had only given them a place to hide.","supportingSentence":"Renata wrote that word in the margin of her plan, because specificity gave the players somewhere to put their effort, whereas volume had only given them somewhere to hide.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq1535$::jsonb,
  '0e2e037455fc709296b6bf164c151456685ace43039b567ca4ab9438a1fb87b1',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '221a1acb-3b21-44dc-a3bf-c9af16c1e079'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4880${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq4880$::jsonb,
  $rbq36448${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"Descending by the Old Cable","story":"The cable had been left behind when the quarry closed, a slack steel line running from a rusted tower on the ridge to a shed in the trees far below. In the old photographs it carried wooden buckets of stone. Now it carried nothing but a long note when the wind was in the north. Sera had walked past it for years and treated it as a relic. She stopped treating it that way on the afternoon when Pavel's ankle folded on the loose spoil and he could not put weight on it again.\n\nThe proper descent was a series of ledges and a scree run that was manageable for a careful walker and impossible for a man who had to be held up. The light was already leaving the west face. Sera looked at the cable, at the little trolley still clamped to it, and at the brake lever stained dark by other people's hands. A notice, barely readable, said that the line was closed and that trespassers would be fined. Fines belonged to a world in which both of them could walk to the road. She tested the lever. It moved, stiffly, and the trolley answered with a short groan.\n\nThey did not pretend the choice was elegant. Pavel sat in the trolley with his bad foot propped on a coil of old wire, and Sera stood at the brake, letting the slope take them only when she allowed it. The cable sagged more than she liked. Halfway down it passed over a gap where the quarry wall had fallen away, and for a few seconds there was nothing under them but grey air and the pale fan of waste stone. She kept her eyes on the lever and on Pavel's hands, which were locked on the rim and no longer shaking. Speed was the enemy. A little motion, then a check, then a little motion again, until the sound of the wheel became something she could trust.\n\nThe shed arrived sooner than her nerves expected. The trolley bumped the stop, the cable hummed and went quiet, and the smell of wet leaves replaced the smell of dust. Pavel's face was the colour of paper, but he was grinning. Sera tied the brake with the notice's own wire, as if closing the line again could make the borrowing respectable. Then she went for help along a path that felt, after the drop, almost indecently flat. Above them the ridge kept the last of the sun. The cable between tower and shed was only a dark thread, and it had been enough.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"relic","definition":"Something old left behind after its original use has ended."},{"word":"spoil","definition":"Loose waste rock left from digging stone."},{"word":"trespassers","definition":"People who go where a notice says they must not."},{"word":"sagged","definition":"Hung down lower than it should in the middle."},{"word":"descent","definition":"The way down from a high place."},{"word":"respectable","definition":"Proper enough to be accepted as right."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why does the threat of a fine not stop Sera?","options":["Pavel says the trolley has always belonged to his family","She has a written permit folded inside the old notice","A penalty belongs to a world in which they can still walk out","The brake is locked, so the warning must be a joke"],"correctAnswer":"A penalty belongs to a world in which they can still walk out","explanation":"The notice assumes two people who can walk to the road, and Pavel no longer can.","supportingSentence":"Fines belonged to a world in which both of them could walk to the road.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Pavel's grin at the shed suggest?","options":["Relief has arrived, even though the fright has not left him","He is amused that Sera missed the proper ledges","He can put his full weight on the injured ankle again","He wants to ride the cable back up before sunset"],"correctAnswer":"Relief has arrived, even though the fright has not left him","explanation":"His face is the colour of paper from the shock, but the grin shows he knows they have reached the bottom.","supportingSentence":"Pavel's face was the colour of paper, but he was grinning.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why could Pavel not use the ordinary way down?","options":["The west face had already collapsed into the pit","His folded ankle would not take his weight","Sera had left their only rope at the tower","The ledges were buried under a layer of new snow"],"correctAnswer":"His folded ankle would not take his weight","explanation":"His ankle folded on the loose spoil, and he could not put weight on it again.","supportingSentence":"She stopped treating it that way on the afternoon when Pavel's ankle folded on the loose spoil and he could not put weight on it again.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does \"relic\" mean in this story?","options":["A fresh track cut through the loose waste rock","An old remnant of work that has already finished","A wooden bucket that is still full of stone","A fine posted against anyone who rides the cable"],"correctAnswer":"An old remnant of work that has already finished","explanation":"Before the injury, Sera treated the abandoned cable as a leftover from the closed quarry.","supportingSentence":"Sera had walked past it for years and treated it as a relic.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer have Sera tie the brake with the notice wire?","options":["To prove the warning had been printed that same morning","To explain how Pavel's ankle was bound at the shed","To show her wish to leave the borrowed line closed again","To suggest they planned to ride the trolley again tomorrow"],"correctAnswer":"To show her wish to leave the borrowed line closed again","explanation":"Using the notice's own wire makes the ending a small attempt to set the closed line back in order.","supportingSentence":"Sera tied the brake with the notice's own wire, as if closing the line again could make the borrowing respectable.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq36448$::jsonb,
  '221a1acb3b2164dcf3bfc9af16c1e0792388728fd1f6bd1ede66be377b01bfa5',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

