  $rbq5380${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":424,"questionCount":5}$rbq5380$::jsonb,
  $rbq59247${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"Drafting the Abstract","story":"Maya's abstract was twelve words over the limit and, in her supervisor's view, several degrees too sure of itself. The conference asked for a summary of no more than one hundred and eighty words. Maya had drafted it late, after a week of checking tables, and she had let the final sentence say that the workshop model improved retention among first-year students. Professor Okonkwo circled that sentence and wrote, in the margin, that the study had found a modest association in one college, not an improvement the profession could yet rely on.\n\nMaya defended the stronger verb because the conference panel favoured clear results, and because a tentative abstract might be passed over by reviewers who read fifty of them in an evening. The professor did not dispute the temptation. She said a clear sentence could still be accurate, and that confidence borrowed from a verb was the first thing a specialist audience would punish. If the data showed a difference only for students who had attended every session, the abstract had to carry that condition, even if the sentence grew less elegant. She also marked a clause that implied the comparison group had received no support at all, which was not what the method section reported.\n\nMaya cut the boast first. In place of improved retention she wrote that the workshops were associated with higher completion of the first assessment among students who attended every session. The new line was uglier and harder to say aloud. It was also closer to the table. They then removed a relative clause about national policy, which had made the project sound as if it could guide a ministry, and replaced a vague phrase about engagement with the actual measure, submitted work. Each cut hurt a little.\n\nWith the limit satisfied, Professor Okonkwo read the paragraph once more from the top. She asked whether a reader could still tell why the study mattered. Maya added a short opening that named the problem of early withdrawal without promising that her workshops had solved it. The professor nodded at that distinction. A hedge, she said, was not an apology. It was a direction to the reader about how far the evidence reached. Maya saved the file under a dull name and resisted the urge to restore the original verb before she uploaded it. If the panel wanted drama, they would not find it in her summary. They would find a claim she could defend if someone in the room asked her to point to the row that justified it.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"tentative","definition":"Said with caution, not as a final certainty"},{"word":"association","definition":"A link between two things, without claiming one caused the other"},{"word":"elegant","definition":"Neat and graceful in the way it is expressed"},{"word":"hedge","definition":"Careful wording that shows the limit of a claim"},{"word":"justified","definition":"Shown to be reasonable by the evidence"},{"word":"modest","definition":"Limited in size, and not exaggerated"}],"quiz":[{"question":"How long was the summary allowed to be?","options":["Twelve words over whatever Maya preferred","A full copy of the method section","Long enough to recommend a national policy","No more than one hundred and eighty words"],"correctAnswer":"No more than one hundred and eighty words","explanation":"The conference asked for a summary of no more than one hundred and eighty words.","supportingSentence":"The conference asked for a summary of no more than one hundred and eighty words.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the supervisor reject the stronger verb in the last sentence?","options":["The findings were too limited for other people to rely on yet","The panel had forbidden any mention of students","The comparison group had received no help of any kind","The draft was already shorter than the conference allowed"],"correctAnswer":"The findings were too limited for other people to rely on yet","explanation":"She noted a modest association in one college, not an improvement the profession could yet rely on.","supportingSentence":"Professor Okonkwo circled that sentence and wrote, in the margin, that the study had found a modest association in one college, not an improvement the profession could yet rely on.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why was the uglier new sentence an improvement?","options":["It added the twelve words Maya had just cut","It stayed nearer to the result the table showed","It brought back the claim about guiding a ministry","It removed the students from the summary entirely"],"correctAnswer":"It stayed nearer to the result the table showed","explanation":"The line was harder to say, but it was closer to the table than the boast had been.","supportingSentence":"It was also closer to the table.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the professor mean by a hedge?","options":["A deletion that only fixes the word limit","An apology for sending in dull work","A direction showing how far the evidence can be taken","A verb chosen to impress a hurried reviewer"],"correctAnswer":"A direction showing how far the evidence can be taken","explanation":"She says a hedge is not an apology, but a signal of how far the evidence reaches.","supportingSentence":"It was a direction to the reader about how far the evidence reached.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does Maya leave the original verb out of the uploaded file?","options":["She hopes nobody will notice the length of the summary","She rejects the new opening about early withdrawal","She decides that drama matters more than the table","She wants a claim she could point back to in the data"],"correctAnswer":"She wants a claim she could point back to in the data","explanation":"The summary should contain a claim she could defend by showing the row that supported it.","supportingSentence":"They would find a claim she could defend if someone in the room asked her to point to the row that justified it.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":424,"questionCount":5}$rbq59247$::jsonb,
  '89292369a0a6222ce81aeb6f8891f17113d8f0303ac70cb1adbc49d05fa09c35',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '89aeb95d-c8e4-4985-a0e0-0de937bfe516'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4702${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq4702$::jsonb,
  $rbq43675${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"Footsteps above the Stacks","story":"The stacks were supposed to be empty after ten. Ruth had checked the reading rooms, silenced the last photocopier, and signed the late book with her own name and the time. She was halfway down the central aisle, turning lights off in pairs, when she heard footsteps above her. The gallery that ran over the stacks led only to the rare-books annex, and the annex had been closed since a leak in September. The footsteps were unhurried. They crossed from the stair end toward the annex door, stopped, and did not come back.\n\nRuth did not go up. The gallery stairs were narrow, and she had no reason to meet, in a closed library, a person she had not admitted. She returned to the desk and opened the door log. The street doors had not moved since nine. The staff entrance recorded only her card, at half past seven, when her shift began. The gallery door was a different line. It had been opened from the inside at ten fourteen, and it had not been closed in the way that writes a second line. Someone was, or had been, up there, and had not come through any door the system was willing to call an entrance.\n\nShe took the torch and the radio and climbed as far as the landing, where she could see the gallery without standing on it. The annex door was ajar. A window at the far end, the one above the lane, showed a slice of night, and the latch hung loose in the way of a latch that has been lifted and not set back. On the floor just inside the annex lay a reading slip of the kind the library no longer used, filled out in pencil for a volume Ruth knew was kept on the locked shelf. The shelf, when she looked from the doorway, had a gap at that number. The slip was dated the previous day. The annex had been locked the previous day. She had locked it herself.\n\nRuth backed down, called the night security desk, and waited in the lit aisle. She did not pick up the slip. When the officer arrived, the gallery was silent, the window was still unlatched, and the gap on the shelf remained. The log had nothing new to add. Whoever had walked above the stacks had known which door opened quietly from the inside, and had left by a window the library had treated as scenery. The building's record of the evening showed one member of staff and a series of doors behaving like invitations.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"gallery","definition":"An upper walkway looking over the floor below."},{"word":"annex","definition":"A separate section of a building, here the closed rare-books room."},{"word":"unhurried","definition":"Calm and not rushed."},{"word":"ajar","definition":"Slightly open."},{"word":"latch","definition":"The fastening that holds a window or door shut."},{"word":"slip","definition":"A small paper form, here one used to request a book."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When did the log record the gallery door opening from the inside?","options":["At half past seven","After the security officer arrived","At nine o'clock","At ten fourteen"],"correctAnswer":"At ten fourteen","explanation":"The gallery door had been opened from the inside at ten fourteen, and the log did not show it closing.","supportingSentence":"It had been opened from the inside at ten fourteen, and it had not been closed in the way that writes a second line.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why is the date on the reading slip difficult to take at face value?","options":["The library dates a slip only after a book is returned.","It carries the date of a day when the annex was locked.","The September leak washed the date off every form.","Ruth wrote the slip for the night officer that evening."],"correctAnswer":"It carries the date of a day when the annex was locked.","explanation":"The slip is dated the previous day, but the annex had been locked that day, and Ruth had locked it herself.","supportingSentence":"The slip was dated the previous day.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What do Ruth's decision to stay off the gallery and her decision to leave the slip share?","options":["She wants the annex alarm to reset by itself.","She has already hidden the missing volume in her desk.","She believes the footsteps came from the heating pipes.","She is avoiding a confrontation and leaving the scene untouched."],"correctAnswer":"She is avoiding a confrontation and leaving the scene untouched.","explanation":"She will not go up to meet a person she did not admit, and she does not pick up the slip before security arrives.","supportingSentence":"She did not pick up the slip.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is the annex in this story?","options":["The security desk in another building","The closed rare-books section reached from the gallery","The staff door that opens onto the street","The room that holds the photocopier"],"correctAnswer":"The closed rare-books section reached from the gallery","explanation":"The gallery leads only to the rare-books annex, and that annex has been closed since a leak in September.","supportingSentence":"The gallery that ran over the stacks led only to the rare-books annex, and the annex had been closed since a leak in September.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What impression does the writer leave by describing the doors as invitations?","options":["Ruth had invited a guest and forgotten the name.","Footsteps in a library after ten are always harmless.","The building's own record made the intrusion look almost permitted.","The library had planned to move the rare books that night."],"correctAnswer":"The building's own record made the intrusion look almost permitted.","explanation":"The final image is of one member of staff and a series of doors behaving like invitations, so the records themselves look strangely welcoming.","supportingSentence":"The building's record of the evening showed one member of staff and a series of doors behaving like invitations.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq43675$::jsonb,
  '89aeb95dc8e4e98530e00de937bfe5161c82902dc9975e65b7569c9359a8adb6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '0db92528-38fb-47e6-a174-faa0aab90463'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4666${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4666$::jsonb,
  $rbq47271${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"Ink That Dried into Roads","story":"The ink in the municipal office was supposed to be ordinary. It sat in a glass well, smelled faintly of iron, and stained the clerks' cuffs in the honest way of ink. Nessa discovered its other habit on a wet Thursday, when she finished a permit for a lane behind the tannery and went out to post the notice. The lane had not been there in the morning. By evening it was, narrow and fresh, with puddles in the exact shape of her last three sentences. She walked the new lane twice, once in each direction, and the puddles repeated her punctuation with embarrassing loyalty. A baker had already set a crate where the lane bent.\n\nShe tested the discovery in small ways, because a large test would have been a crime with witnesses. A line in the margin became a footpath across a vacant lot, useful and a little crooked, which taught her that her handwriting had opinions. A crossed-out word did not vanish the thing it had made. It left a scar in the ground, a sunken stripe where grass would not grow, and people tripped there and blamed the council. Erasure, she learned, was not the opposite of creation. It was a second, ruder act.\n\nThe mayor, once he understood, asked for a bridge. The river had wanted one for years, and the engineers had wanted money for just as long. Nessa wrote the bridge at night, slowly, describing the arches, the height above flood, the place where a person could stand out of the wind. At dawn the arches were there, pale as dried ink, and a boy was already halfway across, shouting that the boards held. She felt pride, then the weight that follows pride. If a sentence could become a road, a careless sentence could become a wall across someone else's door.\n\nShe set rules where no one had written rules. She would add nothing that closed a view a neighbour used to judge the weather. She would build no street that served only the person who paid for the words. And she would keep, in a drawer, a page of unsent lines: a garden stair, a wider market, a quiet path to the river that did not cut the washerwomen's stones. Some ink dried into roads. Some ink dried into patience. When visitors praised the new bridge, Nessa looked at their shoes, not their faces, and was glad the road had been kind to them. Then she capped the well, because the city was already walking on her handwriting, and it did not need another sentence before supper.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"municipal","definition":"Belonging to the town's local government."},{"word":"permit","definition":"An official paper that allows a particular thing to be done."},{"word":"erasure","definition":"The act of rubbing out something that was written."},{"word":"arches","definition":"Curved supports that hold up a bridge."},{"word":"unsent","definition":"Written, but deliberately not released into the world."},{"word":"handwriting","definition":"The particular way a person forms letters by hand."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does \"erasure\" mean in this story?","options":["A fee the mayor pays for each new bridge","The act of rubbing out a mark already made","A fresh lane opened behind the tannery","The pale colour left by ink as it dries"],"correctAnswer":"The act of rubbing out a mark already made","explanation":"Erasure is crossing a word out, and in this story that act still leaves a scar.","supportingSentence":"Erasure, she learned, was not the opposite of creation.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What did a crossed-out word leave behind?","options":["A wide street that served only the person who paid","A crate the baker was no longer able to lift","A second bridge beside the one the mayor ordered","A sunken stripe of ground where grass would not grow"],"correctAnswer":"A sunken stripe of ground where grass would not grow","explanation":"The crossed-out word left a sunken stripe, and people tripped there.","supportingSentence":"It left a scar in the ground, a sunken stripe where grass would not grow, and people tripped there and blamed the council.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the baker's crate tell Nessa?","options":["The tannery has paid her to make the lane wider","Her letters are too crooked to become a real path","Other people are already using a road her words created","The morning puddles have washed her permit away"],"correctAnswer":"Other people are already using a road her words created","explanation":"A crate at the bend means the new lane is already part of someone else's working day.","supportingSentence":"A baker had already set a crate where the lane bent.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer place a boy on the bridge at dawn?","options":["To suggest the arches are still only a drawing","To explain why the engineers have left the town","To reveal that the boy stole the permit during the night","To show that the written arches have become a public way"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the written arches have become a public way","explanation":"The boy is already crossing, and the boards hold, so the sentence has become a bridge people can use.","supportingSentence":"At dawn the arches were there, pale as dried ink, and a boy was already halfway across, shouting that the boards held.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"Why does Nessa look at shoes rather than faces?","options":["She hopes the visitors will pay for another sentence","She is checking whether the road has been kind to the people on it","She is searching their cuffs for stains from her ink","She does not want them to notice that the well is empty"],"correctAnswer":"She is checking whether the road has been kind to the people on it","explanation":"Shoes carry the evidence of the walk, and she is glad when the road has treated people gently.","supportingSentence":"When visitors praised the new bridge, Nessa looked at their shoes, not their faces, and was glad the road had been kind to them.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq47271$::jsonb,
  '0db9252838fb17e66174faa0aab904637bad9b5e3b1baa1f068780c54744daff',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'e8f3fcbc-3b99-4872-ac62-d24dd0b91c5a'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4640${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4640$::jsonb,
  $rbq43097${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"Mapping the Coastal Path","story":"The district had asked for a usable map of the path from Rook Point to the river mouth, and the work went to Petra Elman, who treated the small fee as no excuse for a careless line. She walked the route on three Saturdays with a measuring wheel, a notebook, and a private rule. She would not mark a waymark as connected to the next unless she could see that next one without inventing the link. Where gorse had swallowed the path, she drew a gap and a question rather than a confident curve.\n\nErosion had shifted the cliff since the previous sketch, made ten years earlier by a volunteer who loved a smooth line. Petra's version was less flattering and, she believed, more loyal to a walker's actual feet. It stepped inland around a fresh slump, recorded a stile that had become a climb, and renamed a ditch the old sheet still called a seasonal stream. A farmer met her at the second gate and objected to an arrow that would send strangers through his yard whenever the tide covered the lower stones. She walked the alternative he offered. It was drier, no longer in distance, and visible from one end to the other, which satisfied both his objection and her rule. She entered the change as two facts: his knowledge of the tide, and her measurement of the ground. Neither cancelled the other.\n\nAt the headland she stopped using the wheel for a few minutes, because the light had made her want to be only a visitor. Then she started it again. Wanting to look was not a method. The draft she inked at home carried distances, cautions, and a margin note that the path was an agreement with the weather, renewed or broken each winter, not a promise. She sent the district a copy on which uncertainty remained visible. A clerk replied that the printed leaflet would be kinder if the gaps were closed, since visitors dislike hesitation. Petra wrote back that a gap was already a statement. A continuous line across ground that had failed would be neat, and someone would trust it with a foot. She added an example. At the slump, a straight stroke on the old sketch would have led a walker onto earth that now sat on the beach. The new line cost a little dignity and saved a fall. If the leaflet could not bear to show that bargain, it was not yet a map. She kept the measuring wheel by the door, because a drawing, however careful, goes stale the moment the cliff decides otherwise.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"waymark","definition":"A sign or marker that shows the route of a path."},{"word":"erosion","definition":"The gradual wearing away of land by weather or water."},{"word":"stile","definition":"A step or ladder that lets people cross a fence."},{"word":"measurement","definition":"A figure obtained by measuring something."},{"word":"headland","definition":"A piece of high land that sticks out into the sea."},{"word":"hesitation","definition":"A pause caused by uncertainty."}],"quiz":[{"question":"On how many Saturdays did Petra walk the route?","options":["Fourteen","Three","Ten","One"],"correctAnswer":"Three","explanation":"She walked the route on three Saturdays with her wheel and notebook.","supportingSentence":"She walked the route on three Saturdays with a measuring wheel, a notebook, and a private rule.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Petra's pause at the headland show about her?","options":["She left the wheel behind and ended the survey","She decided the view was too dull to record","She can feel like a visitor and still refuse to map by feeling","She agreed that gaps would look unkind on a leaflet"],"correctAnswer":"She can feel like a visitor and still refuse to map by feeling","explanation":"The light makes her want only to look, but she starts again because wanting to look is not a method.","supportingSentence":"Wanting to look was not a method.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What duty does Petra believe a printed line owes a walker?","options":["It must look smoother than the volunteer's old sketch","It must replace local knowledge with one pleasant curve","It must hide every farmyard along the tide","It must not invite trust in ground that has already failed"],"correctAnswer":"It must not invite trust in ground that has already failed","explanation":"She warns that a neat line across failed ground would be trusted with a foot, so the map should refuse that false confidence.","supportingSentence":"A continuous line across ground that had failed would be neat, and someone would trust it with a foot.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What has erosion done to the coast in this story?","options":["Worn the cliff back so the old line is no longer safe","Raised a new stile at the farmer's second gate","Held the tide off the lower stones all year","Filled the path with water from a seasonal stream"],"correctAnswer":"Worn the cliff back so the old line is no longer safe","explanation":"Erosion had shifted the cliff since the old sketch, so a straight stroke there would now lead onto fallen earth.","supportingSentence":"Erosion had shifted the cliff since the previous sketch, made ten years earlier by a volunteer who loved a smooth line.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include Petra's wish to be only a visitor?","options":["To suggest the farmer had offered her a place to stay","To show that her strictness is a choice, not a lack of feeling","To prove she abandoned the map at the headland","To explain the clerk's request for a kinder leaflet"],"correctAnswer":"To show that her strictness is a choice, not a lack of feeling","explanation":"She is moved by the light, and returning to the wheel shows that the discipline comes after feeling, not instead of it.","supportingSentence":"At the headland she stopped using the wheel for a few minutes, because the light had made her want to be only a visitor.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq43097$::jsonb,
  'e8f3fcbc3b9978723c62d24dd0b91c5a4c523be61ae0ffe40b7ebd9aa01658ef',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'e88677d2-e00f-48ad-a9b9-3da7a6b21fea'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4563${"topic":"travel","wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq4563$::jsonb,
  $rbq80525${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"Missing the Last Ferry","story":"Harun Braye reached the quay at Kerrin just as the ramp began to rise. The evening ferry to the island of Sarn had kept its timetable, and the bus from the ridge had not. He stood with a canvas bag and a crate of replacement parts for his sister's generator, watching the gap of dark water widen. The ticket in his pocket remained valid for the next scheduled crossing, which made the miss feel less like misfortune than like a sum he had got wrong.\n\nA harbour clerk told him the dawn boat would run if the wind stayed under the posted limit. There was a dormitory above the chandlery for stranded passengers, charged by the night, with a rule against cooking and a lock on the shower. He could also hire a private launch at a price that would consume what he expected to save by bringing the parts himself. He walked the length of the quay before deciding, counting bollards because the counting slowed the impulse to buy speed. His sister had said the generator could wait until morning. He had not entirely believed her, because she tended to call a difficulty small until another person arrived to share it, and yet he took the dormitory.\n\nThe room held four beds, one of them occupied by a fisheries inspector who was writing in a ledger and preferred silence. Harun ate bread from his bag, set the crate where he could see it from the pillow, and read the wind notice twice without becoming calmer. Around midnight the gusts strengthened enough to rattle the sash, and he understood that the dawn departure was contingent on a forecast that might still change. He slept in his coat. At first light the inspector touched his shoulder and said the crossing was on, though the sea would be honest. In that harbour, Harun already knew, honest meant rough but workable, a condition people respected because it did not pretend. On the early ferry he kept one hand on the crate and revised his sense of what the night had cost. Missing the last boat had taken time and a measure of pride. Paying for the launch would have preserved the schedule and spent the judgment he was beginning to value. He watched the island clarify through spray and thought of his sister's kitchen, where the generator would either start or it would not, and neither result would be improved by a story of panic. The parts were in the crate. He was on the boat that the weather had actually offered. That, for one morning, was enough precision.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"quay","definition":"A stone platform where boats load and unload."},{"word":"timetable","definition":"A list of the times when transport is due to leave or arrive."},{"word":"chandlery","definition":"A shop that sells supplies for boats."},{"word":"impulse","definition":"A sudden urge to act."},{"word":"contingent","definition":"Dependent on something else that may still change."},{"word":"ledger","definition":"A book in which records or accounts are written."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What was inside the crate Harun hoped to deliver?","options":["Replacement parts for a generator","Cooking pots for the dormitory","A ledger of harbour fees","Tickets for a private launch"],"correctAnswer":"Replacement parts for a generator","explanation":"He was carrying a crate of replacement parts for his sister's generator.","supportingSentence":"He stood with a canvas bag and a crate of replacement parts for his sister's generator, watching the gap of dark water widen.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does taking the dormitory suggest about Harun's decision?","options":["He believed the generator would fail unless he arrived by midnight","He chose to let the repair wait, despite doubting his sister's calm","He missed the ferry on purpose to avoid the launch fare","He preferred the inspector's company to his sister's kitchen"],"correctAnswer":"He chose to let the repair wait, despite doubting his sister's calm","explanation":"He did not fully trust her reassurance, and yet he stayed the night, acting as if morning would be soon enough.","supportingSentence":"He had not entirely believed her, because she tended to call a difficulty small until another person arrived to share it, and yet he took the dormitory.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How does Harun seem to feel about the boat he finally boards?","options":["It shows the inspector was wrong about the sea","It means his sister will reject the parts","It is the crossing the conditions actually allowed, and that is enough","It proves that every private launch is a waste of money"],"correctAnswer":"It is the crossing the conditions actually allowed, and that is enough","explanation":"He is on the boat the weather offered, and he treats that match between plan and conditions as enough for the morning.","supportingSentence":"That, for one morning, was enough precision.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does contingent say about the dawn departure in this story?","options":["It had been cancelled by the inspector","It was included in the price of a launch","It would sail only if the dormitory was full","It depended on a forecast that might still change"],"correctAnswer":"It depended on a forecast that might still change","explanation":"Harun understood that the dawn boat depended on the forecast, so it was not yet certain.","supportingSentence":"Around midnight the gusts strengthened enough to rattle the sash, and he understood that the dawn departure was contingent on a forecast that might still change.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer explain the harbour meaning of the word honest?","options":["To show a local taste for plain speech that fits the night's lesson","To explain the dormitory rule against cooking","To suggest the inspector lied about the crossing","To advertise a second, calmer ferry at noon"],"correctAnswer":"To show a local taste for plain speech that fits the night's lesson","explanation":"People respect the word because it does not pretend, which matches Harun's preference for a measured delay over a dramatic rescue.","supportingSentence":"In that harbour, Harun already knew, honest meant rough but workable, a condition people respected because it did not pretend.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq80525$::jsonb,
  'e88677d2e00f88adc9b93da7a6b21feaf8ca34b593cf52d70b6226cd0d8c6632',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '334c4b53-5c4a-42ec-ae4b-c47bf6e2f1d6'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4888${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq4888$::jsonb,
  $rbq79941${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"Noise after Midnight","story":"The piano started again just after midnight, not loudly, but with a persistence that made sleep unlikely. Helen lay awake in the flat below and counted the same four bars, which her neighbour, Yusuf, repeated until a wrong note sent the passage back to the beginning. The building rules, posted in the lobby when she moved in, stipulated that musical instruments should fall silent by eleven. This week his sessions had become nightly, and the intermittent pauses made them harder to ignore than a steady sound would have been.\n\nOn Thursday she slipped a note under his door rather than knocking while he was still playing. She kept the tone measured, mentioned the rule, and asked whether an earlier finish might be possible during the working week. Yusuf replied the next morning with an apology that did not quite concede the point. He explained that the only free hour in his timetable fell after his last pupil left, and that the piece was for an audition he could not move. If he stopped at eleven, he said, he would lose the one stretch of time in which the building was otherwise still. He offered to close the windows and to avoid the passages that needed force.\n\nHelen tried earplugs and found that they muffled the melody without removing the thud of the pedal. The caretaker suggested mediation before anyone wrote to the managing agent, whose letters tended to escalate a nuisance into a formal warning. Helen agreed, on the condition that the conversation stay specific and that nobody be asked to stop playing altogether.\n\nThey met in the ground-floor room on Monday evening. Yusuf brought a practice diary, and Helen brought a record of the nights she had been woken. The mediator, a resident who trained people in difficult conversations, asked each of them what a tolerable week would look like. Yusuf needed four late sessions before the audition. Helen needed two nights of unbroken sleep in every three, because her hospital shift started at six. The arrangement they reached was narrower than either had wanted. He could play past midnight on Tuesday and Friday, with the windows shut and the soft pedal down, and he would stop by eleven on every other night. Helen would not pursue a warning unless the pattern slipped. If the audition had not been so close, she might have pressed for a stricter limit. As it was, she left with a compromise she could live with, and with the uneasy sense that goodwill, once written into a timetable, would need watching.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"persistence","definition":"The habit of continuing and not stopping"},{"word":"stipulated","definition":"Stated as a requirement"},{"word":"intermittent","definition":"Stopping and starting, rather than steady"},{"word":"mediation","definition":"A talk helped by someone who is not on either side"},{"word":"nuisance","definition":"Something that seriously bothers other people"},{"word":"tolerable","definition":"Hard, but still acceptable to live with"}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why did the late playing disturb Helen more than a continuous sound would have?","options":["Yusuf played more loudly after she wrote to him","The lobby rule allowed only very short bursts of music","Her earplugs turned every pause into a knock","The repeated breaks kept pulling her attention back"],"correctAnswer":"The repeated breaks kept pulling her attention back","explanation":"The pauses in the nightly sessions were harder to ignore than a steady sound.","supportingSentence":"This week his sessions had become nightly, and the intermittent pauses made them harder to ignore than a steady sound would have been.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"On which nights could Yusuf continue after midnight?","options":["Tuesday and Friday","Monday and Thursday, while Helen was at the hospital","Every night, if the windows stayed shut","Only the Sunday before the audition"],"correctAnswer":"Tuesday and Friday","explanation":"He could play past midnight on Tuesday and Friday, and he had to stop by eleven on other nights.","supportingSentence":"He could play past midnight on Tuesday and Friday, with the windows shut and the soft pedal down, and he would stop by eleven on every other night.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the caretaker suggest a meeting rather than a letter to the agent?","options":["The lobby rules had expired when Helen moved in","A formal warning would have made the quarrel sharper","The agent had already refused to read notes from residents","Helen had demanded that the audition be cancelled"],"correctAnswer":"A formal warning would have made the quarrel sharper","explanation":"Letters from the managing agent tended to turn a nuisance into a formal warning.","supportingSentence":"The caretaker suggested mediation before anyone wrote to the managing agent, whose letters tended to escalate a nuisance into a formal warning.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is a tolerable week in this conversation?","options":["A pattern of days that both neighbours could live with","A note pushed under a door late at night","A warning sent by the managing agent","A passage that had to be played with force"],"correctAnswer":"A pattern of days that both neighbours could live with","explanation":"The mediator asks each of them what kind of week they could accept.","supportingSentence":"The mediator, a resident who trained people in difficult conversations, asked each of them what a tolerable week would look like.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention the rule posted in the lobby?","options":["To prove that Yusuf had never met the other residents","To suggest that hospital shifts should end at eleven","To argue that practice should move to a public hall","To show that Helen is relying on a rule that already exists"],"correctAnswer":"To show that Helen is relying on a rule that already exists","explanation":"The posted rule says instruments should be silent by eleven, which is the standard behind her complaint.","supportingSentence":"The building rules, posted in the lobby when she moved in, stipulated that musical instruments should fall silent by eleven.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq79941$::jsonb,
  '334c4b535c4a62ec2e4bc47bf6e2f1d67f7ce9e59005dad2bbb63cf37e7fffbe',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'dfd5e9e4-1d90-4934-ae6a-6c0d2e35561d'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4640${"topic":"science","wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq4640$::jsonb,
  $rbq40515${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"Notes from the Field Station","story":"The handover at the field station took most of a morning, because the outgoing lead would not summarize a season that had not been uniform. The generator log, the freezer chart, and the sample book told different stories if a person read only one of them. Twice during a planned generator service the chest freezer had climbed and then recovered before dawn. Both rises stayed inside the tolerance written in the protocol for that kind of sample. The night tech had annotated the affected boxes and had left them in the set. Discarding them would have pretended that a recorded, permitted excursion was the same thing as a failure. Keeping them without a mark would have pretended the excursion had not happened.\n\nA second break was easier to miss. In week three the crew changed the filter size used for the water samples. The new size was a reasonable response to sediment after a storm, and it was written in the margin of a bench notebook, not in the shared log. Early samples and later samples were therefore not comparable in a direct way. A difference in the counts might be a difference in the water, or a difference in what the filter had been willing to catch. The incoming lead, Tomas Elling, asked for a boundary in the dataset at the week of the change, with the filter size stored beside every count. He initialed each page of the freezer chart, so a later audit could see who had accepted the annotated boxes into the set. He did not ask anyone to rerun the season. The samples could not be unfiltered.\n\nElling also refused a tidy merge that would have filled gaps in the weather sheet by copying the nearest hour. Some gaps were short, caused by a sensor that had been unplugged during the same generator work. Those could be flagged and left empty. An empty cell is a statement that the station does not know. A copied cell is a statement that it does, and the copy would have been hardest to detect exactly where a later user most wanted a continuous line.\n\nThe notes he accepted ran to six pages, which the previous lead apologized for. Elling said the length was the point. A field station's continuity is not the feeling that one team smoothly replaced another. It is the written trail that lets the next person see where the method, the temperature, and the record itself changed, and where they did not. The season remained usable because those edges had been kept sharp.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"handover","definition":"The passing of responsibility and records from one lead to the next."},{"word":"tolerance","definition":"The amount of variation a written rule still allows."},{"word":"excursion","definition":"A departure from the usual temperature that later returned."},{"word":"annotated","definition":"Marked with a note so a later reader can see what happened."},{"word":"comparable","definition":"Able to be set beside one another as the same kind of measurement."},{"word":"protocol","definition":"The written rules for how the samples were to be handled."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did the two overnight freezer rises compare with the limit written for those samples?","options":["Both stayed inside the written limit.","The written rules contained no limit for freezer temperature.","Only the second rise was written down, and it matched the outdoor air.","Both went past it and forced the boxes to be thrown away."],"correctAnswer":"Both stayed inside the written limit.","explanation":"Both rises stayed inside the tolerance written in the protocol for that kind of sample.","supportingSentence":"Both rises stayed inside the tolerance written in the protocol for that kind of sample.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do throwing the warmed boxes away and keeping them with no mark have in common here?","options":["Each would make the freezer chart and the sample book say the same thing.","Each would make the early and later water counts directly alike.","Each would meet the rules by deleting the generator log.","Each would hide the truth about a rise that stayed inside the rules."],"correctAnswer":"Each would hide the truth about a rise that stayed inside the rules.","explanation":"Discarding the boxes would pretend a permitted rise was a failure, and leaving them unmarked would pretend it never happened.","supportingSentence":"Keeping them without a mark would have pretended the excursion had not happened.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is Elling protecting by leaving the short weather gaps empty?","options":["A clear signal that those hours were not actually measured.","Proof that the sensor kept recording throughout the service.","A smoother line that makes the generator log more accurate.","A way to erase the filter change from the sample book."],"correctAnswer":"A clear signal that those hours were not actually measured.","explanation":"An empty cell says the station does not know, while a copied value would claim a measurement that was never made.","supportingSentence":"An empty cell is a statement that the station does not know.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what is an “excursion”?","options":["A change of filter size that was copied into the shared log.","A departure from the usual freezer temperature during the generator service.","A failure that required every marked box to be discarded.","A weather value filled in by copying the nearest hour."],"correctAnswer":"A departure from the usual freezer temperature during the generator service.","explanation":"The excursion is the freezer’s rise during the service, and it was permitted because it stayed inside the written tolerance.","supportingSentence":"Discarding them would have pretended that a recorded, permitted excursion was the same thing as a failure.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage defend six pages of notes instead of a short summary?","options":["To argue that the generator should never be serviced again.","To prove that early and later samples can be pooled with no boundary.","To show that a usable season depends on keeping the breaks visible.","To suggest the outgoing lead should have reduced the season to one line."],"correctAnswer":"To show that a usable season depends on keeping the breaks visible.","explanation":"The length matters because the next person needs a written trail of where the method, the temperature, and the record changed.","supportingSentence":"It is the written trail that lets the next person see where the method, the temperature, and the record itself changed, and where they did not.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq40515$::jsonb,
  'dfd5e9e41d9049341e6a6c0d2e35561ddebfcc590076fe972a065086868e3322',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '11cd909d-ed75-4074-a218-4aaa932ba8e6'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5203${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":439,"questionCount":5}$rbq5203$::jsonb,
  $rbq854${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"Preserving the Old Orchard","story":"The old orchard sat on a slope above the river, and the council's new road was drawn through it in a line so straight that it looked like an opinion. Sixty trees remained, most of them apples with names that did not appear in supermarkets, plus a row of pears that fruited only when the spring was patient. Amira Soltani, who had leased the ground from her uncle, called a Saturday grafting morning and was surprised by the number of people who came. Some wanted scions. Some wanted to be seen defending a place. She needed both, and she needed them to leave with a task, because affection without labour would not impress a planning hearing.\n\nA surveyor had described the orchard as underused. The word offended the volunteers until Amira admitted it was not entirely false. Fruit had fallen uncollected in recent years, and the wasps had enjoyed a better season than the neighbours. If they argued only that the place was beautiful, the road would be called necessary and the beauty would be called a private taste. She wanted a record of use: jars sold, trees adopted, varieties named, and fruit the regional wholesaler did not stock. Nostalgia could open the meeting. Evidence would have to keep it open.\n\nThey grafted in the barn because the wind was unkind. Amira showed the cut, the tongue of green wood, the tape, and a pencil label that would survive the damp. Each scion came from a tree they might lose, and each was set onto rootstock that a local nursery had donated without quite joining the protest. Children could wind tape but could not make the cut. A retired botanist, Yusuf Maren, corrected grips with a kindness that assumed people could improve. By noon there were ninety grafts, which would not stop a road but might continue the orchard somewhere else.\n\nThe continuation was the delicate part. Amira did not want the backup to weaken the fight to keep the slope. She told the group that grafting was prudence, not permission. Letters would still go to the council, and last autumn's jars would sit on the table at the hearing, labelled with variety and week. Anyone who spoke would describe a job, not a feeling: pruning in February, blossom watched for frost, fruit carried down the muddy track. Yusuf added that a heritage tree no one could propagate was only a story, while a heritage tree with living copies was an argument. People laughed, then wrote their names beside the aftercare list. The road remained on the map. So did a plan for making that straight line look expensive.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"scions","definition":"Young shoots cut from a tree so they can be grafted onto another."},{"word":"underused","definition":"Not being used as fully as it could be."},{"word":"nostalgia","definition":"Affection for the past."},{"word":"rootstock","definition":"The rooted plant onto which a graft is set."},{"word":"prudence","definition":"Sensible care taken ahead of a possible loss."},{"word":"propagate","definition":"To grow new living plants from an existing one."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did Amira expect if the orchard was defended only for its beauty?","options":["The wholesaler would start selling their named apples","The volunteers would be asked to leave the barn","The council would cancel the hearing out of respect","The road would be called necessary, and the beauty a private taste"],"correctAnswer":"The road would be called necessary, and the beauty a private taste","explanation":"She says that a beauty-only argument would let the road be called necessary and the beauty a private taste.","supportingSentence":"If they argued only that the place was beautiful, the road would be called necessary and the beauty would be called a private taste.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What balance is Amira trying to keep by grafting before the hearing?","options":["Selling the slope before the council can meet","Replacing the old trees with supermarket varieties","Letting the children decide the line of the road","Preparing for possible loss without making that loss seem acceptable"],"correctAnswer":"Preparing for possible loss without making that loss seem acceptable","explanation":"The grafts might continue the orchard elsewhere, but she calls grafting prudence rather than permission and does not want the backup to weaken the fight.","supportingSentence":"She told the group that grafting was prudence, not permission.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does Amira partly accept the surveyor's description of the orchard?","options":["Uncollected fruit had made the charge of neglect partly fair","Yusuf had asked her to agree with the council","She wanted the road to pass through the oldest pears","The nursery had already bought the slope"],"correctAnswer":"Uncollected fruit had made the charge of neglect partly fair","explanation":"She admits the word underused is not entirely false, and the story has just said fruit had fallen uncollected while the wasps ate well.","supportingSentence":"Fruit had fallen uncollected in recent years, and the wasps had enjoyed a better season than the neighbours.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is a scion in this grafting morning?","options":["A jar of fruit saved from last autumn","The council map of the new road","The rooted plant given by the nursery","A shoot taken from a tree they might lose"],"correctAnswer":"A shoot taken from a tree they might lose","explanation":"Each scion is cut from a tree they might lose and then set onto rootstock.","supportingSentence":"Each scion came from a tree they might lose, and each was set onto rootstock that a local nursery had donated without quite joining the protest.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer list jobs such as pruning and carrying fruit?","options":["To argue that nostalgia by itself would win the hearing","To show that the hearing needed concrete work, not only feeling","To suggest the orchard was already closed in February","To blame the children for the muddy track"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the hearing needed concrete work, not only feeling","explanation":"Speakers are told to describe a job rather than a feeling, because evidence, not nostalgia alone, has to carry the meeting.","supportingSentence":"Anyone who spoke would describe a job, not a feeling: pruning in February, blossom watched for frost, fruit carried down the muddy track.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":439,"questionCount":5}$rbq854$::jsonb,
  '11cd909ded75507492184aaa932ba8e69270bfe06746cb0355ce5795e7d60404',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'fd4ae0f7-1b07-4205-a32e-958948d1b395'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5484${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":430,"questionCount":5}$rbq5484$::jsonb,
  $rbq80743${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"Pricing the Winter Stock","story":"The delivery note for spring arrived while the winter stock was still stacked to the rafters in the back room of Pell's garden shop. Sacks of grit, boxes of gloves, and a line of small electric heaters sat where the seed racks needed to be by the first week of March. Adel, who did the buying, had ordered the heaters in October after a cold forecast that never quite became a cold season. They had sold, but slowly, and every week they remained was a week of rent, insurance, and a staff hour spent shifting them to reach something else.\n\nHe priced the season the way he had been taught, then checked the result against the room itself. Each heater had a unit cost, a modest margin, and a holding cost he usually ignored because winter goods were gone by January. This year the holding cost was no longer theoretical. The stock was occupying the warmest storage in the building, the part that stayed dry. If he kept the original price, he might sell the last units to the next cold snap and protect the margin. He might also still be climbing over them when the seed order landed, paying to store metal that customers had already decided to do without.\n\nA deep markdown would clear the floor, and Adel had used that tool before. It would also teach the street a lesson he did not want it to learn. Two regulars had already asked, lightly, whether the heaters would be cheaper if they waited until February. If he confirmed the habit, next October's stock would sit untouched until the discount appeared, and the margin on a whole category would start its year already wounded. Clearance, he decided, was a door you opened on purpose and only part of the way.\n\nHe split the range. The gloves, which took little space and still sold on wet mornings, stayed at their original price. The grit moved to the pavement display at a small reduction, because once the frost ended it would be worth almost nothing. The heaters were offered at a cut large enough to interest a buyer this week, but not so large that the price looked like a collapse. He put the reason on a handwritten card: space needed for seed, not a fault in the goods. By Friday half the heaters had gone, and the back room smelled of cardboard rather than dust on warm metal. The till showed a thinner profit than the October plan. The floor showed a shop that could take delivery without an apology.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"forecast","definition":"A prediction of what is likely to happen, used here about the weather."},{"word":"margin","definition":"The difference between what goods cost and what they sell for."},{"word":"markdown","definition":"A deliberate cut in the selling price."},{"word":"clearance","definition":"A sale meant to move stock out quickly."},{"word":"reduction","definition":"An amount taken off a price."},{"word":"insurance","definition":"A regular payment made against the risk of loss or damage."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Which goods did Adel leave at the price he had first set?","options":["The electric heaters","The sacks of grit","The spring seed racks","The gloves"],"correctAnswer":"The gloves","explanation":"The gloves still sold and took little space, so they stayed at their original price.","supportingSentence":"The gloves, which took little space and still sold on wet mornings, stayed at their original price.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What links the different prices Adel chooses at the end?","options":["He cuts a price according to the space an item uses and how little it will be worth later, rather than by one discount for everything.","He copies the prices printed on the spring delivery note.","He sells the whole winter range at cost until March.","He raises every price the customers have already asked about."],"correctAnswer":"He cuts a price according to the space an item uses and how little it will be worth later, rather than by one discount for everything.","explanation":"Gloves stay put because they are small and still selling, grit is reduced because frost gives it an end, and the heaters are cut to free the room. One rule does not cover all of them.","supportingSentence":"The grit moved to the pavement display at a small reduction, because once the frost ended it would be worth almost nothing.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How does Adel appear to judge the result of the week?","options":["A thinner profit was acceptable because the shop was ready for the spring delivery.","The cold forecast had been right after all.","Selling half the heaters proved the October price should never have changed.","The gloves should have been cut more deeply than the heaters."],"correctAnswer":"A thinner profit was acceptable because the shop was ready for the spring delivery.","explanation":"The till is down on the October plan, but the cleared floor means the shop can take the delivery without an apology. That is the result he has been working toward.","supportingSentence":"The floor showed a shop that could take delivery without an apology.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what is a markdown?","options":["The weekly cost of keeping goods in a room","A deliberate cut in the selling price","A note written on a delivery","A fine for ordering stock too late"],"correctAnswer":"A deliberate cut in the selling price","explanation":"Adel talks about a deep markdown as a tool that would clear the floor, so it means cutting prices on purpose.","supportingSentence":"A deep markdown would clear the floor, and Adel had used that tool before.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include the regulars who asked about waiting until February?","options":["To introduce two new members of staff","To prove that February is always the coldest month","To suggest the heaters were faulty","To show the customer habit a deep discount might strengthen"],"correctAnswer":"To show the customer habit a deep discount might strengthen","explanation":"Their light question shows that some buyers are already tempted to wait, which is the lesson Adel does not want a large cut to teach.","supportingSentence":"Two regulars had already asked, lightly, whether the heaters would be cheaper if they waited until February.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":430,"questionCount":5}$rbq80743$::jsonb,
  'fd4ae0f71b07c205832e958948d1b395359c9d40752e98a2a525f75c33730715',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '821ffa56-a0c3-43cd-a3c9-5147953746f1'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4883${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq4883$::jsonb,
  $rbq21677${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"Reading the Incoming Weather","story":"Mara stopped on the shoulder above the lake and made her nephew stand still before he reached for the map. The map, she said, could wait. Weather arrived before a person had finished arguing with it, and the ridge they hoped to cross would not care what a printed line had promised the night before. Nils was sixteen, quick, and still in love with the idea that preparation was a list. He had the list. He did not yet have the habit of looking up.\n\nShe asked him to name what he could actually see. A high veil of cloud was spreading from the west, thin enough that the peaks still cut through it, thick enough to take the hard edge off the light. The wind on their faces was mild, but the grass at the col was leaning the other way, which meant the air above them was already moving faster than the air at their boots. Small lenticular patches sat over the farthest summit like stacked plates. Mara did not call them by the textbook name. She called them a reason to be humble.\n\nNils wanted a rule he could reuse. If those clouds meant a storm, then every pale sky was a threat, and nobody would ever leave a hut. Mara shook her head. One sign was a rumour. Three signs, agreeing with each other, were a case. She made him watch the lake. An hour earlier it had been a dark mirror. Now short lines of wind scratched it, and the scratches were travelling towards them. The temperature had not fallen, but the smell of the air had changed, less like warm stone and more like wet metal. That was not poetry. It was information. She had him count the seconds between a gust on the lake and the same gust in the grass, and the delay told them the change was still arriving, not already spent.\n\nThey turned the plan around while there was still time to do it politely. The high traverse could wait for a morning when the grass, the water, and the summits told the same quiet story. Instead they would drop to the lower path, lose the view, and keep the margin that let a person be wrong without being hurt. Nils folded the map along a new crease and did not argue. On the way down he kept glancing back, not in disappointment so much as in practice. Mara let him. A forecast was a letter written far away. The sky in front of them was the only correspondent that had to be answered before noon.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"veil","definition":"A thin layer that partly covers something, here the cloud."},{"word":"lenticular","definition":"Smooth and layered, like plates or lenses stacked in the sky."},{"word":"col","definition":"A high dip in a ridge between higher ground."},{"word":"rumour","definition":"A hint that is not yet solid enough to trust."},{"word":"margin","definition":"An extra allowance of safety if something goes wrong."},{"word":"correspondent","definition":"Someone who sends news; here, the sky that must be answered."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does \"lenticular\" describe in this story?","options":["Smooth cloud patches stacked over a summit","The new crease Nils folds into the map","A sharp drop on the way to the lower path","A gust counted between the lake and the grass"],"correctAnswer":"Smooth cloud patches stacked over a summit","explanation":"The word names the smooth, plate-like clouds sitting over the farthest summit.","supportingSentence":"Small lenticular patches sat over the farthest summit like stacked plates.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does Mara stop Nils from using the map first?","options":["The lake is the only safe place left to camp","The paper is wet and will tear in the wind","She wants the living sky to be heard before a printed line","Nils has already crossed this ridge once before"],"correctAnswer":"She wants the living sky to be heard before a printed line","explanation":"She makes him wait because the ridge will not keep a promise that was made on paper.","supportingSentence":"Weather arrived before a person had finished arguing with it, and the ridge they hoped to cross would not care what a printed line had promised the night before.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How old is the nephew Mara is teaching?","options":["Fourteen","Twenty-two","Sixteen","Nineteen"],"correctAnswer":"Sixteen","explanation":"The story says that Nils was sixteen.","supportingSentence":"Nils was sixteen, quick, and still in love with the idea that preparation was a list.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does the writer keep Mara from using the textbook name?","options":["To prove that the far summit is too distant to name","To suggest that Nils already knows the technical term","To put the warning ahead of a label he could memorise","To show that she has forgotten which cloud it is"],"correctAnswer":"To put the warning ahead of a label he could memorise","explanation":"She calls the clouds a reason to be humble, which matters more to her than adding another item to his list.","supportingSentence":"She called them a reason to be humble.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What can Mara reasonably hope after the walk down?","options":["Nils will keep watching the sky without being ordered","Nils will refuse every lower path from this day on","Nils will throw every plan away and never prepare again","Nils will trust only forecasts written far from the hill"],"correctAnswer":"Nils will keep watching the sky without being ordered","explanation":"His backward looks are practice rather than disappointment, so the habit of watching can continue without her pushing.","supportingSentence":"On the way down he kept glancing back, not in disappointment so much as in practice.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq21677$::jsonb,
  '821ffa56a0c353cd23c95147953746f11fa1d4a85af78ea706681f1f8c2ebf54',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

