  'text',
  $rbq4889${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq4889$::jsonb,
  $rbq75208${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"Replacing the Boiler","story":"The boiler failed on the coldest morning of the month, giving a short cough and then nothing. Elena rang the landlord from a kitchen cold enough to show her breath. Denis was not unkind, but he was slow to spend money, and the lease described the heating as his obligation only when a qualified person judged the appliance beyond economical repair. He told her to buy a small electric heater, keep the receipts, and wait for an engineer he had used before. If the visit showed a simple fault, he would authorise a repair the same day. Elena, whose younger child studied at the table in that room, said an interim heater would not warm the flat through another night of frost.\n\nThe engineer arrived after lunch, unwilling to guess before he had opened the casing. The heat exchanger was cracked, the flue seal had perished, and parts for that model were no longer supplied. A repair, he said, would be a false economy even if a second-hand component could be found. He wrote two figures on a single sheet. One covered a like-for-like replacement that would last a few winters. The other covered a more efficient unit whose running cost would be lower, though the installation would take an extra day and require a new condensate pipe. He left the sheet and noted that either choice was safer than nursing the old boiler through January.\n\nDenis, reading the figures that evening, favoured the cheaper option and suggested Elena contribute if she wanted the efficient model, since she would enjoy the lower bills. Elena answered that the lease did not ask a tenant to fund an upgrade, and that the flat had been marketed with working heat. She also sent the engineer's warning that delay would leave them with no heating at all. She bought one heater, moved the beds away from the outside wall, and kept a record of the hours it ran.\n\nOn the third day Denis agreed to the efficient unit, provided the engineer could start before the weekend and provided Elena accepted that the old boiler's scrap value would not be shared. She agreed at once. What stayed with her was the sequence rather than the machinery. If she had not pressed, the cheaper swap might have been ordered, and the lease would still have been satisfied. The document had set a floor, not a standard of care. Elena filed the sheet beside the tenancy agreement, in case the next failure invited the same argument about who should pay for more than the minimum.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"obligation","definition":"A duty that someone is required to meet"},{"word":"economical","definition":"Worth the money, rather than too costly for the result"},{"word":"interim","definition":"Temporary, until a proper solution is ready"},{"word":"efficient","definition":"Working with less waste"},{"word":"upgrade","definition":"A change to something newer or better"},{"word":"minimum","definition":"The smallest amount that is allowed or acceptable"}],"quiz":[{"question":"What faults did the engineer find in the old boiler?","options":["A missing heater and a window that would not shut","A scrap value Denis had forgotten to share","A cracked heat exchanger and a perished flue seal","A condensate pipe fitted on the wrong wall"],"correctAnswer":"A cracked heat exchanger and a perished flue seal","explanation":"The heat exchanger was cracked, the flue seal had perished, and parts were no longer supplied.","supportingSentence":"The heat exchanger was cracked, the flue seal had perished, and parts for that model were no longer supplied.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the first response to the two prices reveal about Denis?","options":["He reaches for the cheaper option that still covers his duty","He wants the engineer to choose without asking him","He prefers tenants to move out for the whole winter","He usually replaces equipment before it fails"],"correctAnswer":"He reaches for the cheaper option that still covers his duty","explanation":"He favoured the cheaper figure and suggested Elena pay extra if she wanted the better unit.","supportingSentence":"Denis, reading the figures that evening, favoured the cheaper option and suggested Elena contribute if she wanted the efficient model, since she would enjoy the lower bills.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why did waiting through January look like a poor plan?","options":["The cheaper unit could not be installed before spring","Denis had declared the boiler sound during the phone call","Elena had already purchased the efficient model herself","Either replacement was safer than keeping the failed boiler going"],"correctAnswer":"Either replacement was safer than keeping the failed boiler going","explanation":"The engineer said either choice was safer than nursing the old boiler through January.","supportingSentence":"He left the sheet and noted that either choice was safer than nursing the old boiler through January.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does interim describe when the cold flat is discussed?","options":["A heater meant only to bridge the gap","The duty written into the lease","The money raised by scrapping the old machine","The lower of the two figures on the sheet"],"correctAnswer":"A heater meant only to bridge the gap","explanation":"She says a stopgap heater will not warm the flat through another freezing night.","supportingSentence":"Elena, whose younger child studied at the table in that room, said an interim heater would not warm the flat through another night of frost.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer call the lease a floor rather than a standard of care?","options":["To prove that Denis had already broken the agreement","To argue that portable heaters are better than boilers","To show that the basic duty was less than the care Elena pushed for","To suggest the engineer should have picked the model alone"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the basic duty was less than the care Elena pushed for","explanation":"The cheaper replacement could still have satisfied the lease, which only set the lowest acceptable step.","supportingSentence":"The document had set a floor, not a standard of care.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq75208$::jsonb,
  '2e9223bbae3fe00a2f4f151ee999a977e6ad0d00f523141b82bde136a135e9e2',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '0d785ec8-adab-48ad-a5f5-87843dc144bd'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4562${"topic":"travel","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4562$::jsonb,
  $rbq6035${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"Rerouted through the Capital","story":"Soren Talve's flight towards the coastal conference was diverted before the crew had finished the second round of tea. A strike by baggage crews at the destination had closed that airport to incoming flights, and the announcement of a landing in Vessara, the capital, was delivered in a tone that filed inconvenience under ordinary procedure. Passengers would receive a voucher and a revised connection. Argument, the purser added, would not reopen a runway.\n\nThe terminal in Vessara was designed for people who already knew which exit belonged to them. Soren's replacement itinerary offered a morning train and then a regional flight that would set him down after both the panel and the dinner he had come for. He stood in the concourse and separated what could be delegated from what could not. The panel could pass to a colleague if the questions were written so cleanly that another voice could ask them without pretending to be him. The dinner could not be handed over, because a hesitant sponsor had agreed to listen there and might not agree twice. A railway clerk confirmed that an overnight train still had seats, though it would arrive beside a freight yard rather than at the main station. The difference mattered only if he was carrying more than one bag, and he was not. He chose the train.\n\nIn the queue for the voucher he drafted, on the back of a boarding card, three questions his colleague could ask in a voice that was plainly her own. He sent them while the concourse signal was still strong enough to be trusted. On the train he slept in pieces and woke to the industrial edge of the coastal city, where the cranes were still and the strike announced itself as a lack of trucks. He reached the venue as the dinner guests were finding their places. His shirt had travelled through two revisions of the plan, and he decided that changing it would be a costume rather than a courtesy. The sponsor listened. She asked one sharp question about maintenance costs and then a gentler one about whether the project could survive a year of delays like the strike. Soren answered both without the slides he had lost to the morning he no longer had. Afterwards he thought the diversion had pared the trip down to the conversation that had always been the point of going. The panel, he later heard, had gone smoothly in another person's mouth. What he would have regretted was missing the sponsor's second question, the one that asked whether the work could endure a disrupted year.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"diverted","definition":"Sent to a different place from the one planned."},{"word":"voucher","definition":"A paper or record that can be exchanged for a service."},{"word":"itinerary","definition":"A plan of the stages in a journey."},{"word":"concourse","definition":"A large open area in a station or airport."},{"word":"delegated","definition":"Given to another person to do."},{"word":"diversion","definition":"A change that sends someone away from the planned route."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What stopped incoming flights at the destination airport?","options":["The loss of Soren's boarding card","A strike by the baggage crews","Fog over the capital for the whole day","A full overnight train at the freight yard"],"correctAnswer":"A strike by the baggage crews","explanation":"Baggage crews were on strike, and that closed the destination airport to incoming flights.","supportingSentence":"A strike by baggage crews at the destination had closed that airport to incoming flights, and the announcement of a landing in Vessara, the capital, was delivered in a tone that filed inconvenience under ordinary procedure.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Soren's decision to take the overnight train reveal?","options":["He wanted to join the crews who had stopped work","He still hoped to chair the opening panel from the yard","He would accept a rough arrival in order to meet the sponsor","He preferred to miss dinner and keep his slides"],"correctAnswer":"He would accept a rough arrival in order to meet the sponsor","explanation":"The replacement connection would miss the dinner, and he treats that conversation as the part nobody else can hold for him.","supportingSentence":"The dinner could not be handed over, because a hesitant sponsor had agreed to listen there and might not agree twice.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What can a reader conclude about the sponsor from her questions?","options":["She would speak only to people who had kept their slides","She had come chiefly to deliver the opening panel","She already knew the three questions sent to the colleague","She was ready to test the project against real disruption"],"correctAnswer":"She was ready to test the project against real disruption","explanation":"She asks whether the project could survive a year of delays like the strike, testing it against the trouble Soren has just lived through.","supportingSentence":"She asked one sharp question about maintenance costs and then a gentler one about whether the project could survive a year of delays like the strike.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does itinerary mean in the account of Soren's new route?","options":["The revised plan for how he would continue","The written script of the opening panel","The menu for the conference dinner","The strike notice pinned up at the airport"],"correctAnswer":"The revised plan for how he would continue","explanation":"His replacement itinerary is the new arrangement of train and flight in place of the original journey.","supportingSentence":"Soren's replacement itinerary offered a morning train and then a regional flight that would set him down after both the panel and the dinner he had come for.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include Soren's choice to keep the same shirt?","options":["To suggest the voucher paid for a hotel laundry","To show he valued the meeting above a neater appearance","To explain the sponsor's question about maintenance","To prove the freight yard had no washroom"],"correctAnswer":"To show he valued the meeting above a neater appearance","explanation":"He decides that changing would be a costume rather than a courtesy, so the detail marks his priorities before he meets the sponsor.","supportingSentence":"His shirt had travelled through two revisions of the plan, and he decided that changing it would be a costume rather than a courtesy.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq6035$::jsonb,
  '0d785ec8adaba8ad75f587843dc144bd070c4b308d91ebd358c483b208cae150',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '0fb98173-9cb8-466f-a6e6-01e7695bba77'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4637${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq4637$::jsonb,
  $rbq63073${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"Restoring a Wall Clock","story":"Edith Lang lifted the wall clock down from the stairwell of the rooming house because it had started to strike each hour twice. The second strike was not an echo people could ignore, since two tenants had already left for work an hour early. The casing was walnut, darkened by hands and by years of smoke the present landlord insisted belonged to a previous generation. She carried the clock to her bench in a blanket. A pendulum rod will take a bend from one careless corner, and she had no wish to repair a damage she had caused herself.\n\nShe worked in the evenings, after her shift at the clinic. The movement was grimy more than broken. She cleaned the wheels with a solvent whose smell she kept out of the kitchen, checked the pivot holes for uneven wear, and found the fault in the striking train. A worn pin allowed the rack hook to catch a second time. She made a replacement of the proper diameter and polished it until it seated without play. When she oiled the pivots she did so lightly, because an excess would collect fresh grit and recreate the drag she had just removed. The faded numerals on the dial offered a different temptation. Repainting them would photograph as restoration and would also erase the clock's own age. Edith left the dial alone. A colleague at the clinic, if shown a photograph, might ask whether the numbers had been too difficult to match. She could have matched them. Matching them was the sort of help the clock had not requested. The tenants needed one truthful hour, not a face that looked newly printed.\n\nGetting the rate right took three further nights. A clock of that age did not respond well to a modern demand for the exact second. It wanted a rate a household could trust across a week of ordinary mornings. She turned the pendulum bob a little at a time and wrote each day's gain or loss on a card propped against the lamp. On the fourth evening she carried the clock upstairs and hung it on the old nail, which had polished a pale crescent into the wood. The first strike was single. A tenant on the landing nodded, as if a shared nuisance had been settled without a meeting. Edith kept the worn pin in a tin in her bench drawer. It no longer belonged to the clock, yet it explained why she had been asked to intervene. She distrusted any restoration that discarded its evidence in the name of a clean result.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"casing","definition":"The outer box that holds and protects a mechanism."},{"word":"pendulum","definition":"A hanging weight that swings to regulate a clock."},{"word":"solvent","definition":"A liquid used to dissolve and clean away dirt."},{"word":"pivot","definition":"A point on which a wheel or other part turns."},{"word":"diameter","definition":"The width of a circle, measured straight across its centre."},{"word":"restoration","definition":"Work that brings an old object back towards its earlier appearance or condition."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What was the clock doing that disturbed the house?","options":["Refusing to strike at all","Keeping perfect time with a new face","Chiming only on the fourth evening","Striking each hour a second time"],"correctAnswer":"Striking each hour a second time","explanation":"Edith took the clock down because it had started to strike each hour twice.","supportingSentence":"Edith Lang lifted the wall clock down from the stairwell of the rooming house because it had started to strike each hour twice.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Edith's decision about the numerals suggest she values?","options":["The clock's real age more than a newly restored look","The landlord's account of who used to smoke","Finishing the repair before she understands the fault","A photograph impressive enough for the clinic wall"],"correctAnswer":"The clock's real age more than a newly restored look","explanation":"Repainting would look like restoration and would erase the clock's age, so she leaves the dial as it is.","supportingSentence":"Repainting them would photograph as restoration and would also erase the clock's own age.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the saved pin reveal about Edith's view of a good repair?","options":["Evidence matters only when a landlord requests it","It should keep the clue that explains the original fault","Every old part should be thrown away the same night","The pin ought to go back in if anyone complains"],"correctAnswer":"It should keep the clue that explains the original fault","explanation":"She keeps the worn pin because it shows why she was called, and she distrusts work that throws such evidence away.","supportingSentence":"She distrusted any restoration that discarded its evidence in the name of a clean result.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is the solvent used for in Edith's workshop?","options":["Darkening the walnut casing","Painting fresh numerals on the dial","Removing grime from the wheels","Scenting the blanket around the clock"],"correctAnswer":"Removing grime from the wheels","explanation":"She cleaned the wheels with a solvent, so the word names the liquid she used to remove dirt.","supportingSentence":"She cleaned the wheels with a solvent whose smell she kept out of the kitchen, checked the pivot holes for uneven wear, and found the fault in the striking train.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention the tenants who left for work an hour early?","options":["To suggest Edith herself was late for the clinic","To explain the smell of smoke in the casing","To prove the old nail had been moved","To show that the fault was disturbing daily life in the house"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the fault was disturbing daily life in the house","explanation":"The second strike had already sent people out at the wrong time, so the repair matters to ordinary mornings, not only to the mechanism.","supportingSentence":"The second strike was not an echo people could ignore, since two tenants had already left for work an hour early.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq63073$::jsonb,
  '0fb981739cb8866f66e601e7695bba776a8bd0a06dc54e897aaf01d7fc629e76',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'df23ff97-c9d9-42a1-a0a3-3a10c9b857db'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4993${"topic":"technology","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq4993$::jsonb,
  $rbq75538${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"Retiring the Old Server","story":"Everyone in the library called the booking machine the kettle, because of the fan. It had been installed as a temporary host for room reservations nine years earlier, during a renovation that was supposed to end with a proper move. The move happened. The machine stayed, on a shelf in the plant room, running a system that the new calendar had officially replaced. The facilities manager wanted it unplugged before the electrical inspection in May. A retired server, he said, should not be drawing current and heat in a cupboard that failed its last check for clutter.\n\nThe systems librarian, Idris Pell, agreed that the original function was finished. Patrons reserved rooms on the new calendar. What he doubted was the claim that the machine now did only that. No one had written the dependencies down. The install notes had left with a contractor, and the staff who remembered the renovation had mostly retired. Pell spent a week watching what still called the old host. A script on it built the Saturday club's mailing list from a table the new calendar did not contain. Another task copied closed-day exceptions to the door signs. Neither job appeared in the project that had justified the machine, and both would fail quietly if the plug were pulled on the announced date.\n\nHe asked for a parallel run rather than a ceremonial switch-off. For three Saturdays the club list would be produced twice, once by the old script and once by a replacement he would write against the new calendar, and the two lists would be compared before anything was sent. The door-sign task would be rebuilt in the same interval. Only when both outputs matched, or when the differences were understood and accepted, would he call the cutover done. The manager disliked the delay. Pell disliked discovering a public mailing list by watching who complained after a silent failure. A parallel run costs three Saturdays. A wrong list costs the trust of people who come only because the message arrived.\n\nUnder the keyboard he found a sticker with a home number for the colleague who had set the script up. The call confirmed what the traffic had suggested: the list was not a leftover test, and several members received no other notice of a cancelled class. Pell added that fact to the decommission sheet. Retiring the machine was still the right end. The interim was for naming every side effect the official replacement had never been told to cover, and for refusing to learn those names from a dark Saturday.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"dependencies","definition":"Other tasks that still rely on a system and may fail with it."},{"word":"parallel","definition":"Running the old method and the new one at the same time for comparison."},{"word":"cutover","definition":"The switch from an old system to the one that replaces it."},{"word":"decommission","definition":"The formal process of taking a machine out of service."},{"word":"interim","definition":"The in-between period before the final change is made."},{"word":"ceremonial","definition":"Done for show, without a practical test of what would break."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What produced the Saturday club’s mailing list?","options":["The new calendar, which had officially replaced room booking","The electrical inspection scheduled for May","A door sign that staff rewrote by hand each closed day","A script on the old host, drawing on a table the new calendar lacked"],"correctAnswer":"A script on the old host, drawing on a table the new calendar lacked","explanation":"A script on the old machine built the list from a table the new calendar did not contain.","supportingSentence":"A script on it built the Saturday club's mailing list from a table the new calendar did not contain.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What has the facilities manager missed in wanting the machine unplugged for the inspection?","options":["Tasks that still run on it and were never part of the project that justified it.","A contractor who holds the only key to the plant room.","Patrons who still reserve every room on the old booking screen.","A duty to keep the noisy fan running until May is over."],"correctAnswer":"Tasks that still run on it and were never part of the project that justified it.","explanation":"Hidden jobs still depend on the machine, so unplugging it would break more than the retired booking function.","supportingSentence":"Neither job appeared in the project that had justified the machine, and both would fail quietly if the plug were pulled on the announced date.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the need to call a number found under the keyboard suggest about the library’s records?","options":["The new calendar already stored the closed-day table.","Knowledge of how the script was set up lived with a former colleague, not in notes the library still held.","The keyboard was the only part of the machine the inspection would permit.","The Saturday club had been typing the list by hand for nine years."],"correctAnswer":"Knowledge of how the script was set up lived with a former colleague, not in notes the library still held.","explanation":"The install notes were gone, and Pell had to call the person who had set the script up.","supportingSentence":"Under the keyboard he found a sticker with a home number for the colleague who had set the script up.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what is the “cutover”?","options":["The electrical inspection of the plant-room cupboard.","The three Saturdays during which two lists are still being produced.","The switch to the replacement, made only after the outputs are compared.","The sticker that keeps a former colleague’s phone number."],"correctAnswer":"The switch to the replacement, made only after the outputs are compared.","explanation":"Pell will call the cutover done only when the old and new outputs match or their differences are accepted.","supportingSentence":"Only when both outputs matched, or when the differences were understood and accepted, would he call the cutover done.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage set the cost of three Saturdays against the cost of a wrong list?","options":["To argue that the Saturday club should be closed.","To show that the facilities manager wants a slower inspection.","To recommend that every library notice be sent on paper.","To justify the delay as protection for people who rely on the message."],"correctAnswer":"To justify the delay as protection for people who rely on the message.","explanation":"The contrast asks the reader to see the wait as the price of not breaking trust with people who depend on the list.","supportingSentence":"A wrong list costs the trust of people who come only because the message arrived.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq75538$::jsonb,
  'df23ff97c9d9f2a1a0a33a10c9b857db357ab1f3a9aebd48f338160dc644d3ad',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'f1fa3809-e280-44a2-adc3-cbcdcd85d23e'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4639${"topic":"hobbies","wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq4639$::jsonb,
  $rbq79537${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Hobbies","title":"The Amateur Observatory","story":"Ivo Marsh kept his telescope in a shed he had insulated himself, on a rise above Loden where the streetlamps stopped before the track did. He was an amateur, and he said so before anyone could mistake the plywood roof for the property of an institute. On clear nights he opened a slit in that roof, set his clock by a radio time signal, and entered the sky with the patience of a clerk balancing a book. The programme came from a public handbook, amended until it suited one observer and a second-hand mirror.\n\nThat week he was timing the fades of a variable star the handbook marked as suitable for a modest aperture. The task depended on comparison. He looked at the target, then at two neighbouring stars whose brightness was already established, and he wrote a figure he was prepared to defend an hour later. Frost worked its way along the metal of the mount. He stopped to warm his hands and to record a drift in the drive that would ruin the sequence if he pretended it was still true. Calibration, for him, was less a ritual than a willingness to admit that the instrument had wandered.\n\nA neighbour's son came up the track with a homework question about planets and hovered at the door. Ivo usually sent visitors away. He let this one stay because the boy had brought a pencil and an empty page, which suggested he expected to work rather than to be amazed. Ivo allowed a look at a bright planet, then required a second look and a judgment about which of two stars was brighter. The boy chose the larger-looking one and was wrong. The correction pleased them both more than the first sight had, because it replaced a guess with a reason. When the boy left, Ivo returned to the variable and found that one estimate in the series was missing. He wrote the gap into the logbook instead of inventing a number that would make the row look complete. By dawn the page showed an honest interruption. He locked the shed satisfied, not because the night had been full, but because he had refused to tidy the record into a lie.\n\nOutside, the town was beginning its ordinary noise, and the stars were already losing their authority to it. Ivo did not mind. The handbook had never promised spectacle. It had promised that a careful person, working within the limits of a small mirror, could add a line that another careful person might trust. The missing estimate, left visible, was part of that promise.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"aperture","definition":"The opening in a telescope that gathers light."},{"word":"drift","definition":"A slow unintended movement away from the right position."},{"word":"calibration","definition":"Checking an instrument and admitting when it is no longer accurate."},{"word":"variable","definition":"Changing in brightness rather than staying constant."},{"word":"logbook","definition":"A book in which observations are recorded as they happen."},{"word":"spectacle","definition":"An impressive sight meant to amaze people."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where did Ivo keep the telescope?","options":["In the boy's kitchen beside the handbook","In a public institute with a clerk","In a shed he had insulated above Loden","On a streetlamp at the end of the track"],"correctAnswer":"In a shed he had insulated above Loden","explanation":"He kept the telescope in a shed he had insulated, on a rise above Loden.","supportingSentence":"Ivo Marsh kept his telescope in a shed he had insulated himself, on a rise above Loden where the streetlamps stopped before the track did.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How does Ivo's handling of the boy echo his own way of observing?","options":["He refuses every question and sends the boy home at once","He wants a judgment that can be checked, not mere wonder","He tells the boy to invent a figure and fill the gap","He decides brightness by which object looks bigger"],"correctAnswer":"He wants a judgment that can be checked, not mere wonder","explanation":"Ivo's own work depends on a comparison he can defend, and he makes the boy compare two stars rather than simply admire the view.","supportingSentence":"Ivo allowed a look at a bright planet, then required a second look and a judgment about which of two stars was brighter.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why is the gap in the logbook more trustworthy than a completed row?","options":["A later reader can see where the evidence actually stops","The boy agreed to copy the missing number at school","Frost makes any full set of figures impossible","The handbook pays a higher fee for shorter notes"],"correctAnswer":"A later reader can see where the evidence actually stops","explanation":"Ivo leaves the missing estimate visible instead of inventing a number, so another person can trust the line he did record.","supportingSentence":"He wrote the gap into the logbook instead of inventing a number that would make the row look complete.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does calibration mean for Ivo in this story?","options":["A public ceremony held at the institute","Copying a finished page from the handbook","The boy's guess about the larger-looking star","Admitting that the instrument had ceased to be exact"],"correctAnswer":"Admitting that the instrument had ceased to be exact","explanation":"For him, calibration means being willing to say the instrument has wandered, rather than carrying on as if it had not.","supportingSentence":"Calibration, for him, was less a ritual than a willingness to admit that the instrument had wandered.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer bring the neighbour's son up to the shed?","options":["To explain how the plywood roof was insulated","To prove that planets outshine every variable star","To see whether Ivo's standards hold when a person interrupts the work","To replace the handbook with a school exercise"],"correctAnswer":"To see whether Ivo's standards hold when a person interrupts the work","explanation":"The visit leaves a gap in the series, and Ivo's choice to record that gap shows the standard surviving the interruption.","supportingSentence":"When the boy left, Ivo returned to the variable and found that one estimate in the series was missing.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq79537$::jsonb,
  'f1fa3809e28064a2fdc3cbcdcd85d23e1012392db03b9518b69ecabcd8df242f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

