  $rbq4701${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq4701$::jsonb,
  $rbq96027${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"The Archive after Hours","story":"The municipal archive closed to the public at five, but the reading room kept its lamps on for staff until the night warden walked the aisles at eight. Lena was still at the enquiry desk, finishing a list of files requested for the morning, when she noticed the trolley. It stood halfway to the loading bay, stacked with boxes that belonged in the strong room. She had seen those boxes at four, on the locked side of the inner door, because a researcher had asked for one and she had refused, politely, until the curator returned from a meeting.\n\nShe checked the door. It was shut, and the indicator showed it locked, which meant someone had opened it and closed it again in the hour she had spent on the telephone. The night log, kept on a clipboard beside the bay, carried a signature in the slot for the six o'clock internal transfer. The name was the duty archivist's. The archivist had left at half past four, and had said so twice, because Lena was supposed to set the alarm. The signature leaned the wrong way. The letters were the right ones. The hand was not. Lena copied the odd signature into her notebook so the clipboard could not be the only record.\n\nLena did not follow the trolley to the bay, and she did not call out. She photographed the log, the trolley, and the strong-room indicator with the desk phone, then wheeled the boxes back herself, slowly, so the oldest files would not slip. One box was lighter than it should have been. Its label promised the planning papers for a footbridge that the town had argued about for years. Inside, the folder for the final year of the argument was missing. The earlier years were there, neat, tied, and undisturbed, which somehow made the gap more deliberate.\n\nShe wrote a note for the curator and stayed until the warden arrived, sitting where she could see the bay door. No one came for the trolley. In the morning the curator compared the photograph of the signature with the archivist's real hand and agreed they did not match. The missing folder was not in the reading room, the store, or the desk drawers. The alarm had been set by Lena at eight, and the system showed no opening after that. Whoever had moved the boxes had done it while the building was still, officially, in the care of people who worked there, and had left the log looking busy enough that a tired colleague might have nodded and gone home.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"archive","definition":"A place where official records are kept."},{"word":"curator","definition":"The person in charge of a collection."},{"word":"indicator","definition":"A sign that shows a condition, here whether a door is locked."},{"word":"signature","definition":"A person's name written by hand."},{"word":"deliberate","definition":"Done on purpose, not by accident."},{"word":"warden","definition":"A person who checks a building after hours."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Which part of the footbridge papers was gone from the lighter box?","options":["The night log from the loading bay","The curator's notes from the afternoon meeting","Every year of the planning papers","The folder for the final year of the argument"],"correctAnswer":"The folder for the final year of the argument","explanation":"The box was light because the folder for the final year of the argument was missing. Earlier years were still inside.","supportingSentence":"Inside, the folder for the final year of the argument was missing.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do the six o'clock signature and the archivist's departure suggest?","options":["Lena signed his name with his permission.","Someone other than the duty archivist wrote that name.","The curator moved the boxes before the meeting.","The archivist returned at six and signed in a hurry."],"correctAnswer":"Someone other than the duty archivist wrote that name.","explanation":"The archivist had already left, and the curator later agreed that the signature did not match his real hand.","supportingSentence":"In the morning the curator compared the photograph of the signature with the archivist's real hand and agreed they did not match.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does Lena photograph the scene before she returns the boxes?","options":["She hopes the desk phone will unlock the strong room.","She needs the pictures for the researcher who asked for a file.","She wants a record of what she found, in case the scene is later tidied away.","She is about to wheel the trolley out through the loading bay."],"correctAnswer":"She wants a record of what she found, in case the scene is later tidied away.","explanation":"She photographs the log, the trolley, and the lock indicator, and she also copies the signature so the clipboard will not be the only record. Then she puts the boxes back.","supportingSentence":"She photographed the log, the trolley, and the strong-room indicator with the desk phone, then wheeled the boxes back herself, slowly, so the oldest files would not slip.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does deliberate say about the gap in the papers?","options":["The gap was made on purpose, not by accident","The box was heavier than its label","The reading room was still open to the public","The night warden had written the label"],"correctAnswer":"The gap was made on purpose, not by accident","explanation":"The earlier years were neat and untouched, which makes the missing final year look deliberate: done on purpose.","supportingSentence":"The earlier years were there, neat, tied, and undisturbed, which somehow made the gap more deliberate.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer end with a busy-looking log and a tired colleague?","options":["To blame Lena for setting the alarm too early","To show that the footbridge was never discussed","To recommend that the archive close at noon","To suggest the mover wanted the change to pass as ordinary staff work"],"correctAnswer":"To suggest the mover wanted the change to pass as ordinary staff work","explanation":"The ending stresses that the boxes were moved during staff hours and that the log was left looking busy enough to satisfy a tired colleague.","supportingSentence":"Whoever had moved the boxes had done it while the building was still, officially, in the care of people who worked there, and had left the log looking busy enough that a tired colleague might have nodded and gone home.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq96027$::jsonb,
  '3810e8f7277fde032fc2c26e4963e1705ed02ce17616afd83d155b170d155c9f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ec0501da-60b5-4ccc-acdf-8a3afc2178e3'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5380${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq5380$::jsonb,
  $rbq74487${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Archive Placement","story":"Sofia's placement was in the municipal archive, a quiet building behind the library. Her supervisor, Tomas, spent the first morning on the handling rules. He showed her gloves for the photographs, weights rather than fingers to hold a page open, and a ban on water near any box. The collection she was to catalogue had arrived from a local clinic that had closed, and the donor agreement set a restriction on anything that could identify a patient. Names could be recorded in the private accession file but were not to appear in the public list.\n\nThe work was slow. Sofia learned to describe a letter without quoting the line that made it interesting, and to note a date only when the date was written on the item. Tomas checked a sample each afternoon. He was strict about consistency and surprisingly kind about pace, saying that a careful description saved a later researcher from handling the paper twice. It felt like translation, turning a fragile object into a record that would let someone find it without wearing it out.\n\nThen she nearly broke the rule she had started to admire. A letter from the clinic's matron discussed a donation of blankets and, in a postscript, named a child who had stayed on the ward through a winter. The story was moving, and the name seemed harmless after so many decades. Sofia typed it into the public description, thinking a single name would help a family historian and would not violate the spirit of the restriction. Tomas, reading over her shoulder, asked her to stop and to delete the line before the record was saved. The agreement, he said, did not become optional because a case felt sympathetic or because the people concerned were likely to be dead. If a descendant searched that name, the archive would have published a medical stay that the donor had tried to keep off the open shelves.\n\nSofia rewrote the entry so that the blankets remained and the child became an unnamed patient. She also added a cross-reference in the private file, where the name could be retrieved by staff if a formal request justified it. Tomas did not treat the mistake as a scandal, but he did ask her to reread the donor clause and to mark every future postscript before she summarised it. Discretion, she realised, was not a mood. It was a procedure that had to survive the moments when a story seemed too human to leave incomplete. By the end of the placement she could catalogue a box without hoping it would make her interesting.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"catalogue","definition":"To describe and list items so they can be found"},{"word":"restriction","definition":"A rule that limits what may be shown or used"},{"word":"accession","definition":"The private record made when new material enters a collection"},{"word":"fragile","definition":"Easily damaged, and needing careful handling"},{"word":"discretion","definition":"Careful judgement about what should be left unsaid"},{"word":"sympathetic","definition":"Arousing pity or fellow feeling"}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why does Tomas stop the public description from being saved?","options":["A family historian had already reserved that file","A name would have revealed a stay the donor wanted kept private","The blankets were too damaged to be listed at all","The matron's letter had no date written on the page"],"correctAnswer":"A name would have revealed a stay the donor wanted kept private","explanation":"The agreement did not become optional just because the case felt sympathetic or the people were likely dead.","supportingSentence":"The agreement, he said, did not become optional because a case felt sympathetic or because the people concerned were likely to be dead.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Sofia decide that discretion actually is?","options":["A public list of every name found in a box","A feeling that depends on how moving a letter is","A procedure that holds even when a story feels unfinished","A glove worn while turning the pages"],"correctAnswer":"A procedure that holds even when a story feels unfinished","explanation":"She realises it is a procedure that has to survive the moments when a story seems too human to leave incomplete.","supportingSentence":"It was a procedure that had to survive the moments when a story seemed too human to leave incomplete.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Where could a patient's name be recorded?","options":["On the public list of the collection","On the sign-in sheet by the library door","On the label inside the photograph gloves","In the private file for new material"],"correctAnswer":"In the private file for new material","explanation":"Names could go in the private accession file, but not on the public list.","supportingSentence":"Names could be recorded in the private accession file but were not to appear in the public list.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the child's name seem acceptable to Sofia at first?","options":["So much time had passed that she thought the harm had faded","The clinic had donated blankets and nothing else","The matron had signed a fresh agreement that morning","Tomas had asked her to make the public list more personal"],"correctAnswer":"So much time had passed that she thought the harm had faded","explanation":"The story was moving, and the name seemed harmless after so many decades.","supportingSentence":"The story was moving, and the name seemed harmless after so many decades.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer begin with gloves, weights, and a ban on water?","options":["To argue that names belong on the open shelves","To show that protecting the objects is part of the work itself","To prove that the clinic papers were already ruined","To suggest Sofia should have turned the placement down"],"correctAnswer":"To show that protecting the objects is part of the work itself","explanation":"The handling rules introduce the idea that care is a set of practices, not a feeling added later.","supportingSentence":"He showed her gloves for the photographs, weights rather than fingers to hold a page open, and a ban on water near any box.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq74487$::jsonb,
  'ec0501da60b54cccdcdf8a3afc2178e3e26a3ef6d4095b46adb61e8c9e04ad3d',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'e96a1a08-5928-4ccd-a466-d7ca261463bd'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4664${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq4664$::jsonb,
  $rbq44588${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The Cartographer Who Would Not Finish","story":"The cartographer's table faced a window that refused to show the same street twice. In the morning the bakery stood on the corner. By afternoon the corner belonged to a fountain, and the bakery had moved itself, without apology, three doors to the left. People in the city were used to this. They gave directions by habit rather than by landmark, and they laughed when visitors arrived holding a map that had already expired. Only the cartographer suffered. His profession required him to finish, and the city required him not to.\n\nHis name was Osric, and he had ink under his nails in every season. He drew coastlines that stayed still, because the sea, for all its temper, kept its edge. He drew the mountains, which were vain and motionless. When he came to the city, the lines began to wander. A street completed in the morning would, by the time the ink dried, have sprouted a lane he had not been told about. If he ignored the lane, a child would later fall into the gap between the paper and the world. He had seen it happen once, a bruised knee and a furious mother, and he had never again pretended that a map was only a picture. He slept badly when a street changed under a finished line.\n\nThe council offered him a fee to produce one final chart, framed, signed, and hung in the hall where laws were read. A finished map, they said, would let them tax the houses and number the doors. Osric worked until the last blank was a single square beside the river, a place the city used for arrivals that had not decided what they were. Markets began there. Funerals sometimes ended there. On certain evenings the square was water, and boats knocked softly against steps that did not exist at noon. To close that square with a line would be to choose one of its truths and exile the others.\n\nHe left the square unfinished and returned the fee in a cloth bag. The council called the act a refusal. Osric called it a measurement. A map that could not bear an empty space was too small for a city that lived by changing its mind. He kept the incomplete sheet above his table, and visitors learned to read the blank as carefully as the ink. When a stranger asked where to go, Osric would tap the unmarked square and say that the city would meet them there, provided they did not insist on knowing, in advance, which city it was going to be.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"cartographer","definition":"A person whose work is drawing maps."},{"word":"expired","definition":"No longer valid because the thing it describes has changed."},{"word":"sprouted","definition":"Grown suddenly where nothing had been drawn."},{"word":"exile","definition":"To force something out and refuse it a place."},{"word":"incomplete","definition":"Not finished, with a part deliberately left open."},{"word":"measurement","definition":"A careful judgement of size, value, or truth."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why does the writer include the child's fall?","options":["To suggest the mother wanted the bakery moved","To explain why Osric stopped drawing coastlines","To show that a missing map line can injure a living person","To prove the council had already cancelled his fee"],"correctAnswer":"To show that a missing map line can injure a living person","explanation":"The bruised knee stops a map from being only a picture and makes the empty spaces matter.","supportingSentence":"He had seen it happen once, a bruised knee and a furious mother, and he had never again pretended that a map was only a picture.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What did the council want a finished map for?","options":["Taxing houses and numbering the doors","Opening a new harbour under the mountains","Moving the fountain back beside the bakery","Teaching visitors to ignore every street"],"correctAnswer":"Taxing houses and numbering the doors","explanation":"They say a completed map would let them tax the houses and number the doors.","supportingSentence":"A finished map, they said, would let them tax the houses and number the doors.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does giving the fee back reveal about Osric?","options":["He hopes the council will offer twice the money","He wants the cloth bag hung in the law hall","He will not be paid to pretend the city has settled","He has lost his ink and can no longer draw"],"correctAnswer":"He will not be paid to pretend the city has settled","explanation":"He returns the money because he leaves the square blank rather than force the city to hold still.","supportingSentence":"He left the square unfinished and returned the fee in a cloth bag.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"expired\" mean about a visitor's map?","options":["It no longer agrees with streets that have moved","It shows only the sea and the still mountains","It has been signed and framed by the council","It was drawn with ink that never dried"],"correctAnswer":"It no longer agrees with streets that have moved","explanation":"People laugh because a finished map goes out of date as soon as the streets change.","supportingSentence":"They gave directions by habit rather than by landmark, and they laughed when visitors arrived holding a map that had already expired.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What do visitors learn from the sheet above the table?","options":["The city can be entered only through the bakery","The ink becomes too faint to follow after noon","The empty square deserves as much attention as the ink","Osric has hidden the true map inside the fee bag"],"correctAnswer":"The empty square deserves as much attention as the ink","explanation":"They learn to read the blank as carefully as the drawn lines, so the gap itself is information.","supportingSentence":"He kept the incomplete sheet above his table, and visitors learned to read the blank as carefully as the ink.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq44588$::jsonb,
  'e96a1a0859284ccde466d7ca261463bddc1db301e846797f5e4f854c203154b6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '6989f9a2-896b-4ab7-ac7a-2206f9eb9117'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5316${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq5316$::jsonb,
  $rbq75801${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"The Clinic Waiting Room","story":"The waiting room at the sports clinic had been designed to look unlike a hospital, which only made the magazines and the pale blue chairs seem more deliberate. Jonah Hale, a rower with the national development squad, sat with his scan envelope unopened on his knee. Across from him an older woman in a faded club jacket worked through a crossword and did not, at first, look up. Her name, he later learned, was Esther Lind, and she had come for a shoulder that had argued with thirty years of fencing. Neither of them was bleeding. Both of them had arranged the day around a professional opinion.\n\nWhen the receptionist stepped away, Esther nodded at the envelope. People hold paper like that, she said, when they already know the sentence and are waiting to see whether the doctor will soften it. Jonah almost denied it. The team doctor had spoken of a stress response, then of a possible fracture, and had booked the scan before Jonah could finish explaining that selection camp began on Monday. He had told his coach he would have results by Friday. It was Friday, and the envelope felt heavier than the pictures inside it.\n\nEsther said she had no wish to become his coach, which made the rest easier to hear. A camp, she told him, can be postponed by a person and cannot be postponed by a bone. If he travelled on a compromised bone, he would spend the next season explaining a brave week. If he stayed and the scan was kinder than he feared, he would lose a camp and keep a career. She returned to her crossword as if she had commented on the weather. Jonah noticed that her left hand rested under the elbow of the sore arm, supporting it without display.\n\nHis name was called before hers. In the consulting room the doctor confirmed a stress fracture of the rib, small, manageable, and wholly incompatible with a week of seat racing. The plan was rest, breathing practice that did not load the bone, and a review in three weeks. There would be no heroics, the doctor added, because small cracks become lost seasons. Jonah asked for wording he could send his coach. The doctor wrote it down, plain enough to prevent a creative interpretation. Back in the waiting room Esther was being led through the other door. She lifted the crossword in a brief salute. On the bus Jonah typed the message he had been postponing since dawn, and for the first time the missing camp felt like a decision rather than a theft.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"deliberate","definition":"Done on purpose, rather than by accident."},{"word":"compromised","definition":"Already weakened and unsafe to load."},{"word":"incompatible","definition":"Impossible to combine with something else."},{"word":"heroics","definition":"Bold efforts that ignore a medical limit."},{"word":"interpretation","definition":"A personal reading of what words allow."},{"word":"fracture","definition":"A crack in a bone."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What injury did the doctor confirm after looking at the scan?","options":["A broken shoulder from fencing","No injury, only ordinary tiredness","A small stress fracture in a rib","A torn muscle in the knee"],"correctAnswer":"A small stress fracture in a rib","explanation":"The doctor confirmed a small stress fracture of the rib and said it could not be combined with a week of seat racing.","supportingSentence":"In the consulting room the doctor confirmed a stress fracture of the rib, small, manageable, and wholly incompatible with a week of seat racing.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the unopened envelope weigh so heavily on Jonah before the appointment?","options":["He already feared the result would keep him out of selection camp","He knew the pictures belonged to another athlete","Esther had told him the shoulder injury was his","He was ashamed of being seen with a crossword"],"correctAnswer":"He already feared the result would keep him out of selection camp","explanation":"A possible fracture had already been mentioned, camp began on Monday, and he had promised his coach results by Friday.","supportingSentence":"The team doctor had spoken of a stress response, then of a possible fracture, and had booked the scan before Jonah could finish explaining that selection camp began on Monday.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What comparison is Esther asking Jonah to make?","options":["Friday's bus timetable against the receptionist's list","His rowing titles against her years of fencing","The cost of a missed camp against the cost of loading a damaged bone","The crossword against the magazines on the table"],"correctAnswer":"The cost of a missed camp against the cost of loading a damaged bone","explanation":"She sets a brave week on a damaged bone, which could cost a season, against losing a camp but keeping a career.","supportingSentence":"If he stayed and the scan was kinder than he feared, he would lose a camp and keep a career.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does incompatible say about the rib injury and seat racing?","options":["They could not safely be combined that week","Seat racing was the treatment the doctor chose","The fracture had already healed","Jonah was being asked to leave the squad"],"correctAnswer":"They could not safely be combined that week","explanation":"The fracture was manageable, but a week of seat racing could not be done at the same time.","supportingSentence":"In the consulting room the doctor confirmed a stress fracture of the rib, small, manageable, and wholly incompatible with a week of seat racing.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer include the plain wording the doctor wrote for the coach?","options":["To suggest the crack was too small to mention","To show that the message had to be hard for a coach to twist","To explain why the clinic had no receptionist","To prove that Esther had written the medical plan"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the message had to be hard for a coach to twist","explanation":"Jonah asks for words he can send, and the doctor writes them plainly so they cannot be creatively reinterpreted.","supportingSentence":"The doctor wrote it down, plain enough to prevent a creative interpretation.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq75801$::jsonb,
  '6989f9a2896bbab7ac7a2206f9eb91171ac42849aef4f296c0897cd5a8bfac19',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ef5aea07-7ef2-4d1b-ad02-6a538b8c4c7d'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5491${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq5491$::jsonb,
  $rbq3437${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"The Cooperative Accounts","story":"When the bakery cooperative closed its books in March, the surplus looked healthier than anyone in the workroom had expected. Nia, who kept the ledger after her shifts at the oven, did not trust the figure. Sales had been steady, flour had grown more expensive, and two delivery vans had needed repairs that the members still discussed in the yard. A generous surplus, arriving just before the annual meeting, felt less like luck than like a story the numbers had been asked to tell.\n\nShe began with a reconciliation of the member accounts. Several colleagues had lent the cooperative money during a thin winter, on the understanding that the loans would be repaid before any dividend was declared. In the spreadsheet the outgoing treasurer had circulated, those loans appeared as ordinary sales to unnamed catering clients. The cash was real. The description was not. She also noted which meeting had approved each loan, so the circle could see that the debt was not a private arrangement. Each invented sale reduced the recorded debt and enlarged the surplus by the same amount. Nia marked the lines in pencil and then, because pencil could be dismissed, copied them into a separate notebook with the dates, the amounts, and the names of the members who were still owed money.\n\nAt the finance circle on Thursday she did not accuse anyone of theft. She set the notebook beside the printed accounts and asked the circle to read both. The outgoing treasurer said he had been trying to present the cooperative as solvent to a supplier who wanted reassurance before extending credit. The loans, he argued, were internal and would only worry an outsider. Mara, who had lent four hundred pounds in January and had not been repaid, asked whether a dividend built on unpaid internal debt was a dividend at all. The room went quiet in the particular way of people who are counting, not sulking.\n\nThey voted to postpone any distribution and to commission an independent examination of the year, paid from the operating budget rather than from anyone's pocket. Nia was asked to remain as bookkeeper while the examination took place, with a second member signing every transfer. The loans would be restored to the ledger as liabilities before the next meeting, and nothing would be shared out until the arrears to members had been cleared or formally rescheduled. Nobody proposed expelling the treasurer. The sharper decision was the quieter one. The cooperative would rather show a smaller, truer number than a surplus that could not survive a careful reading.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"surplus","definition":"Money left over after costs have been met."},{"word":"ledger","definition":"The record of money coming in, going out, and still owed."},{"word":"reconciliation","definition":"A check that makes two sets of figures agree."},{"word":"dividend","definition":"A share of a surplus paid out to members."},{"word":"solvent","definition":"Able to pay the amounts that are owed."},{"word":"arrears","definition":"Money that is overdue and still unpaid."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How was the independent examination of the year supposed to be paid for?","options":["From the outgoing treasurer's own savings","From a new sale to the catering clients","From the unpaid loans of individual members","From the operating budget"],"correctAnswer":"From the operating budget","explanation":"The members voted for an examination paid from the operating budget, not from a person's pocket.","supportingSentence":"They voted to postpone any distribution and to commission an independent examination of the year, paid from the operating budget rather than from anyone's pocket.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does the treasurer's defence fail to face?","options":["The loan money had never reached the bakery.","The annual meeting had been moved to another town.","The people who had lent the money were members, so the debt was already inside the cooperative.","The supplier had already refused any further credit."],"correctAnswer":"The people who had lent the money were members, so the debt was already inside the cooperative.","explanation":"He said the loans would worry only an outsider, but members such as Mara were the ones still waiting to be repaid.","supportingSentence":"The loans, he argued, were internal and would only worry an outsider.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is likely to happen to the reported surplus once the loans are entered as debts again?","options":["It will grow by the cost of the van repairs.","It will be paid at once to the unnamed catering clients.","It will get smaller, because those amounts will no longer swell it by being called sales.","It will be given to the supplier as a cash gift."],"correctAnswer":"It will get smaller, because those amounts will no longer swell it by being called sales.","explanation":"Each mislabelled loan had enlarged the surplus by the same amount. Putting the loans back as debts reverses that effect.","supportingSentence":"Each invented sale reduced the recorded debt and enlarged the surplus by the same amount.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what does solvent mean?","options":["Able to pay what is owed","Closed until the next annual meeting","Recently sold to a new owner","Willing to publish every transaction"],"correctAnswer":"Able to pay what is owed","explanation":"The treasurer wanted a supplier to see the cooperative as solvent before giving it more credit, meaning able to meet its debts.","supportingSentence":"The outgoing treasurer said he had been trying to present the cooperative as solvent to a supplier who wanted reassurance before extending credit.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer finish on the figure the cooperative prefers to show?","options":["To argue that member loans are against the law","To suggest the supplier should take over the bakery","To prove that Nia wanted the treasurer removed","To show that a figure people can trust mattered more than an impressive one"],"correctAnswer":"To show that a figure people can trust mattered more than an impressive one","explanation":"The ending turns on a smaller, truer number rather than a surplus that a careful reading would undo.","supportingSentence":"The cooperative would rather show a smaller, truer number than a surplus that could not survive a careful reading.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":428,"questionCount":5}$rbq3437$::jsonb,
  'ef5aea077ef2ad1bad026a538b8c4c7db7cc9a6cfe724badda8fd401d430be07',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

