  'text',
  $rbq4641${"topic":"science","wordCount":438,"questionCount":5}$rbq4641$::jsonb,
  $rbq49925${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"The Estuary Survey","story":"The survey boat left the jetty at Marrow Mouth on a falling tide, with five stations marked on a transect from the road bridge to the outer buoy. At each station the crew lowered a probe for salinity and turbidity, then towed a small net for a fixed two minutes. They kept two nets ready so that a torn mesh would not force them to reuse a contaminated one. They recorded the time of each cast, because a falling tide keeps moving while the crew is still writing. The aim that morning was modest. They wanted a description of that tide, at those points, under the weather they actually had, which was a gray sky and a light wind from the land.\n\nStation four broke the pattern the tide model had led them to expect. Salinity fell sharply while the neighboring stations stayed brackish, and the water in the sample bottle looked clearer than the plume they had crossed upstream. One technician thought the probe housing had trapped a pocket of river water and then released it at the wrong moment. The other thought they had crossed a real freshwater pulse. Arguing on the deck would not settle it. A second instrument, kept sealed in a dry box and not used for the routine casts, could settle the narrower question of whether the first probe was lying.\n\nThe duplicate reading agreed with the odd one. The drop was in the water, not in the housing. That agreement did not identify the cause. Overnight, the board that manages the upper sluice had opened a gate for maintenance, and a pulse of river water could have traveled down on the ebb. The crew had not seen the gate, had not measured the release, and had not repeated the station on the next tide. A matching pair of probes supports the claim that station four was fresher at that hour. It does not support a claim about the whole estuary, and it does not, by itself, name the sluice as the source. Cause would require a record the boat did not carry.\n\nThey wrote the station up as an exception with both readings attached, and they left the model untouched. Models are updated from a pattern, not from a single defended point. Before they claimed even the local fact in the shared report, they logged the net count separately, so that a later reader would not treat the clearer water and the animal tally as one result. The survey could stand for what it had repeated. It would not be asked to stand for what it had only happened to meet.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"transect","definition":"A line of sampling points laid out across an area."},{"word":"salinity","definition":"How salty the water is."},{"word":"turbidity","definition":"How cloudy the water is because of material mixed in it."},{"word":"brackish","definition":"Slightly salty, between fresh water and seawater."},{"word":"pulse","definition":"A short-lived body of different water moving through the estuary."},{"word":"sluice","definition":"A gate that can be opened to let river water through."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the duplicate reading show about the odd salinity result?","options":["The drop existed only in the tide model, which the crew then rewrote.","The drop appeared at every station from the bridge to the outer buoy.","The drop came from a torn net reused at that station.","The drop was in the water, not in the first probe’s housing."],"correctAnswer":"The drop was in the water, not in the first probe’s housing.","explanation":"The second reading agreed with the first, so the drop was in the water rather than in the housing.","supportingSentence":"The drop was in the water, not in the housing.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does it matter that the second instrument had stayed sealed and out of the routine casts?","options":["It could test the first probe without having shared the same handling.","The crew needed it as a spare net if the mesh tore.","The tide model could be changed only by an instrument kept in a box.","It had already been used at the outer buoy and was known to read low."],"correctAnswer":"It could test the first probe without having shared the same handling.","explanation":"Because it had not been used for the routine casts, it could check whether the first probe was at fault.","supportingSentence":"A second instrument, kept sealed in a dry box and not used for the routine casts, could settle the narrower question of whether the first probe was lying.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why would it be a mistake to treat the overnight gate opening as a proven cause of the fresher reading?","options":["The sluice board had written to confirm that the gate stayed shut.","The crew neither saw the release nor measured it, so the link stays possible rather than shown.","The second probe disagreed with the first and left the drop itself in doubt.","Station four was saltier than the model had expected, not fresher."],"correctAnswer":"The crew neither saw the release nor measured it, so the link stays possible rather than shown.","explanation":"The crew did not see or measure the release, so the open gate remains a possible cause, not a proven one.","supportingSentence":"The crew had not seen the gate, had not measured the release, and had not repeated the station on the next tide.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what is the “transect”?","options":["The fixed two-minute tow used to collect animals.","The freshwater movement that may have come from the gate.","The line of stations from the road bridge to the outer buoy.","The dry box in which the spare probe was stored."],"correctAnswer":"The line of stations from the road bridge to the outer buoy.","explanation":"The five stations were marked on a transect running from the road bridge to the outer buoy.","supportingSentence":"The survey boat left the jetty at Marrow Mouth on a falling tide, with five stations marked on a transect from the road bridge to the outer buoy.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage refuse to let the survey stand for what the crew only happened to meet?","options":["To argue that surveys should stop measuring how salty the water is.","To recommend that the gate be closed for good.","To show that the tide model had already predicted the odd station.","To keep one unexpected encounter from being reported as a general pattern."],"correctAnswer":"To keep one unexpected encounter from being reported as a general pattern.","explanation":"The writer wants a repeated result to be reported, and a single unexpected meeting not to be stretched into a general claim.","supportingSentence":"It would not be asked to stand for what it had only happened to meet.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":438,"questionCount":5}$rbq49925$::jsonb,
  '05ce7927c6b813c47f4f777dcd91282e656a2b2643d7fdeebb0cc1043d803870',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '3e1e2113-201d-41d2-a041-c4f8a5107502'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5381${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq5381$::jsonb,
  $rbq86285${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Ethics Seminar","story":"The ethics seminar met in a room that was too warm for argument. Imani, who led the module, set out a hypothetical case rather than a news story. A student intern in a hospital laboratory notices that a senior researcher has quietly removed two inconvenient results from a chart before a review meeting. The intern has no permanent post, the researcher has been a generous mentor, and the altered chart would make a treatment look more reliable than the full data allow. Imani asked the group not to race for a verdict. She wanted the obligations mapped before anyone claimed the moral high ground.\n\nLeila spoke first. She argued that confidentiality inside a team did not extend to concealment, and that a patient who might rely on the chart had a stronger claim than a mentor's reputation. If the intern stayed silent, the omission would harden into the record. Jonas disagreed, though not because he trusted the researcher. He said an intern who disclosed the change without understanding the statistical context might damage a study that was otherwise sound, and that going public before an internal check was a way of performing integrity rather than practising it. A third student suggested a quiet question at the next supervision, which the others treated as hopeful and possibly naive.\n\nWhat made the hour difficult was Imani's refusal to rescue them. Each time a speaker looked at her for a nod, she asked what fact would change that speaker's mind. Leila admitted that a pre-registered plan explaining the removed results would weaken her case for outside reporting. Jonas admitted that if the intern had already raised the gap inside the lab and been told to forget it, delay would become complicity. The dissent was no longer a clash of personalities. It was a disagreement about sequence: whether loyalty to a process came before loyalty to the people who would never read the chart.\n\nFor the written task, Imani told them to draft the strongest argument against their own first position, using only the facts she had given. Students who added dramatic details, she warned, would be marked down. Leila left irritated and more careful. She had wanted a rule she could carry into a real placement. If the seminar had ended with a single recommended action, she might have felt finished. Instead she had a set of conditions under which silence stopped being neutral. That, Imani said as they packed up, was the point of an ethics class that respected its subject. A clean answer, offered too early, would have taught them only how to sound certain.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"hypothetical","definition":"Imagined in order to test an idea"},{"word":"confidentiality","definition":"The duty to keep information private"},{"word":"concealment","definition":"The act of hiding something that others may need to know"},{"word":"disclosed","definition":"Made known after it had been held back"},{"word":"complicity","definition":"Taking part in a wrong by going along with it"},{"word":"neutral","definition":"Not favouring either side"}],"quiz":[{"question":"What does complicity mean when Jonas talks about further delay?","options":["Sharing the wrong by agreeing to leave it alone","Repeating a calculation without help","Asking a mentor for a permanent job","Publishing the chart before the review meeting"],"correctAnswer":"Sharing the wrong by agreeing to leave it alone","explanation":"If the intern has already been told to forget the gap, staying quiet would mean joining in the wrong.","supportingSentence":"Jonas admitted that if the intern had already raised the gap inside the lab and been told to forget it, delay would become complicity.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What did Imani ask the students to write?","options":["A report sending the researcher to the hospital board","A news story about a real laboratory scandal","A dramatic version with facts she had not given","The strongest case against the view they had started with"],"correctAnswer":"The strongest case against the view they had started with","explanation":"The task was to argue against their own first position, using only the facts from the case.","supportingSentence":"For the written task, Imani told them to draft the strongest argument against their own first position, using only the facts she had given.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What did the argument turn into by the end of the hour?","options":["A question about which loyalty ought to come first","A quarrel about whether the intern liked the mentor","A vote that Imani settled with one instruction","A plan to invent results that were never collected"],"correctAnswer":"A question about which loyalty ought to come first","explanation":"It was no longer a clash of personalities but a disagreement about which duty came before the other.","supportingSentence":"It was a disagreement about sequence: whether loyalty to a process came before loyalty to the people who would never read the chart.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does Leila leave without feeling the problem is finished?","options":["Imani has told the intern to stay quiet forever","She has limits on silence, not one action for every case","The written task has already been marked and returned","Jonas has shown that the chart was never changed"],"correctAnswer":"She has limits on silence, not one action for every case","explanation":"A single recommended action might have let her feel finished, but she leaves with conditions instead.","supportingSentence":"Instead she had a set of conditions under which silence stopped being neutral.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does Imani refuse to name one right action?","options":["The hospital has banned discussion of research","She wants the rest of the room to side with Jonas","An early neat answer would have taught them only how to sound sure","She has not decided which essay will score highest"],"correctAnswer":"An early neat answer would have taught them only how to sound sure","explanation":"A clean answer, given too early, would have taught them only how to sound certain.","supportingSentence":"A clean answer, offered too early, would have taught them only how to sound certain.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq86285$::jsonb,
  '3e1e2113201d81d29041c4f8a5107502e4aeb964927f1ca32a41b72dc7a6ec2d',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '58b082a8-fa4e-4f4c-ac36-a5deb302ae97'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5379${"topic":"school_college","wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq5379$::jsonb,
  $rbq75871${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"School / College","title":"The Faculty Open Meeting","story":"The faculty open meeting had been called to discuss a single proposal, though everyone arrived ready to discuss the workload hiding inside it. The draft asked schools to bring the deadline for the spring essay forward by ten days, so that markers would finish before the examination board. Adeel, who chaired the meeting, reminded the room that this was a consultation, not a vote. Staff from the larger programmes said the change would stop a crush of scripts in the same week as invigilation. Others, particularly those who taught students with jobs, said an earlier deadline would simply move the crush into the students' lives, where several essays would now fall on one weekend.\n\nThe first contribution came from Nora, who ran the evening classes. She described a cohort that wrote after shifts, and she asked what support would be removed if the calendar tightened. A colleague in assessment answered that the proposal included an extra drafting tutorial, which might offset the lost days for students who could attend it. That reply split the room. People who already taught over their contracted hours heard another task. People who marked into the night heard a chance to protect the board from hurried judgements. Adeel kept a running list on the board, two columns that refused to become a consensus.\n\nHalfway through, a student representative asked whether the consultation had included the people who would lose the ten days. The question was fair, and it exposed a gap. The paper had been circulated to staff and to programme boards, but not to the student forum, because the chair of that forum had been away and nobody had wanted to delay. Adeel did not defend the omission. He said a change to assessment timing was exactly the sort of decision that became contentious when it was explained after it was finished. If the faculty approved the shift and only then discovered a clash with placements, the reversal would cost more goodwill than the original delay.\n\nNo vote was taken. Adeel adjourned the decision until a revised paper could set the staff saving beside a proper account of student clustering, including the evening cohort Nora had described. He asked for the comparison within two weeks and promised that the next open meeting would begin with it, rather than with another general defence of being busy. The minutes, which went out the next morning, were unusually plain. They recorded the proposal, the objection, and the condition on which discussion would resume. Anyone reading them could see that the faculty had chosen not to confuse speed with agreement.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"proposal","definition":"A formal suggestion offered for discussion"},{"word":"workload","definition":"The amount of work someone is expected to do"},{"word":"consensus","definition":"General agreement in a group"},{"word":"circulated","definition":"Sent round so that people can read it"},{"word":"contentious","definition":"Likely to cause serious disagreement"},{"word":"adjourned","definition":"Stopped for now and left until later"}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did staff who already taught extra hours hear the offered tutorial?","options":["As a reason to close the evening classes","As proof that students had approved the change","As one more demand on time they were already giving","As a way to finish the examination board early"],"correctAnswer":"As one more demand on time they were already giving","explanation":"People teaching over their contracted hours heard the extra tutorial as another task.","supportingSentence":"People who already taught over their contracted hours heard another task.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How much earlier did the draft want the spring deadline?","options":["A full fortnight earlier","One weekend later","Until after the examination board","Ten days earlier"],"correctAnswer":"Ten days earlier","explanation":"The draft asked schools to bring that deadline forward by ten days.","supportingSentence":"The draft asked schools to bring the deadline for the spring essay forward by ten days, so that markers would finish before the examination board.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does adjourned say about the decision?","options":["It was delayed until a revised paper was ready","It was deleted from the faculty record","It was passed by a vote that same evening","It was left to the evening students alone"],"correctAnswer":"It was delayed until a revised paper was ready","explanation":"Adeel put the decision off until a paper could set the staff saving beside the effect on students.","supportingSentence":"Adeel adjourned the decision until a revised paper could set the staff saving beside a proper account of student clustering, including the evening cohort Nora had described.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What risk does Adeel see in deciding before the clash with placements is known?","options":["The examination board would reject every script","Changing course later would cost more goodwill than waiting","The minutes would be too short to send out","Nora would have to cancel the evening cohort"],"correctAnswer":"Changing course later would cost more goodwill than waiting","explanation":"Approving the shift and only then finding a clash would cost more goodwill than the original delay.","supportingSentence":"If the faculty approved the shift and only then discovered a clash with placements, the reversal would cost more goodwill than the original delay.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the final sentence place speed beside agreement?","options":["To blame students for missing the open meeting","To show that the chair lost a vote","To present the delay as a deliberate choice","To announce that the earlier deadline has been approved"],"correctAnswer":"To present the delay as a deliberate choice","explanation":"A reader of the minutes can see that the faculty refused to treat speed as if it were agreement.","supportingSentence":"Anyone reading them could see that the faculty had chosen not to confuse speed with agreement.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq75871$::jsonb,
  '58b082a8fa4e0f4c2c36a5deb302ae9769eeed25a55a6f059165b3522c375d70',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '4aa01bdb-8149-47cb-ad70-2da7696c7119'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4639${"topic":"science","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq4639$::jsonb,
  $rbq57376${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"The Footbridge Load Test","story":"The footbridge over the canal had been closed since the bearings were replaced in April. Before the town reopened it, the engineering office ran a load test that a crowd could not provide. Water tanks were rolled to midspan in stages, filled from a metered standpipe, and held until the deflection gauges stopped moving. A tank can be emptied in a known order. A Saturday crowd cannot, and an uncontrolled unloading is a poor way to learn whether a span comes back to its original line. The test asked a static question: under this much weight, applied gradually, how far does the deck move, and does it recover?\n\nAt the full planned load the midspan deflection sat a little above the figure in the design model and comfortably inside the allowance in the code the office was required to use. Nothing cracked. Nothing failed to recover when the tanks were drained. The gauges were read by two people, and the two figures had to agree before a stage was accepted. A single glance at a flickering dial was not going to carry a reopening. A councillor, thinking of the weekend market, treated that margin as a reason to remove every restriction and let the bridge carry a dense crowd from the first hour. The project engineer, Samir Adeyemi, separated the result from that wish. The model had been slightly stiff, which is a reason to trust the model less as a forecast, not a reason to ignore the measurement. The measurement itself had answered only the question the tanks were built to ask.\n\nWhat the tanks did not reproduce was rhythmic walking. A crowd that falls into step can drive a light footbridge farther than the same weight standing still, and the code's static allowance does not, by itself, describe that motion. Adeyemi would reopen the bridge with a posted limit on the number of people, a request that walkers not stop in a tight group at midspan, and a repeat measurement on the first busy afternoon, taken from gauges left in place. If that afternoon stayed calm, the limit could be reviewed. If it did not, the office would already have evidence gathered under the use people actually make of the span.\n\nThe councillor accepted the posted limit once the alternative was explained as a gap in the test rather than as caution for its own sake. Passing a staged load test is a real pass. It certifies the answers to the questions that were loaded onto the deck, and it leaves the others still open.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"bearings","definition":"The parts that support the bridge and were replaced before the test."},{"word":"midspan","definition":"The middle of the bridge’s length, between its supports."},{"word":"deflection","definition":"How far the deck bends downward under a load."},{"word":"allowance","definition":"The amount of movement the required code permits."},{"word":"static","definition":"Still, rather than produced by people walking in step."},{"word":"rhythmic","definition":"Regular and repeated, like footsteps that fall into the same timing."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did the engineering office apply the weight during the test?","options":["With a dense crowd standing still at the middle","With people walking in step from the first minute","With one sudden unloading of the whole deck","With water tanks brought on in stages"],"correctAnswer":"With water tanks brought on in stages","explanation":"Water tanks were rolled to the middle in stages and filled from a metered pipe.","supportingSentence":"Water tanks were rolled to midspan in stages, filled from a metered standpipe, and held until the deflection gauges stopped moving.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What gap in the tank test makes the code’s limit for a load held still an incomplete answer?","options":["The tanks never reproduced the extra movement that walking in step can cause.","The movement at the middle went far outside the code’s permitted limit.","The design model had been too soft and had predicted too much bend.","The tanks cracked the new supports and the deck did not rise again."],"correctAnswer":"The tanks never reproduced the extra movement that walking in step can cause.","explanation":"A crowd walking in step can move a light bridge more than the same weight standing still, and the tanks did not copy that.","supportingSentence":"What the tanks did not reproduce was rhythmic walking.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the demand for two matching gauge readings suggest about the decision that follows the test?","options":["The code limit could be read only on a weekday morning.","It was too important to rest on one unchecked look at the dial.","The dials were known to be broken and were only there for show.","A councillor had to carry each tank onto the deck in person."],"correctAnswer":"It was too important to rest on one unchecked look at the dial.","explanation":"Two people had to agree, because one quick look at the dial was not enough to carry a reopening.","supportingSentence":"A single glance at a flickering dial was not going to carry a reopening.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what is the “allowance”?","options":["The extra distance caused by people walking in time.","The number of market stalls planned for the weekend.","The limit of movement the code permits under the required check.","The volume of water one tank holds before it is drained."],"correctAnswer":"The limit of movement the code permits under the required check.","explanation":"The measured bend stayed inside the allowance, meaning inside the movement the code permits.","supportingSentence":"At the full planned load the midspan deflection sat a little above the figure in the design model and comfortably inside the allowance in the code the office was required to use.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage end by saying a real pass still leaves other questions open?","options":["To cancel the reopening even if a person limit is posted.","To recommend a denser crowd from the first hour of the market.","To show that the tanks never returned to their starting line.","To stop a successful still-weight test from being treated as the answer to every use of the bridge."],"correctAnswer":"To stop a successful still-weight test from being treated as the answer to every use of the bridge.","explanation":"The ending treats the pass as real for the questions that were loaded, and it warns the reader that other questions remain open.","supportingSentence":"It certifies the answers to the questions that were loaded onto the deck, and it leaves the others still open.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq57376$::jsonb,
  '4aa01bdb814937cb8d702da7696c7119158030c9bb3c161a0e7db5a05fd419d0',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'aca11073-eee9-4506-a1b7-e9c01f21f44f'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5200${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq5200$::jsonb,
  $rbq50358${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"The Grain Cooperative","story":"The grain cooperative met in the storehouse because the office was too small for an argument about money. Forty members sat on benches between pallets of wheat, and the smell of clean grain made the quarrel feel slightly indecent. The harvest had been decent, the moisture readings were safe, and the buyer from the mill was offering a price that would cover costs. Chairperson Ruth Alemayehu read the offer once, then read the cost of keeping the grain until March, when the price might be kinder and the rats would certainly be interested.\n\nThe split in the room was not between optimists and pessimists. It was between members who could wait and members who could not. Marek Dolny, who farmed the smallest acreage and had a loan due in November, said storage was a luxury described as strategy. If the cooperative held everything, he would have to sell his share outside the group at a worse price, which made unity look like a fine word for other people's patience. Several older members, who remembered a year when holding grain had paid for new roofs, answered that breaking the pool would teach the mill to deal with them one by one. Both speeches were about fear, and both were accurate.\n\nRuth refused a show of hands until the numbers were on the board. Storage would cost each tonne a fee. A March sale at the average of the last five years would still beat the present offer, but the average was not a promise, and two of those years had been unusual. She proposed a division that pleased no one completely. Sixty percent of the pooled grain would be sold now. Forty percent would be stored, and members who needed cash could assign their share to the sale without leaving the cooperative. The mill would be told the truth about the volume, because a surprise in January was a good way to earn a worse contract later.\n\nMarek asked who would own the stored grain if prices fell. Everyone who left a share in, Ruth said, and no one who had been forced to pretend they could wait. The vote passed by a margin wide enough to act and narrow enough to require good manners. They set a rota for checking temperature in the bins, since damp grain becomes a collective loss faster than a low price does. Before the benches were stacked, Ruth wrote the agreement in sentences a tired person could not misread. The cooperative had not discovered the future of the market. It had discovered which risks the members were actually able to share.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"moisture","definition":"The amount of water held in the grain."},{"word":"acreage","definition":"The amount of land a person farms."},{"word":"pooled","definition":"Put together into one shared supply."},{"word":"margin","definition":"The size of the gap between the two sides of a vote."},{"word":"rota","definition":"A schedule of turns."},{"word":"collective","definition":"Shared by the whole group."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What share of the pooled grain did Ruth propose to sell at once?","options":["Sixty percent","Forty percent","The entire harvest","Only Marek's part"],"correctAnswer":"Sixty percent","explanation":"Her division sold sixty percent now and kept forty percent in store.","supportingSentence":"Sixty percent of the pooled grain would be sold now.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does a proposal that pleased nobody completely suggest about this meeting?","options":["Ruth wanted the mill to set the price without a discussion","The cooperative had voted to close after November","The group could move only if each side received part of what it wanted","Every member could afford to wait until March"],"correctAnswer":"The group could move only if each side received part of what it wanted","explanation":"Some members needed cash and others feared facing the mill alone, so a total win for either side would not have held the room.","supportingSentence":"She proposed a division that pleased no one completely.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How does the final rule answer Marek's complaint about unity?","options":["The older members will pay his loan from their own roofs","He can take money now and still remain in the cooperative","His grain will be sold in secret at a lower price","He must wait until March or lose his membership"],"correctAnswer":"He can take money now and still remain in the cooperative","explanation":"Marek feared that unity meant waiting he could not afford, but members who need cash may assign their share to the sale without leaving.","supportingSentence":"Forty percent would be stored, and members who needed cash could assign their share to the sale without leaving the cooperative.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does rota mean in the plan for the stored grain?","options":["A schedule of turns for checking the bins","The mill's written offer from the morning","A promise that the March price will rise","The fee charged on every tonne"],"correctAnswer":"A schedule of turns for checking the bins","explanation":"They set a rota so members would take turns checking the temperature of the stored grain.","supportingSentence":"They set a rota for checking temperature in the bins, since damp grain becomes a collective loss faster than a low price does.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer say the cooperative had not discovered the future of the market?","options":["To suggest Ruth had hidden the mill's letter","To admit that the vote had failed","To show that the real gain was knowing which risks they could share","To warn that the moisture readings were false"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the real gain was knowing which risks they could share","explanation":"The next sentence locates their success in the risks they could actually share, not in predicting the price.","supportingSentence":"The cooperative had not discovered the future of the market.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq50358$::jsonb,
  'aca11073eee9350671b7e9c01f21f44fa78ac7902dc7c412ce72efbc715cc96d',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

