  'text',
  $rbq5201${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq5201$::jsonb,
  $rbq80480${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"The Harvest Contract","story":"Laleh Moradi spread the draft contract on a crate between the bean rows, because she wanted Tomas Rehn to see the field that the clauses were describing. He bought for three restaurants in the city and had driven out with a cooler, a pencil, and a reputation for paying on time, which mattered more to her than praise. The document promised a weekly delivery of salad leaves, herbs, and tomatoes from June to September, at a price fixed in April. The price looked fair on a dry morning. Laleh had farmed long enough to know that April could not sign for August.\n\nShe had marked two paragraphs. The first let the buyer reduce an order with three days' notice. The second let the grower miss a delivery only in a case of force beyond control, a phrase that sounded legal and meant whatever a disappointed cook decided. Hail, she said, was beyond control. A soft week of rain that split the tomatoes was also beyond control. Tomas said he could not run a menu on maybes. She said she could not insure his menu with her family's field. They were both describing risk, and neither wanted to be the only one holding it.\n\nThey walked the irrigation line while the argument cooled. Tomas asked what a good contract would feel like in a bad week. Laleh answered with numbers rather than with mood. If she warned him by Sunday evening, he would accept a substitute crop of equal value, chard for spinach, or smaller cucumbers for the ones his cooks preferred. If she failed to warn him, she would discount the following week. If he cancelled inside three days, he would pay half, because seed, water, and labour did not cancel themselves. He copied the three sentences onto the back of the draft, where amendments can be more honest.\n\nThere was still the matter of quality, which could ruin a fair price. They agreed that leaves would be picked before dawn, rinsed once, and rejected only for slime, wilt, or grit that a second rinse would not move. A bruise on a tomato was not a moral failure. It was a reason to send the fruit for sauce at a lower rate, which Laleh would rather do than watch good food become a lecture. Tomas initialled the margin. He would take the revised pages to his partners, who liked local produce in the abstract and liked predictable plates in practice. Laleh kept a copy of his handwritten lines. Until the signatures existed, the field remained hers, and the menu remained a hope with a date on it.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"clauses","definition":"The separate rules written into the contract."},{"word":"discount","definition":"A reduction in the price."},{"word":"amendments","definition":"Changes added to a document."},{"word":"wilt","definition":"The soft, drooping state of leaves that are no longer fresh."},{"word":"abstract","definition":"Existing as a general idea, not in daily practice."},{"word":"initialled","definition":"Marked with initials to show agreement."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What would Tomas pay if he cancelled an order inside three days?","options":["Nothing, provided the morning was dry","Only the cost of seed for the next year","Half the agreed amount","The full price plus a fine for the cooler"],"correctAnswer":"Half the agreed amount","explanation":"If he cancelled inside three days, he would pay half, because the seed, water, and labour would already have been spent.","supportingSentence":"If he cancelled inside three days, he would pay half, because seed, water, and labour did not cancel themselves.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What danger did Laleh see in the phrase about force beyond control?","options":["Tomas would stop paying for herbs from June onward","The family field would have to be sold before September","The cooler could not keep salad leaves cold","Weather losses could be treated as her failure if a cook decided so"],"correctAnswer":"Weather losses could be treated as her failure if a cook decided so","explanation":"The phrase sounded legal, but in practice it meant whatever a disappointed cook decided, even when hail or splitting rain was outside her control.","supportingSentence":"The second let the grower miss a delivery only in a case of force beyond control, a phrase that sounded legal and meant whatever a disappointed cook decided.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What principle do the three handwritten conditions put into practice?","options":["A late surprise by either side creates a cost for the person who causes it","The restaurants may change the price every morning","The grower must deliver tomatoes even after hail","Quality no longer matters once the April price is fixed"],"correctAnswer":"A late surprise by either side creates a cost for the person who causes it","explanation":"If she fails to warn him she discounts the next week, and if he cancels late he still pays half.","supportingSentence":"If she failed to warn him, she would discount the following week.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Among the reasons for rejecting leaves, what does wilt describe?","options":["Rain that splits ripe fruit","Leaves that have gone limp","A lower price paid for sauce tomatoes","A signature at the bottom of the page"],"correctAnswer":"Leaves that have gone limp","explanation":"Leaves could be rejected for slime, wilt, or grit, so wilt names a kind of spoilage rather than a price or a signature.","supportingSentence":"They agreed that leaves would be picked before dawn, rinsed once, and rejected only for slime, wilt, or grit that a second rinse would not move.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer have Laleh open the contract between the bean rows?","options":["To show that Tomas had forgotten his pencil","To prove the price had been fixed in August","To tie the written rules to the living crop they would govern","To suggest the city restaurants had already closed"],"correctAnswer":"To tie the written rules to the living crop they would govern","explanation":"She wants Tomas to see the field that the clauses describe, so the agreement is not only words on paper.","supportingSentence":"Laleh Moradi spread the draft contract on a crate between the bean rows, because she wanted Tomas Rehn to see the field that the clauses were describing.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":437,"questionCount":5}$rbq80480$::jsonb,
  'b8fe8a7f60fe83ed10ab7f39afda69dd2f7802b38de3d25f55b5a419e6cce16a',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '5cfa0d3c-8d0f-4b5e-acd6-dd6da87da699'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4560${"topic":"travel","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq4560$::jsonb,
  $rbq49572${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"The Hill Town in Winter","story":"The bus climbed into Branth on a road the driver took at a respectful crawl, and Anwen Corven was the only passenger left when it stopped beside a shuttered bakery. She had come to close her uncle's house for the cold months, a duty she had delayed until the risk to the pipes, rather than family feeling, made delay look careless. Snow lay in a thin, decisive sheet. The main street kept a grocer, a post counter, and a cafe that heated one room and left the other in the dark.\n\nThe house smelled of cold ash and of a soap her uncle had bought in bulk. Anwen lit the stove, found the stopcock, and drained the lines into buckets she emptied behind the shed. A neighbour, Marta Quill, arrived with a flask of soup and the news that the lane to the upper fields would not be cleared before Thursday. She did not ask how long Anwen meant to stay. In Branth, help often came as a useful fact rather than an embrace, and the form of it left a person free to refuse without making a speech. Anwen accepted the soup and declined the spare bed. She wanted to hear the house in the night, in case a pipe still held a pocket of water that would freeze and split.\n\nAfter dark the town contracted to a few lit squares and the occasional tick of timber in the roof. Anwen sat with her uncle's accounts, orderly through most of the year and then, in the final month, suddenly brief, with sums rounded as if exactness had begun to tire him. She added a cover note for the solicitor and wound the kitchen clock, which gained a minute she decided not to chase. In the morning Marta knocked again, this time to warn of a thaw that would soften the lane into mud before it removed the snow. Anwen looked at the locked piano and left it locked. She had come to make the building safe, not to test how much of her uncle she could bear to hear. When the bus descended she carried one box of papers and a jar of the bulk soap she could not quite justify leaving to freeze. The rest stayed where he had arranged it. On the way down she thought that closing a house was a form of reading. She learned what a person had maintained, what he had let blur, and what a visitor had no right to tune. The piano was one of those last items. So was the clock's extra minute.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"shuttered","definition":"Closed up, with shutters or similar covers over the windows."},{"word":"stopcock","definition":"A valve that turns a water pipe on or off."},{"word":"exactness","definition":"The quality of being precise and careful about details."},{"word":"solicitor","definition":"A lawyer who handles legal papers and advice."},{"word":"thaw","definition":"A period of warmer weather that melts snow and ice."},{"word":"justify","definition":"To give a good reason for a choice."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What danger finally pushed Anwen to close the house?","options":["The solicitor demanded the piano at once","Cold could damage the pipes if they were left full","The bakery was about to open for the season","Marta needed help in the upper fields"],"correctAnswer":"Cold could damage the pipes if they were left full","explanation":"She had put the visit off until the risk to the pipes made more delay look careless.","supportingSentence":"She had come to close her uncle's house for the cold months, a duty she had delayed until the risk to the pipes, rather than family feeling, made delay look careless.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do the final month's figures suggest about her uncle?","options":["He had moved out of the house before the snow","He had hidden a debt from the solicitor","His careful habits with numbers had begun to weaken","He paid the grocer only in rounded coins"],"correctAnswer":"His careful habits with numbers had begun to weaken","explanation":"The accounts were orderly for most of the year, then suddenly brief, as if being exact had started to tire him.","supportingSentence":"Anwen sat with her uncle's accounts, orderly through most of the year and then, in the final month, suddenly brief, with sums rounded as if exactness had begun to tire him.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why might Anwen remove the soap and still leave the piano untouched?","options":["Both objects were already promised to Marta","The piano was the only item the solicitor would insure","The bus driver had asked for a jar and a song","One act protects the house, while the other would disturb her grief"],"correctAnswer":"One act protects the house, while the other would disturb her grief","explanation":"She takes the soap so it will not freeze, but she will not open the piano and test how much of her uncle she can bear to hear.","supportingSentence":"She had come to make the building safe, not to test how much of her uncle she could bear to hear.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does thaw mean in Marta's morning warning?","options":["A warming that starts to melt the snow","A lock fitted to a winter piano","A formal visit from the solicitor","A holiday when the lane stays shut"],"correctAnswer":"A warming that starts to melt the snow","explanation":"Marta warns that the thaw will turn the lane to mud as it takes the snow away.","supportingSentence":"In the morning Marta knocked again, this time to warn of a thaw that would soften the lane into mud before it removed the snow.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer return to the clock's extra minute at the end?","options":["To prove the downhill bus was running late","To explain why the cafe heated only one room","To set it among the things Anwen chose not to correct","To show that Marta had wound the clock on Thursday"],"correctAnswer":"To set it among the things Anwen chose not to correct","explanation":"After she thinks of closing the house as reading what she has no right to tune, the extra minute belongs with the locked piano.","supportingSentence":"So was the clock's extra minute.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq49572$::jsonb,
  '5cfa0d3c8d0f8b5eacd6dd6da87da699b8d9c94ba4251ebef1599ca44bd6723f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '846d7c00-6a10-41b5-a10a-74d4806ced2c'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4665${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq4665$::jsonb,
  $rbq46361${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The Lighthouse with No Sea","story":"The lighthouse stood in a field of barley, which visitors found funny until they stayed for the night. There was no sea. The nearest harbour was two days' walk and had its own lamp, a sensible one, pointed at water. This tower pointed at the land. Its keeper, a woman named Nell, trimmed the wick at dusk and set the beam turning, and the farmers who understood the arrangement did not laugh at all.\n\nWhat the light kept back was not water but a tide of pale fog that rose from the sunken pastures when the ground cooled. The fog was beautiful and dishonest. It laid a smooth floor over ditches, wells, and the low walls that stopped cattle from wandering into the bog. A person could walk into it believing the field continued, and the field would fail to catch them. Before the tower, the district had lost someone most autumns, usually a neighbour who knew the paths and therefore did not think to doubt them. Knowledge, Nell liked to say, was a fair-weather instrument.\n\nOn the night the mechanism jammed, she climbed the stair with a can of oil and the particular anger of a person whose tool has betrayed a promise. The beam had stopped with its face towards the empty hill, leaving the lane and the pastures in the dark. Fog was already pooling, bright where starlight touched it and grey in its folds. She freed the seized wheel, burned two fingers, and set the lamp moving again. Down in the lane she heard a shout, then a laugh that was mostly relief. Someone had been about to step where the wall should have been, and the returning light had drawn the wall back into the world. She stayed at the lamp until the lane was a clear dark ribbon and the fog had drawn back to the pasture line.\n\nIn the morning the council suggested, as councils do, that a fence would be cheaper than a keeper. Nell took them to the pasture edge and asked them to find the corner post in daylight, which they did, and then to imagine the same post after dark under a white floor. A fence unseen is a story people tell about safety. A light is a fact that arrives in the eye. They renewed her stores without quite admitting she had won. That evening the beam turned over barley, stone, and the pale beginning of fog, and the sea, wherever it was, went on without them. The tower kept its own shore, which moved, and which would not be argued with.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"dishonest","definition":"Pleasant on the surface while hiding a real danger."},{"word":"mechanism","definition":"The moving parts that make the lamp turn."},{"word":"seized","definition":"Stuck so tightly that it will not move."},{"word":"arrangement","definition":"A working plan that the people involved understand."},{"word":"instrument","definition":"A tool used to do or judge something."},{"word":"renewed","definition":"Supplied or continued again for another period."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why do the farmers refuse to laugh at the tower?","options":["They use the light to ripen the barley after dark","They have never seen fog lie on the pastures","They know the turning beam is holding a real danger back","They are paid to pretend the harbour is close by"],"correctAnswer":"They know the turning beam is holding a real danger back","explanation":"The farmers understand the arrangement, so the inland light is a serious tool rather than a joke.","supportingSentence":"Its keeper, a woman named Nell, trimmed the wick at dusk and set the beam turning, and the farmers who understood the arrangement did not laugh at all.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"seized\" mean about the wheel?","options":["Stuck fast so that it would not turn","Newly oiled and ready for the next dusk","Stolen from the sensible lamp at the harbour","Polished until it threw a clean white beam"],"correctAnswer":"Stuck fast so that it would not turn","explanation":"Nell has to free the wheel before the lamp can move again, so the word means jammed.","supportingSentence":"She freed the seized wheel, burned two fingers, and set the lamp moving again.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What rises from the sunken pastures?","options":["Smoke from a fire lit in the barley","A night tide rolling in from the harbour","A pale fog when the ground grows cool","Oil running down from the jammed lamp"],"correctAnswer":"A pale fog when the ground grows cool","explanation":"The beam holds back pale fog that rises from the pastures as the ground cools, not seawater.","supportingSentence":"What the light kept back was not water but a tide of pale fog that rose from the sunken pastures when the ground cooled.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does Nell think a fence would fail after dark?","options":["Once the fog hides it, an unseen fence cannot warn anybody","Cattle would pull the corner posts out by morning","The council has already spent every coin on oil","The pasture edge is too wet to hold any wood"],"correctAnswer":"Once the fog hides it, an unseen fence cannot warn anybody","explanation":"She calls an unseen fence only a story about safety, while light is a fact that reaches the eye.","supportingSentence":"A fence unseen is a story people tell about safety.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention neighbours who knew the paths?","options":["To show that familiarity can make the fog more dangerous","To explain why visitors came to the tower only in autumn","To prove that the old paths were drawn incorrectly","To blame Nell for refusing to walk beside them"],"correctAnswer":"To show that familiarity can make the fog more dangerous","explanation":"The people who trusted what they already knew were the ones who did not doubt the covered ground.","supportingSentence":"Before the tower, the district had lost someone most autumns, usually a neighbour who knew the paths and therefore did not think to doubt them.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq46361$::jsonb,
  '846d7c006a10f1b5d10a74d4806ced2c22455e91c3ba2086b508107d60656b6b',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '1717c143-1c1b-45df-ad51-1469a8a958b7'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4992${"topic":"technology","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4992$::jsonb,
  $rbq50281${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"The Migration Window","story":"The records office had reserved the migration window six weeks earlier, because the deed room was closed on Sundays and the scanning contractor could spare two operators overnight. During those hours the old catalog would accept no new entries, no corrections, and no loans. On Friday the clerks had tied a paper slip to every folder still sitting in the exception tray, so that a half-described packet would not be copied as if it were complete. The window was a pause the office had agreed to keep. It was not a setting buried in the machines.\n\nAt 01:40 the report on the scanned deeds came back short. Four hundred and twelve image sets had crossed onto the new catalog, and nineteen had arrived with a page count that did not match the manifest prepared on Thursday. Mara Quinn, the lead archivist, refused to treat the shortfall as a rounding error. A missing page in a deed packet could be a map, a signature, or the clause that later settled a boundary dispute. She stopped the copy job and asked the operators to reopen the nineteen folders on the bench, not on the screen.\n\nThe night supervisor wanted a full rollback, with the new catalog wiped and the attempt repeated the following Sunday. Quinn argued for a narrower repair. Folders already verified could remain, each stamped with the time it had passed the check, while the nineteen stayed on the old system under a hold that blocked lending and editing. An extra forty minutes would let the operators rescan those packets and nothing else. What she would not accept was a completed status on a batch that had failed its own count. The supervisor disliked leaving two catalogs live past dawn, because Monday staff might edit the wrong one out of habit, and a later reconciliation would then have to unpick those fresh edits as well as the original copy.\n\nThey settled the dispute in the corridor, beside the trolley of packets that had never been scanned. The window would stretch, but only for the named folders, and a handwritten list would be taped to both terminals before anyone went home. At 06:20 the nineteen sets matched. Quinn did not call the migration finished. She called the verified portion provisionally accepted and left the hold in place until a second reader, arriving with the day shift, compared ten packets drawn from the list at random. A window, she told the supervisor, is a promise about what will not happen while the copy is in motion. It is not a certificate that the copy is already right.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"manifest","definition":"A list of what a batch is supposed to contain."},{"word":"shortfall","definition":"An amount that is missing compared with what was expected."},{"word":"rollback","definition":"A return of a system to the state it had before a change."},{"word":"reconciliation","definition":"The work of making two records agree again."},{"word":"provisionally","definition":"For the moment only, until a further check is done."},{"word":"hold","definition":"A block that stops a record from being lent or edited."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How many image sets arrived with a page count that failed to match the list prepared on Thursday?","options":["Nineteen","Ten","Four hundred and twelve","Forty"],"correctAnswer":"Nineteen","explanation":"Nineteen sets arrived with a page count that did not match the manifest.","supportingSentence":"Four hundred and twelve image sets had crossed onto the new catalog, and nineteen had arrived with a page count that did not match the manifest prepared on Thursday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Quinn’s decision to stop the whole copy, after most sets had already crossed, suggest about her standard?","options":["She would stop a transfer only if most of the image sets failed.","A small number of doubtful deed packets was enough to halt the transfer.","She believed the new catalog should be wiped before any page was checked.","She wanted the night supervisor to cancel the scanning contract."],"correctAnswer":"A small number of doubtful deed packets was enough to halt the transfer.","explanation":"She halted the whole job over nineteen doubtful packets, so the small count was enough.","supportingSentence":"She stopped the copy job and asked the operators to reopen the nineteen folders on the bench, not on the screen.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does a day-shift reader still sample packets after the night crew has made the nineteen sets match?","options":["The handwritten list had been lost before anyone went home.","The old catalog had started accepting new loans again at 06:20.","The night match still needed a check by someone who had not done the repair.","The day shift was expected to rescan every deed in the building."],"correctAnswer":"The night match still needed a check by someone who had not done the repair.","explanation":"She kept the result provisional until a second reader from the day shift checked a sample.","supportingSentence":"She called the verified portion provisionally accepted and left the hold in place until a second reader, arriving with the day shift, compared ten packets drawn from the list at random.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this passage, what does “provisionally” say about acceptance of the verified portion?","options":["It was accepted forever because the night counts had matched.","It was rejected and deleted from both catalogs.","It was certified by the contractor as a finished legal deed.","It was accepted only until a further check was done."],"correctAnswer":"It was accepted only until a further check was done.","explanation":"The portion was accepted for the moment, with a hold left in place until another reader checked it.","supportingSentence":"She called the verified portion provisionally accepted and left the hold in place until a second reader, arriving with the day shift, compared ten packets drawn from the list at random.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage end by calling the window a promise rather than a certificate?","options":["To separate a rule about pausing work from proof that the copy is correct.","To blame the contractor for every packet that was never scanned.","To argue that Sunday is the only night a catalog can be copied.","To recommend that paper folders replace the new catalog."],"correctAnswer":"To separate a rule about pausing work from proof that the copy is correct.","explanation":"The ending wants the reader to see that the window limits activity during the copy and does not prove the copy is right.","supportingSentence":"A window, she told the supervisor, is a promise about what will not happen while the copy is in motion.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq50281$::jsonb,
  '1717c1431c1bc5df4d511469a8a958b70d87cab6b741e7936986a3829b35386f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'd733a67f-b2ef-4759-a694-b673f9ca5d71'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4992${"topic":"technology","wordCount":426,"questionCount":5}$rbq4992$::jsonb,
  $rbq76212${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"The Municipal Sensor Grid","story":"The city installed its sensor grid over one winter, mostly where a crew could find power and a pole that already belonged to the streets department. That practical rule shaped the map more than the scientific brief did. Boxes measuring airborne particles and traffic noise clustered along the central corridor and the bus routes, where the poles were close together and the electric supply was easy to share. The warehouse streets by the river, and the courtyards behind the older blocks, received fewer instruments, not because the air there was presumed clean, but because a crew would have had to dig, negotiate access, or wait.\n\nBy spring the public dashboard was publishing hourly means. The means were real, and they were also smooth. A short spike from a queue of buses, or from a delivery engine left running in an alley, could rise and fall inside the hour and barely move the published figure. A clinic near the market asked for the ungrouped readings because it was studying cough complaints on weekday mornings. The contract with the installer allowed the city to publish the hourly aggregates and to use the raw stream for its own work. It did not allow the city to redistribute the raw files to outside groups. The clinic could be shown a chart in a meeting. It could not be handed the file.\n\nA councillor proposed moving two boxes to the park, where families would see them and where the readings would look kinder in a press note. The analyst, Ruth Hale, argued that a kinder reading would be a different measurement, not a corrected one. The park sits back from the idling buses. A sensor there would be easier to calibrate, because the air around it changes more slowly, and it would be worse as a witness to the exposure people actually walk through on the way to work. Siting, she told the committee, is part of the result. A grid reports the places it was permitted to stand, on the terms the contract allows the city to repeat.\n\nThe committee left the boxes along the corridor and asked Hale for a second product: a note, beside the hourly mean, marking hours in which the raw stream had crossed a stated threshold even if the average had not. She asked that the note name the threshold in ordinary units, so a reader could see what had been hidden rather than being told only that a mark existed. The grid would not become denser that season. It would become harder to misread.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"corridor","definition":"The main route through the city where many of the boxes were placed."},{"word":"spike","definition":"A short, sharp rise in a measurement that may not last."},{"word":"aggregates","definition":"Figures that combine many readings, such as an average for an hour."},{"word":"redistribute","definition":"To pass files on to people or groups outside the city."},{"word":"calibrate","definition":"To check an instrument so its readings can be trusted."},{"word":"siting","definition":"The choice of the places where instruments are put."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What form of the measurements was the city allowed to publish?","options":["The hourly aggregates","Only the readings taken beside the park","A list of weekday cough complaints","The raw files, sent to any outside clinic"],"correctAnswer":"The hourly aggregates","explanation":"The contract allowed the city to publish the hourly aggregates.","supportingSentence":"The contract with the installer allowed the city to publish the hourly aggregates and to use the raw stream for its own work.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What can happen during an hour even when the published hourly figure barely changes?","options":["The instruments stop recording until the next morning.","The hourly figure is replaced by a yearly average.","The contract requires the raw files to be sent to the clinic.","A short burst of particles or noise can rise and fall."],"correctAnswer":"A short burst of particles or noise can rise and fall.","explanation":"A short spike can rise and fall inside the hour without much changing the published figure.","supportingSentence":"A short spike from a queue of buses, or from a delivery engine left running in an alley, could rise and fall inside the hour and barely move the published figure.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What judgment is visible in the committee’s choice to keep the boxes where they were and add a threshold note?","options":["The present sites should stay, and the hourly figures should reveal when a short burst was smoothed away.","The boxes should be moved to the park before the next press note.","The clinic should receive the raw files despite the contract.","The grid should be turned off until every courtyard has an instrument."],"correctAnswer":"The present sites should stay, and the hourly figures should reveal when a short burst was smoothed away.","explanation":"The committee kept the corridor boxes and asked for a note when the raw stream crossed a threshold even if the average did not.","supportingSentence":"The committee left the boxes along the corridor and asked Hale for a second product: a note, beside the hourly mean, marking hours in which the raw stream had crossed a stated threshold even if the average had not.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In Hale’s remark, what does “siting” refer to?","options":["The hourly figure published on the public dashboard.","The clinic’s study of coughs on weekday mornings.","The choice of places where the instruments are allowed to stand.","A contract clause that forbids showing charts in meetings."],"correctAnswer":"The choice of places where the instruments are allowed to stand.","explanation":"Hale uses siting for the places the grid was permitted to stand, which she says is part of the result.","supportingSentence":"Siting, she told the committee, is part of the result.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the passage point out that the quieter streets were not skipped because anyone thought the air there was clean?","options":["To stop the reader from mistaking a practical gap for a scientific judgment.","To argue that the streets department should stop sharing power.","To recommend that the clinic end its weekday study.","To prove that courtyard air is always cleaner than air on the bus routes."],"correctAnswer":"To stop the reader from mistaking a practical gap for a scientific judgment.","explanation":"The writer includes the reason so the reader sees an installation limit, not a decision that those places were clean.","supportingSentence":"The warehouse streets by the river, and the courtyards behind the older blocks, received fewer instruments, not because the air there was presumed clean, but because a crew would have had to dig, negotiate access, or wait.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":426,"questionCount":5}$rbq76212$::jsonb,
  'd733a67fb2ef5759e694b673f9ca5d71eab3293c179ad75e451726f5b4e17a26',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

