  'text',
  $rbq4667${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq4667$::jsonb,
  $rbq30915${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"The Orchard behind the Clock","story":"Behind the town clock, if a person slipped through the narrow door marked for the keeper and did not stop at the weights, there was an orchard that the street had no business containing. The trees grew in rows that followed the gears. Apples hung on the hour side, pears on the quarter, and a single dark plum ripened only when the clock had been allowed to run a little slow. Children were not supposed to know. Children knew.\n\nThe keeper, Maud, wound the mechanism each evening with a key as long as her forearm. Time, in that town, was not a river. It was a store of hours that had to be lifted. When she wound too tightly, the apples blushed before they were sweet, and people who ate them arrived early to appointments that had not begun. When she wound too lightly, the pears stayed hard, and the afternoon stretched until errands forgot why they had been started. Maud had learned to stop at the sound the spring made, a low note that meant enough and not more.\n\nOne October the key broke. The clock continued for a day on what it had already been given, and then the hands began to hesitate, as if embarrassed. Fruit loosened and fell all at once, sweet and brown at the edges, and the town discovered how many of its courtesies depended on a promise the tower had been keeping. Bread cooled at the wrong hour. The school bell and the train disagreed. Maud went into the orchard with a basket and a quieter fear than she showed. The trees were not dying. They were spending, in a rush, the time she could no longer portion out.\n\nShe repaired the key with a pin and a great deal of patience. She oiled the pin, tested the turn, and listened for the low note before she trusted the key again. She did not wind the clock all the way back to pride. A little slowness, she decided, was a tax the town could pay in exchange for fruit that tasted of the day it was picked. The plum, which had waited through the whole emergency, darkened at last. Maud left it on the sill for whoever came to complain about the minutes. Most of them ate it without noticing the bargain. That was all right. An orchard behind a clock does not require belief. It requires a hand willing to stop before the spring is hurt, and a town willing to live inside the hours it can actually carry.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"containing","definition":"Holding something inside a space."},{"word":"mechanism","definition":"The working parts of the clock."},{"word":"portion","definition":"To share something out in measured amounts."},{"word":"hesitate","definition":"To pause, as if unsure about going on."},{"word":"courtesies","definition":"The small polite timings of ordinary shared life."},{"word":"bargain","definition":"An exchange in which each side gives something up."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why does the writer follow the secret with the fact that children know it?","options":["To prove that Maud has forgotten how to get in","To warn readers that the keeper's door stays locked","To explain why the single dark plum never ripens","To show the orchard is a secret the town only pretends to keep"],"correctAnswer":"To show the orchard is a secret the town only pretends to keep","explanation":"The short correction shows that the forbidden orchard is already known, whatever the rule says.","supportingSentence":"Children knew.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What happens to daily life when the key breaks?","options":["The trees die and the town gives up fruit altogether","The train leaves early and the school closes forever","Ordinary timings slip because the stored hours run out","People reach appointments before any apple has colour"],"correctAnswer":"Ordinary timings slip because the stored hours run out","explanation":"Bread, the school bell, and the train all lose their shared timing once the tower cannot portion out its hours.","supportingSentence":"Fruit loosened and fell all at once, sweet and brown at the edges, and the town discovered how many of its courtesies depended on a promise the tower had been keeping.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Which fruit grows on the hour side?","options":["The dark plum","The late figs","The apples","The pears"],"correctAnswer":"The apples","explanation":"Apples hang on the hour side, pears on the quarter, and the plum only when the clock runs a little slow.","supportingSentence":"Apples hung on the hour side, pears on the quarter, and a single dark plum ripened only when the clock had been allowed to run a little slow.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does Maud refuse to wind the clock all the way back?","options":["The repaired pin cannot lift a full store of hours","The children have asked her to stop the town completely","A little delay keeps the fruit tied to the day it is picked","A tighter spring would drop the plum before morning"],"correctAnswer":"A little delay keeps the fruit tied to the day it is picked","explanation":"She accepts a little slowness as the price of fruit that still tastes of the day it was picked.","supportingSentence":"A little slowness, she decided, was a tax the town could pay in exchange for fruit that tasted of the day it was picked.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"portion\" mean in this story?","options":["Hide it in a basket beneath the trees","Eat something quickly before it turns brown","Repair it with a pin and a little oil","Share it out in measured amounts"],"correctAnswer":"Share it out in measured amounts","explanation":"To portion the time out is to give it in controlled shares, which she cannot do while the key is broken.","supportingSentence":"They were spending, in a rush, the time she could no longer portion out.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":427,"questionCount":5}$rbq30915$::jsonb,
  'e64e15a40205c59a996042d62740e5e849f14d313c46080d5342afb09459c5cc',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '1859af8d-fd1a-413d-a9fe-07f45f9bf552'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4559${"topic":"travel","wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq4559$::jsonb,
  $rbq61299${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Travel","title":"The Overnight Sleeper","story":"Mira Elland boarded the overnight sleeper at Calden with a ticket that named a lower berth and a departure just after midnight. The compartment was narrower than the diagram on her phone had suggested, and the ladder to the upper bed left barely enough room for her coat. Across from her, a man in an unassuming grey jacket was already sorting maps into a stiff folder. He nodded once, the sort of greeting that declines conversation without quite refusing it. She returned the nod and set her bag beneath the seat, careful not to scrape his case.\n\nThe train left late. A conductor paused in the corridor to say that a signal fault beyond Virelle had made the schedule provisional, and that breakfast would be served only if they reached the port before the kitchen closed. Mira asked whether the delay would cost her the morning meeting. He could not promise either outcome, though he spoke as if lateness were a weather condition rather than a failure. When he had gone, her companion offered, without looking up, that the northern line often recovered time on the long straight after the river.\n\nShe had planned to read a brief and then sleep. Instead she listened to the change in the wheels whenever the train took a curve, and to the soft tick of the heating pipe above the door. Near two, the man closed his folder and said he was a surveyor hired to check a flood wall, not a tourist, and that he disliked arriving with opinions formed from a window. Mira understood the remark as a tacit request: he would share the space, but he would not perform the journey for her. She switched off her lamp. In the dark the compartment felt less cramped, as if the missing light had returned a little width to it.\n\nBy dawn the delay had narrowed to twenty minutes. She washed her face at the small metal basin and found the surveyor already dressed, his maps tied with cotton tape. On the platform at Hest he wished her a useful meeting, a phrase so exact that it stayed with her longer than a warmer farewell would have. Walking towards the tram, she realised she had spent the night practising a restraint the brief had never mentioned: how to occupy a confined place without claiming more of it than she had paid for. The meeting, when it came, would ask her for numbers. The journey had asked her for proportion, and she suspected that the second demand would be the one she answered less well.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"berth","definition":"A bed on a ship or train."},{"word":"unassuming","definition":"Quiet and not trying to attract attention."},{"word":"provisional","definition":"Temporary and still able to change."},{"word":"corridor","definition":"A long narrow passage between rooms or carriages."},{"word":"tacit","definition":"Understood without being said openly."},{"word":"confined","definition":"Small and limited in space."}],"quiz":[{"question":"By dawn, how much of the delay still remained?","options":["Twenty minutes","Half a day","A full morning","Two hours"],"correctAnswer":"Twenty minutes","explanation":"The story says that by dawn the delay had narrowed to twenty minutes.","supportingSentence":"By dawn the delay had narrowed to twenty minutes.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What habit seems to shape the way the surveyor approaches a job?","options":["He forms his report from the view out of the train","He accepts only work that comes with a private cabin","He asks fellow passengers to confirm his first impression","He waits to judge a site until he can examine it directly"],"correctAnswer":"He waits to judge a site until he can examine it directly","explanation":"He disliked arriving with opinions formed from a window, so he will not decide about the wall before he sees it.","supportingSentence":"Near two, the man closed his folder and said he was a surveyor hired to check a flood wall, not a tourist, and that he disliked arriving with opinions formed from a window.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What appears to worry Mira more as she leaves the station?","options":["Whether the kitchen will reopen at the port","Whether the surveyor will attend her meeting","Whether she will handle a shared space with the right restraint","Whether her lamp failed during the night"],"correctAnswer":"Whether she will handle a shared space with the right restraint","explanation":"She expects the meeting to ask for numbers, but she suspects she will handle the demand for proportion less well.","supportingSentence":"The journey had asked her for proportion, and she suspected that the second demand would be the one she answered less well.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does provisional mean when the conductor uses it about the schedule?","options":["Guaranteed to include breakfast","Still uncertain and open to change","Officially cancelled for the night","Reserved for first-class tickets only"],"correctAnswer":"Still uncertain and open to change","explanation":"A signal fault had unsettled the timetable, so the schedule was not yet fixed.","supportingSentence":"A conductor paused in the corridor to say that a signal fault beyond Virelle had made the schedule provisional, and that breakfast would be served only if they reached the port before the kitchen closed.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer describe the compartment feeling wider after the lamp goes out?","options":["To show that the space felt easier once she stopped filling it with attention","To suggest the surveyor needed the dark for his maps","To explain a fault in the heating pipe above the door","To reveal that the train had entered a broader tunnel"],"correctAnswer":"To show that the space felt easier once she stopped filling it with attention","explanation":"The extra width exists only in her perception, just after she accepts sharing the compartment quietly.","supportingSentence":"In the dark the compartment felt less cramped, as if the missing light had returned a little width to it.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq61299$::jsonb,
  '1859af8dfd1af13d39fe07f45f9bf552d4c05fa8b35b841e772683ff8b20c156',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '9cc01290-d0c1-4d8d-afbf-cd9dc4913c75'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4882${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4882$::jsonb,
  $rbq58318${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"The Pass before Dawn","story":"Before the eastern sky had gathered any colour, Lena left the tent and stood with her headlamp pointed at the snow. The pass was a pale notch between two dark shoulders of rock, and the wind coming through it carried a dry, metallic cold. Tomas was already roped in, checking each knot without rushing. They had studied the route for three evenings, tracing every contour on a map that made the slope look gentle, and both of them knew the paper had been kind. What lay ahead was a traverse across wind-packed snow, then a short rise to the saddle, and after that a descent that would not forgive a careless step.\n\nLena switched the lamp off. In the thin light of the stars she could see the line they had agreed on, a faint darker seam where yesterday's sun had softened the surface and the night had frozen it again. That crust would hold them if they moved early and kept their weight spread. If they waited for the comfort of daylight, the same crust would loosen into grains under their boots. Tomas heard the caution in the way she tested the first foothold twice before she committed her weight.\n\nThey moved without speaking. The rope between them stayed almost slack, a courtesy and a warning at the same time. Halfway across the slope, Lena's axe struck a hollow note. She stopped, probed with the shaft, and found the lip of a crevasse hidden under a bridge of frozen snow no wider than her pack. For a moment the whole morning narrowed to that dark gap. She backed away along her own steps, chose a line higher on the slope, and waited until Tomas had copied the detour exactly. Neither of them looked down for long.\n\nNear the saddle the wind strengthened and threw hard grains of ice against their cheeks. Lena's fingers had gone clumsy inside her gloves, and the buckle of her hip belt took three attempts. She thought of the hut on the far side and of the pride waiting there, then put the thought away. Pride was heavy, and she needed her attention for the last twenty metres, where the snow thinned over rock and every placement had to be precise. When they finally stood in the notch, the valley beyond was still in shadow, but a pale band had appeared above the farthest ridge. Tomas breathed out, and the wind tore the small cloud apart at once. They checked the rope, drank a little water that tasted of metal, and started down while the crust still answered their boots.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"traverse","definition":"A crossing that goes along the side of a slope."},{"word":"contour","definition":"A line on a map that shows the shape and height of the land."},{"word":"crevasse","definition":"A deep crack hidden in ice or snow."},{"word":"crust","definition":"A hard frozen layer on the surface of the snow."},{"word":"saddle","definition":"A low dip between higher parts of a mountain ridge."},{"word":"precise","definition":"Exact, with no careless space for error."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did Lena discover when the axe sounded hollow?","options":["A broken rope left by an earlier pair","A narrow snow bridge over a hidden crevasse","A metal marker pointing toward the hut","A glove frozen into the afternoon track"],"correctAnswer":"A narrow snow bridge over a hidden crevasse","explanation":"The hollow note leads her to the lip of a crevasse under a thin bridge of snow.","supportingSentence":"She stopped, probed with the shaft, and found the lip of a crevasse hidden under a bridge of frozen snow no wider than her pack.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why does Lena leave her first line and move higher?","options":["The wind is quieter on the upper rocks","Their lamp has failed and they need the dawn","Tomas has asked for a clearer view of the valley","The lower snow is hiding a gap she will not cross"],"correctAnswer":"The lower snow is hiding a gap she will not cross","explanation":"After finding the gap, she retraces her steps and waits until Tomas copies the higher line.","supportingSentence":"She backed away along her own steps, chose a line higher on the slope, and waited until Tomas had copied the detour exactly.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does \"traverse\" mean in this story?","options":["A steep climb straight to the summit","A rest taken in the mountain notch","A crossing that travels along the slope","A rope tied between two climbers"],"correctAnswer":"A crossing that travels along the slope","explanation":"The traverse is the sideways route across the wind-packed snow before the short rise.","supportingSentence":"What lay ahead was a traverse across wind-packed snow, then a short rise to the saddle, and after that a descent that would not forgive a careless step.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why do they move on from the notch rather than rest there?","options":["Their water has frozen and must be thawed below","They still depend on a crust that will not hold later","They have agreed to meet a guide at the trees","They are disappointed by the shadowed valley"],"correctAnswer":"They still depend on a crust that will not hold later","explanation":"They start down while the crust still holds, because waiting for daylight would loosen it.","supportingSentence":"They checked the rope, drank a little water that tasted of metal, and started down while the crust still answered their boots.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer mention the gentle map?","options":["To explain why they left the rope in the tent","To prove that the pair had never climbed before","To show that paper made the route seem safer than the snow","To suggest that the pass was closed for the season"],"correctAnswer":"To show that paper made the route seem safer than the snow","explanation":"The kind map makes the real slope feel harder, which is why they treat every step with care.","supportingSentence":"They had studied the route for three evenings, tracing every contour on a map that made the slope look gentle, and both of them knew the paper had been kind.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq58318$::jsonb,
  '9cc01290d0c16d8d1fbfcd9dc4913c756f1cf7dd21976cc31645002bc975cd19',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ee61a230-8fc7-4e40-a536-ae2b614807be'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5486${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":423,"questionCount":5}$rbq5486$::jsonb,
  $rbq85069${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"The Reserve Fund","story":"The housing cooperative had been proud of its reserve fund, and the pride had a spreadsheet to prove it. For eight years the residents of Block C had paid a little extra each month so that a roof, a lift, or a boiler could fail without becoming an emergency loan. In September the roof failed anyway. After the storms, water came through the top-floor ceilings, and the fund did exactly what it had been built to do. By October the roof was sound, the ceilings were dry, and the reserve that was supposed to cover a decade of major repairs had been cut almost in half.\n\nThe boiler was the problem the fund had been saving for next. The engineer's report, read aloud at the November meeting, gave it two winters at best, and one if the cold came early. A full replacement would cost more than the fund now held. Irene, who chaired the meeting and disliked drama, wrote two columns on the whiteboard. One was a special levy, collected over six months from every flat according to its size. The other was a loan from the local credit union, repaid over five years, with interest that would outlast the boiler itself. People who had already paid for the roof did not like the look of either column.\n\nThe argument that followed was less about the machine than about fairness across time. Residents who planned to move within a year preferred the loan, because a levy asked them to fund a boiler they might never use. Residents who intended to stay preferred the levy, because interest was a fee paid for the comfort of waiting. The treasurer added a third fact that cooled the room. If they borrowed, the cooperative's rules required them to keep a minimum cash balance, and the thinned reserve would sit under that line unless the repayments started high. A cheap loan on paper could become a covenant problem in practice.\n\nThey chose a narrower path. A modest levy would restore the reserve to its minimum by spring, and a smaller loan would cover the rest of the boiler, taken only after two further quotes. The work would be scheduled for late summer, when a failure would be an inconvenience rather than a freeze. Irene wrote the decision into the minutes in plain figures, without the adjectives the debate had attracted. The fund was no longer a cushion they could point to with satisfaction. It was a tool they had used, and now had to rebuild on purpose.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"reserve","definition":"Money kept aside for large future costs."},{"word":"levy","definition":"A special charge collected from the people who share a cost."},{"word":"interest","definition":"The extra money paid for borrowing."},{"word":"covenant","definition":"A binding rule, here one that sets how much cash must be kept."},{"word":"minimum","definition":"The lowest amount allowed."},{"word":"quotes","definition":"Prices offered by different suppliers for the same job."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the engineer's report say about how long the boiler might last?","options":["It would last another decade without any work.","It had already failed during the September storms.","It could be repaired from the roof money in October.","Two winters at best, and only one if the cold came early"],"correctAnswer":"Two winters at best, and only one if the cold came early","explanation":"The report, read at the November meeting, gave the boiler two winters at best, or one if the cold came early.","supportingSentence":"The engineer's report, read aloud at the November meeting, gave it two winters at best, and one if the cold came early.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What are the two groups of residents really disagreeing about?","options":["Whether people who may leave soon should pay now, or people who stay should pay to borrow","Whether the roof should be opened again in winter","Whether Irene should stop chairing the meetings","Whether the credit union should buy the building"],"correctAnswer":"Whether people who may leave soon should pay now, or people who stay should pay to borrow","explanation":"Residents who expect to move prefer a loan, while those who will stay prefer a levy. The dispute is about who carries the cost across time.","supportingSentence":"The argument that followed was less about the machine than about fairness across time.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What was the awkward result of using the fund to repair the roof?","options":["The residents immediately stopped every monthly payment.","The credit union cancelled the cooperative's right to meet.","The cooperative no longer held enough for the next large repair.","The ceilings were left wet until the summer."],"correctAnswer":"The cooperative no longer held enough for the next large repair.","explanation":"The roof repair did its job, but it cut the reserve sharply, and a new boiler would cost more than the fund then held.","supportingSentence":"By October the roof was sound, the ceilings were dry, and the reserve that was supposed to cover a decade of major repairs had been cut almost in half.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what is a levy?","options":["A discount on next year's payments","A special sum collected from the flats","A loan from the local credit union","A written warning from an engineer"],"correctAnswer":"A special sum collected from the flats","explanation":"Irene's first column is a special levy collected over six months from every flat, so the word means a charge on the residents.","supportingSentence":"One was a special levy, collected over six months from every flat according to its size.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer bother to say that the minutes used plain figures and no adjectives?","options":["To show the decision being recorded without the heat of the debate","To praise the credit union's advertising","To complain that Irene cannot write vividly","To hint that the boiler repair was cancelled"],"correctAnswer":"To show the decision being recorded without the heat of the debate","explanation":"The plain figures set the final choice apart from the strong language the argument had attracted.","supportingSentence":"Irene wrote the decision into the minutes in plain figures, without the adjectives the debate had attracted.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":423,"questionCount":5}$rbq85069$::jsonb,
  'ee61a2308fc7ce40b536ae2b614807bee10b41c391958cfcbe2668786e5c81d7',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '20bdef0a-a39f-4682-a146-a54044065c71'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq5315${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq5315$::jsonb,
  $rbq63628${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"The Return to Training","story":"Mara Ellison had been away from the track for eleven weeks when the physiotherapist finally cleared her to run. The clearance was narrower than the message she wanted to send her club. She could jog on grass, twice a week, for twenty minutes, and she was to stop at the first sharp pain along the metatarsal that had cracked in March. Mara read the note twice in the car park, then folded it into the glove box as if paper could be negotiated with later.\n\nHer coach, Idris Okonkwo, refused to treat the note as a suggestion. On the first evening he met her at the park with a watch, a soft path, and no stopwatch display she could see. He wanted the session to feel ordinary, he said, because drama had a way of inviting extra laps. Mara laughed, then fell quiet when the first kilometre took longer than her pride expected. The bone itself did not protest. What protested was her calf, tight from weeks of careful walking, and the impatient part of her mind that kept converting minutes into the races she had already missed.\n\nIdris let her finish the twenty minutes and then walked the long way back to the gate. He talked about load, not about medals. A tendon, he reminded her, does not read a calendar, and a club championship in June would still be there if she arrived able to accelerate. Mara wanted a date. He offered a review in ten days, provided the pain stayed dull and never localised. If it sharpened, they would stop and call the clinic before the next session, not after a heroic week.\n\nOver the following fortnight the disagreement became a routine. Mara arrived early, warmed up alone, and proposed one extra interval with the careful politeness of someone who already knew the answer. Idris declined each time in the same even voice, then gave her something else to measure: how she slept, whether the morning stairs were easy, and whether she could hop on the injured foot without bracing. Those were not romantic markers of fitness, and that was their value. By the third week she could admit, without sounding defeated, that the return had begun when she stopped trying to outrun the instructions. On the tenth evening the grass was wet, and she shortened her stride without being asked. Idris noticed and said nothing, which she understood as praise. At the gate he told her to keep the note where she would see it, so she moved it from the glove box into the side pocket of her bag.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"clearance","definition":"Official permission to start training again."},{"word":"metatarsal","definition":"One of the long bones in the foot."},{"word":"localised","definition":"Limited to one exact place."},{"word":"fortnight","definition":"A period of two weeks."},{"word":"interval","definition":"A short, faster section of a training session."},{"word":"bracing","definition":"Holding the body stiff in order to protect it."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What limit did the physiotherapist put on Mara's first runs?","options":["Twenty minutes on grass, twice a week","Full speed if she wore racing shoes","One easy lap whenever the club met","An hour on the track every morning"],"correctAnswer":"Twenty minutes on grass, twice a week","explanation":"The note allowed jogging on grass twice a week for twenty minutes, and told her to stop if the sharp pain returned.","supportingSentence":"She could jog on grass, twice a week, for twenty minutes, and she was to stop at the first sharp pain along the metatarsal that had cracked in March.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What risk sat behind Idris's refusal to give Mara a fixed race date?","options":["The club would move the championship to another city","The physiotherapist would ban her from the park","A date would make her calf loose too quickly","She might keep training through a warning sign in order to meet it"],"correctAnswer":"She might keep training through a warning sign in order to meet it","explanation":"Mara wanted a date, but Idris talked about a tendon that does not follow a calendar and about stopping before a heroic week.","supportingSentence":"If it sharpened, they would stop and call the clinic before the next session, not after a heroic week.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did Mara take Idris's silence to mean on the wet tenth evening?","options":["The sharp pain had come back in the bone","He wanted the clinic note hidden in the car again","She should stop running until June","Her own decision to shorten the stride was worthy of praise"],"correctAnswer":"Her own decision to shorten the stride was worthy of praise","explanation":"She shortened her stride without being asked, and she understood his silence after that choice as praise.","supportingSentence":"Idris noticed and said nothing, which she understood as praise.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In the condition for the ten-day review, what does localised describe?","options":["A championship date written on the note","Tiredness from the walk back to the gate","Pain that gathers in one exact spot","Stiffness that always disappears by morning"],"correctAnswer":"Pain that gathers in one exact spot","explanation":"Idris would review her progress only if the pain stayed dull and did not settle in one precise place.","supportingSentence":"He offered a review in ten days, provided the pain stayed dull and never localised.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does the writer call sleep, stairs, and hopping unromantic markers?","options":["To prove that Mara would miss the championship in June","To argue that Idris had given up on any improvement","To suggest the physiotherapist had written a careless note","To make those ordinary checks seem like the real signs of progress"],"correctAnswer":"To make those ordinary checks seem like the real signs of progress","explanation":"The writer says these plain signs were valuable exactly because they were not glamorous measures of fitness.","supportingSentence":"Those were not romantic markers of fitness, and that was their value.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":434,"questionCount":5}$rbq63628$::jsonb,
  '20bdef0aa39f56826146a54044065c71f20e9ad56e00e03b0c225ae39673ea79',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

