  'text',
  $rbq4880${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq4880$::jsonb,
  $rbq49301${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"The River Crossing at Noon","story":"By late morning the river had stopped looking like the river they had camped beside. Overnight it had been a clear run over pale stones, cold enough to ache and shallow enough that Ellis had crossed it in boots just to fill the pots. At noon it was a different animal. Snowmelt from the high basins had arrived all at once, and the water ran brown, shouldering against the bank and tugging at the exposed roots of a leaning pine. The ford they had marked on the map was still there in the sense that the map had not moved. The stones that made it a ford were not.\n\nEllis and Ruth stood on a gravel bar and watched a branch go past. It did not drift. It travelled, and where the channel bent it stood on end for a moment before the current took it under. Ruth threw a larger stick to the place they had intended to cross. The stick did not reach the far bank. It slid sideways and disappeared into the white seam where two currents met. That was enough measurement for her. Speed, depth, and the colour of the water were three arguments, and none of them was on their side.\n\nEllis felt the day pressing. The pass on the far side was the reason they had carried the extra food, and a delay here would mean a night in the open or a retreat down a valley they already knew. He waded in to the knee, roped, and felt the push arrive later than his eyes had promised, a soft heavy hand at the back of the leg. When he lifted his boot the gravel rolled. He came out without speaking, which Ruth understood as the end of the experiment. Pride could suggest a second try. The river had already given its answer.\n\nThey moved upstream to a place where the water widened and slowed, and they waited. Waiting looked like idleness from a distance. Up close it was work: drying boots, checking the rope, eating a little, and watching the colour thin as the afternoon cooled the high snow. Near evening the branch they threw landed, tumbled, and came to rest against a rock they could see. They crossed then, one at a time, facing upstream, each step searched for before it was trusted. On the far side Ruth emptied her boots and laughed once, not because the delay had been funny, but because they still had a day to spend and a river behind them that had not been allowed to spend it for them.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"snowmelt","definition":"Water released when snow higher up begins to thaw."},{"word":"ford","definition":"A shallow place where a river can be crossed on foot."},{"word":"current","definition":"The moving force of water flowing in a channel."},{"word":"gravel","definition":"Small loose stones on a riverbed or bar."},{"word":"idleness","definition":"The appearance of doing nothing useful."},{"word":"upstream","definition":"In the direction the water is coming from."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What reached the river around the middle of the day?","options":["A party of walkers crossing from the far bank","A fallen pine that dammed the whole channel","A sudden load of meltwater from the high basins","A night of rain that filled both cooking pots"],"correctAnswer":"A sudden load of meltwater from the high basins","explanation":"Snowmelt from the high basins arrives all at once and turns the clear river brown and strong.","supportingSentence":"Snowmelt from the high basins had arrived all at once, and the water ran brown, shouldering against the bank and tugging at the exposed roots of a leaning pine.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does \"ford\" mean in this story?","options":["A shallow crossing they had marked for walkers","A gravel camp on the bank nearest their tent","A rope bridge hung above the brown water","A deep bend where floating branches vanish"],"correctAnswer":"A shallow crossing they had marked for walkers","explanation":"The ford is the crossing marked on their map, even though the stones that made it usable have disappeared.","supportingSentence":"The ford they had marked on the map was still there in the sense that the map had not moved.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"Why does Ruth treat the thrown stick as a measurement?","options":["It marks a hidden bridge under the brown water","It shows the wind is stronger than the river","Its path shows a person would be swept off the crossing","It proves the far bank is only one step away"],"correctAnswer":"Its path shows a person would be swept off the crossing","explanation":"The stick slides off the line and vanishes where two currents meet, so the crossing would take a walker too.","supportingSentence":"It slid sideways and disappeared into the white seam where two currents met.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer list the small jobs of waiting?","options":["To show that patience here is active care, not laziness","To prove that Ruth wanted to wade across at once","To suggest they have forgotten the river entirely","To explain why the extra food has already run out"],"correctAnswer":"To show that patience here is active care, not laziness","explanation":"From far away the wait looks idle, but the list shows them drying, checking, and watching the water change.","supportingSentence":"Up close it was work: drying boots, checking the rope, eating a little, and watching the colour thin as the afternoon cooled the high snow.","skill":"intent"},{"question":"What does the evening branch tell them?","options":["Their rope is finally dry enough to be packed away","The rock on the far side has blocked the whole river","The water has calmed enough for a careful crossing","The noon melt is about to rise for a second time"],"correctAnswer":"The water has calmed enough for a careful crossing","explanation":"This branch stays visible against a rock, unlike the stick at noon, and only then do they cross.","supportingSentence":"Near evening the branch they threw landed, tumbled, and came to rest against a rock they could see.","skill":"inference"}],"wordCount":433,"questionCount":5}$rbq49301$::jsonb,
  '5e40ba1ba1233241f884bc6fd8abfc676b0819617bb5c2682e83f3b9248ced29',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'b7974202-a865-477e-a042-81a387bbbcc5'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4702${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq4702$::jsonb,
  $rbq90032${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"The Second Set of Minutes","story":"The minutes of the community board were four pages long, neatly typed, and already signed when Helen found the second set. The signed copy, which had been posted on the hall noticeboard that morning, recorded a short meeting: apologies, a report on the roof fund, and a vote to delay the summer fair. It did not mention the plot of land behind the hall. Helen remembered the plot being discussed, because she had spoken about it, and because the chair had asked for a show of hands.\n\nShe found the other pages in the secretary's draft folder, tucked behind a receipt for tea urns. They ran to six pages. They included the roof fund and the fair, in almost the same words, and then a resolution to seek offers for the plot, passed by five votes to two. The attendance list was longer than the one on the noticeboard. Two names appeared on the draft and not on the signed copy, and one name appeared on the signed copy only. The draft was dated the same evening and marked with the secretary's usual initials. It was not signed. It had, however, been printed, which meant somebody had considered it finished enough to leave the screen.\n\nHelen did not put either set back without reading them twice. A board could amend its minutes; that was ordinary. An amendment belonged in the next meeting's record, with the correction stated, not in a silent exchange of one evening for a shorter one. The posted minutes had been proposed, seconded, and approved at a brief gathering she had not been told about, held three days later, with just enough people present to make a quorum under the rules. The people present were the people whose names remained. The vote on the plot had not been corrected. It had been left in a folder.\n\nShe copied both sets on the hall machine and took the copies home, leaving the originals where they were. At the next open meeting she asked, under matters arising, which document the board regarded as its decision. The chair said the signed minutes were the record, and that drafts were only drafts. He did not explain the missing resolution. Nobody at the table claimed the shorter pages had been a clerical slip. Helen placed her copies on the table and said she was content for the room to read them. The mystery was no longer whether a second account existed. It was why the shorter one had been given the signature, and who had expected the longer one to stay behind a receipt.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"minutes","definition":"The written record of what was decided at a meeting."},{"word":"resolution","definition":"A formal decision put to a meeting."},{"word":"quorum","definition":"The smallest number of people needed for a meeting to decide."},{"word":"amend","definition":"To correct a record officially."},{"word":"draft","definition":"An earlier version that is not yet the official copy."},{"word":"initials","definition":"The first letters of a person's names, used here as a mark."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did the vote on the plot turn out in the longer pages?","options":["It was a tie, broken by the chair","It delayed the summer fair instead","It refused to discuss the land at all","It passed by five votes to two"],"correctAnswer":"It passed by five votes to two","explanation":"The longer pages include a resolution to seek offers for the plot, passed by five votes to two.","supportingSentence":"They included the roof fund and the fair, in almost the same words, and then a resolution to seek offers for the plot, passed by five votes to two.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do the changed attendance list and the later small meeting suggest?","options":["The plot was voted on again and defeated.","The fair was cancelled because nobody came.","Both sets of pages were lost before anyone could read them.","The shorter record was confirmed by a group that no longer matched the first meeting."],"correctAnswer":"The shorter record was confirmed by a group that no longer matched the first meeting.","explanation":"Names differ between the two lists, and the people who approved the posted minutes were the people whose names remained on that shorter list.","supportingSentence":"The people present were the people whose names remained.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What is Helen trying to do by placing both copies on the table?","options":["She is asking the board to sell the plot that same night.","She wants the chair to sign the longer set on the spot.","She is returning the receipt for the tea urns.","She wants the open meeting to read the two accounts for itself."],"correctAnswer":"She wants the open meeting to read the two accounts for itself.","explanation":"She brings copies to the open meeting, asks which document is the decision, and says she is content for the room to read them.","supportingSentence":"Helen placed her copies on the table and said she was content for the room to read them.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does quorum mean in this story?","options":["The plot of land behind the hall","A vote that counts only when nobody objects","A spare copy of the minutes kept at home","The smallest attendance the rules allow for a valid decision"],"correctAnswer":"The smallest attendance the rules allow for a valid decision","explanation":"The later gathering had just enough people present to make a quorum under the rules, meaning the minimum needed for the decision to count.","supportingSentence":"The posted minutes had been proposed, seconded, and approved at a brief gathering she had not been told about, held three days later, with just enough people present to make a quorum under the rules.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What shift of attention does the ending ask for?","options":["From whether two accounts exist to why the shorter one was signed","From the secretary's folder to a new chairperson","From the summer fair to a plan for repainting the hall","From the roof fund to the price of the tea urns"],"correctAnswer":"From whether two accounts exist to why the shorter one was signed","explanation":"The narrator says the mystery is no longer whether a second account exists. It is why the shorter one received the signature.","supportingSentence":"It was why the shorter one had been given the signature, and who had expected the longer one to stay behind a receipt.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":432,"questionCount":5}$rbq90032$::jsonb,
  'b7974202a865977e504281a387bbbcc5c8da9a901f582fc6284da7c0e75e3fdc',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '2e79ccaf-eab9-4e7f-a233-de95a596f938'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4887${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq4887$::jsonb,
  $rbq34225${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"The Shared Storage Cage","story":"The storage cages in the basement had been allocated years ago, one to each flat, though the wire grids had never kept people as tidy as the plan assumed. Over the autumn, the cage belonging to the flat above had begun to encroach on the shared aisle. A rolled carpet leaned into the walkway, and two unmarked crates were stacked where the fire notice said the route should stay clear.\n\nLena mentioned it to Samir, who was still learning which rules the building enforced. He moved the carpet and left the crates, saying he would sort them at the weekend. The weekend passed. When Lena went down for a suitcase she needed for a family visit, the crates had been joined by a lamp and a box of records. She could still open her own padlock, but only by turning sideways. She sent a photograph to the residents' chat and asked whether the committee had a view. Some neighbours treated the aisle as a communal margin that nobody should police. Others argued that a blocked route was a safety matter, and that goodwill had already been tried.\n\nAt the next house meeting the chair asked Samir to explain. He said the flat was smaller than he had expected, that the cage was already full, and that he had not understood the aisle to be forbidden rather than merely discouraged. A long-term resident read out the house note, which made each owner liable for items left outside the allocated space. Samir accepted the wording and promised to remove the overflow within a week. Then another problem surfaced. Lena's large suitcase, the one she had come to collect, was not in her cage. She was sure she had left it there after a trip in September. Samir looked uncomfortable and said a black case had been standing in the aisle when he moved his crates, and that he had set it on the low shelf beside the bikes so that nobody would trip.\n\nThey found it there, scuffed but intact. The meeting agreed a modest reform rather than a penalty. Every item kept in the basement would carry the flat number, the aisle would be marked with tape, and anything still outside a cage after the following Sunday could be moved by the caretaker and reclaimed for a small fee. Samir apologised properly once the suitcase was back in Lena's hands. If the shelf had not been checked, the loss might have hardened into an accusation. As it was, the basement stayed shared, but it would no longer depend on people guessing where a neighbour's patience ended.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"allocated","definition":"Set aside for a particular person"},{"word":"encroach","definition":"To spread slowly into space that is not yours"},{"word":"communal","definition":"Shared by the people who live in a place"},{"word":"liable","definition":"Legally responsible if something is wrong"},{"word":"reform","definition":"A change made to improve the way things work"},{"word":"accusation","definition":"A claim that someone has done something wrong"}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where had the black case been put?","options":["On the low shelf beside the bikes","In the flat directly above the basement","Under the fire notice in the lobby","Inside the cage Lena had locked"],"correctAnswer":"On the low shelf beside the bikes","explanation":"Samir said he had set the case on that shelf so that nobody would trip.","supportingSentence":"Samir looked uncomfortable and said a black case had been standing in the aisle when he moved his crates, and that he had set it on the low shelf beside the bikes so that nobody would trip.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does encroach describe here?","options":["A plan to rent the empty cages to outsiders","A number written on a box to show its owner","The small fee for collecting a moved item","One cage spreading into the shared walkway"],"correctAnswer":"One cage spreading into the shared walkway","explanation":"The cage above Lena had begun to push stored things out into the aisle.","supportingSentence":"Over the autumn, the cage belonging to the flat above had begun to encroach on the shared aisle.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What might the missing suitcase have become if the shelf had not been checked?","options":["A demand that Samir give up his flat","A reason to remove the tape from the aisle","A suspicion that someone had taken it","A decision to delete the fire notice"],"correctAnswer":"A suspicion that someone had taken it","explanation":"If nobody had checked the shelf, the loss might have hardened into an accusation.","supportingSentence":"If the shelf had not been checked, the loss might have hardened into an accusation.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What kind of response did the meeting choose?","options":["A bill that Lena should pay for a new lock","A future rule, rather than a punishment","An order that Samir leave the building","A plan to take away every cage"],"correctAnswer":"A future rule, rather than a punishment","explanation":"They agreed a modest reform rather than a penalty, then set labels, tape, and a reclaim fee.","supportingSentence":"The meeting agreed a modest reform rather than a penalty.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the closing sentence focus on patience?","options":["To show that sharing works only when the limit is visible","To recommend that residents abandon the basement","To suggest the fire notice caused the case to vanish","To blame Lena for arguing about an old suitcase"],"correctAnswer":"To show that sharing works only when the limit is visible","explanation":"The basement stays shared, but people will no longer have to guess where patience ends.","supportingSentence":"As it was, the basement stayed shared, but it would no longer depend on people guessing where a neighbour's patience ended.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":435,"questionCount":5}$rbq34225$::jsonb,
  '2e79ccafeab9fe7f4233de95a596f938c702598a985fa60dc1e12cdded4a38ca',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '6fb28a21-d08b-4fce-a452-6fb7e7814b73'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4888${"topic":"daily life","wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq4888$::jsonb,
  $rbq89786${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Daily Life","title":"The Sublet Agreement","story":"When Nadia agreed to sublet her flat for the spring, she expected the arrangement to stay simple. A colleague needed somewhere near the hospital, the dates did not clash with her research trip, and the rent would cover the months she was away. After the landlord gave only provisional consent, the building manager sent a four-page document whose clauses were more cautious than the conversation had been. One sentence, which dealt with liability for damage discovered after the subtenant left, was especially ambiguous. Nadia read it twice and still could not tell whether a mark on the hallway carpet would be charged to her or to the person who had been living there.\n\nThe subtenant, Marco, arrived with two suitcases and a careful inventory of everything already in the flat. He photographed the kettle, the curtains, and a hairline crack above the bathroom window. Nadia appreciated the caution, although she felt faintly inspected in her own home. They initialled each page, and she kept a copy in the drawer where the original lease lay. Before leaving, she explained which window stuck in damp weather and which neighbour objected if shoes were left in the corridor. Marco took notes and said he would write if any instruction proved unclear.\n\nThree weeks later an email reached her abroad. A pipe under the sink had leaked overnight, soaking the cabinet and warping two shelves. Marco had called a plumber, paid the call-out fee, and asked whether the cost could come from the deposit, which the landlord held in Nadia's name. The agreement mentioned urgent repairs, yet it never said who could authorise them when the named tenant was in another country. Nadia replied that he had acted reasonably, then forwarded the photographs and the receipt. The landlord answered that the deposit could not fund a repair nobody had approved in advance, and that any shortfall would remain her responsibility until the sublet ended.\n\nShe compared the clause with the lease for most of an evening. If the leak had come from Marco's negligence, she would have asked him to pay. The plumber's note, however, described a worn joint that would have failed no matter who was living there. She proposed a compromise. Marco would replace the shelves he had loaded with books, she would pay the plumber, and the landlord would inspect the pipe before anyone mentioned the deposit again. Both men accepted, although the landlord added that further consent would depend on clearer wording. Nadia saved the exchange. If she sublet the flat again, she would refuse any agreement whose key sentence could be read in two ways.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"provisional","definition":"Temporary, and not yet a final decision"},{"word":"liability","definition":"Legal responsibility when something goes wrong"},{"word":"ambiguous","definition":"Able to be understood in more than one way"},{"word":"inventory","definition":"A careful list of items"},{"word":"negligence","definition":"A failure to take proper care"},{"word":"compromise","definition":"An agreement in which each side gives something up"}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did Marco record as soon as he arrived at the flat?","options":["A claim for the cost of the plumber","A careful list of the things already there","A complaint about shoes left in the corridor","A request that the landlord rewrite every clause"],"correctAnswer":"A careful list of the things already there","explanation":"He arrived with an inventory of everything already in the flat, and he photographed the details.","supportingSentence":"The subtenant, Marco, arrived with two suitcases and a careful inventory of everything already in the flat.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did a leaking pipe turn into an argument about who should pay?","options":["The written rules never said who could approve an urgent repair","The landlord had already paid the deposit back to Marco","Marco hid the receipt and refused to name the plumber","The building manager blamed a stain on the hallway carpet"],"correctAnswer":"The written rules never said who could approve an urgent repair","explanation":"The agreement mentioned urgent repairs but not who could authorise them while Nadia was abroad.","supportingSentence":"The agreement mentioned urgent repairs, yet it never said who could authorise them when the named tenant was in another country.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In the damage clause, what does ambiguous tell us?","options":["The deposit had already been returned in full","The photographs were too dark to use as proof","The landlord had refused consent in writing","The wording could be read in more than one way"],"correctAnswer":"The wording could be read in more than one way","explanation":"Nadia still could not tell who would be charged for a mark on the carpet.","supportingSentence":"One sentence, which dealt with liability for damage discovered after the subtenant left, was especially ambiguous.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What remains true of Nadia after Marco moves in?","options":["The deposit may be spent the moment a pipe leaks","The neighbour must pay for any worn joint","The landlord can still leave unpaid costs with her","Marco replaces her as the only named tenant"],"correctAnswer":"The landlord can still leave unpaid costs with her","explanation":"Any shortfall stayed her responsibility until the sublet ended, even though Marco was living there.","supportingSentence":"The landlord answered that the deposit could not fund a repair nobody had approved in advance, and that any shortfall would remain her responsibility until the sublet ended.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why does the writer end with Nadia thinking about another sublet?","options":["To prove that the landlord had broken the law","To show that she now sees unclear wording as a risk","To argue that tenants should avoid travelling","To suggest that Marco should manage the building"],"correctAnswer":"To show that she now sees unclear wording as a risk","explanation":"She decides she would refuse a future agreement if its key sentence could be read in two ways.","supportingSentence":"If she sublet the flat again, she would refuse any agreement whose key sentence could be read in two ways.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":436,"questionCount":5}$rbq89786$::jsonb,
  '6fb28a21d08bbfce44526fb7e7814b73cbdb393c148c9285732adf33eae2a28e',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'c7328ede-58d7-47fa-aa08-9df518ed3dc9'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
  'text',
  $rbq4701${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq4701$::jsonb,
  $rbq78212${"mode":"reading","level":"C1","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"The Uncollected Crate","story":"The crate had been sitting in the parcel room of the river station since Monday, and by Thursday it had become furniture. Passengers rested bags against it. The night staff walked around it. Its label, addressed to a bookshop that had closed in the spring, gave a collection deadline that had already passed. On Friday morning the clerk, Hester, was told to process it as uncollected and move it to the holding cage before the weekend trains filled the room.\n\nShe turned the label toward the lamp and stopped. A second address had been written over the first in a darker ink, forwarding the crate to a storage unit on the industrial estate. The new line used the bookshop's name but a different contact number. The original consignee, still visible at the edge, was the shop. The consignment note in the pouch told a third story. It described the contents as paper samples, declared a modest value, and listed a weight that Hester checked twice against the scale by the door. Paper of that weight would have filled a crate twice the size, or this crate would have weighed half as much. She weighed it a third time, in case the scale had stuck, and got the same figure. The pouch held no sender's correction. The numbers could not describe the same object.\n\nHester did not ring the new number. A rewritten label was a request, not an instruction, and the request had arrived with no amendment from the sender and no identification from anyone claiming the goods. She photographed the label, the note, and the scale, then copied the measurements into the station log in the blunt sentences the company preferred. Under the rule for doubtful freight, an uncollected item with altered paperwork went to the holding cage and stayed there until the sender confirmed the contents in writing. The cage had a camera. The parcel room, at busy hours, did not.\n\nBefore she moved the crate, she looked again at the corner where the original label had been scraped. Fibres from an earlier sticker sat in the glue. The bookshop's name had been stuck on top of something else, which meant the closed shop was not the first story the crate had worn, only the latest. Hester noted that too. She wheeled the crate into the cage, sealed the record, and sent the photographs to the duty manager rather than to the number written in the dark ink. Whatever was inside could wait. The paperwork had already said too many things, and only one of them could be true.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"uncollected","definition":"Not picked up by the person it was sent to."},{"word":"consignee","definition":"The person or business named to receive a delivery."},{"word":"consignment","definition":"A load of goods sent together."},{"word":"declared","definition":"Stated officially, here about what is inside and what it is worth."},{"word":"amendment","definition":"An official change to a document."},{"word":"freight","definition":"Goods carried from one place to another."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Who received the photographs Hester took?","options":["The owner of the closed bookshop","The passengers waiting in the parcel room","The duty manager","The contact number written in darker ink"],"correctAnswer":"The duty manager","explanation":"Hester sent the photographs to the duty manager, not to the number written on the rewritten label.","supportingSentence":"She wheeled the crate into the cage, sealed the record, and sent the photographs to the duty manager rather than to the number written in the dark ink.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why is a stuck scale a weak way to explain the weight?","options":["A further weighing gave the same figure, and the pouch held no correction from the sender.","The duty manager had already replaced what was inside.","Paper samples are always heavier than books.","The scale was built only for passenger luggage."],"correctAnswer":"A further weighing gave the same figure, and the pouch held no correction from the sender.","explanation":"Hester weighed the crate again in case the scale had stuck, and the figure did not change. There was also no sender's correction in the pouch.","supportingSentence":"She weighed it a third time, in case the scale had stuck, and got the same figure.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does Hester treat as more important than opening the crate?","options":["A new label in her own handwriting","Selling the samples before the weekend trains","Agreeing a storage price by phone","A written confirmation from the sender, under the station's rule for doubtful freight"],"correctAnswer":"A written confirmation from the sender, under the station's rule for doubtful freight","explanation":"The rule keeps altered freight in the cage until the sender confirms the contents in writing. Hester follows that rule, and she decides the contents can wait.","supportingSentence":"Under the rule for doubtful freight, an uncollected item with altered paperwork went to the holding cage and stayed there until the sender confirmed the contents in writing.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"In this story, what is the consignee?","options":["The party named to receive the goods","The clerk who logs uncollected freight","The scale beside the parcel-room door","The darker ink used to change a label"],"correctAnswer":"The party named to receive the goods","explanation":"The original consignee, still visible at the edge of the label, was the shop the crate was supposed to reach.","supportingSentence":"The original consignee, still visible at the edge, was the shop.","skill":"vocabulary"},{"question":"What is the writer underlining in the final sentence?","options":["The documents tell conflicting stories, and they cannot all be honest.","Paper samples have no value of any kind.","Hester should deliver the crate herself that night.","The crate should be given back to the passengers."],"correctAnswer":"The documents tell conflicting stories, and they cannot all be honest.","explanation":"The ending says the paperwork had already said too many things, and only one of them could be true.","supportingSentence":"The paperwork had already said too many things, and only one of them could be true.","skill":"intent"}],"wordCount":431,"questionCount":5}$rbq78212$::jsonb,
  'c7328ede58d7d7fa7a089df518ed3dc9be0a54c2f35bcd9703e1cba88d7092ce',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'a3f6b222-e111-4219-ad01-1acb12a609fb'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'C1',
